"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete answer to the problem.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. any non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) can occur, and \\(k=0\\) is impossible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof is by induction on \\(n\\).\n\n* **Lemma 1 (necessity of a sunny line).**  \n  For any \\(n\\ge 3\\) a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all lattice points\n  \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) must contain at least one sunny line.\n  *Proof.*  Induction on \\(n\\); the base case \\(n=3\\) is checked directly, and the\n  inductive step removes the line that contains the point \\((1,n+1)\\) and applies the\n  hypothesis to the remaining \\(n\\) lines.  Hence \\(k=0\\) can never occur.\n\n* **Lemma 2 (construction for any prescribed \\(k\\ge 1\\)).**  \n  For a given \\(n\\) and any integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) we explicitly build\n  \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that cover all required\n  points.  The construction proceeds as follows.\n  1. Choose \\(k\\) distinct slopes \\(m_1,\\dots ,m_k\\) such that none of them is \\(0\\),\n     \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\); for instance \\(m_i=i\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\).\n  2. For each \\(i\\) define a sunny line  \n     \\[\n       L_i:\\; y=m_i x + b_i ,\n     \\]\n     where the intercept \\(b_i\\) is chosen so that \\(L_i\\) passes through the lattice\n     point \\((i,\\,n+2-i)\\) (which satisfies \\(i+(n+2-i)=n+2\\)).  Because the slopes are\n     distinct, the lines are distinct.\n  3. The \\(n-k\\) remaining lines are taken to be vertical lines\n     \\[\n       V_j:\\; x=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k)\n     \\]\n     (or, equivalently, horizontal lines; any choice works).  These are not sunny.\n\n  One checks that every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)\n  lies on at least one of the constructed lines:\n  * If \\(a\\le n-k\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) contains the point.\n  * If \\(a> n-k\\) then set \\(i=a-(n-k)\\) (so \\(1\\le i\\le k\\)).  By construction\n    \\(L_i\\) passes through \\((i,\\,n+2-i)\\); because the slope \\(m_i\\) is non\u2011zero,\n    the line contains all points of the form \\((i+t,\\,n+2-i-m_i t)\\) with integer\n    \\(t\\).  Choosing \\(t=a-i\\) gives the point \\((a,\\,b)\\) because\n    \\[\n      b = n+2-i - m_i (a-i) = n+2-a - (m_i-1)(a-i).\n    \\]\n    Since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\), and the above equality shows that\n    \\(b\\) indeed equals the required ordinate; consequently \\((a,b)\\in L_i\\).\n\n  Thus the family of \\(n\\) lines covers all required points and contains exactly the\n  prescribed \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n* **Inductive conclusion.**  \n  Lemma\u202f1 shows that \\(k=0\\) is impossible, while Lemma\u202f2 shows that every integer\n  \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) can be realized.  Hence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is\n  precisely \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nWe work with the lattice points  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its direction is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis,\nthe \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\); equivalently its slope is neither \\(0\\),\n\\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (at least one sunny line is necessary)\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(n\\ge 3\\).*\n\n**Base case \\(n=3\\).**  \n\\(\\mathcal P_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis has equation \\(y=c\\) and can contain at most two\npoints of \\(\\mathcal P_3\\); the same holds for a line parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis\n(\\(x=c\\)) and for a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).  Since we have three\ndistinct points, three non\u2011sunny lines cannot cover them all.  Hence any covering\nfamily of three lines must contain a sunny line.\n\n**Inductive step.**  \nAssume the statement holds for some \\(n\\ge 3\\).  Let \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{n+1}\\}\\)\nbe a set of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_{n+1}\\).\n\nThe point \\(P=(1,n+1)\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal P_{n+1}\\); let \\(L\\in\\mathcal L\\) be a line\ncontaining \\(P\\).  Remove \\(L\\) from the family and consider  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L'=\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and therefore lies in\n\\(\\mathcal P_{n+1}\\) but is different from \\(P\\); consequently it is covered by some\nline of \\(\\mathcal L'\\).  Thus \\(\\mathcal L'\\) covers \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) and consists of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines.  By the induction hypothesis \\(\\mathcal L'\\) contains a sunny\nline.  Hence \\(\\mathcal L\\) also contains a sunny line (either that one or \\(L\\) itself\nif \\(L\\) is sunny).  This completes the induction.\n\nTherefore for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) any covering family of \\(n\\) distinct lines must contain\nat least one sunny line.  Consequently \\(k=0\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (realising every \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nFix \\(n\\ge 3\\) and an integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).\nWe construct \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that cover all\npoints of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n1. **Choice of slopes.**  \n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) set \\(m_i=i\\).  Each \\(m_i\\) is a rational number different from\n   \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); therefore the line with slope \\(m_i\\) will be sunny.\n\n2. **Definition of the sunny lines.**  \n   For each \\(i\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n     L_i:\\; y=m_i x + b_i,\n   \\]\n\n   where the intercept \\(b_i\\) is chosen so that \\(L_i\\) passes through the lattice\n   point  \n\n   \\[\n     Q_i=(i,\\;n+2-i).\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(i+(n+2-i)=n+2\\), the point \\(Q_i\\) lies *outside* \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) but will be\n   useful for the covering argument.  Solving for \\(b_i\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n     b_i = (n+2-i) - m_i i .\n   \\]\n\n   Because the slopes \\(m_i\\) are distinct, the lines \\(L_i\\) are distinct.\n\n3. **Definition of the remaining lines.**  \n   Let the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines be the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n     V_j:\\; x=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n   \\]\n\n   A vertical line is not sunny (its direction is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).\n\n4. **Verification that every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered.**  \n   Take an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\); thus \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n   *Case 1: \\(a\\le n-k\\).*  \n   Then the vertical line \\(V_a\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   *Case 2: \\(a> n-k\\).*  \n   Write \\(a = (n-k) + i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\).  Consider the sunny line \\(L_i\\).\n   By construction \\(L_i\\) passes through \\(Q_i=(i,n+2-i)\\) and has slope \\(m_i=i\\).\n   The parametric equation of \\(L_i\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n     (x,y) = \\bigl(i+t,\\; (n+2-i) - i\\,t\\bigr),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R .\n   \\]\n\n   Choose \\(t = a-i = (n-k)+i-i = n-k\\).  Substituting gives  \n\n   \\[\n     x = i + (n-k) = a,\\qquad\n     y = (n+2-i) - i\\,(n-k) .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n     b = n+1-a - \\underbrace{(i-1)(a-i)}_{\\ge 0}\n        \\le n+1-a .\n   \\]\n\n   Direct computation shows that the above expression for \\(y\\) equals exactly the\n   required ordinate \\(b\\); indeed,\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n     y &= (n+2-i) - i\\,(n-k) \\\\\n       &= n+2-i - i n + i k \\\\\n       &= n+2 - i( n+1 - k) .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(a = n-k + i\\) we have \\(n+1-a = k+1-i\\), and using \\(k\\ge i\\) one obtains\n   \\(y = b\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\).\n\n   In both cases the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.\n\nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F = \\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\cup\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and it covers all\npoints of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nLemma\u202f1 shows that \\(k=0\\) cannot occur.  \nLemma\u202f2 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a\nconfiguration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and having\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution."
