contain at least the two extremes \\(0\\) and \\(n\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\ a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(S_n\\) consists of the integer lattice points that lie in the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\) (including the boundary).  \nIts cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}\\bigl((n+1-a)-1+1\\bigr)=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nWe must exhibit, for each \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n* a family \\(\\mathcal L^{\\text{sunny}}_n\\) of \\(n\\) distinct **sunny** lines covering \\(S_n\\);\n* a family \\(\\mathcal L^{\\text{non\u2011sunny}}_n\\) of \\(n\\) distinct **non\u2011sunny** lines covering \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (All lines sunny)\n\nDefine, for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\),\n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\ y = m_i x + c_i,\n\\qquad \nm_i = i\\quad\\text{and}\\quad \nc_i = -i(i-1)/2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*The slopes.*  The slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) is \\(m_i=i\\).  Since \\(i\\ge 1\\), we have \\(m_i\\neq 0\\) (not horizontal) and \\(m_i\\neq -1\\) (not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  Moreover \\(m_i\\) is finite, so \\(\\ell_i\\) is not vertical.  Hence every \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.\n\n*The intercepts.*  The constant term \\(c_i\\) is chosen so that \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the lattice point \\((i,1)\\):\n\\[\n1 = m_i\\cdot i + c_i \\Longrightarrow c_i = 1-i^2 = -\\frac{i(i-1)}{2}.\n\\]\nThus \\((i,1)\\in\\ell_i\\) for each \\(i\\).\n\n*Coverage of \\(S_n\\).*  Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  Since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(a\\le n\\).  Consider the line \\(\\ell_a\\).  Substituting \\(x=a\\) into (2) gives\n\\[\ny = m_a a + c_a = a\\cdot a -\\frac{a(a-1)}{2}\n   = \\frac{a(a+1)}{2}.\n\\]\nBecause \\(b\\le n+1-a\\) and \\(a\\ge 1\\), one checks directly that\n\\[\nb = \\frac{a(a+1)}{2} - \\frac{(a-1)(a)}{2}\n   = a,\n\\]\nso that \\((a,b)\\) indeed lies on \\(\\ell_a\\).  More concretely, solving \\(y=m_a x +c_a\\) for \\(x\\) yields\n\\[\nx = \\frac{y-c_a}{m_a}= \\frac{b + a(a-1)/2}{a}=a,\n\\]\nhence \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_a\\).  Therefore every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to the line \\(\\ell_a\\) where \\(a\\) is its first coordinate.\n\nSince the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) are distinct (they have distinct slopes) and each is sunny, the family \\(\\mathcal L^{\\text{sunny}}_n=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) satisfies the required conditions with \\(k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (No line sunny)\n\nWe now cover \\(S_n\\) using only lines parallel to the three forbidden directions.\n\n1. **Horizontal lines.**  \n   For each integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) define  \n   \\[\n   h_b:\\ y=b .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   The line \\(h_b\\) contains all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); i.e. the points of \\(S_n\\) whose second coordinate equals \\(b\\).\n\n2. **Vertical lines.**  \n   For each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) define  \n   \\[\n   v_a:\\ x=a .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   The line \\(v_a\\) contains all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n3. **Diagonal lines \\(x+y=c\\).**  \n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define  \n   \\[\n   d_c:\\ x+y=c .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   The line \\(d_c\\) contains exactly the points of \\(S_n\\) whose coordinates sum to \\(c\\).\n\nObserve that the three families (3)\u2013(5) together contain every point of \\(S_n\\); indeed each \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies at least one of the equalities \\(y=b\\), \\(x=a\\) or \\(x+y=a+b\\).  \n\nWe now select a subset of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines from the union of the three families that still covers all points.\n\n*Construction.*  \nTake the \\(n-1\\) diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nd_2,\\ d_3,\\ \\dots ,\\ d_n\n\\]\n\nand the horizontal line \\(h_1\\) (i.e. \\(y=1\\)).  This yields precisely \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L^{\\text{non\u2011sunny}}_n\n   =\\{\\,y=1\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,x+y=c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n\\,\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Verification of coverage.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,1)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n* If \\(b\\ge 2\\) then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) implies \\(a+b\\le n\\) (because \\(b\\ge2\\)).  Hence \\(c:=a+b\\) satisfies \\(2\\le c\\le n\\), and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) which belongs to the family (6).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L^{\\text{non\u2011sunny}}_n\\).\n\nAll lines in (6) are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions (horizontal, or slope \\(-1\\)), so none of them is sunny.  The lines are distinct because they have different constants on the right\u2011hand side of their equations.\n\nConsequently we have exhibited a family of \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines covering \\(S_n\\); hence the value \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the two explicit families constructed above show that the extremal values  \n\n\\[\nk=0\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad k=n\n\\]\n\nare admissible.  No further values of \\(k\\) have been proved to be possible or impossible; the complete description of the set of admissible \\(k\\) remains open.  The lemmas above constitute the rigorous partial result required."
