y=0\\).  \nTherefore none of the lines in (1) is sunny.\n\n*The lines are distinct.*  \n\\(L_1\\) has equation \\(y=1\\) while each \\(L_c\\) has a different constant term on the right\u2011hand side of \\(x+y=c\\); consequently all \\(n\\) lines are different.\n\n*Coverage of \\(S_n\\).*  \nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  \nTwo cases occur.\n\n1. **If \\(b=1\\).**  \n   Then \\((a,1)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(L_1\\).\n\n2. **If \\(b\\ge 2\\).**  \n   Because \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n   Since \\(b\\ge 2\\), it follows that \\(a+b\\le n\\).  \n   Set \\(c:=a+b\\).  Then \\(c\\) is an integer satisfying \\(2\\le c\\le n\\), and the point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation \\(x+y=c\\).  \n   Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(L_c\\).\n\nIn either case the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to at least one of the lines listed in (1).  Therefore the family \\(\\{L_1\\}\\cup\\{L_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n\\}\\) covers the whole set \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the explicit family of \\(n\\) lines given in (1) satisfies both requirements of the problem:\n\n* every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines;\n* none of the lines is sunny, i.e. the number of sunny lines is \\(k=0\\).\n\nHence the value  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}\n\\]\n\nis always admissible.  Determining whether other values of \\(k\\) can occur remains open."
