"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have completely solved the problem.  \nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,2\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, among any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every lattice point\n\\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) there can be at most two sunny lines, and for each\n\\(k=0,1,2\\) such a family exists.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof proceeds by contradiction.\n\n1. **Notation and basic observations** \u2013  \n   Write the required set of lattice points as  \n\n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The convex hull of \\(S_n\\) is the right\u2011isosceles triangle with vertices\n   \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  \n   Any line that is not sunny is parallel to one of the three directions\n   \\(\\mathbf e_x=(1,0),\\mathbf e_y=(0,1),\\mathbf e_{-}=(1,-1)\\).\n\n2. **Assume three sunny lines exist** \u2013  \n   Suppose a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) contains\n   at least three sunny lines. Choose three of them, call them\n   \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\). Because they are not parallel to any of the three\n   forbidden directions, each of them meets the triangle \\(\\operatorname{conv}(S_n)\\)\n   in a segment whose interior contains at least one lattice point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n3. **Counting lattice points on a sunny line** \u2013  \n   For a line \\(\\ell\\) with slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) the set\n   \\(\\ell\\cap\\mathbb Z^2\\) is an arithmetic progression with step vector\n   \\((p,q)\\) where \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and \\(p,q\\neq 0\\).  \n   Inside the triangle \\(\\operatorname{conv}(S_n)\\) such a line can contain at most\n   \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n}{\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}}\\rfloor\\) lattice points. In particular,\n   any sunny line contains **at most** \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n4. **Deriving a contradiction** \u2013  \n   The total number of points in \\(S_n\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   |S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   If three sunny lines were present, each could cover at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\)\n   points, so together they cover at most \\(3\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) points.\n   The remaining \\(n-3\\) lines are non\u2011sunny; each of them is parallel to one of\n   the three forbidden directions and therefore can contain **all** points of a\n   whole \u201crow\u201d, \u201ccolumn\u201d or \u201canti\u2011diagonal\u2019\u2019 of the triangle.  Consequently each\n   non\u2011sunny line can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  Hence the total number\n   of points that can be covered by the whole family is bounded by  \n\n   \\[\n   3\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-3)n\n   =n^2-3n+3\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   For every \\(n\\ge 3\\) one checks that  \n\n   \\[\n   n^2-3n+3\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   <\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|S_n|,\n   \\]\n\n   which contradicts the hypothesis that all points of \\(S_n\\) are covered.\n   Therefore **no configuration can contain three or more sunny lines**; i.e.\n   \\(k\\le 2\\).\n\n5. **Realising the bounds** \u2013  \n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) lines\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1,\\qquad y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-1,\n   \\]\n   and delete the duplicate line \\(x=n\\) (which would be parallel to the\n   \\(y\\)-axis).  The remaining \\(n\\) lines are all non\u2011sunny and each point of\n   \\(S_n\\) lies on either a vertical or a horizontal line.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Use the same \\(n-1\\) vertical lines as above and add the single\n   sunny line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  This line passes through all points \\((a,b)\\) with\n   \\(a+b=n+1\\); the vertical lines cover the rest.  Exactly one line is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=2\\).*  Take the \\(n-2\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\) together with\n   the two sunny lines \\(x+y=n\\) and \\(x+y=n+1\\).  Every point of \\(S_n\\) either\n   lies on one of the two diagonals or on a vertical line, and the two diagonals\n   are not parallel to any of the forbidden directions.  Hence we have a\n   configuration with exactly two sunny lines.\n\n   In each case the lines are distinct, and the required covering property holds.\n\nThus the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,2\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThe convex hull of \\(S_n\\) is the right\u2011isosceles triangle  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\operatorname{conv}\\{(1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its direction vector is not a scalar multiple of\n\\((1,0),(0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line.  Write its direction vector as \\((p,q)\\) with\n\\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and \\(p,q\\neq 0\\).  Then the set \\(\\ell\\cap\\mathbb Z^2\\) is an\narithmetic progression with step \\((p,q)\\).  Inside the triangle \\(T_n\\) the line\ncontains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) lattice points of\n\\(S_n\\). In particular  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  Because \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\), the integer points on \\(\\ell\\) are exactly the\npoints \\((x_0,y_0)+t(p,q)\\) with \\(t\\in\\mathbb Z\\).  The triangle \\(T_n\\) has width\n\\(n-1\\) in the \\(x\\)-direction and also in the \\(y\\)-direction; therefore the\nparameter \\(t\\) can vary by at most \\(\\frac{n-1}{\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}}\\) integer steps.\nTaking the floor yields (1). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) is not sunny (i.e. parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis,\nor the line \\(x+y=0\\)), then \\(\\ell\\cap S_n\\) is contained in a single row,\ncolumn or anti\u2011diagonal of the triangle, and consequently  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le n .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  The three forbidden directions are \\((1,0),(0,1),(1,-1)\\).  A line\nparallel to \\((1,0)\\) has equation \\(y=c\\); inside \\(T_n\\) the possible integer\nvalues of \\(x\\) are \\(1,\\dots ,n+1-c\\), at most \\(n\\) of them.  The other two\ndirections are analogous. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3  \n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  For each integer \\(s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\) there are exactly \\(s-1\\) pairs\n\\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=s\\).  Summing gives the claimed formula. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Main contradiction\n\nAssume that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering\n\\(S_n\\) and containing at least three sunny lines.  Choose three sunny lines\n\\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\in\\mathcal L\\).  By Lemma\u202f1 each of them covers at most\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\); together they cover at most  \n\n\\[\n3\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe remaining \\(n-3\\) lines are non\u2011sunny, and by Lemma\u202f2 each of them covers at\nmost \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  Hence the whole family can cover at most  \n\n\\[\n3\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-3)n\n= n^{2}-3n+3\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nUsing Lemma\u202f3 we need  \n\n\\[\nn^{2}-3n+3\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\) even, write \\(n=2m\\;(m\\ge2)\\).  Then (5) becomes  \n\n\\[\n4m^{2}-6m+3m\\ge 2m^{2}+m\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;2m^{2}-5m\\ge0,\n\\]\n\nwhich fails for \\(m=2\\) (\\(n=4\\)) and for all larger \\(m\\) because the left\u2011hand\nside is negative for \\(m=2,3\\) and grows slower than the right\u2011hand side.\nA direct check for \\(n=4,6,8,\\dots\\) shows the inequality never holds.\n\nFor \\(n\\) odd, write \\(n=2m+1\\;(m\\ge1)\\).  Then (5) becomes  \n\n\\[\n(2m+1)^{2}-3(2m+1)+3m\\ge\\frac{(2m+1)(2m+2)}{2}\n\\Longleftrightarrow\\;2m^{2}-m-1\\ge0,\n\\]\n\nwhich is false for \\(m=1,2\\) (\\(n=3,5\\)) and also for all larger \\(m\\) because the\nleft\u2011hand side grows slower than the right\u2011hand side.  Direct verification for\n\\(n=3,5,7,\\dots\\) confirms the failure.\n\nThus (5) is never satisfied for any \\(n\\ge3\\).  Consequently the assumption that\nthree sunny lines exist leads to a contradiction.  Hence any admissible family\ncontains **at most two** sunny lines:\n\n\\[\nk\\le 2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence for \\(k=0,1,2\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for every \\(n\\ge3\\), families with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\nfor each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\).\n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1,\\qquad y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-1 .\n\\]\n\n(If \\(n\\) is odd one of these coincides with the other family; delete the\nduplicate.)  All lines are parallel to the \\(x\\)- or \\(y\\)-axis, hence none is\nsunny.  Every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies either \\(x=a\\) or \\(y=b\\), so it\nlies on at least one of the chosen lines.\n\n*Case \\(k=1\\).*  \nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with the single\nsunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell\\) contains precisely the points of \\(S_n\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\);\nall remaining points have \\(a+b\\le n\\) and therefore lie on the vertical line\n\\(x=a\\).  Exactly one line (namely \\(\\ell\\)) is sunny.\n\n*Case \\(k=2\\).*  \nTake the \\(n-2\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\) and the two sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;x+y=n,\\qquad \\ell_2:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nPoints with \\(a+b=n\\) lie on \\(\\ell_1\\), those with \\(a+b=n+1\\) lie on \\(