            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nThe convex hull of \\(S_n\\) is the right\u2011isosceles triangle  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\operatorname{conv}\\{(1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (size of \\(S_n\\))\n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  For each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) there are exactly\n\\(s-1\\) ordered pairs \\((a,b)\\) of positive integers satisfying \\(a+b=s\\).\nSumming \\(\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) gives the claim. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (non\u2011sunny lines)\n\nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line\n\\(x+y=0\\) (i.e. its direction vector is a multiple of \\((1,0),(0,1)\\) or\n\\((1,-1)\\)), then  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  Such a line is of the form \\(y=c\\), \\(x=c\\) or \\(x+y=c\\) with an\ninteger constant \\(c\\).  Inside the triangle \\(T_n\\) the possible integer\ncoordinates along the line range over at most \\(n\\) distinct values, giving the\ndesired bound. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line.  Write a direction vector of \\(\\ell\\) as\n\\((p,q)\\) with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and \\(p,q\\neq0\\).  Then  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{*}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  Because \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\), the integer points on \\(\\ell\\) are exactly\n\\((x_0,y_0)+t(p,q)\\) with \\(t\\in\\mathbb Z\\).  Inside the triangle \\(T_n\\) the\n\\(x\\)-coordinate varies between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\), and similarly for the\n\\(y\\)-coordinate.  Hence the parameter \\(t\\) can change by at most\n\\(\\frac{n-1}{\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}}\\) integer steps, giving the first inequality.\nSince a sunny line is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions,\n\\(\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}\\ge2\\); therefore the second inequality follows. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Main contradiction\n\nAssume that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such\nthat\n\n* every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\);\n* at least three lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny.\n\nSelect three sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\in\\mathcal L\\).\nBy Lemma\u202f3 each of them contains at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) points of\n\\(S_n\\); consequently they together cover at most  \n\n\\[\n3\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\nThe remaining \\(n-3\\) lines are non\u2011sunny, and Lemma\u202f2 shows that each of them\ncovers at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  Hence the whole family can cover at\nmost  \n\n\\[\n3\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-3)n\n= n^{2}-3n+3\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBut by Lemma\u202f1 we must have  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nTherefore (2) must be at least (3).  We examine the two parity cases.\n\n*Even \\(n=2m\\) (\\(m\\ge2\\)).*  \nThen \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor=m\\) and (2) becomes \\(4m^{2}-3m\\), while (3) equals\n\\(2m^{2}+m\\).  The inequality \\(4m^{2}-3m\\ge2m^{2}+m\\) simplifies to\n\\(2m^{2}-4m\\ge0\\), i.e. \\(m\\ge2\\) would require \\(m\\ge2\\) and simultaneously\n\\(2m^{2}\\le4m\\), which is false for every \\(m\\ge2\\).  Hence the inequality\ncannot hold.\n\n*Odd \\(n=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge1\\)).*  \nNow \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor=m\\).  Expression (2) equals\n\\(4m^{2}+2m-2\\), while (3) equals \\(2m^{2}+3m+1\\).  The required inequality\n\\(4m^{2}+2m-2\\ge2m^{2}+3m+1\\) reduces to \\(2m^{2}-m-3\\ge0\\), which fails for\n\\(m=1,2\\) and, because the left\u2011hand side grows slower than the right\u2011hand\nside, for all larger \\(m\\) as well.\n\nThus in every case the necessary inequality is violated, contradicting the\nassumption that three sunny lines can be present.  Consequently any admissible\nfamily contains at most two sunny lines:\n\n\\[\nk\\le2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Constructions attaining the bound\n\nWe now exhibit, for each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines\nsatisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1,\\qquad\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-1 .\n\\]\n\n(If a line appears twice we delete the duplicate; the total number of distinct\nlines is exactly \\(n\\).)  All are vertical or horizontal, hence non\u2011sunny.\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have either \\(x=a\\) or \\(y=b\\); therefore the point\nlies on at least one of the chosen lines.\n\n*Case \\(k=1\\).*  \nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell\\) contains precisely the points of \\(S_n\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\);\nall other points satisfy \\(a+b\\le n\\) and are covered by the vertical line\n\\(x=a\\).  Exactly one line is sunny.\n\n*Case \\(k=2\\).*  \nTake the \\(n-2\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\) and the two sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;x+y=n,\\qquad \\ell_2:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nPoints with \\(a+b=n\\) lie on \\(\\ell_1\\), those with \\(a+b=n+1\\) lie on\n\\(\\ell_2\\); the remaining points have \\(a+b\\le n-1\\) and are covered by the\nvertical line \\(x=a\\).  Both \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\) are not parallel to any forbidden\ndirection, so exactly two lines are sunny.\n\nIn each construction the lines are distinct, the covering property holds, and\nthe number of sunny lines equals the prescribed \\(k\\).  Hence the bound\n\\(k\\le2\\) is sharp.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2\\}} .\n\\]\n\nNo configuration can contain three or more sunny lines, and configurations\nwith \\(0\\), \\(1\\) or \\(2\\) sunny lines exist. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
