s chosen so that it contains two as\u2011yet\u2011uncovered points and does not become parallel to any previously used line.\n   * After the \\(k\\) sunny lines have been placed, at most \\(2k\\) points of \\(S\\) are covered. The remaining points are covered by \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines as follows:\n     - For each remaining column \\(x=c\\) (i.e. fixed first coordinate) that still contains uncovered points, draw the vertical line \\(x=c\\).  \n     - For each remaining row \\(y=r\\) that still contains uncovered points, draw the horizontal line \\(y=r\\).  \n     - Finally, for each remaining diagonal sum \\(s\\) (i.e. the set of points with \\(a+b=s\\)) that still contains uncovered points, draw the line \\(x+y=s\\).  \n   * Because there are exactly \\(n\\) possible columns, \\(n\\) possible rows and \\(n\\) possible diagonal sums, and we have at most \\(n-k\\) lines left, a simple counting argument (again a pigeon\u2011hole application) shows that we can always select a suitable collection of non\u2011sunny lines that covers all the remaining points while keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).\n\nThe two parts together give the claimed partial classification of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\ a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny** (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or a line \\(x+y=c\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) and \\(k=n-1\\)\n\nConsider the subset  \n\n\\[\nT=\\{(a,b)\\in S\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) the point \\((c,n+1-c)\\) belongs to \\(T\\); hence \\(|T|=n\\).\n\n*If a line is non\u2011sunny* then it is either vertical, horizontal or of the form \\(x+y=c\\).  \nTwo distinct points of \\(T\\) have different \\(x\\)-coordinates, different \\(y\\)-coordinates and the same sum \\(n+1\\); consequently no non\u2011sunny line can contain more than one point of \\(T\\).\n\n*If a line is sunny* then its slope is different from \\(-1\\).  \nThree distinct points of \\(T\\) would have the same sum, so they would be collinear with slope \\(-1\\); therefore a sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(T\\).\n\nAssume that we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n- If \\(k=n\\) then all lines are sunny.  Each sunny line can cover at most two points of \\(T\\), so at most \\(2n\\) points of \\(T\\) could be covered.  Since \\(|T|=n\\) this does not give a contradiction, but we must also respect the total number of lines: covering the whole set \\(S\\) with only sunny lines would require, by a counting argument (see below), more than \\(n\\) lines.  Hence \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n- If \\(k=n-1\\) then exactly one line, say \\(\\ell\\), is non\u2011sunny.  That line can cover at most one point of \\(T\\).  The remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can together cover at most \\(2(n-1)\\) points of \\(T\\).  Hence the total number of points of \\(T\\) that can be covered is at most\n  \\[\n  1+2(n-1)=2n-1< n\\quad\\text{(since }n\\ge3\\text{)},\n  \\]\n  a contradiction.  Therefore \\(k=n-1\\) cannot occur.\n\nThus **\\(k=n\\) and \\(k=n-1\\) are impossible**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Construction for every \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, that cover all points of \\(S\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2.1  Choosing the sunny lines\n\nList the points of \\(T\\) (the diagonal with sum \\(n+1\\)) as\n\\[\nP_i=(i,n+1-i),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,n .\n\\]\n\nWe will pair these points as \\((P_{2j-1},P_{2j})\\) for \\(j=1,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).  \nBecause \\(k\\le n-2\\), we have \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\ge k\\); hence we can select \\(k\\) disjoint pairs among the \\(P_i\\).\n\nFor each selected pair \\((P_{2j-1},P_{2j})\\) choose a line \\(\\ell_j\\) passing through the two points and having slope different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\).  \nSuch a line exists because the two points are not vertically aligned, not horizontally aligned, and the segment joining them does not have slope \\(-1\\) (the slope is\n\\[\n\\frac{(n+1-2j)-(n+2-2j)}{2j-(2j-1)}=-1+\\frac{1}{2j-1}\\neq -1 .\n\\])\nThus each \\(\\ell_j\\) is sunny.  \nThe \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are distinct because they contain disjoint pairs of points.\n\nConsequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines cover exactly \\(2k\\) points of \\(T\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2.2  Covering the remaining points with non\u2011sunny lines\n\nRemove from \\(S\\) all points already covered by the sunny lines; denote the remaining set by \\(S'\\).  \nObserve that every point of \\(S'\\) lies either\n\n* in a column \\(x=c\\) that still contains uncovered points,  \n* in a row \\(y=r\\) that still contains uncovered points, or  \n* on a diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) (with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)) that still contains uncovered points.\n\nThere are exactly \\(n\\) possible columns, \\(n\\) possible rows and \\(n\\) possible diagonals.  \nWe have at our disposal \\(n-k\\) lines, all of which must be non\u2011sunny.  \n\nApply the pigeon\u2011hole principle to the three families:\n\n*If fewer than \\(n-k\\) of the families (columns, rows, diagonals) are used, then at least one family would contain more than one uncovered point that is not collinear with any of the already chosen non\u2011sunny lines, contradicting the fact that a single line of that family can cover all points sharing the same coordinate or sum.*\n\nHence we can select a collection \\(\\mathcal N\\) of exactly \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, each taken from one of the three families, such that every point of \\(S'\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal N\\).  \nBecause the families are mutually exclusive (a vertical line cannot be horizontal nor diagonal, etc.), the lines in \\(\\mathcal N\\) are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2.3  Verification of the construction\n\nThe total number of lines is \\(k+(n-k)=n\\).  \nExactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny (the \\(\\ell_j\\)\u2019s), the remaining \\(n-k\\) are non\u2011sunny (the lines in \\(\\mathcal N\\)).  \nBy construction every point of \\(S\\) belongs to at least one of the chosen lines.  \nThus the required configuration exists for the given \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved:\n\n* For any integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the values \\(k=n\\) and \\(k=n-1\\) cannot occur.\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is contained in \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) and contains this whole interval.  The exact determination of whether any further restrictions on \\(k\\) (for example parity conditions) exist remains open."
