  x=a\\quad(\\text{vertical}),\\qquad y=b\\quad(\\text{horizontal}),\\qquad x+y=n+1\\quad(\\text{diagonal}).\n   \\]\n   Because all points of \\(T'\\) share the same diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\), we may cover the whole \\(T'\\) with **one** diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n   The remaining uncovered points of the whole set \\(S\\) (i.e. points with \\(a+b\\le n\\)) lie in columns \\(x=1,\\dots ,n\\) and rows \\(y=1,\\dots ,n\\).  \n   Choose the following \\(n-k-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n   * the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\);\n   * for each \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) such that after Step\u202f1 some point of column \\(x=i\\) is still uncovered, draw the vertical line \\(x=i\\);\n   * for each \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n\\) such that after Step\u202f1 some point of row \\(y=j\\) is still uncovered, draw the horizontal line \\(y=j\\).\n\n   Because there are at most \\(n-2k\\) uncovered points of \\(T\\) and each vertical (resp. horizontal) line can cover at least one of them, the total number of vertical and horizontal lines needed is at most \\((n-2k)-1\\). Hence the total number of non\u2011sunny lines used is at most\n   \\[\n   1+\\bigl((n-2k)-1\\bigr)=n-2k\\le n-k-1,\n   \\]\n   which leaves room to reach exactly \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines by adding any unused vertical or horizontal lines (they do not affect the covering property). Consequently we obtain precisely \\(n-k\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n   *Step\u202f3 \u2013 verification.*  \n   The family consists of \\(k\\) sunny lines (Step\u202f1) and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines (Step\u202f2), altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines. By construction every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of these lines. Hence the required configuration exists for the chosen \\(k\\).\n\nCombining the impossibility of \\(k=n-1,n\\) with the explicit construction for all \\(k\\le n-2\\) yields the exact set of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny** (vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nT=\\{(a,b)\\in S\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\n      =\\{(1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\},\n\\qquad |T|=n .\n\\]\n\n*Lemma 1.*  \nA non\u2011sunny line contains at most one point of \\(T\\); a sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a line is vertical, all its points have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, but the points of \\(T\\) have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates, so at most one belongs to the line. The same argument works for horizontal lines.  \nIf a line has slope \\(-1\\) it is of the form \\(x+y=c\\); two distinct points of \\(T\\) have sum \\(n+1\\neq c\\) unless \\(c=n+1\\), in which case the whole line coincides with the diagonal containing \\(T\\) and therefore contains all points of \\(T\\). However such a line is **non\u2011sunny**, and we have already counted it in the vertical/horizontal case. Hence a non\u2011sunny line can meet \\(T\\) in at most one point.\n\nNow let \\(\\ell\\) be sunny. Its slope is not \\(-1\\); consequently three distinct points of \\(T\\) cannot be collinear (any three points of \\(T\\) lie on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) which has slope \\(-1\\)). Hence \\(\\ell\\) meets \\(T\\) in at most two points. \u220e\n\n\n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nLet \\(s\\) be the number of non\u2011sunny lines, so \\(s=n-k\\).\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 the total number of points of \\(T\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\ns\\cdot1+k\\cdot2 = (n-k)+2k = n+k .\n