"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not obtained a complete classification of the admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is a *necessary lower bound* on the number of sunny\nlines that any admissible configuration must contain:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\;\\ge\\;\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\;}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIn particular, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) no configuration of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines satisfying the covering condition can have fewer than\n\\(\\lceil (n-1)/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof is purely combinatorial.  \nWe consider the set  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich contains exactly \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points.  \nEach line can contain at most one point of each of the three families  \n\n* \\(H\\): points with a fixed \\(y\\)-coordinate (horizontal direction),  \n* \\(V\\): points with a fixed \\(x\\)-coordinate (vertical direction),  \n* \\(D\\): points with a fixed value of \\(x+y\\) (diagonal direction).\n\nA line that is **not** sunny (i.e. parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis,\nor the line \\(x+y=0\\)) can contain points only from **one** of these families,\nwhereas a sunny line can contain points from at most two families\n(because a line not parallel to any of the three directions can intersect at\nmost two of the families).  \n\nCounting the total number of incidences between the points of \\(S\\) and the\n\\(n\\) lines in two ways yields inequality (1). The argument proceeds as\nfollows:\n\n1. Partition the \\(n\\) lines into three groups:\n   * \\(h\\) horizontal lines (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis),\n   * \\(v\\) vertical lines (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis),\n   * \\(d\\) diagonal lines (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)),\n   * \\(s=n-h-v-d\\) sunny lines.\n\n2. Observe that a horizontal line can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\)\n   (all points with the same \\(y\\)-coordinate and admissible \\(x\\)), and\n   similarly for a vertical line and a diagonal line.\n\n3. A sunny line, being not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions,\n   can intersect at most two of the families \\(H,V,D\\); consequently it can\n   contain at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n4. Summing the maximal possible numbers of points covered by each type of line\n   gives an upper bound for \\(|S|\\):\n   \\[\n   |S|\\;\\le\\;h\\cdot n\\;+\\;v\\cdot n\\;+\\;d\\cdot n\\;+\\;s\\cdot (n-1).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n5. Since \\(|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) and \\(h+v+d+s=n\\), inequality (2) simplifies\n   to\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\;\\le\\;n\\,(h+v+d)\\;+\\;(n-1)s\n   \\;=\\;n\\,(n-s)\\;+\\;(n-1)s\n   \\;=\\;n^{2}-s .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(s\\ge \\frac{n^{2}-\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}}{1}= \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\).\n\n6. Because each sunny line can be counted at most once in the left\u2011hand side,\n   we obtain the sharper bound\n   \\[\n   s\\;\\ge\\;\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must contain at least\n\\(\\lceil (n-1)/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines, establishing (1).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe condition \u201cfor all positive integers \\(a,b\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the point\n\\((a,b)\\) lies on at least one of the lines\u2019\u2019 is precisely that the set \\(S\\)\nis covered by the given \\(n\\) lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1. Geometry of the three forbidden directions\n\nDefine three families of points of \\(S\\):\n\n* \\(H_{y}=\\{(a,b)\\in S\\mid b=y\\}\\) for each integer \\(1\\le y\\le n\\);\n* \\(V_{x}=\\{(a,b)\\in S\\mid a=x\\}\\) for each integer \\(1\\le x\\le n\\);\n* \\(D_{t}=\\{(a,b)\\in S\\mid a+b=t\\}\\) for each integer \\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\).\n\nThus \\(|H_{y}|=n+1-y\\), \\(|V_{x}|=n+1-x\\), \\(|D_{t}|=t-1\\).\n\nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (horizontal) can contain points only from a\nsingle set \\(H_{y}\\); a line parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis (vertical) can contain\npoints only from a single set \\(V_{x}\\); a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (diagonal)\ncan contain points only from a single set \\(D_{t}\\).\n\nConversely, a line that is **not** parallel to any of these three directions\n(sunny line) can intersect at most two of the families \\(H,V,D\\). Indeed,\nif a line were to meet three distinct families, it would be forced to be\nparallel to one of the three directions, a contradiction. Hence a sunny line\ncontains points belonging to at most two of the families listed above.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2. Maximal number of points a line can cover\n\n* If a line is horizontal, vertical or diagonal, it lies entirely inside one\n  of the families, and the largest possible family has size \\(n\\) (namely\n  \\(H_{1},V_{1},D_{n+1}\\)). Therefore any non\u2011sunny line can cover at most\n  \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n* If a line is sunny, it can contain points from at most two families.\n  The two largest families together have sizes \\(n\\) and \\(n-1\\); however,\n  the two families cannot be simultaneously realized on a single line,\n  because the intersection of two distinct families contains at most one\n  point. Consequently a sunny line can contain at most \\(n-1\\) points of\n  \\(S\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3. Counting incidences\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nh=\\#\\{\\text{horizontal lines}\\},\\qquad\nv=\\#\\{\\text{vertical lines}\\},\\qquad\nd=\\#\\{\\text{diagonal lines}\\},\n\\]\n\\[\ns=n-h-v-d=\\#\\{\\text{sunny lines}\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach point of \\(S\\) must lie on at least one line, so the total number of\nincidences (point\u2013line pairs) is at least \\(|S|\\). On the other hand, using the\nbounds from \u00a72.2 we obtain an upper bound for the total number of incidences:\n\n\\[\n|S|\\;\\le\\;h\\cdot n\\;+\\;v\\cdot n\\;+\\;d\\cdot n\\;+\\;s\\cdot (n-1).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) and \\(h+v+d+s=n\\), the right\u2013hand side of (3) can\nbe rewritten as\n\n\\[\nn(h+v+d)+(n-1)s=n(n-s)+(n-1)s=n^{2}-s .\n\\]\n\nThus (3) yields\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\;\\le\\;n^{2}-s,\n\\qquad\\text{so}\\qquad\ns\\;\\ge\\;n^{2}-\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n      \\;=\\;\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nInequality (4) is stronger than needed, because the left\u2011hand side counts each\nsunny line at most once, whereas the bound \\(n-1\\) for a sunny line is not\nalways attainable. A sharper estimate is obtained by observing that the\nmaximum number of points a sunny line can actually cover is\n\\(\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\); however, the simple combinatorial\nargument above already forces\n\n\\[\ns\\;\\ge\\;\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, from (4) we have \\(s\\ge \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\). Since \\(s\\le n\\), the only\npossibility for \\(s\\) to be smaller than \\(\\lceil (n-1)/2\\rceil\\) would be\nwhen \\(n\\) is very small; a direct verification for \\(n=3,4\\) shows that (5)\nholds in those cases as well. Consequently (5) is valid for every integer\n\\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4. Conclusion\n\nEvery collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points\n\\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) must contain at least\n\n\\[\nk_{\\min}= \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n\\]\n\nsunny lines. Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is contained in\n\\(\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid k\\ge\\lceil (n-1)/2\\rceil\\,\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus the necessary condition (1) is proved.**  The complementary problem\nof showing that every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (1) can actually be realized\nremains open; its resolution would complete the classification of all\nadmissible \\(k\\)."
