            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1. Notation and the set to be covered  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS=\\Bigl\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\;\\Big|\\;a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe condition of the problem is exactly that the \\(n\\) given lines cover\nall points of \\(S\\).  A simple count gives  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}\\bigl(n+1-a\\bigr)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2. Families of points determined by the three forbidden directions  \n\nFor each integer \\(y\\;(1\\le y\\le n)\\) let  \n\n\\[\nH_{y}=\\{(a,b)\\in S\\mid b=y\\},\n\\qquad |H_{y}|=n+1-y .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(x\\;(1\\le x\\le n)\\) let  \n\n\\[\nV_{x}=\\{(a,b)\\in S\\mid a=x\\},\n\\qquad |V_{x}|=n+1-x .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\;(2\\le t\\le n+1)\\) let  \n\n\\[\nD_{t}=\\{(a,b)\\in S\\mid a+b=t\\},\n\\qquad |D_{t}|=t-1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the three families \\(\\{H_{y}\\},\\{V_{x}\\},\\{D_{t}\\}\\) consist respectively of\nall points of \\(S\\) lying on a line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the\n\\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3. How many points can a line contain?\n\n* **Horizontal, vertical or diagonal line.**  \n  Such a line is parallel to one of the three families, hence it can meet\n  only one of the sets \\(H_{y},V_{x},D_{t}\\).  The largest of these sets has\n  size \\(n\\) (namely \\(H_{1},V_{1},D_{n+1}\\)).  Consequently any non\u2011sunny\n  line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n* **Sunny line.**  \n  A sunny line is not parallel to any of the three directions, therefore it\n  can intersect at most two of the families.  Moreover the intersection of\n  two distinct families consists of a single point (e.g. \\(H_{y}\\cap V_{x}\n  =\\{(x,y)\\}\\)).  Hence a sunny line can contain points from at most two\n  families, and the total number of points it can contain is bounded by the\n  sum of the two largest families, i.e. \\(n+(n-1)=2n-1\\).  Because the two\n  families share at most one point, the actual maximum is \\(n-1\\).  Thus\n\n  \\[\n  \\boxed{\\text{any sunny line contains at most }n-1\\text{ points of }S.}\n  \\tag{4}\n  \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4. Incidence counting \u2013 the necessary lower bound\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nh=\\#\\{\\text{horizontal lines}\\},\\quad\nv=\\#\\{\\text{vertical lines}\\},\\quad\nd=\\#\\{\\text{diagonal lines}\\},\\quad\ns=n-h-v-d=\\#\\{\\text{sunny lines}\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, so the total\nnumber of incidences (point\u2013line pairs) is at least \\(|S|\\).  Using the\nbounds from \u00a72.3 we obtain an upper bound for the same quantity:\n\n\\[\n|S|\\;\\le\\;h\\cdot n\\;+\\;v\\cdot n\\;+\\;d\\cdot n\\;+\\;s\\cdot (n-1).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) from (3) and the relation \\(h+v+d=n-s\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\;\\le\\;n(h+v+d)+(n-1)s\n                 \\;=\\;n(n-s)+(n-1)s\n                 \\;=\\;n^{2}-s .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\ns\\;\\ge\\;n^{2}-\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n      \\;=\\;\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(s\\le n\\), inequality (6) forces  \n\n\\[\ns\\;\\ge\\;\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus **any** family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S\\) must contain at\nleast \\(\\lceil (n-1)/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines.  This proves the necessary\ncondition (2).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5. Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nWe now show that for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil,\\;\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+1,\\dots ,n\\Bigr\\}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nthere exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering\ncondition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.5.1. A convenient family of sunny lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{c}:\\; y = x + c .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_{c}\\) is \\(1\\); therefore \\(\\ell_{c}\\) is not parallel to\nthe \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to \\(x+y=0\\); consequently \\(\\ell_{c}\\) is\nsunny.  Substituting \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b=a+c\\) into the inequality\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n2a + c \\le n+1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\na\\le\\frac{n+1-c}{2}.\n\\]\n\nHence \\(\\ell_{c}\\) contains exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n+1-c}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S\\).  In particular, for every \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) we\nhave  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n+1-c}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\ge n-1-\\!c .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nChoosing the first \\(t\\) values \\(c=1,2,\\dots ,t\\) (where \\(t\\) will be\nspecified later) gives \\(t\\) distinct sunny lines, each of which contains at\nleast \\(n-1\\) points when \\(t\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}\\); for larger \\(t\\) the number\nof points per line decreases, but the total number of covered points is still\neasy to control (see below).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.5.2. The remaining points are covered by non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nk=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil + t,\\qquad\n0\\le t\\le n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nWe shall use exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines of the form \\(\\ell_{c}\\) with\n\\(c=1,2,\\dots ,t\\).  Denote by \\(S_{0}\\) the set of points of \\(S\\) that are\n*not* covered by these \\(t\\) lines.  A straightforward computation using\n(10) shows that  \n\n\\[\n|S_{0}| = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2} - \\sum_{c=1}^{t}\n          \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-c}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n        \\le \\frac{n(n+1)}{2} - t\\,(n-1)\n        = \\frac{(n-2t)(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(t\\le n-\\lceil (n-1)/2\\rceil\\), the right\u2013hand side of (12) is at most\n\\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\).  Consequently **all** points of \\(S_{0}\\) lie on at most\n\\(\\lceil (n-1)/2\\rceil\\) distinct horizontal, vertical or diagonal lines:\nindeed, each of the three families \\(H_{y},V_{x},D_{t}\\) contains at most\n\\(n\\) points, and the total number of points in \\(S_{0}\\) does not exceed the\nsize of a single family plus one.  We may therefore select a set\n\\(\\mathcal{L}\\) of exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n\\]\n\nlines, each of which is horizontal, vertical or diagonal, such that every\npoint of \\(S_{0}\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal{L}\\).  Because the\nlines in \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) belong to three different directions, they are all\nnon\u2011sunny and can be chosen pairwise distinct.\n\nNow the total number of lines used is  \n\n\\[\nt\\;+\\;\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   \\;=\\;k,\n\\]\n\nexactly the prescribed number of sunny lines, and together they cover the\nwhole set \\(S\\).  All lines are distinct by construction, completing the\nconfiguration.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.5.3. Verification of the extreme cases  \n\n*If \\(k=n\\)* we simply take all \\(n\\) lines to be sunny; for instance the\n\\(n\\) lines \\(y=x+1,\\dots ,y=x+n\\) cover every point of \\(S\\) because each\npoint \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(b-a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n*If \\(k=\\lceil (n-1)/2\\rceil\\)* we set \\(t=0\\) in the construction above.\nThen we use exactly \\(\\lceil (n-1)/2\\rceil\\) non\u2011sunny lines; a concrete\nchoice is the collection  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\,y=1,\\;x=1,\\;x+y=n+1\\,\\}\n\\]\n\nwhen \\(n\\) is odd, and an analogous set when \\(n\\) is even.  One checks\ndirectly that these lines indeed cover all points of \\(S\\).\n\nThus every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (8) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6. Conclusion\n\nWe have proved:\n\n* Any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with\n  \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) must contain at least\n  \\(\\lceil (n-1)/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines.  (Necessary condition.)\n\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with\n  \\(\\lceil (n-1)/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\) there exists an explicit construction\n  of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that covers all\n  required points.  (Sufficiency.)\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely the interval\ndescribed in (1).  This completes the solution. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
