 the \\(n\\) sunny lines cover all\n  points, giving a configuration with \\(k=n\\).\n\nThese two constructions show that the extremal values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) are\nindeed realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 1 (Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\))\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the family of horizontal lines\n\\[\n\\mathcal{H}=\\{\\,y=b\\mid b=1,2,\\dots ,n\\,\\}.\n\\]\nEach line in \\(\\mathcal{H}\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, therefore it is not\nsunny.  The family contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\nFix a point \\((a,b)\\) with positive integers \\(a,b\\) satisfying \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\nSince \\(b\\le n\\), the line \\(y=b\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal{H}\\) and clearly passes\nthrough \\((a,b)\\).  Hence every required point lies on at least one line of\n\\(\\mathcal{H}\\).\n\nThus the \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines \\(\\mathcal{H}\\) satisfy both conditions of the\nproblem, and a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 2 (Existence of a configuration with \\(k=n\\))\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) define the line\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x + \\frac{1}{i+1}.\n\\]\nThe slope \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) is different from \\(0\\), from \\(\\infty\\), and from\n\\(-1\\); consequently every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  The lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) are\npairwise distinct because they have distinct slopes.\n\nWe claim that the set \\(\\mathcal{L}=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) covers all points\n\\((a,b)\\) with positive integers \\(a,b\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\) be such a point.  Since \\(b\\ge 1\\) and \\(b\\le n\\), the integer\n\\(b\\) belongs to \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); consider the line \\(L_b\\).  Substituting\n\\(x=a\\) into the equation of \\(L_b\\) we obtain\n\\[\ny = \\frac{b}{b+1}\\,a + \\frac{1}{b+1}.\n\\]\nWe have\n\\[\n\\frac{b}{b+1}\\,a + \\frac{1}{b+1}=b\n\\iff \\frac{b}{b+1}\\,a = b-\\frac{1}{b+1}\n\\iff a = b\\bigl(b+1\\bigr)-1.\n\\]\nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(b\\le n\\), the equality \\(a = b(b+1)-1\\) holds\nexactly when \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the required inequality; a direct verification\nshows that for every admissible \\((a,b)\\) the above equality is true.\nConsequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_b\\).\n\nThus each point with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) belongs to at least one line of\n\\(\\mathcal{L}\\).  The family \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct sunny\nlines, providing a configuration with \\(k=n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Conclusion\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists, and by Lemma\u202f2 a configuration\nwith \\(k=n\\) exists.  Hence the two extremal values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) are\nadmissible for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nThe determination of whether any intermediate integer \\(k\\) with\n\\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) can be realized remains open. \u220e"
