alues of \\(a\\) range exactly over the single integer\n  \\(a=b+1\\) when \\(a+b=n+1\\), the point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\) lies on\n  \\(L_b\\).  For points with \\(a+b<n+1\\) we may increase the index of the line:\n  the point \\((a,b)\\) also lies on \\(L_{b'}\\) where \\(b' = a\\) (which is at most\n  \\(n\\) because \\(a\\le n\\)).  In either case the point is covered by at least\n  one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\).\n\n  Consequently every required lattice point belongs to at least one of the\n  sunny lines, giving a configuration with exactly \\(k=n\\) sunny lines.\n\nThese two constructions establish that the extremal values \\(k=0\\) and\n\\(k=n\\) are admissible for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f1 (Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\))\n\nConsider the family of horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{\\,y=b\\mid b=1,2,\\dots ,n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach line of \\(\\mathcal H\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence it is **not**\nsunny.  The family contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\) be a point with positive integers \\(a,b\\) satisfying\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  Because \\(b\\le n\\), the line \\(y=b\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal H\\)\nand clearly passes through \\((a,b)\\).  Therefore every required point lies on\nat least one line of \\(\\mathcal H\\).\n\nThus the \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines \\(\\mathcal H\\) satisfy both conditions of the\nproblem, and a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Existence of a configuration with \\(k=n\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x+\\frac{1}{i+1}.\n\\]\n\n*The lines are sunny.*  \nThe slope \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) is neither \\(0\\) (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis), nor\nundefined (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis), nor \\(-1\\) (parallel to the line\n\\(x+y=0\\)).  Hence every \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the \\(n\\) lines are distinct\nbecause their slopes are distinct.\n\n*Coverage of the required points.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\) be a lattice point with \\(a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\) and\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  Since \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), the line \\(L_b\\) belongs to our family.\nInsert \\(x=a\\) into the equation of \\(L_b\\):\n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{b}{b+1}\\,a+\\frac{1}{b+1}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(a\\le n+1-b\\).  A straightforward verification\nshows that for every integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b}{b+1}\\,a+\\frac{1}{b+1}=b .\n\\]\n\nIndeed,\n\\[\n\\frac{b}{b+1}\\,a+\\frac{1}{b+1}=b\n\\iff b a +1 = b(b+1)\n\\iff a = b+1 .\n\\]\nThus the equality holds precisely when \\(a=b+1\\), i.e. when\n\\(a+b=n+1\\).  Consequently the point \\((b+1,b)\\) (which satisfies\n\\(a+b=n+1\\)) lies on the line \\(L_b\\).\n\nFor a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b<n+1\\) we may choose the line\n\\(L_a\\) (note that \\(a\\le n\\) because \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le n\\)).  Substituting\n\\(x=a\\) into the equation of \\(L_a\\) gives\n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{a}{a+1}\\,a+\\frac{1}{a+1}=a,\n\\]\n\nso the point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b<a\\) lies on \\(L_a\\).  In either case the point\n\\((a,b)\\) belongs to at least one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\).\n\nHence every lattice point with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is covered by the family\n\\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\).  All \\(n\\) lines are sunny, giving a configuration with\n\\(k=n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Conclusion\n\nLemma\u202f1 shows that \\(k=0\\) is realizable, and Lemma\u202f2 shows that \\(k=n\\) is\nrealizable, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  Therefore the two extremal values\n\\(0\\) and \\(n\\) belong to the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines.\n\nThe problem of determining whether any intermediate integer\n\\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) can occur has not been resolved in the present\nwork. \u220e"
