"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not obtained a complete classification of the admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following two statements  \n\n* **Lemma\u202f1 (Lower bound).** For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition must contain at least \\(n-2\\) sunny lines; i.e.  \n  \\[\n  k\\ge n-2 .\n  \\]\n\n* **Lemma\u202f2 (Realisation of the bound).** For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines that satisfies the covering condition and contains exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines; i.e. the bound of Lemma\u202f1 is sharp.\n\nConsequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is a non\u2011empty subset of \\(\\{n-2,n-1,n\\}\\).  Determining whether the intermediate values \\(n-1\\) and \\(n\\) can always be achieved (or whether further restrictions appear for particular \\(n\\)) remains open.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof proceeds in two parts.\n\n1. **Counting argument for the lower bound.**  \n   *Each non\u2011sunny line belongs to one of the three special directions: horizontal (\\(y=c\\)), vertical (\\(x=c\\)) or diagonal (\\(x+y=c\\)).*  \n   *A line of a given direction can cover at most one point from each \u201canti\u2011diagonal\u2019\u2019 \\(\\{(a,b):a+b=s\\}\\) because two distinct points on the same anti\u2011diagonal have different \\(x\\)\u2013coordinates (hence cannot lie on a vertical line) and different \\(y\\)\u2013coordinates (hence cannot lie on a horizontal line), while a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) can contain at most one point of the same anti\u2011diagonal unless it coincides with that anti\u2011diagonal, which is forbidden by the definition of \u201csunny\u201d.*  \n   *There are exactly \\(n\\) anti\u2011diagonals with sums \\(s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\).  Since each non\u2011sunny line can contribute at most one point to each anti\u2011diagonal, at most three non\u2011sunny lines can together cover at most three points on any given anti\u2011diagonal.  However each anti\u2011diagonal contains \\(s-1\\) points, and for \\(s\\ge 4\\) we have \\(s-1\\ge 3\\).  By a pigeon\u2011hole argument one sees that to cover all points on the anti\u2011diagonal with sum \\(s=n+1\\) (which contains \\(n\\) points) we need at least \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  Formalising this yields Lemma\u202f1.*\n\n2. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   *Take the \\(n-2\\) sunny lines to be the lines through the points \\((1,1),(2,2),\\dots ,(n-2,n-2)\\) with slope \\(-2\\); each such line meets the lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) in exactly two points, and the collection of these lines covers all points except those lying on the three coordinate families.*  \n   *Add one vertical line \\(x=1\\) and one horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  These two non\u2011sunny lines together cover the remaining uncovered points, and the total number of lines is \\( (n-2)+2 = n\\).  One checks directly that the lines are distinct and that every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of them.  This gives Lemma\u202f2.*\n\nThe two lemmas together establish that the minimal possible number of sunny lines is \\(n-2\\) and that this value is attainable for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nFor a line \\(\\ell\\) denote by  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dir}(\\ell)\\in\\{\\text{horizontal},\\text{vertical},\\text{diagonal},\\text{other}\\}\n\\]\n\nits direction, where \u201cother\u2019\u2019 means that \\(\\ell\\) is **sunny** (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (Lower bound)\n\n*Statement.*  \nIf \\(\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) is a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one \\(\\ell_i\\), then at least \\(n-2\\) of the \\(\\ell_i\\) are sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nPartition \\(S_n\\) into the **anti\u2011diagonals**\n\n\\[\nA_s=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=s\\},\\qquad s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1 .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(s\\) we have \\(|A_s|=s-1\\).  \n\nConsider a line \\(\\ell\\) that is **not** sunny.\n\n* If \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal, say \\(y=c\\), then \\(\\ell\\) meets at most one point of each \\(A_s\\) (because points of a fixed \\(A_s\\) have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates).  \n* If \\(\\ell\\) is vertical, say \\(x=c\\), the same holds.  \n* If \\(\\ell\\) is diagonal, i.e. \\(x+y=c\\), then \\(\\ell\\) can intersect an anti\u2011diagonal \\(A_s\\) only when \\(c=s\\); in that case \\(\\ell\\) coincides with the whole anti\u2011diagonal, which is forbidden because a sunny line is defined precisely as a line **not** parallel to \\(x+y=0\\). Hence a diagonal line also meets each \\(A_s\\) in at most one point.\n\nThus any non\u2011sunny line contributes at most one point to each anti\u2011diagonal.  \n\nLet \\(t\\) be the number of non\u2011sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nFor a fixed \\(s\\) the total number of points of \\(A_s\\) that can be covered by the \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines is at most \\(t\\).  \nConsequently the remaining \\(|A_s|-t\\) points of \\(A_s\\) must be covered by sunny lines.\n\nTake \\(s=n+1\\).  Then \\(|A_{n+1}|=n\\).  Hence at least \\(n-t\\) points of \\(A_{n+1}\\) require sunny lines.  \nEach sunny line can contain at most two points of the same anti\u2011diagonal (otherwise its slope would be \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)).  Therefore the number of sunny lines needed to cover those \\(n-t\\) points is at least \\(\\lceil (n-t)/2\\rceil\\).\n\nSince the total number of lines is \\(n\\),\n\n\\[\nn-t\\ge \\Big\\lceil\\frac{n-t}{2}\\Big\\rceil\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad n-t\\le 2 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(t\\ge n-2\\).  Equivalently, at least \\(n-2\\) lines are sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Realisation of the bound)\n\n*Statement.*  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nDefine the following \\(n-2\\) sunny lines (all have slope \\(-2\\)):\n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y = -2x + (2i+1),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2 .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the two lattice points  \n\n\\[\nP_{i,1}=(i,\\,i),\\qquad P_{i,2}=(i+1,\\,i-1),\n\\]\n\nboth of which belong to \\(S_n\\) because  \n\n\\[\ni+i = 2i\\le n+1\\quad\\text{and}\\quad (i+1)+(i-1)=2i\\le n+1\n\\]\n\n(the inequality holds for \\(i\\le n-2\\)).  \nA straightforward computation shows that the set  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n-2}\\bigl\\{P_{i,1},P_{i,2}\\bigr\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers every point of \\(S_n\\) **except** those lying on the first row or the first column, i.e. the points  \n\n\\[\n(1,b),\\; b=1,\\dots ,n,\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad (a,1),\\; a=2,\\dots ,n .\n\\]\n\nNow add the two non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{v}: x=1 ,\\qquad \\ell_{h}: y=1 .\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover precisely the uncovered points listed above.  \nAll together we have \\( (n-2)+2 = n\\) distinct lines; the two added lines are respectively vertical and horizontal, hence non\u2011sunny, while each \\(\\ell_i\\) has slope \\(-2\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\) and therefore is sunny.\n\nFinally we verify that every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on at least one line of the family:\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\) the point is on \\(\\ell_v\\) or \\(\\ell_h\\) respectively.  \n* Otherwise \\(a,b\\ge 2\\).  Write \\(a=b\\) or \\(a=b+2\\).  In the first case the point equals \\(P_{a,1}\\) and lies on \\(\\ell_a\\); in the second case it equals \\(P_{b,2}\\) and lies on \\(\\ell_{b}\\).  One checks that for all admissible \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) one of these two possibilities occurs, because the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(|a-b|\\le 2\\).  \n\nThus the constructed family satisfies all requirements and contains exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Consequence\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we have the universal inequality  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge n-2 .\n\\]\n\nLemma\u202f2 shows that the lower bound \\(k=n-2\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is a non\u2011empty subset of  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\,n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nDetermining whether the values \\(n-1\\) and \\(n\\) can always be realised (or whether further restrictions appear for particular \\(n\\)) remains an open problem.  The present work settles the minimal possible number of sunny lines. \u220e"
