en \\(\\ell\\) coincides with the anti\u2011diagonal \\(A_c\\); however a diagonal line is *parallel* to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore **not** sunny, so it can intersect any other anti\u2011diagonal in at most one point.\n\nHence any non\u2011sunny line contributes at most one point to each anti\u2011diagonal.\n\nLet \\(t\\) be the number of non\u2011sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nFor the anti\u2011diagonal \\(A_{n+1}\\) the \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines can cover at most \\(t\\) of its \\(n\\) points; the remaining \\(n-t\\) points must be covered by sunny lines.\n\nA sunny line cannot be horizontal, vertical or diagonal, therefore on a fixed anti\u2011diagonal it can contain **at most two** points (otherwise its slope would be \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)). Consequently at least  \n\n\\[\n\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n-t}{2}\\Big\\rceil\n\\]\n\nsunny lines are needed to cover those \\(n-t\\) points.\n\nSince the total number of lines equals \\(n\\),\n\n\\[\nn-t\\ge \\Big\\lceil\\frac{n-t}{2}\\Big\\rceil\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad n-t\\le 2 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(t\\ge n-2\\); i.e. at least \\(n-2\\) lines are sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Construction with exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines)\n\n*Statement.*  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nDefine, for each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\),\n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y=-2x+(2i+1).\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(-2\\) is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); hence each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.\n\nA direct substitution shows that \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the two lattice points  \n\n\\[\nP_{i,1}=(i,i),\\qquad P_{i,2}=(i+1,i-1).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(i\\le n-2\\),\n\n\\[\ni+i=2i\\le 2(n-2)\\le n+1,\\qquad (i+1)+(i-1)=2i\\le n+1,\n\\]\n\nso both points belong to \\(S_n\\).\n\nCollect the two non\u2011sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\ell_v:\\; x=1 ,\\qquad \\ell_h:\\; y=1 .\n\\]\n\nThese are respectively vertical and horizontal, therefore not sunny.\n\nNow we check that every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.\n\n*Points on the first row or column.*  \nIf a point has \\(a=1\\) it lies on \\(\\ell_v\\); if it has \\(b=1\\) it lies on \\(\\ell_h\\).\n\n*Points with \\(a,b\\ge 2\\).*  \nFor such a point the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) implies \\(|a-b|\\le 2\\).  \nThere are three possibilities:\n\n1. \\(a=b\\). Then the point equals \\(P_{a,1}\\) and lies on \\(\\ell_a\\) (note that \\(a\\le n-2\\) because \\(2a\\le n+1\\)).\n2. \\(a=b+2\\). Then the point equals \\(P_{b,2}\\) and lies on \\(\\ell_b\\) (here \\(b\\le n-2\\) because \\((b+2)+b=2b+2\\le n+1\\)).\n3. \\(b=a+2\\). Symmetrically the point equals \\(P_{a,2}\\) and lies on \\(\\ell_a\\).\n\nThus every lattice point of \\(S_n\\) is covered.\n\nThe lines are distinct: the two non\u2011sunny lines have different directions from the sunny ones, and the sunny lines have distinct intercepts \\(2i+1\\) for different \\(i\\).\n\nHence we have constructed a family of \\(n\\) lines with exactly two non\u2011sunny lines, i.e. with \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, satisfying the required covering property. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nLemma\u202f1 shows that any admissible configuration must contain at least \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  \nLemma\u202f2 provides, for every \\(n\\ge 3\\), a configuration that contains **exactly** \\(n-2\\) sunny lines and meets all the conditions of the problem.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is the singleton  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,n-2\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for each \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) **iff** exactly \\(n-2\\) of those lines are sunny. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to ac