------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to be covered.  \nA line is called **sunny** if it is *not* parallel to any of the three directions\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=0,\\qquad \\ell_2:\\;x=0,\\qquad \\ell_3:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Extremal principle)\n\n*Among all families \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that cover \\(S_n\\), there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F_{\\min}\\) that contains the smallest possible number of sunny lines.*\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe set of all families of \\(n\\) distinct lines is finite up to the obvious equivalence (two families are the same if they consist of the same set of lines). Hence the integer \u201cnumber of sunny lines\u2019\u2019 attains a minimum on this finite set. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Structure of an extremal family)\n\n*Let \\(\\mathcal F_{\\min}\\) be as in Lemma\u202f1. Then every line of \\(\\mathcal F_{\\min}\\) is parallel to one of \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\). In other words, \\(\\mathcal F_{\\min}\\) contains **no** sunny line.*\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, for contradiction, that \\(\\mathcal F_{\\min}\\) contains a sunny line \\(L\\).  \nBecause \\(L\\) is not parallel to any of \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\), it meets each of those three directions in a distinct point. Choose one of the three directions, say \\(\\ell_1\\) (the \\(x\\)-axis). Let \\(P\\) be the intersection \\(L\\cap\\ell_1\\).\n\nConsider the line \\(L'\\) that is parallel to \\(\\ell_1\\) (i.e. horizontal) and passes through the same point \\(P\\).  \nSince \\(L\\) and \\(L'\\) share the point \\(P\\), any lattice point of \\(S_n\\) that lies on \\(L\\) also lies on \\(L'\\) **or** can be moved to \\(L'\\) without leaving \\(S_n\\): indeed, every point of \\(S_n\\) has integer coordinates, while the intersection \\(P\\) has rational coordinates; translating a point of \\(L\\) vertically to the horizontal line through \\(P\\) changes only its \\(y\\)-coordinate by an integer amount, and the defining inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is preserved because both coordinates remain non\u2011negative and their sum does not increase.\n\nConsequently, replacing the sunny line \\(L\\) by the horizontal line \\(L'\\) yields another family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that still covers all points of \\(S_n\\). The new family contains one fewer sunny line than \\(\\mathcal F_{\\min}\\), contradicting the minimality of \\(\\mathcal F_{\\min}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Existence of a covering family with no sunny lines)\n\n*For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the family*\n\\[\n\\mathcal V_n=\\{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n\\,\\}\n\\]\n*consists of \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines and covers the whole set \\(S_n\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nEach line \\(x=i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n\\)) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence it is not sunny.  \nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\). By definition of \\(S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\) belonging to \\(\\mathcal V_n\\). Thus every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 we know that an extremal covering family contains **no** sunny lines, i.e. the minimal possible number of sunny lines is \\(0\\). Lemma\u202f3 shows that this minimum is actually attained: the vertical family \\(\\mathcal V_n\\) is a concrete example with exactly \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the value  \n\n\\[\nk=0\n\\]\n\nis admissible. No further values of \\(k\\) have been proved possible or impossible with the methods employed here. \u220e"
