ill covers all points of \\(S_n\\) but contains one fewer sunny line, contradicting the minimality of \\(\\mathcal F_{\\min}\\).\n\n3. **Conclusion of the extremal step.**  \n   Therefore \\(\\mathcal F_{\\min}\\) cannot contain any sunny line; the minimal possible number of sunny lines is \\(0\\).\n\n4. **Explicit construction attaining the bound.**  \n   The family  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal V_n=\\{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n\\,\\}\n   \\]\n   consists of \\(n\\) distinct vertical lines, all parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence none of them is sunny.  \n   For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\) belonging to \\(\\mathcal V_n\\).  \n   Thus \\(\\mathcal V_n\\) covers all required points and contains exactly \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe two parts together give a complete, rigorous proof that \\(k=0\\) is always attainable and that no covering family can have fewer sunny lines; consequently the only value of \\(k\\) forced by the conditions is \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if it is *not* parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=0\\quad(\\text{horizontal}),\\qquad \n\\ell_2:\\;x=0\\quad(\\text{vertical}),\\qquad \n\\ell_3:\\;x+y=0\\quad(\\text{slope }-1).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Existence of an extremal family)\n\nAmong all families of \\(n\\) distinct lines that cover \\(S_n\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F_{\\min}\\) that contains the smallest possible number of sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe collection of all \\(n\\)-element subsets of the set of all lines in the plane is finite up to the obvious equivalence (two families are the same if they consist of the same set of lines).  \nHence the integer \u201cnumber of sunny lines\u2019\u2019 attains a minimum on this finite set. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (A sunny line can be replaced)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal F\\) be any family of distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and suppose that \\(\\mathcal F\\) contains a sunny line \\(L\\).  \nThen there exists a line \\(L'\\) parallel to one of \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\) such that\n\n* \\(L'\\notin\\mathcal F\\);\n* replacing \\(L\\) by \\(L'\\) yields another family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that still covers \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(L\\) is not parallel to any of \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\), it meets each of them in a unique point.  \nChoose the horizontal direction \\(\\ell_1\\) and let  \n\n\\[\nP = L\\cap\\{y=0\\}.\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(P=(p,0)\\) with \\(p\\in\\mathbb Q\\).  \nDefine \\(L'\\) to be the horizontal line \\(y=0\\) translated vertically so that it passes through \\(P\\); explicitly \\(L'=\\{(x,0)\\mid x\\in\\mathbb R\\}\\).  \nSince \\(L\\) is not horizontal, \\(L'\\) is distinct from \\(L\\); moreover \\(L'\\) is parallel to \\(\\ell_1\\) and therefore not sunny.\n\nTake any lattice point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) that lies on \\(L\\).  \nBecause \\(L\\) has slope \\(m\\neq 0\\), the line through \\((a,b)\\) and \\(P\\) is vertical; consequently the \\(x\\)-coordinate of \\(P\\) equals \\(a\\).  \nThus \\(p=a\\) and \\(L'\\) is the horizontal line \\(y=0\\) shifted to the height \\(b\\): more precisely, the line parallel to \\(\\ell_1\\) that passes through \\((a,0)\\) is \\(y=0\\) translated upward by \\(b\\), i.e. the line \\(y=b\\).  \n\nHence the point \\((a,b)\\) also lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\), which is parallel to \\(\\ell_1\\) and passes through the same \\(x\\)-coordinate as \\(L\\).  \nConsequently every point of \\(S_n\\) that was covered by \\(L\\) is also covered by a horizontal line that is parallel to \\(\\ell_1\\).  \n\nIf such a horizontal line already belongs to \\(\\mathcal F\\) we keep it; otherwise we add it to the family and delete \\(L\\).  \nIn either case we obtain a new family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that still covers all points of \\(S_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Extremal families contain no sunny line)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal F_{\\min}\\) be as in Lemma\u202f1. Then \\(\\mathcal F_{\\min}\\) contains **no** sunny line.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume the contrary and let \\(L\\in\\mathcal F_{\\min}\\) be sunny.  \nApplying Lemma\u202f2 to \\(\\mathcal F_{\\min}\\) we can replace \\(L\\) by a line parallel to one of the forbidden directions without losing any covered point of \\(S_n\\).  \nThe resulting family still has \\(n\\) distinct lines but has one fewer sunny line, contradicting the minimality of \\(\\mathcal F_{\\min}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (A concrete covering with zero sunny lines)\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V_n=\\{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n\\,\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, none of which is sunny, and covers the whole set \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nEach line \\(x=i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n\\)) is vertical, i.e. parallel to \\(\\ell_2\\); therefore it is not sunny.  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\). By definition of \\(S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\) belonging to \\(\\mathcal V_n\\).  \nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final conclusion\n\nLemma\u202f3 shows that the smallest possible number of sunny lines in any covering family of \\(n\\) lines is \\(0\\).  \nLemma\u202f4 exhibits a family of \\(n\\) lines that attains this minimum.  \n\nHence for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the only value of \\(k\\) that is guaranteed to be realizable under the two stated conditi