tin\\mathcal F\\);\n* replacing \\(L\\) by \\(L'\\) yields another family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that still covers \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(L\\) is not parallel to any of \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\), it meets each of them in a unique point.  \nChoose the horizontal direction \\(\\ell_1\\) and let  \n\n\\[\nP = L\\cap\\{y=0\\}.\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(P=(p,0)\\) with \\(p\\in\\mathbb Q\\).  \nDefine \\(L'\\) to be the horizontal line \\(y=0\\) translated vertically so that it passes through \\(P\\); explicitly \\(L'=\\{(x,0)\\mid x\\in\\mathbb R\\}\\).  \nSince \\(L\\) is not horizontal, \\(L'\\) is distinct from \\(L\\); moreover \\(L'\\) is parallel to \\(\\ell_1\\) and therefore not sunny.\n\nTake any lattice point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) that lies on \\(L\\).  \nBecause \\(L\\) has slope \\(m\\neq 0\\), the line through \\((a,b)\\) and \\(P\\) is vertical; consequently the \\(x\\)-coordinate of \\(P\\) equals \\(a\\).  \nThus \\(p=a\\) and \\(L'\\) is the horizontal line \\(y=0\\) shifted to the height \\(b\\): more precisely, the line parallel to \\(\\ell_1\\) that passes through \\((a,0)\\) is \\(y=0\\) translated upward by \\(b\\), i.e. the line \\(y=b\\).  \n\nHence the point \\((a,b)\\) also lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\), which is parallel to \\(\\ell_1\\) and passes through the same \\(x\\)-coordinate as \\(L\\).  \nConsequently every point of \\(S_n\\) that was covered by \\(L\\) is also covered by a horizontal line that is parallel to \\(\\ell_1\\).  \n\nIf such a horizontal line already belongs to \\(\\mathcal F\\) we keep it; otherwise we add it to the family and delete \\(L\\).  \nIn either case we obtain a new family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that still covers all points of \\(S_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Extremal families contain no sunny line)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal F_{\\min}\\) be as in Lemma\u202f1. Then \\(\\mathcal F_{\\min}\\) contains **no** sunny line.\n\n*