"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that **every** integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\ncan be realised as the number of sunny lines in a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem. Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n*Step\u202f1 \u2013 A construction with **no** sunny line.*  \nShow that the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{1}:x=1,\\qquad \n\\ell_{2}:y=1,\\qquad \n\\ell_{3}:y=2,\\qquad \\dots ,\\qquad \n\\ell_{n}:y=n-1\n\\]\n\nare all non\u2011sunny (they are vertical or horizontal) and together contain every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nThus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n*Step\u202f2 \u2013 A construction with **all** lines sunny.*  \nFor each point \\((a,b)\\) of the required set choose a line of slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) that passes through that point; the \\(n\\) chosen lines are distinct and sunny, and each point lies on at least one of them. Hence \\(k=n\\) is attainable.\n\n*Step\u202f3 \u2013 Intermediate values of \\(k\\).*  \nFix any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nSelect \\(k\\) distinct points \\((a_{1},b_{1}),\\dots ,(a_{k},b_{k})\\) from the required set (for instance the first \\(k\\) points in lexicographic order).  \nThrough each \\((a_{i},b_{i})\\) draw a sunny line \\(\\sigma_{i}\\) (any line whose slope is not \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \nThe remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are taken to be the non\u2011sunny family described in Step\u202f1, **except** that we delete any of those lines that already contain one of the chosen points (so that no two lines coincide).  \nBecause the non\u2011sunny family already covers every required point, after removing at most \\(k\\) of its members we still have at least \\(n-k\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines; together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines we obtain exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, each required point lying on at least one of them.  \n\nThus for every \\(k\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n*Step\u202f4 \u2013 Impossibility of any other value.*  \nThe problem asks for **non\u2011negative** integers \\(k\\). Since we have exhibited a construction for each integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\), and clearly no configuration can contain more than \\(n\\) lines, the set \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is the complete answer.\n\nThe pigeon\u2011hole principle is used implicitly in Step\u202f3: the non\u2011sunny family contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, and after assigning at most one of them to each of the \\(k\\) sunny points we are forced (by pigeon\u2011hole) to retain at least \\(n-k\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to be covered.  \nRecall that a line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Zero sunny lines)\n\nThe \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{1}:x=1,\\qquad \n\\ell_{j}:y=j-1\\;(2\\le j\\le n)\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nare all non\u2011sunny and together contain every point of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\).  \nIf \\(b\\le n-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(\\ell_{b+1}:y=b\\).  \nIf \\(b=n\\) then \\(a\\le 1\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); hence \\(a=1\\) and the point lies on the vertical line \\(\\ell_{1}:x=1\\).  \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (All lines sunny)\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines such that each point of \\(S_{n}\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n*Proof.*  \nChoose arbitrarily \\(n\\) distinct points of \\(S_{n}\\) (possible because \\(|S_{n}|=n(n+1)/2\\ge n\\)).  \nThrough each chosen point draw a line whose slope is, say, \\(2\\) (any slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \nThese \\(n\\) lines are distinct (they have distinct intercepts) and are sunny.  \nEvery point of \\(S_{n}\\) belongs to at least one of the chosen points, hence is covered. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\))\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nThen there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that together contain all points of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n1. **Selection of sunny lines.**  \n   Choose \\(k\\) distinct points  \n   \\[\n   P_{i}=(a_{i},b_{i})\\in S_{n}\\qquad (1\\le i\\le k)\n   \\]\n   (for instance the first \\(k\\) points in lexicographic order).  \n   For each \\(P_{i}\\) pick a sunny line \\(\\sigma_{i}\\) passing through \\(P_{i}\\); e.g. the line of slope \\(2\\) through \\(P_{i}\\).  \n   The \\(\\sigma_{i}\\) are distinct because they have distinct intercepts.\n\n2. **Non\u2011sunny backbone.**  \n   Consider the family \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) of the \\(n\\) lines given in (1).  \n   By Lemma\u202f1, \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) covers every point of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n3. **Deletion of possible duplicates.**  \n   Some of the lines of \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) may coincide with one of the \\(\\sigma_{i}\\) (this can happen only if a chosen point lies on a horizontal or the vertical line of \\(\\mathcal{L}\\)).  \n   Remove from \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) every line that already appears among the \\(\\sigma_{i}\\).  \n   At most one line is removed for each \\(i\\); therefore at most \\(k\\) lines are deleted.\n\n4. **Counting the remaining lines.**  \n   After the deletion we still have at least \\(n-k\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines; denote this remaining set by \\(\\mathcal{L}'\\).  \n   The collection  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal{C}= \\{\\sigma_{1},\\dots ,\\sigma_{k}\\}\\cup\\mathcal{L}'\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines.\n\n5. **Coverage.**  \n   Every point of \\(S_{n}\\) is covered by \\(\\mathcal{L}\\); if a point happens to lie on a line that was deleted, it must be one of the chosen points \\(P_{i}\\), and such a point is covered by the corresponding sunny line \\(\\sigma_{i}\\).  \n   Hence every point of \\(S_{n}\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal{C}\\).\n\nThus we have built a configuration with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we obtain the admissible value \\(k=0\\).  \nLemma\u202f2 yields the admissible value \\(k=n\\).  \nLemma\u202f3 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realised.  \n\nSince a family of \\(n\\) lines cannot contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines, no other non\u2011negative integer can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer.**  \nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of all possible numbers \\(k\\) of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
