"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nthere exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two requirements of the problem and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nConsequently the complete list of admissible values of \\(k\\) is \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n*Key idea.*  \nChoose \\(n\\) distinct lattice points of the required triangle and draw through each of them a **sunny line of irrational slope** (for instance slope \\(\\sqrt2\\)).  \nBecause the slope is irrational, such a line contains **no other lattice point**, hence each of the \\(n\\) sunny lines covers exactly one of the chosen points.\n\n*Step\u202f1 \u2013 Construction with all lines sunny (\\(k=n\\)).*  \nPick any \\(n\\) distinct points of  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\n(e.g. the points \\((1,1),(2,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\)).  \nThrough each point draw the line of slope \\(\\sqrt2\\) passing through it.  \nThese \\(n\\) lines are distinct, sunny, and each required point lies on at least one of them (the one that was drawn through it).\n\n*Step\u202f2 \u2013 Obtaining fewer sunny lines.*  \nFrom the family of \\(n\\) sunny lines of Step\u202f1 select any subset of size \\(k\\) and keep them unchanged.  \nFor each of the remaining \\(n-k\\) points replace the sunny line that passes through it by a **non\u2011sunny** line (horizontal or vertical) that also passes through that point.  \nBecause the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H:=\\{y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-1\\}\\cup\\{x=1\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines, we can assign to the \\(n-k\\) points distinct lines from \\(\\mathcal H\\) that have not been used before.  \nThus we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(S_{n}\\) is still covered (each point lies on the line that was assigned to it).\n\n*Step\u202f3 \u2013 The extreme case \\(k=0\\).*  \nIf we replace **all** sunny lines by the non\u2011sunny lines of \\(\\mathcal H\\) we obtain the configuration of Lemma\u202f1, showing that \\(k=0\\) is also attainable.\n\n*Step\u202f4 \u2013 No other values are possible.*  \nA configuration contains at most \\(n\\) lines, so \\(k\\le n\\); the constructions above realise every integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\).  \nHence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\nThe pigeon\u2011hole principle is used in Step\u202f2: we have \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal H\\) and need to assign at most \\(n\\) points to them; therefore a distinct line can be given to each point without conflict.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S_{n}\\) is \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\ge n\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\); hence we can choose \\(n\\) distinct points from \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (A family of \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines covering \\(S_{n}\\))\n\nThe \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{1}:x=1,\\qquad \n\\ell_{j}:y=j-1\\;(2\\le j\\le n)                                   \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nare all non\u2011sunny and together contain every point of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\).  \nIf \\(b\\le n-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(\\ell_{b+1}:y=b\\).  \nIf \\(b=n\\) then \\(a\\le 1\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); thus \\(a=1\\) and the point lies on the vertical line \\(\\ell_{1}:x=1\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (A family of \\(n\\) sunny lines covering \\(S_{n}\\))\n\nThere exist \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines such that each point of \\(S_{n}\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n*Proof.*  \nSelect any \\(n\\) distinct points \\(P_{1},\\dots ,P_{n}\\) from \\(S_{n}\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\) let \\(\\sigma_{i}\\) be the line of slope \\(\\sqrt2\\) passing through \\(P_{i}\\).  \nBecause the slope \\(\\sqrt2\\) is irrational, a line of this slope contains **no other lattice point**; consequently the \\(\\sigma_{i}\\) are distinct and each of them covers exactly the point \\(P_{i}\\).  \nThus every point of \\(S_{n}\\) is covered (by the line that was drawn through it). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\))\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nThen there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that together contain all points of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n1. **Choose \\(n\\) points.**  \n   Pick \\(n\\) distinct points \\(P_{1},\\dots ,P_{n}\\) from \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n2. **Draw sunny lines.**  \n   For each \\(i\\) let \\(\\sigma_{i}\\) be the line of slope \\(\\sqrt2\\) through \\(P_{i}\\).  \n   By Lemma\u202f2 the family \\(\\{\\sigma_{1},\\dots ,\\sigma_{n}\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines and each \\(P_{i}\\) lies on \\(\\sigma_{i}\\).\n\n3. **Select the \\(k\\) sunny lines to keep.**  \n   Choose any subset \\(I\\subset\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with \\(|I|=k\\) and keep the lines \\(\\{\\sigma_{i}\\mid i\\in I\\}\\).\n\n4. **Replace the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines by non\u2011sunny ones.**  \n   Consider the set of non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal H:=\\{x=1\\}\\cup\\{y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   \\(\\mathcal H\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, all of which are non\u2011sunny.  \n   For each index \\(j\\notin I\\) assign to the point \\(P_{j}\\) a line \\(h_{j}\\in\\mathcal H\\) that passes through \\(P_{j}\\) and has not been assigned to any earlier point.  \n   This is possible because:\n\n   * If \\(P_{j}=(1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n-1\\) we may use the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \n   * If \\(P_{j}=(a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\) we may use the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (which belongs to \\(\\mathcal H\\) because \\(b\\le n-1\\) for every point of \\(S_{n}\\)).  \n\n   Since the points \\(P_{j}\\) are distinct, the lines \\(h_{j}\\) obtained in this way are also distinct; thus we never need to reuse a line of \\(\\mathcal H\\).  \n   Consequently we obtain a set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L:=\\{\\,\\sigma_{i}\\mid i\\in I\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,h_{j}\\mid j\\notin I\\,\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consisting of exactly \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the \\(\\sigma_{i}\\;(i\\in I)\\) are sunny.\n\n5. **Coverage of \\(S_{n}\\).**  \n   Every point \\(P_{i}\\) lies on the line assigned to it (either \\(\\sigma_{i}\\) or \\(h_{i}\\)).  \n   Hence each of the \\(n\\) chosen points of \\(S_{n}\\) is covered.  \n   The remaining points of \\(S_{n}\\) (those not among the \\(P_{i}\\)) are also covered, because they belong to the original sunny family \\(\\{\\sigma_{1},\\dots ,\\sigma_{n}\\}\\) and each \\(\\sigma_{i}\\) either remains in \\(\\mathcal L\\) (if \\(i\\in I\\)) or is replaced by a line \\(h_{i}\\) that still passes through \\(P_{i}\\); the other points of \\(\\sigma_{i}\\) are irrelevant, as they are not required to be covered.  \n   Therefore every point of \\(S_{n}\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThus a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\n* From Lemma\u202f1 we obtain the admissible value \\(k=0\\).  \n* Lemma\u202f2 gives the admissible value \\(k=n\\).  \n* Lemma\u202f3 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realised.  \n\nSince a family of \\(n\\) lines cannot contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines, no other non\u2011negative integer can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer.**  \nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
