n-k+2\\) and \\(c\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n2a-c+1 \\le 2a-(n-k+2)+1 = 2a-n+k-1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(c\\le n+1\\), inequality (8) yields \\(1\\le i\\le k\\).  \nConsequently \\(i\\) is an integer belonging to \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) and (7) can be rewritten as \\(b = a-i+1\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of the sunny line \\(S_i\\) in (4). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(S_i\\).\n\nThus in every possible situation the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L(k)\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Proof that exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L(k)\\) are sunny\n\nAll lines \\(S_i\\) have slope \\(+1\\); this slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), undefined (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)). Hence each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  \n\nLines \\(H\\) and \\(D_j\\) are respectively horizontal and of slope \\(-1\\); both directions are forbidden, so none of them is sunny.  \n\nTherefore the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L(k)\\) equals the number of lines of type (4), namely \\(k\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (Attainability of every \\(k\\))\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) the family \\(\\mathcal L(k)\\) constructed in Lemma\u202f2 satisfies all requirements of the problem. Consequently \\(k\\in\\mathcal K_n\\).\n\n*Proof.* Lemma\u202f2 shows that \\(\\mathcal L(k)\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers all points of \\(S_n\\), and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem (Complete determination of admissible \\(k\\))\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_n = \\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.* By Lemma\u202f1 any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy \\(k\\le n\\). Lemma\u202f3 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is indeed admissible. Hence the set of all admissible values is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nThe problem is completely solved: for each \\(n\\ge 3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines in a suitable family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0,\\ a+b\\le n+1\\) are precisely the integers from \\(0\\) up to \\(n\\). The construction given in Lemma\u202f2 provides an explicit example for every such \\(k\\)."
