most one** point of \\(S_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Upper bound on the number of sunny lines)\n\nThe set \\(S_n\\) contains exactly  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\npoints (for each possible sum \\(s=a+b\\) there are \\(s-1\\) choices of \\((a,b)\\)).  \n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfy the covering condition and contain \\(k\\) sunny lines. By Lemma\u202f1 the sunny lines together cover at most \\(k\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n\nEach non\u2011sunny line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions. Such a line can intersect the region \\(\\{(x,y)\\mid x\\ge 1,\\ y\\ge 1,\\ x+y\\le n+1\\}\\) in at most \\(n\\) lattice points (a simple inspection of the three possible directions shows that the maximal number of integer points on any such line inside the triangle is \\(n\\)). Consequently the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines cover at most \\((n-k)\\,n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nHence the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\cdot 1+(n-k)\\cdot n = n^{2}-k(n-1).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFor the covering condition to hold we need (2) \\(\\ge |S_n|\\). Using (1),\n\n\\[\nn^{2}-k(n-1)\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n2n^{2}-2k(n-1)\\;\\ge\\;n^{2}+n\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nn^{2}-2k(n-1)-n\\;\\ge\\;0.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n\\ge 3\\), the left\u2011hand side is decreasing in \\(k\\). Substituting \\(k=1\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nn^{2}-2(n-1)-n = n^{2}-3n+2 = (n-1)(n-2) >0\n\\]\n\nonly for \\(n=2\\), which is excluded. For any \\(k\\ge 1\\) and \\(n\\ge 3\\) the inequality fails. Therefore the only possible value is  \n\n\\[\nk=0.\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (Existence for \\(k=0\\))\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; x+y=i+1,\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,n.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEach line \\(