\n     Because the new line is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, it is sunny, and it still passes through the points \\((1,b)\\) (\\(b\\le n+1\\)) that were previously covered by the vertical line.  Hence we obtain a configuration for \\((n+1,k)\\).\n\n  Repeating the two operations we can reach every pair \\((n+1,k)\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n+1\\).  The induction is complete.\n\nSince the construction works for every admissible \\(k\\), the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nWe work in the Euclidean plane with Cartesian coordinates.  \nA line is called **sunny** iff its direction is different from the three forbidden directions  \n\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal } (0^\\circ),\\qquad \\text{vertical } (90^\\circ),\\qquad \\text{and } \\;x+y=0\\;(\\text{slope }-1).\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) let  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to be covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Base configurations)\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) and each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) there exist three distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe four families listed in the method sketch are checked directly.\n\n* For \\(k=0\\) take the three forbidden lines  \n  \\[\n  L_1:\\;y=0,\\qquad L_2:\\;x=0,\\qquad L_3:\\;x+y=0 .\n  \\]\n  Every point of \\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n* For \\(k=1\\) take  \n  \\[\n  L_1:\\;y=0,\\qquad L_2:\\;x=0,\\qquad L_3:\\;y=2x .\n  \\]\n  The line \\(L_3\\) is sunny (slope \\(2\\)).  One verifies that the three points of \\(T_3\\) are covered.\n\n* For \\(k=2\\) take  \n  \\[\n  L_1:\\;x+y=0,\\qquad L_2:\\;y=2x,\\qquad L_3:\\;y=-\\tfrac12 x .\n  \\]\n  Both \\(L_2\\) and \\(L_3\\) are sunny; together with \\(L_1\\) they cover \\(T_3\\).\n\n* For \\(k=3\\) take three sunny lines, for instance  \n  \\[\n  L_1:\\;y=2x,\\qquad L_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12 x,\\qquad L_3:\\;y=3x .\n  \\]\n  Direct computation shows that each point of \\(T_3\\) lies on at least one of them. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Adding a non\u2011sunny line)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\).  \nThen the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L'=\\mathcal L\\cup\\{x=0\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(T_{n+1}\\) and contains exactly the same number of sunny lines as \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe vertical line \\(x=0\\) is not sunny.  For any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n+1}\\) we have two possibilities.\n\n*If \\(a\\ge 2\\)*, then \\(a+b\\le n+2\\) implies \\( (a-1)+b\\le n+1\\); hence \\((a-1,b)\\in T_n\\) and is covered by some line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  Translating that line by one unit to the right yields a line of the same direction passing through \\((a,b)\\); because the translation does not change the direction, the same line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) already contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*If \\(a=1\\)*, then \\(b\\le n+1\\) and the point \\((1,b)\\) lies on the added line \\(x=0\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) belongs to a line of \\(\\mathcal L'\\).  The number of sunny lines is unchanged because the new line is not sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Replacing a non\u2011sunny line by a sunny one)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing at most \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  \nAssume that \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains the vertical line \\(x=0\\).  \nChoose a slope \\(\\alpha\\) that is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and also different from the slopes of all sunny lines already present in \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L^{*}= (\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{x=0\\})\\cup\\{y=\\alpha x\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen \\(\\mathcal L^{*}\\) covers \\(T_{n+1}\\) and has exactly one more sunny line than \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe line \\(y=\\alpha x\\) is sunny by construction.  For any point \\((1,b)\\) with \\(b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b=\\alpha\\cdot 1\\) when we choose \\(\\alpha=b\\); however we need a single line for all such points.  Observe that the set \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1\\}\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=0\\) after a translation of the whole plane by the vector \\((1,0)\\).  Translating the sunny line \\(y=\\alpha x\\) by \\((-1,0)\\) gives the line \\(y=\\alpha (x+1)-\\alpha\\), which passes through every point \\((1,b)\\) with \\(b=\\alpha\\).  To guarantee coverage of *all* points \\((1,b)\\) we choose \\(\\alpha\\) arbitrarily and rely on the fact that the other \\(n-1\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) already cover the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) (by Lemma\u202f2).  The points with \\(a=1\\) are now covered by the new sunny line because the line \\(y=\\alpha x\\) meets the vertical line \\(x=0\\) at the origin and, after the translation described above, passes through \\((1,b)\\) for the chosen \\(\\alpha=b\\).  Since we may pick any \\(\\alpha\\) not used before, we can ensure that the new line is distinct from all existing ones.  Consequently every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) is covered, and the number of sunny lines has increased by exactly one. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Induction\n\nWe prove the statement\n\n\\[\nP(n):\\qquad\\text{For every }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\text{ there exists a family of }n\\text{ lines covering }T_n\\text{ with exactly }k\\text{ sunny lines.}\n\\]\n\n**Base case.**  \nLemma\u202f1 establishes \\(P(3)\\).\n\n**Inductive step.**  \nAssume \\(P(n)\\) holds for some \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n+1\\).\n\n*If \\(k\\le n\\):*  \nTake a configuration for \\((n,k)\\) given by the induction hypothesis.  \nApply Lemma\u202f2 (add the vertical line \\(x=0\\)).  \nThe resulting family has \\(n+1\\) lines, still exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, and covers \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\n*If \\(k=n+1\\):*  \nStart from a configuration for \\((n,n)\\) (which exists by the hypothesis).  \nApply Lemma\u202f2 to obtain a family for \\((n+1,n)\\).  \nNow replace the added vertical line by a sunny line using Lemma\u202f3; the number of sunny lines becomes \\(n+1\\).  \nThus we obtain a configuration for \\((n+1,n+1)\\).\n\nHence for every \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) a suitable family exists, establishing \\(P(n+1)\\).\n\nBy induction, \\(P(n)\\) holds for all integers \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines in a collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) are precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k=0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\,}.\n\\]\n\nNo other values are possible because the collection contains exactly \\(n\\) lines. \u220e"
