b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\) and \\(1\\le a,b\\le n\\).  Together they cover all points of \\(S\\) whose sum is either \\(1\\) or \\(n+1\\).\n\n   - The remaining points are those with \\(2\\le a+b\\le n\\).  Their number is\n     \\[\n     |S|-|L_{1}\\cap S|-|L_{2}\\cap S|=\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2 .\n     \\]\n\n   - For any prescribed integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we now take \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines, each of which passes through exactly one of the remaining points and through no other point of \\(S\\).  This is possible because a sunny line with irrational slope meets the integer lattice in at most one point; we may choose \\(k\\) different irrational slopes and translate each line so that it passes through a distinct uncovered point.  These \\(k\\) lines are automatically sunny.\n\n   - After placing the \\(k\\) sunny lines there are still\n     \\[\n     \\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2-k\n     \\]\n     uncovered points.  Since \\(k\\le n-2\\), this number is at most \\(\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2-(n-2)=\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}2\\).  We can cover the remaining points with the remaining \\(n-2-k\\) lines by taking each of them to be a vertical line \\(x=c\\) for a suitable integer \\(c\\) (these lines are non\u2011sunny).  A vertical line \\(x=c\\) contains all points of \\(S\\) with first coordinate \\(c\\); by choosing the appropriate set of distinct integers \\(c\\) we can cover all still\u2011uncovered points because each column contains at most \\(n-c\\) points and the total number of columns needed never exceeds \\(n-2-k\\).\n\n   - Altogether we have used exactly \\(2\\) non\u2011sunny lines (\\(L_{1},L_{2}\\)), \\(k\\) sunny lines, and \\(n-2-k\\) additional non\u2011sunny vertical lines, for a total of \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.  All points of \\(S\\) lie on at least one of these lines, as required.\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Points on a sunny line)\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line.  Write its equation in reduced rational form  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{p}{q}x+r\\qquad(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1)\n\\]\n\nor, if the slope is irrational, write \\(y=mx+r\\) with \\(m\\notin\\mathbb Q\\).\n\n*If the slope is rational*, any two integer points on \\(L\\) differ by a vector \\((q,p)\\); consequently the set \\(L\\cap\\mathbb Z^{2}\\) is an arithmetic progression with step \\((q,p)\\).  Hence the number of points of \\(L\\) that belong to \\(S\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{q}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*If the slope is irrational*, \\(L\\) contains at most one integer point, so (1) holds as well (with \\(q\\ge n+1\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Upper bound for the number of sunny lines)\n\nAssume that among the \\(n\\) required lines exactly \\(k\\) are sunny.  Let the sunny lines have reduced rational slopes \\(\\frac{p_{i}}{q_{i}}\\) (\\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\)); for irrational slopes we set \\(q_{i}=n+1\\).  By Lemma\u202f1 the total number of points of \\(S\\) covered by the sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{k}\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{q_{i}}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le\n\\sum_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{n+1}{q_{i}} .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA non\u2011sunny line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or to \\(x+y=0\\).  In each case it can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\) (a horizontal line meets at most the points \\((a,1)\\), a vertical line at most the points \\((1,b)\\), and a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) at most the points with a fixed sum \\(a+b\\)).  Hence the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines together cover at most \\((n-k)n\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\nAdding the contributions of sunny and non\u2011sunny lines and using (2) we obtain\n\n\\[\n|S|\n\\le (n-k)n+\\sum_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{n+1}{q_{i}}\n\\le (n-k)n+k\\frac{n+1}{2},\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(q_{i}\\ge 2\\) for every sunny line (its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  Simplifying (3) gives\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n^{2}-kn+\\frac{k(n+1)}2,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus **at least two of the \\(n\\) lines must be non\u2011sunny**, and consequently \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Construction for any \\(k\\le n-2\\))\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  We exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n1. **Two fixed non\u2011sunny lines**\n\n   \\[\n   L_{1}:\\;y=1\\qquad(\\text{parallel to the }x\\text{-axis}),\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   L_{2}:\\;x+y=n+1\\qquad(\\text{parallel to }x+y=0).\n   \\]\n\n   \\(L_{1}\\) contains the points \\((a,1)\\) for \\(1\\le a\\le n\\);  \n   \\(L_{2}\\) contains the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\) and \\(1\\le a,b\\le n\\).\n\n   Hence the points of \\(S\\) with \\(a+b=1\\) or \\(a+b=n+1\\) are already covered.\n\n2. **The remaining points**\n\n   The set of uncovered points is  \n\n   \\[\n   S' :=\\{(a,b)\\in S\\mid 2\\le a+b\\le n\\},\n   \\qquad |S'|=\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n3. **\\(k\\) sunny lines**\n\n   Choose \\(k\\) distinct irrational numbers \\(\\alpha_{1},\\dots ,\\alpha_{k}\\).  \n   For each \\(i\\) pick a point \\((a_{i},b_{i})\\in S'\\) (all chosen points are distinct) and define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   M_{i}:\\;y=\\alpha_{i}x+(b_{i}-\\alpha_{i}a_{i}).\n   \\]\n\n   Because the slope \\(\\alpha_{i}\\) is irrational, \\(M_{i}\\) meets the integer lattice only at \\((a_{i},b_{i})\\); consequently each