            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Points of \\(S\\) on a sunny line)\n\nWrite a sunny line in reduced rational form  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{p}{q}x+r\\qquad(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1)\n\\]\n\nor, if the slope is irrational, as \\(y=mx+r\\) with \\(m\\notin\\mathbb Q\\).\n\n*If the slope is rational*, any two integer points on the line differ by the vector \\((q,p)\\); therefore the integer points on the line form an arithmetic progression with step \\((q,p)\\).  Consequently the number of points of \\(S\\) that lie on the line is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{q}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*If the slope is irrational*, the line contains at most one integer point, so (1) holds as well (with \\(q\\ge n+1\\)).  \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Upper bound for the number of sunny lines)\n\nAssume that among the required \\(n\\) lines exactly \\(k\\) are sunny.\nLet the sunny lines have reduced rational slopes \\(\\frac{p_{i}}{q_{i}}\\) (\\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\));\nfor an irrational slope we set \\(q_{i}=n+1\\).\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 the total number of points of \\(S\\) covered by the sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{k}\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{q_{i}}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\le\\sum_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{n+1}{q_{i}}\n\\le k\\frac{n+1}{2},\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nbecause every sunny line has \\(q_{i}\\ge2\\).\n\nA non\u2011sunny line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or to the line \\(x+y=0\\).\nIn each of these three directions a line can meet at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\)\n(e.g. a horizontal line meets at most the points \\((a,1)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\)).\nHence the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines together cover at most \\((n-k)n\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\nAdding the contributions of sunny and non\u2011sunny lines and using (2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n|S|\n\\le (n-k)n+k\\frac{n+1}{2}\n= n^{2}-kn+\\frac{k(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|S|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\), the inequality simplifies to  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n^{2}-kn+\\frac{k(n+1)}2\n\\Longrightarrow k\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus at least two of the \\(n\\) lines must be non\u2011sunny, i.e. \\(k\\le n-2\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Construction for any admissible \\(k\\))\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\nWe exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem\nand having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n1. **Vertical non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) consider the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   V_{c}:\\;x=c .\n   \\]\n\n   \\(V_{c}\\) contains all points of \\(S\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(c\\); in\n   particular it contains the points \\((c,1),\\dots ,(c,n+1-c)\\).\n\n2. **Choice of columns for sunny lines.**  \n   Choose \\(k\\) distinct columns  \n\n   \\[\n   c_{1},c_{2},\\dots ,c_{k}\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   (The set \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) has size \\(n-2\\ge k\\), so this