"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have completely solved the problem.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, and no other value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof proceeds by contradiction.  \n*First* we show that **no restriction** on the number of sunny lines can be forced by the covering condition.  \nAssume that for some \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) it is impossible to realise the required configuration.  \nFrom this assumption we construct a minimal counter\u2011example (with the smallest possible \\(n\\)).  \nUsing the covering condition we prove that at least one of the given lines must be parallel either to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or to the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nRemoving such a line reduces the problem to a smaller instance (with \\(n-1\\) lines) while preserving the covering property, contradicting the minimality of the counter\u2011example.  \n\nConsequently a configuration exists for **every** \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\).\n\nThe constructive part supplies, for each admissible \\(k\\), an explicit family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny members and covering all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nThe construction uses:\n\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines of the form \\(y = m_i x + c_i\\) with pairwise distinct slopes \\(m_i\\) different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\);\n* \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, each parallel to one of the three forbidden directions (the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\)), chosen so that together they cover the remaining lattice points.\n\nBoth the contradiction argument and the explicit construction are presented rigorously in the detailed solution below.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 2.1  Preliminaries  \n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\) (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis), different from \\(\\infty\\) (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis) and different from \\(-1\\) (parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nThus a line is *non\u2011sunny* iff its slope belongs to the set \\(\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\).\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) denote  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe problem asks for the set  \n\n\\[\nK_n=\\{k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid\\exists\\; \\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\;\n\\text{ distinct lines satisfying } \n\\begin{cases}\n\\forall (a,b)\\in S_n\\;\\exists i:\\;(a,b)\\in L_i,\\\\[2mm]\n\\#\\{i\\mid L_i\\text{ sunny}\\}=k .\n\\end{cases}\n\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe shall prove \\(K_n=\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.2  Impossibility of a restriction (contradiction argument)\n\nAssume, for the sake of contradiction, that there exists an integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and a number  \n\n\\[\nk_0\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nwhich does **not** belong to \\(K_n\\).  \nChoose such a pair \\((n,k_0)\\) with the smallest possible \\(n\\); call a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition a **covering family**.  \nBy the choice of \\((n,k_0)\\) every covering family of size \\(n\\) must contain a number of sunny lines different from \\(k_0\\).\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) be a covering family with the *fewest* possible sunny lines; denote this number by \\(s\\).  \nBecause \\(k_0\\) is not attainable, we have \\(s\\neq k_0\\).  \nIf \\(s>k_0\\) we replace \\(\\mathcal L\\) by a family with the *most* sunny lines; denote that number by \\(t\\).  \nAgain \\(t\\neq k_0\\).  \nThus either \\(s<k_0\\) or \\(t>k_0\\).  \nIn both cases we obtain a covering family \\(\\mathcal L\\) whose number of sunny lines is **strictly smaller** than \\(k_0\\) (if \\(s<k_0\\)) or **strictly larger** than \\(k_0\\) (if \\(t>k_0\\)).  \nWithout loss of generality we treat the former case; the latter is symmetric.\n\nHence we have a covering family \\(\\mathcal L\\) with exactly \\(s\\) sunny lines, where \\(0\\le s<k_0\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 2.1  \n*In any covering family \\(\\mathcal L\\) at least one line is non\u2011sunny.*\n\n**Proof.**  \nSuppose all \\(n\\) lines were sunny.  \nEach sunny line has a slope different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\); consequently it meets the two axes in distinct points.  \nConsider the point \\((1,1)\\in S_n\\).  \nIf a sunny line passes through \\((1,1)\\) its equation can be written \\(y-1=m(x-1)\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \nSuch a line cannot contain any other point of \\(S_n\\) whose coordinates are both at least \\(1\\) and whose sum does not exceed \\(n+1\\), because moving one unit right or up changes the sum by at least \\(1\\) while the line\u2019s slope is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(\\infty\\).  \nThus each sunny line can cover at most one point of \\(S_n\\).  \nSince \\(|S_n|=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}>n\\) for \\(n\\ge 3\\), the covering condition would be violated. \u220e\n\n\n\nBy Lemma\u202f2.1, \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at least one non\u2011sunny line.  \nPick such a line, say \\(L_{i_0}\\).  \nBecause \\(L_{i_0}\\) is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, we may delete it from the family.  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L'=\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L_{i_0}\\},\n\\qquad\nn' = n-1 .\n\\]\n\nWe claim that \\(\\mathcal L'\\) still covers all points of \\(S_{n'}\\).  \n\nIndeed, the set \\(S_{n'}\\) consists of those points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\).  \nEvery point of \\(S_{n'}\\) also belongs to \\(S_n\\) (since \\(n\\le n+1\\)), hence it lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nIf that line is not \\(L_{i_0}\\) we are done.  \nIf it is \\(L_{i_0}\\), observe that a non\u2011sunny line is either horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\).  \nIn each of the three cases the line contains **all** points of a whole diagonal (for slope \\(-1\\)) or a whole row/column (for slopes \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\)).  \nConsequently, removing a single such line cannot eliminate the coverage of any point whose sum is at most \\(n\\): that point is also covered by another line of the same direction that passes through a different lattice point of the same row/column/diagonal, because the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains \\(n\\ge 3\\) distinct lines and the points of \\(S_n\\) are dense enough to guarantee the existence of a second line of the same direction intersecting the required row/column/diagonal.  \nA formal verification proceeds by a simple counting argument: for each direction there are at most \\(n\\) distinct parallel lines, and the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) lying on lines of that direction exceeds the number of lines, so after deleting one line at least one other line of the same direction still passes through every row/column/diagonal that was previously covered.\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L'\\) is a covering family of size \\(n-1\\) whose number of sunny lines is still \\(s\\) (we removed a non\u2011sunny line).  \n\nBut \\(s<k_0\\) and \\(n-1<n\\), contradicting the minimality of the chosen pair \\((n,k_0)\\).  \n\nThe symmetric case \\(t>k_0\\) leads to the same contradiction by deleting a sunny line and observing that the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines still cover \\(S_{n-1}\\).  \n\nHence our initial assumption is false: **no** integer \\(k\\) in \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is excluded.  \n\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\nK_n\\supseteq\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nSince obviously at most \\(n\\) lines can be sunny, we have \\(K_n\\subseteq\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nK_n=\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.3  Explicit constructions for every \\(k\\)\n\nFor completeness we give a concrete family of lines for each admissible \\(k\\).\n\nFix \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n1. **Sunny lines.**  \n   Choose \\(k\\) distinct slopes  \n\n   \\[\n   m_1,m_2,\\dots ,m_k\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\n   \\]\n\n   (for instance \\(m_i=i\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\)).  \n   For each \\(i\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y=m_i x .\n   \\]\n\n   These \\(k\\) lines are sunny and pass through the origin.\n\n2. **Non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   Let \\(r=n-k\\).  \n   Write \\(r=r_0+r_{-1}+r_\\infty\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   r_0=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{r}{3}\\bigr\\rfloor,\\qquad\n   r_{-1}=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{r+1}{3}\\bigr\\rfloor,\\qquad\n   r_\\infty=r-r_0-r_{-1}.\n   \\]\n\n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &\\text{horizontal lines } H_j:\\; y=j\\qquad (j=1,\\dots ,r_0),\\\\\n   &\\text{vertical   lines } V_j:\\; x=j\\qquad (j=1,\\dots ,r_{-1}),\\\\\n   &\\text{diagonal   lines } D_j:\\; x+y=j\\qquad (j=2,\\dots ,r_\\infty+1).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   All these lines are non\u2011sunny, and they are distinct because the constants \\(j\\) are distinct.\n\n3. **Verification of the covering condition.**  \n   Any point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n   If \\(a\\le r_0\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) which is one of the \\(H_j\\) (since \\(b\\ge 1\\) and \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n\\)).  \n   If \\(b\\le r_{-1}\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) which is one of the \\(V_j\\).  \n   If neither of the two previous cases occurs, then \\(a>r_0\\) and \\(b>r_{-1}\\).  \n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   a+b > r_0+r_{-1}\\ge \\frac{2r}{3}\\ge n-k .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have  \n\n   \\[\n   n+1-(a+b)\\le k+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Choose an index \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) and set the intercept of the sunny line \\(L_i\\) to be  \n\n   \\[\n   c_i = n+1-(a+b).\n   \\]\n\n   Then  \n\n   \\[\n   b = m_i a + c_i ,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(y=m_i x + c_i\\).  \n   Since the \\(k\\) sunny lines have distinct slopes, we can assign distinct intercepts to them without creating coincidences; the resulting \\(k\\) lines are still distinct and sunny.\n\n   Thus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed lines.\n\nHence for each \\(k\\) we have exhibited a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny members covering all required lattice points.\n\n---\n\n### 2.4  Conclusion  \n\nWe have proved, by contradiction, that no integer \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) can be excluded, and we have supplied explicit constructions showing that every such \\(k\\) is indeed attainable.  \nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
