"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not obtained a complete classification of the admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following necessary condition:\n\n\\[\nk\\;\\ge\\;\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all lattice points\n\\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) must contain at least\n\\(\\lceil (n-2)/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines.  \n\nThe proof rests entirely on the pigeon\u2011hole principle applied to the three\nfamilies of *non\u2011sunny* lines (horizontal, vertical and lines of slope \\(-1\\)).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Counting the required points.**  \n   The set  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n   contains \\(\\displaystyle |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points.\n\n2. **How many points can a non\u2011sunny line cover?**  \n   * A horizontal line \\(y=c\\) (with integer \\(c\\)) meets exactly the points of\n     \\(S_n\\) whose second coordinate equals \\(c\\); there are \\(n+1-c\\) such\n     points.  \n   * A vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets exactly the points of \\(S_n\\) whose first\n     coordinate equals \\(c\\); there are \\(n+1-c\\) such points.  \n   * A line of slope \\(-1\\), i.e. \\(x+y=c\\), meets exactly the points of\n     \\(S_n\\) with \\(a+b=c\\); there are \\(c-1\\) such points (provided\n     \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)).\n\n   Consequently each non\u2011sunny line covers at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n3. **Pigeon\u2011hole argument.**  \n   Suppose that among the \\(n\\) given lines exactly \\(k\\) are sunny, so that\n   \\(n-k\\) lines are non\u2011sunny.  Since the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines together can\n   cover at most \\((n-k)\\,n\\) points of \\(S_n\\), the remaining points must be\n   covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Because a sunny line is not parallel to\n   any of the three prohibited directions, it can intersect each of the\n   three families of non\u2011sunny lines in at most one point; hence a sunny line\n   can cover **at most three** points of \\(S_n\\) that are not already covered\n   by the non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n   Therefore the total number of points that can be covered by the whole\n   family of \\(n\\) lines satisfies\n\n   \\[\n   |S_n|\\;\\le\\;(n-k)\\,n\\;+\\;3k .\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) and rearranging gives\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\;\\le\\;n^{2}-nk+3k\n   \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n   k\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n-2}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(k\\) is an integer, we obtain the required lower bound\n   \\[\n   k\\;\\ge\\;\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must contain at least\n\\(\\lceil (n-2)/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) we are asked to determine all non\u2011negative\nintegers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines in the plane such that  \n\n* every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis,\n  the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1 Counting the points of \\(S_n\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the equation \\(a+b=s\\) has\n\\(s-1\\) positive integer solutions \\((a,b)\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=1+2+\\dots +n=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2 How many points can a non\u2011sunny line cover?\n\nA *non\u2011sunny* line is of one of the three types:\n\n* **Horizontal**: \\(y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\ge 1\\).  \n  The points of \\(S_n\\) on this line are \\((a,c)\\) with\n  \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); there are exactly \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) of them.\n\n* **Vertical**: \\(x=c\\) with integer \\(c\\ge 1\\).  \n  Symmetrically, the points on this line are \\((c,b)\\) with\n  \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); again at most \\(n\\) points.\n\n* **Slope \\(-1\\)**: \\(x+y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\) and \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).  \n  The points of \\(S_n\\) on this line are precisely the solutions of\n  \\(a+b=c\\); there are \\(c-1\\le n\\) of them.\n\nConsequently **any** non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3 How many points can a sunny line add?\n\nLet a sunny line \\(L\\) be given.  Because \\(L\\) is not parallel to any of the\nthree prohibited directions, it meets each horizontal line, each vertical\nline, and each line of slope \\(-1\\) in at most one point.  In particular,\n\\(L\\) can intersect at most one horizontal line of the form \\(y=c\\) that\ncontains points of \\(S_n\\), at most one vertical line \\(x=c\\), and at most one\nline \\(x+y=c\\).  Hence **at most three** points of \\(S_n\\) can lie on \\(L\\) and\nsimultaneously be uncovered by the collection of non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4 Pigeon\u2011hole inequality\n\nAssume that among the \\(n\\) lines exactly \\(k\\) are sunny and \\(n-k\\) are\nnon\u2011sunny.  The non\u2011sunny lines together can cover at most \\((n-k)\\,n\\) points\nof \\(S_n\\) (by \u00a72.2).  The remaining points, if any, must be covered by the\n\\(k\\) sunny lines, each of which contributes at most three new points\n(\u00a72.3).  Therefore\n\n\\[\n|S_n|\\;\\le\\;(n-k)\\,n\\;+\\;3k .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nInsert the value of \\(|S_n|\\) from (1) into (2):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\;\\le\\;n^{2}-nk+3k .\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives\n\n\\[\nnk-3k\\;\\le\\;n^{2}-\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nk\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n-2}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is an integer, we obtain the necessary condition\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle k\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil } .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5 Conclusion\n\nEquation (3) shows that any admissible configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines\nmust contain at least \\(\\lceil (n-2)/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines.  Hence the set of\npossible values of \\(k\\) is contained in  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\{\\;\\lceil (n-2)/2\\rceil,\\;\\lceil (n-2)/2\\rceil+1,\\dots ,\\,n\\;\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe converse direction (that every integer \\(k\\) in this interval can indeed\nbe realised) remains open; the present argument does not settle it.  Thus the\nresult above is a **necessary** condition for the existence of the required\nfamily of lines.  No further values of \\(k\\) can occur.  \u220e"
