n\\)) is trivial: any extra sunny line can\n   be taken arbitrarily, because the covering condition is already satisfied.\n\n   The construction proceeds as follows.\n\n   *Let*  \n\n   \\[\n   m=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   - **Horizontal and vertical lines.**  \n     Take the \\(m\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=m\\) and the \\(m\\) vertical\n     lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\).  These \\(2m\\) lines are non\u2011sunny and together\n     cover every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(\\min\\{a,b\\}\\le m\\).\n\n   - **Remaining points.**  \n     The points of \\(S_n\\) not yet covered are exactly those with\n     \\(a,b>m\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  Their number is  \n\n     \\[\n     r=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(2m)\\,n\n       =\\begin{cases}\n          0, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\n          3, & n\\text{ odd}.\n        \\end{cases}\n     \\]\n\n     *If \\(n\\) is even* (\\(r=0\\)) the \\(2m=n\\) non\u2011sunny lines already cover\n     all of \\(S_n\\); we may add any \\(k_0\\) sunny lines (for instance three\n     lines of slopes \\(2,3,5\\)) to reach the required total of \\(n\\) lines.\n     Since \\(k_0=\\frac{n-2}{2}=m-1\\), we simply replace one of the non\u2011sunny\n     lines by a sunny line, obtaining exactly \\(k_0\\) sunny lines.\n\n     *If \\(n\\) is odd* (\\(r=3\\)) the uncovered points are\n     \\((m+1,m+1),\\;(m+1,m+2),\\;(m+2,m+1)\\).  These three points are not collinear,\n     so they can be covered by three distinct sunny lines (any three lines,\n     each passing through one of the points, and none of them parallel to the\n     prohibited directions).  Together with the \\(2m=n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines we\n     obtain a family of \\(n\\) lines containing exactly  \n\n     \\[\n     k_0=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil=\\frac{n-1}{2}\n     \\]\n\n     sunny lines.\n\n   Thus for both parities we have built a configuration with the minimal\n   admissible number of sunny lines \\(k_0\\).  Adding any further sunny lines\n   (up to \\(n\\)) preserves the covering property, so every integer\n   \\(k\\) with \\(k_0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable.\n\nCombining the necessary bound (\u2605) with the explicit construction shows that the\nset of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\Bigl\\{\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil,\\;\n               \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+1,\\dots ,n\\Bigr\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1 Number of points to be covered\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the equation \\(a+b=s\\) has\n\\(s-1\\) positive integer solutions.  Hence  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=1+2+\\dots +n=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2 Non\u2011sunny lines\n\nA line is *non\u2011sunny* iff it is parallel to one of the three directions\n\\(x\\)-axis, \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n*Horizontal line* \\(y=c\\) (\\(c\\ge 1\\)).  \nPoints of \\(S_n\\) on it are \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); there are\n\\(n+1-c\\le n\\) of them.\n\n*Vertical line* \\(x=c\\) (\\(c\\ge 1\\)).  \nSymmetrically it contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Diagonal line* \\(x+y=c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)).  \nIts points in \\(S_n\\) are the solutions of \\(a+b=c\\); there are \\(c-1\\le n\\).\n\nThus **any** non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\). \\tag{2}\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3 Sunny lines\n\nA *sunny* line is not parallel to any of the three prohibited directions.\nConsequently it meets each horizontal line, each vertical line and each line\n\\(x+y=c\\) in at most one point.  Therefore a sunny 