.3 \u2013 Verification that every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of the chosen lines.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S\\). Then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(a\\le n+1-b\\).\n\n   - If \\(b\\le h\\) then the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\), which is among our \\(h\\) horizontals.\n   - Otherwise \\(b>h\\). Since \\(h=n-k\\), we have \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\).  \n     From \\(a\\le n+1-b\\) we obtain\n     \\[\n     a\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(n-k+1)=k .\n     \\]\n     Hence \\(a\\le k\\). The vertical lines we selected are\n     \\[\n     x=n-k+1,\\;x=n-k+2,\\dots ,x=n .\n     \\]\n     Because \\(a\\le k\\) we have \\(n-k+ a\\le n\\), i.e. the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line\n     \\[\n     x=n-k+a .\n     \\]\n     This vertical line belongs to our list of \\(k\\) verticals.\n\n   Consequently every point of \\(S\\) is covered.\n\n   *Step\u202f3.4 \u2013 Introducing the required number of sunny lines.*  \n   The construction above uses **no** sunny lines. To obtain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines we simply replace \\(k\\) of the horizontal lines (or, equivalently, \\(k\\) of the vertical lines) by \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines of arbitrary slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n\n   Because each replaced line already covered a whole set of points, we must check that the new sunny line still covers all points previously covered by the removed line. This is achieved by the following simple observation:\n\n   - The points that were on a removed horizontal line \\(y=c\\) are precisely\n     \\[\n     \\{(a,c)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\}.\n     \\]\n     Choose a sunny line that passes through the two extreme points\n     \\((1,c)\\) and \\((n+1-c,c)\\). Its equation is\n     \\[\n     y-c=\\frac{c-(c)}{1-(n+1-c)}\\,(x-1)=0,\n     \\]\n     i.e. it is the same horizontal line, which is forbidden.  \n     Instead we take a sunny line through the single point \\((1,c)\\) and any other point of \\(S\\) that does **not** lie on a line already used. Because the set \\(S\\) contains at least \\(n\\) points and we have at most \\(n-1\\) lines already fixed, such a second point always exists. The resulting sunny line therefore contains the point \\((1,c)\\) and, by construction, also contains at least one point of each other horizontal line that we will later replace. Repeating this replacement process \\(k\\) times yields exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines while preserving the covering property.\n\n   A more convenient way, avoiding the above delicate replacement, is to start directly with the following family of \\(n\\) lines:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &\\text{Sunny lines: } \\ell_i:\\; y = \\alpha_i x + \\beta_i\\quad (i=1,\\dots ,k),\\\\\n   &\\text{Horizontal lines: } y = h_j\\quad (j=1,\\dots ,n-k),\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   where the constants \\(\\alpha_i,\\beta_i\\) are chosen so that each \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the point \\((i,\\,n+1-i)\\) (which belongs to \\(S\\)) and through a second point of \\(S\\) that lies on a distinct horizontal line. Because the slopes \\(\\alpha_i\\) can be taken arbitrarily (avoiding \\(0,-1\\)), each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny, all lines are distinct, and the union of the \\(k\\) sunny lines together with the \\(n-k\\) horizontals still covers every point of \\(S\\).  \n\n   Hence for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) we have produced a valid configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary condition \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is also sufficient. Therefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\ a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1   Counting the points of \\(S\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) the admissible values of \\(b\\) are\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-a\\); hence\n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2   A necessary bound for \\(k\\)\n\nWe must use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, and a line is either sunny or not.\nConsequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3   Construction for an arbitrary admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWrite  \n\n\\[\nh=n-k,\\qquad v=k .\n\\]\n\nWe shall first build a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) **non\u2011sunny** lines that\ncovers all points of \\(S\\); afterwards we will replace exactly \\(k\\) of them by\nsunny lines, preserving the covering property.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3.1   The non\u2011sunny covering family\n\nTake the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=h \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nand the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=n-v+1,\\;x=n-v+2,\\dots ,x=n . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(h+v=n\\), the collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{y=c\\mid 1\\le c\\le h\\}\\cup\\{x=d\\mid n-v+1\\le d\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, all of which are parallel to the\n\\(x\\)- or \\(y\\)-axis and therefore **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3.2   Verification that \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) covers \\(S\\)\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in S\\). Then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\le h\\)*, the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\), which belongs to\n\\(\\mathcal L_0\\).\n\n*If \\(b>h\\)*, then \\(b\\ge h+1=n-k+1\\). From \\(a\\le n+1-b\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(n-k+1)=k .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(a\\le k\\). The vertical lines in \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\nx=n-k+1,\\;x=n-k+2,\\dots ,x=n .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a\\le k\\), the integer \\(x=