            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 2.1   The set of points that must be covered  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) the admissible values of \\(b\\) are\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-a\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.2   A necessary condition for \\(k\\)\n\nWe are required to use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines; a line is either sunny or\nnot sunny.  Hence the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.3   Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).  Put  \n\n\\[\nh=n-k\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad v=k .\n\\]\n\nWe first exhibit a covering family that uses only horizontal and vertical\nlines, and then replace the \\(v\\) vertical lines by \\(v\\) sunny lines while\npreserving the covering property.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.3.1   The non\u2011sunny covering family  \n\nTake the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=h \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nand the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=n-k+1,\\;x=n-k+2,\\dots ,x=n . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAltogether we have \\(h+v=n\\) distinct lines; none of them is sunny because they\nare parallel to the \\(x\\)- or \\(y\\)-axis.\n\n**Claim.** Every point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) lies on at least one of the lines\n(3)\u2013(4).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(b\\le h\\) then \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).  \nOtherwise \\(b>h=n-k\\).  From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(n-k+1)=k .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(a\\le k\\) and the vertical line \\(x=n-k+a\\) (which is among the lines\n(4)) contains \\((a,b)\\). \u220e\n\nThus the family \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) consisting of (3) and (4) covers all points\nof \\(S\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.3.2   Replacing the vertical lines by sunny lines  \n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define two points of \\(S\\):\n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;n+1-i),\\qquad Q_i=(i+1,\\;1).\n\\]\n\nBoth belong to \\(S\\) because  \n\n\\[\ni+(n+1-i)=n+1\\le n+1,\\qquad (i+1)+1\\le n+1\\;(i\\le k\\le n-1\\text{ or }i=n).\n\\]\n\nLet \\(\\ell_i\\) be the line through \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\).  Its slope is  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{1-(n+1-i)}{(i+1)-i}=i-n .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(i-n\\neq0\\), \\(i-n\\neq-1\\) and \\(i-n\\neq\\infty\\) for all\n\\(1\\le i\\le k\\le n\\), each \\(\\ell_i\\) is **sunny**.  The slopes \\(m_i\\) are\npairwise distinct, so the lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are distinct.\n\nWe now replace the vertical line \\(x=n-k+i\\) (the \\(i\\)-th line of (4)) by the\nsunny line \\(\\ell_i\\).  It remains to verify that every point that was on the\nremoved vertical line is still covered.\n\nA point on the removed line has the form \\((a,b)\\) with  \n\n\\[\na=n-k+i,\\qquad 1\\le b\\le n+1-a=n+1-(n-k+i)=k+1-i . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(b=k+1-i-t\\) with \\(t\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-i\\}\\).  Then  \n\n\\[\n(a,b)=\\bigl(i+(n-k),\\;k+1-i-t\\bigr)\n      =\\bigl(i+t',\\;k+1-i-t'\\bigr) \\quad\\text{with }t'=n-k-t .\n\\]\n\nBecause the line \\(\\ell_i\\) contains all points \\((i+s,\\;k+1-i-s)\\) for\n\\(s=0,1,\\dots ,k-i\\), the point \\((a,b)\\) (which corresponds to \\(s=t\\)) lies\non \\(\\ell_i\\).  Hence the replacement does not lose any covered point.\n\nAfter performing the replacement for every \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) we obtain the\nfamily  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}\\cup\\{y=1,\\dots ,y=h\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny,\nand, by the argument above, still covers every point of \\(S\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.4   Conclusion\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* The necessary condition \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) follows from the fact that we must use\n  exactly \\(n\\) lines.\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have constructed \\(n\\) distinct\n  lines satisfying the covering requirement and containing precisely \\(k\\)\n  sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
