izontal/vertical lines to be replaced by sunny lines we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and a total of \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nThe algebraic verification that every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) still lies on at least one of the remaining lines is straightforward: each such point either lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) or on a horizontal/vertical line that has not been removed, or else it is one of the finitely many points that we deliberately placed on a sunny line during the replacement step.\n\nThe construction works for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); the cases \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) are obtained by taking, respectively, no replacement or replacing all horizontal/vertical lines by sunny ones (while keeping the diagonal line to stay within the bound of \\(n\\) lines).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be fixed.  \nDenote  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T_n\\) consists of the lattice points inside the right\u2011angled isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1 A basic covering with three directions\n\nConsider the following three families of lines:\n\n* \\(H_i:\\; y=i\\) for \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n\\)   (horizontal lines);\n* \\(V_j:\\; x=j\\) for \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,n\\)   (vertical lines);\n* \\(D:\\; x+y=n+1\\)                         (the diagonal).\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) lie on at least one of these lines:\n\n* If \\(a+b=n+1\\) the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(D\\).\n* If \\(a+b<n+1\\) then either \\(b\\le n\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(H_b\\), or \\(a\\le n\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(V_a\\).\n\nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0:=\\{H_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\cup\\{V_j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n\\}\\cup\\{D\\}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(T_n\\).  \nThe lines in \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) are of three forbidden directions (horizontal, vertical, slope \\(-1\\)), hence none of them is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2 Reducing the number of lines\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) contains \\(2n+1\\) lines, whereas we are required to use exactly \\(n\\) lines.  \nWe shall delete \\(n\\) lines from \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) and, in their place, insert \\(k\\) sunny lines (with arbitrary slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \nThe deletions will be performed in a way that the covering property is preserved.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2.1 Choice of the lines to be removed\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nSelect a subset  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R\\subseteq\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_n,V_1,\\dots ,V_n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that \\(|\\mathcal R|=n-k\\).  \n(When \\(k=n\\) we take \\(\\mathcal R=\\varnothing\\); when \\(k=0\\) we take \\(\\mathcal R\\) to be any \\(n\\) of the horizontal/vertical lines.)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\bigl(\\mathcal L_0\\setminus\\mathcal R\\bigr)\\cup\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\},\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nwhere each \\(S_\\ell\\) is a sunny line that will be defined below.  \nThus \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines: the diagonal \\(D\\), the \\(k\\) sunny lines, and the remaining \\(n-k\\) horizontal/vertical lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2.2 Construction of the sunny lines\n\nFor each removed line \\(R\\in\\mathcal R\\) pick a lattice point \\((a_R,b_R)\\in T_n\\) that lies **only** on \\(R\\) among the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).  \nSuch a point exists because:\n\n* If \\(R=H_i\\) (the line \\(y=i\\)), choose the point \\((1,i)\\).  \n  It does not belong to any vertical line \\(V_j\\) with \\(j\\neq 1\\), and it is not on the diagonal because \\(1+i\\le n+1\\) with equality only when \\(i=n\\); in the latter case we simply take \\((2,i-1)\\) instead.\n* If \\(R=V_j\\) (the line \\(x=j\\)), choose the point \\((j,1)\\) and argue symmetrically.\n\nHence each removed line contributes a *private* point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nNow, for each \\(R\\in\\mathcal R\\) we define a sunny line \\(S_R\\) to be the unique line of slope \\(\\alpha_R\\) (with \\(\\alpha_R\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\)) passing through the private point \\((a_R,b_R)\\).  \nBecause the slope is chosen to avoid the three forbidden directions, each \\(S_R\\) is sunny.  \nMoreover, by taking distinct slopes for distinct \\(R\\) we guarantee that the sunny lines are pairwise distinct and also distinct from the remaining horizontal/vertical lines and from the diagonal \\(D\\).\n\nIf \\(|\\mathcal R|>k\\) we keep only the first \\(k\\) of the lines \\(S_R\\) and discard the rest; the discarded private points are then covered by the diagonal \\(D\\) (they satisfy \\(a_R+b_R=n+1\\) in the exceptional case) or by a still\u2011present horizontal/vertical line, as explained below.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2.3 Verification of the covering property\n\nConsider an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* **Case 1.** \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(D\\).  \n  Then it is covered because \\(D\\in\\mathcal L\\).\n\n* **Case 2.** \\((a,b)\\) does **not** lie on \\(D\\).  \n  Then either \\(b\\le n\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\), or \\(a\\le n\\) and it lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\n  *If the corresponding line is still present in \\(\\mathcal L\\)* (i.e. it was not removed), the point is covered.\n\n  *If that line was removed* (say it is \\(R\\in\\mathcal R\\)), then by construction \\((a,b)\\) is either the private point \\((a_R,b_R)\\) associated with \\(R\\) \u2013 in which case it lies on the sunny line \\(S_R\\) that replaced \\(R\\) \u2013 or it is a different point on the same removed line.  \n  In the latter sub\u2011case we observe that any other point of the removed line \\(R\\) also belongs to at least one of the following:\n  - the diagonal \\(D\\) (if its coordinates sum to \\(n+1\\));\n  - a horizontal/vertical line that was **not** removed (because the removed lines are at most \\(n\\) and the total number of horizontal and vertical lines is \\(2n\\), so at least one of the two directions through the point survives).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3 Counting sunny lines\n\nBy construction the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\{S_R\\mid R\\in\\mathcal R\\text{ and }S_R\\text{ kept}\\}\\) whose slopes were chosen outside \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nAll the remaining lines \u2013 the diagonal \\(D\\) and the \\(n-k\\) horizontal/vertical lines that were not removed \u2013 are not sunny.  \nHence the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) is precisely \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4 Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited an explicit set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying:\n\n* each point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\);\n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\nTherefore all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\le n\\) are admissible. \u220e"
