thcal R\\) we select a *private* point of \\(T_n\\) that lies on \\(R\\) but on no other line of the original covering.  \n   - If \\(R=H_i\\) we take \\((1,i)\\) (unless \\(i=n\\), in which case we take \\((2,n-1)\\)).  \n   - If \\(R=V_j\\) we take \\((j,1)\\) (unless \\(j=n\\), in which case we take \\((n-1,2)\\)).  \n   In every case the chosen point satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and is not on the diagonal \\(D\\); it is also not on any other horizontal or vertical line because its other coordinate equals \\(1\\) (or \\(2\\) in the exceptional case).\n\n5. **Sunny replacements.**  \n   For each discarded line \\(R\\) we draw a line \\(S_R\\) through its private point with a slope \\(\\alpha_R\\) chosen from \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \n   By taking distinct slopes for distinct \\(R\\) we guarantee that the lines \\(S_R\\) are pairwise distinct and also distinct from every remaining horizontal, vertical or diagonal line.  Consequently each \\(S_R\\) is sunny.\n\n6. **The final family.**  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\n   \\bigl\\{D\\bigr\\}\\cup\n   \\bigl\\{ \\text{horizontal/vertical lines not in }\\mathcal R \\bigr\\}\\cup\n   \\bigl\\{S_R\\mid R\\in\\mathcal R\\bigr\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The first two groups contain exactly \\(k\\) non\u2011sunny lines (the diagonal plus the \\(k-1\\) horizontal/vertical lines that survive), and the third group contains exactly \\(n-k\\) sunny lines.  Hence \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines with precisely \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\n7. **Verification of the covering property.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \n   - If \\(a+b=n+1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in D\\subset\\mathcal L_k\\).  \n   - If \\(a+b<n+1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) and on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).  \n     *If at least one of these two lines survived (i.e. is not in \\(\\mathcal R\\)), the point is covered.*  \n     *If both were discarded, then \\((a,b)\\) must be the private point of one o