o any selection of \\(n\\) horizontal/vertical lines.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2.2 Private points for the removed lines\n\nFor each \\(R\\in\\mathcal R\\) we select a point \\(P_R=(a_R,b_R)\\in T_n\\) satisfying:\n\n* \\(P_R\\) lies on \\(R\\);\n* \\(P_R\\) does **not** lie on any other line of the family (2).\n\nSuch a point exists:\n\n* If \\(R=H_i\\) with \\(i<n\\) we take \\(P_R=(1,i)\\).  \n  It is on \\(H_i\\); its \\(x\\)-coordinate equals \\(1\\), so it is not on any vertical line \\(V_j\\) with \\(j\\neq1\\); moreover \\(1+i\\le n\\) so \\(P_R\\notin D\\).\n\n* If \\(R=H_n\\) we take \\(P_R=(2,n-1)\\).  \n  Then \\(2+(n-1)=n+1\\) would place it on \\(D\\); to avoid this we instead take \\(P_R=(2,n-2)\\), which satisfies \\(2+(n-2)=n\\le n+1\\) and is not on any other horizontal or vertical line.\n\n* The cases \\(R=V_j\\) are symmetric: for \\(j<n\\) we use \\(P_R=(j,1)\\); for \\(j=n\\) we use \\(P_R=(n-2,2)\\).\n\nIn every case \\(P_R\\in T_n\\) and \\(P_R\\) is private to \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2.3 Sunny replacements\n\nFor each \\(R\\in\\mathcal R\\) choose a real number \\(\\alpha_R\\) with  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_R\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nand let \\(S_R\\) be the unique line of slope \\(\\alpha_R\\) passing through the private point \\(P_R\\).  \nBecause of (4) each \\(S_R\\) is sunny.  \nBy taking the \\(\\alpha_R\\)\u2019s pairwise distinct we ensure that the lines \\(\\{S_R\\mid R\\in\\mathcal R\\}\\) are pairwise distinct and also distinct from every line that remains from the original family (the diagonal \\(D\\) and the horizontal/vertical lines not in \\(\\mathcal R\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2.4 Definition of the final family\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\n\\bigl\\{D\\bigr\\}\\cup\n\\bigl\\{H_i,V_j\\mid H_i\\notin\\mathcal R,\\;V_j\\notin\\mathcal R\\