\) be fixed and define  \n> \\[\n> T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n> \\tag{1}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:** Correct definition of the set of lattice points that must be covered. No issue.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Introduction of the three families of lines\n\n> \u201cConsider the families  \n> \\[\n> H_i:\\;y=i\\qquad (1\\le i\\le n),\\qquad \n> V_j:\\;x=j\\qquad (1\\le j\\le n),\\qquad \n> D:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n> \\tag{2}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:** Correct description of horizontal, vertical, and diagonal lines. No issue.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Claim that the three families cover all points of \\(T_n\\)\n\n> \u201c*Claim.* Every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of (2).  \n> *Proof.* Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \n> If \\(a+b=n+1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in D\\).  \n> If \\(a+b<n+1\\) then either \\(b\\le n\\) and \\((a,b)\\in H_b\\), or \\(a\\le n\\) and \\((a,b)\\in V_a\\). \u220e\u201d\n\n**Analysis:** The argument is valid. For any \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\) we have \\(a\\le n\\) and \\(b\\le n\\), so the point lies on both a horizontal and a vertical line. The claim holds. No issue.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Observation that all lines in (2) are non\u2011sunny\n\n> \u201cAll lines in (2) are non\u2011sunny because their slopes are respectively \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:** Correct. No issue.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Goal: produce a family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of exactly \\(n\\) lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\n> \u201cFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n> We shall produce a set \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\u201d\n\n**Analysis:** This is the intended construction. The rest of the proof must deliver such a family. The statement itself is fine; the difficulty lies in the construction that follows.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Choice of the lines to be removed\n\n> \u201cAmong the \\(2n\\) horizontal/vertical lines in (2) choose a subset  \n> \\[\n> \\mathcal R\\subseteq\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_n,V_1,\\dots ,V_n\\}\n> \\]  \n> with  \n> \\[\n> |\\mathcal R|=n-k .\n> \\tag{3}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:** Selecting \\(n-k\\) lines to be replaced is a reasonable idea. No logical error yet. However, the subsequent steps must guarantee that the replacement works for *any* such \\(\\mathcal R\\). This will turn out to be false.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Claim of existence of \u201cprivate points\u201d\n\n> \u201cFor each \\(R\\in\\mathcal R\\) we select a point \\(P_R=(a_R,b_R)\\in T_n\\) satisfying:\n> * \\(P_R\\) lies on \\(R\\);\n> * \\(P_R\\) does **not** lie on any other line of the family (2).  \n> Such a point exists:\n> * If \\(R=H_i\\) with \\(i<n\\) we take \\(P_R=(1,i)\\).  \n>   It is on \\(H_i\\); its \\(x\\)-coordinate equals \\(1\\), so it is not on any vertical line \\(V_j\\) with \\(j\\neq1\\); moreover \\(1+i\\le n\\) so \\(P_R\\notin D\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis (Critical Error):**  \nThe point \\((1,i)\\) lies on **both** \\(H_i\\) and the vertical line \\(V_1\\). The condition \u201cdoes not lie on any other line of the family (2)\u201d is violated unless \\(V_1\\) is also removed (i.e., \\(V_1\\in\\mathcal R\\)). The construction does **not** guarantee that \\(V_1\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal R\\). Since \\(\\mathcal R\\) can be any subset of size \\(n-k\\), it may happen that \\(V_1\\) is *not* removed, making \\((1,i)\\) non\u2011private. Therefore the claim that a private point exists for an arbitrary \\(R\\) is false. The same problem appears for the symmetric case of vertical lines.\n\n> \u201c* If \\(R=H_n\\) we take \\(P_R=(2,n-1)\\).  \n>   Then \\(2+(n-1)=n+1\\) would place it on \\(D\\); to avoid this we instead take \\(P_R=(2,n-2)\\), which satisfies \\(2+(n-2)=n\\le n+1\\) and is not on any other horizontal or vertical line.\n\n> * The cases \\(R=V_j\\) are symmetric: for \\(j<n\\) we use \\(P_R=(j,1)\\); for \\(j=n\\) we use \\(P_R=(n-2,2)\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis (Justification Gap / Critical Error):**  \nEven the \u201csymmetric\u201d choices suffer the same issue: \\((j,1)\\) lies on both \\(V_j\\) and the horizontal line \\(H_1\\). Unless \\(H_1\\) is also removed, the point is not private. The solution does not address this dependency. Hence the existence of a private point for an arbitrary \\(\\mathcal R\\) is not established. This is a **justification gap** that actually undermines the whole construction, making it a **critical error**.\n\n---\n\n### 8. Sunny replacements\n\n> \u201cFor each \\(R\\in\\mathcal R\\) choose a real number \\(\\alpha_R\\) with  \n> \\[\n> \\alpha_R\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\n> \\tag{4}\n> \\]  \n> and let \\(S_R\\) be the unique line of slope \\(\\alpha_R\\) passing through the private point \\(P_R\\).  \n> Because of (4) each \\(S_R\\) is sunny.  \n> By taking the \\(\\alpha_R\\)\u2019s pairwise distinct we ensure that the lines \\(\\{S_R\\mid R\\in\\mathcal R\\}\\) are pairwise distinct and also distinct from every line that remains from the original family (the diagonal \\(D\\) and the horizontal/vertical lines not in \\(\\mathcal R\\)).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:** Assuming the private points exist, this step is correct: a line of non\u2011forbidden slope through a given point is sunny, and distinct slopes guarantee distinct lines. No issue here, but it depends on the flawed previous step.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Definition of the final family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\)\n\n> \u201cSet  \n> \\[\n> \\mathcal L_k=\n> \\bigl\\{D\\bigr\\}\\cup\n> \\bigl\\{H_i,V_j\\mid H_i\\notin\\mathcal R,\\;V_j\\notin\\mathcal R\\bigr\\}\\cup\n> \\bigl\\{S_R\\mid R\\in\\mathcal R\\bigr\\}.\n> \\tag{5}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:** This definition follows the intended plan: keep the diagonal, keep all horizontal/vertical lines not removed, and replace each removed line by its sunny counterpart. The definition itself is syntactically correct.\n\n---\n\n### 10. Counting the number of lines (first attempt)\n\n> \u201cCounting the lines:\n> * The diagonal contributes \\(1\\) line (non\u2011sunny).\n> * From the \\(2n\\) horizontal/vertical lines we removed exactly \\(|\\mathcal R|=n-k\\); therefore \\(2n-(n-k)=n+k\\) of them remain.  \n>   However, among those \\(n+k\\) lines the diagonal already accounts for one, so the number of *additional* non\u2011sunny lines is \\((n+k)-1=n+k-1\\).  \n>   Since we need a total of \\(n\\) lines, we keep only the first \\(k\\) of these non\u2011sunny lines together with the diagonal; the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are precisely the sunny lines \\(S_R\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis (Critical Error):**  \nThe arithmetic is incorrect. After removal we have \\(2n-(n-k)=n+k\\) horizontal/vertical lines left. Adding the diagonal gives \\(n+k+1\\) non\u2011sunny lines. Adding the \\(n-k\\) sunny lines yields a total of \\((n+k+1)+(n-k)=2n+1\\) lines, **not** \\(n\\). The statement \u201cwe keep only the first \\(k\\) of these non\u2011sunny lines together with the diagonal\u201d is not justified: the construction never specifies *which* non\u2011sunny lines are discarded, nor does it prove that discarding them preserves coverage of all points. Consequently the claim that the final family contains exactly \\(n\\) lines is false. This is a **critical error** that invalidates the entire construction.\n\n---\n\n### 11. Alternative counting (second attempt)\n\n> \u201cA more transparent way is to observe directly from (5) that the total number of lines is  \n> \\[\n> 1\\;(\\text{the diagonal})\\;+\\;(2n-|\\mathcal R|)\\;-\\;(n-|\\mathcal R|)\\;+\\;|\\mathcal R|\n> = n .\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis (Critical Error):**  \nThe expression is algebraically incorrect. Expanding:\n\\[\n1 + (2n-|\\mathcal R|) - (n-|\\mathcal R|) + |\\mathcal R|\n= 1 + 2n - |\\mathcal R| - n + |\\mathcal R| + |\\mathcal R|\n= 1 + n + |\\mathcal R|\n= n + |\\mathcal R| + 1.\n\\]\nSince \\(|\\mathcal R| = n-k\\), this equals \\(2n - k + 1\\), not \\(n\\). The derivation is therefore erroneous, and the conclusion that the family has exactly \\(n\\) lines is unfounded. This is another **critical error**.\n\n---\n\n### 12. Claim about the number of sunny lines\n\n> \u201cThe sunny lines are exactly the \\(|\\mathcal R|=n-k\\) lines \\(S_R\\); consequently the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) is  \n> \\[\n> k = n-|\\mathcal R|.\n> \\tag{6}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis (Critical Error):**  \nThere is a logical inconsistency. The sunny lines are precisely the set \\(\\{S_R\\mid R\\in\\mathcal R\\}\\), whose cardinality is \\(|\\mathcal R| = n-k\\). Therefore the number of sunny lines is \\(n-k\\), not \\(k\\). The equation \\(k = n-|\\mathcal R|\\) is merely a restatement of the definition \\(|\\mathcal R| = n-k\\), but the preceding sentence incorrectly identifies the sunny lines as \\(|\\mathcal R|\\) of them while simultaneously claiming the number of sunny lines equals \\(k\\). This contradictory statement undermines the claim that the construction yields exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. **Critical error**.\n\n---\n\n### 13. Verification that every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered\n\n> \u201cLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n> *If \\(a+b=n+1\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\in D\\subset\\mathcal L_k\\).\n> *Assume \\(a+b<n+1\\).*  \n> Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) and on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n> *Case 1.* At least one of \\(H_b, V_a\\) is **not** in \\(\\mathcal R\\).  \n> That line belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) by construction, so \\((a,b)\\) is covered.\n> *Case 2.* Both \\(H_b\\) and \\(V_a\\) belong to \\(\\mathcal R\\).  \n> Because the private points were chosen with the second coordinate equal to \\(1\\) (or \\(2\\) in the exceptional case), the only point of \\(H_b\\) that is also on a vertical line of the form \\(V_j\\) with \\(j\\in\\mathcal R\\) is the private point of one of those two lines.  \n> Hence \\((a,b)\\) must be the private point of either \\(H_b\\) or \\(V_a\\).  \n> If it is the private point of \\(H_b\\) then, by definition, it lies on the sunny line \\(S_{H_b}\\in\\mathcal L_k\\); similarly for \\(V_a\\).  \n> Thus \\((a,b)\\) is again covered.\u201d\n\n**Analysis (Critical Error):**  \nThe argument in *Case\u202f2* is false. Suppose both \\(H_b\\) and \\(V_a\\) are removed (i.e., belong to \\(\\mathcal R\\)). The point \\((a,b)\\) is the intersection of those two removed lines. The construction of private points does **not** guarantee that \\((a,b)\\) coincides with a private point of either line. For example, if \\(\\mathcal R\\) contains \\(H_2\\) and \\(V_3\\), the point \\((3,2)\\) lies on both removed lines but is not \\((1,2)\\) (the private point of \\(H_2\\) in the given scheme) nor \\((3,1)\\) (the private point of \\(V_3\\)). Since the sunny line \\(S_{H_2}\\) passes only through \\((1,2)\\) and the sunny line \\(S_{V_3}\\) passes only through \\((3,1)\\), the point \\((3,2)\\) would remain uncovered. The claim that \u201cthe only point of \\(H_b\\) that is also on a vertical line of the form \\(V_j\\) with \\(j\\in\\mathcal R\\) is the private point\u201d is therefore incorrect. Consequently the proof that every lattice point is covered fails. This is a **critical error**.\n\n---\n\n### 14. Final conclusion\n\n> \u201cFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines \u2026 Consequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\). \u220e\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nBecause the construction does not actually produce a family of exactly \\(n\\) lines, does not guarantee the existence of private points for arbitrary \\(\\mathcal R\\), and fails to cover all points of \\(T_n\\), the conclusion is unsupported. This final statement inherits the critical errors identified above.\n\n---\n\n## Overall Assessment\n\nThe solution attempts a constructive proof but contains multiple **critical errors** that break the logical chain:\n\n1. **Private points** are not guaranteed to exist for an arbitrary choice of the removed set \\(\\mathcal R\\); the provided examples conflict with the requirement that the point be exclusive to its line.\n2. **Line count** is mis\u2011computed; the construction yields \\(2n+1\\) lines, not the required \\(n\\), and the subsequent \u201ctransparent\u201d formula is algebraically wrong.\n3. **Sunny\u2011line count** is contradictory: the construction yields \\(n-k\\) sunny lines, not \\(k\\).\n4. **Coverage argument** for points where both the horizontal and vertical lines are removed is invalid; many lattice points remain uncovered.\n\nBecause these errors are fundamental, the solution does **not** correctly solve the problem. The correct answer (the set of admissible \\(k\\)) remains unestablished by this proof."
