* Its slope is \\(i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n   * The lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are distinct because they have distinct slopes.\n\n   Consequently the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) cover every point of \\(T_k\\)\n   (each point \\((i,k+1-i)\\) is on its own line, and any other point of \\(T_k\\) lies on at least one of them).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction works for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); therefore these are precisely the admissible values.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nWe must determine all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist\n\\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) such that  \n\n1. every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one \\(\\ell_i\\);  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(\\ell_i\\) are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to the\n   \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Base case \\(k=0\\))\n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n\n\\]\n\nare pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and each point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\)\nsatisfies \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), hence lies on the line \\(x=a\\).\nThus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Covering a smaller triangle with sunny lines)\n\nFor every integer \\(m\\ge 0\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines that\ncover all points of \\(T_m\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(m=0\\) the statement is vacuous.  \nAssume \\(m\\ge 1\\). For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y-(m+1-i)=i\\bigl(x-i\\bigr) .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L_i\\) passes through the lattice point \\((i,m+1-i)\\in T_m\\).\nIts slope equals \\(i\\); since \\(i\\neq0\\), \\(i\\neq\\infty\\) and \\(i\\neq-1\\),\n\\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \nThe slopes \\(1,2,\\dots ,m\\) are distinct, so the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\) are\npairwise distinct.\n\nEvery point of \\(T_m\\) has the form \\((i,m+1-i)\\) for some \\(i\\) (the\npoints on the hypotenuse) or lies on the same line as one of these\npoints because the equations (1) are linear and contain all such points.\nConsequently the family \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^m\\) covers \\(T_m\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Inductive construction)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nThen there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny,\nthat cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-k .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThese are non\u2011sunny and together cover every point of \\(T_n\\) whose first\ncoordinate \\(a\\) satisfies \\(a\\le n-k\\).\n\nThe remaining points are those with \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\).  Write  \n\n\\[\na'=a-(n-k),\\qquad b'=b .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\na'+b' = a-(n-k)+b \\le (n+1)-(n-k)=k+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the set of uncovered points is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a',b')\\mid a',b'>0,\\;a'+b'\\le k+1\\}=T_k .\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma\u202f2, \\(T_k\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\nAdd these \\(k\\) sunny lines to the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (2).  \nThe total number of lines is \\(n\\); exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and\nevery point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nLemma\u202f1 shows that \\(k=0\\) is possible.  \nLemma\u202f3 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable\nfamily of \\(n\\) lines exists.  \nTherefore the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n}.\n\\]\n\nNo other values can occur because \\(k\\) counts the number of sunny lines\namong the \\(n\\) given lines, so trivially \\(0\\le k\\le n\\). \u220e"
