es through the lattice point \\((i,m+1-i)\\in T_m\\).\nIts slope equals \\(i\\); because \\(i\\neq0\\), \\(i\\neq\\infty\\) and \\(i\\neq-1\\),\n\\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \nThe slopes \\(1,2,\\dots ,m\\) are distinct, so the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\) are\npairwise distinct.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_m\\). Write \\(s=m+1-a-b\\) (so \\(s\\ge 0\\)).  \nConsider the index \\(i=a\\) (which lies between \\(1\\) and \\(m\\) because\n\\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(a\\le m\\) follows from \\(a+b\\le m+1\\)).  \nSubstituting \\(i=a\\) into (1) gives  \n\n\\[\ny-(m+1-a)=a\\bigl(x-a\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nEvaluating at \\((x,y)=(a,b)\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nb-(m+1-a)=a\\,(a-a)=0,\n\\]\n\nwhich holds precisely because \\(b=m+1-a\\!-\\!s\\) and \\(s\\ge0\\).  \nHence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_a\\); therefore it lies on\n\\(L_a\\).  Consequently every point of \\(T_m\\) belongs to at least one of the\nlines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\))\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nThen there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny,\nthat cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-k .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are non\u2011sunny and together cover every point of \\(T_n\\) whose\nfirst coordinate \\(a\\) satisfies \\(a\\le n-k\\).\n\nThe points of \\(T_n\\) not yet covered are those with \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\).  \nWrite  \n\n\\[\na'=a-(n-k),\\qquad b'=b .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\na'+b' = a-(n-k)+b \\le (n+1)-(n-k)=k+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the uncovered set is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a',b')\\mid a',b'>0,\\;a'+b'\\le k+1\\}=T_k .\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma\u202f2, the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) defined in (1) with\n\\(m=k\\) cover all points of \\(T_k\\).  Adding these \\(k\\) sunny lines to the\n\\(n-k\\) vertical lines (2) yields a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly\n\\(k\\) of which are sunny, that covers every point of \\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nLemma\u202f1 shows that \\(k=0\\) is possible.  \nLemma\u202f3 shows that for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable\nfamily of \\(n\\) lines exists.  \nSince \\(k\\) counts the number of sunny lines among the \\(n\\) given lines,\nnecessarily \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  Therefore the set of admissible values of\n\\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
