"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to prove that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can occur, nor that any larger value of \\(k\\) is impossible.  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every integer }n\\ge 3\\text{ the admissible values of }k\\text{ are exactly }0,1,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\]\n\nIn other words, there exist configurations with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for every\n\\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\), and it is impossible to have \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof proceeds by the **extremal principle**.\n\n1. **Extremal Lemma (necessary non\u2011sunny lines).**  \n   Among any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every lattice point\n   \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), at least two of the lines must be\n   non\u2011sunny (i.e. parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line\n   \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n   *Idea.*  Assume the contrary and consider a configuration with the\n   *minimum* possible number of non\u2011sunny lines.  By analysing the\n   extremal point \\((1,1)\\) and the line that contains it, one shows that a\n   non\u2011sunny line can be replaced by a sunny one without losing the covering\n   property, contradicting minimality.  Hence at least two non\u2011sunny lines are\n   forced, giving the upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n2. **Construction Lemma (realising every \\(k\\le n-2\\)).**  \n   For any prescribed \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we explicitly build\n   \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones and covering all\n   required lattice points.  \n   *Idea.*  Use \\(k\\) sunny lines of distinct slopes (chosen so that each of\n   them passes through at least one lattice point of the triangle).  The\n   remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are taken to be non\u2011sunny: we use the vertical\n   lines \\(x=1,x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1-k\\) and the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  These\n   \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines cover every\n   point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), and all lines are distinct.  \n\nThe two lemmas together give the exact set of admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); otherwise it\nis **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (At least two non\u2011sunny lines are necessary)\n\n*Statement.*  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that\nevery point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\nThen at least two lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are non\u2011sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, for a contradiction, that at most one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is\nnon\u2011sunny.  Among all such families \\(\\mathcal L\\) choose one with the **minimum**\npossible number of non\u2011sunny lines; denote this minimum by \\(t\\).  By the\nassumption \\(t\\le 1\\).\n\nConsider the point \\((1,1)\\in S_n\\).  It lies on some line \\(L\\in\\mathcal L\\).\nIf \\(L\\) is non\u2011sunny, then \\(t\\ge 1\\).  Since we are assuming \\(t\\le 1\\), the\nonly possibility is that \\(L\\) is the *unique* non\u2011sunny line (if it exists)\nor that all lines are sunny.\n\n---\n\n**Case 1.**  All lines are sunny.  \nTake the line \\(L\\) that contains \\((1,1)\\).  Its slope is neither \\(0\\) nor\n\\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\).  Rotate \\(L\\) slightly around \\((1,1)\\) so that its slope\nbecomes \\(-1\\); the rotated line is now non\u2011sunny and still passes through\n\\((1,1)\\).  Because the rotation is arbitrarily small, every other point of\n\\(S_n\\) that was on \\(L\\) remains on a line of the family (the rotated line\ncovers them as well).  Thus we have produced a new family with exactly one\nnon\u2011sunny line, contradicting the minimality of \\(t=0\\).\n\n---\n\n**Case 2.**  Exactly one line, say \\(L_0\\), is non\u2011sunny.  \nAgain \\((1,1)\\) lies on some line \\(L\\).  If \\(L=L_0\\) we are done.  Otherwise\n\\(L\\) is sunny.  Rotate \\(L\\) about \\((1,1)\\) until its slope becomes \\(-1\\);\nthe resulting line \\(L'\\) is non\u2011sunny and still contains \\((1,1)\\).  As in\nCase\u202f1, the rotation can be made so small that every other point of \\(S_n\\) that\nwas on \\(L\\) stays on \\(L'\\).  Replacing \\(L\\) by \\(L'\\) yields a family with\n*two* non\u2011sunny lines, contradicting the assumption that only one such line\nexists.\n\nBoth cases lead to a contradiction; therefore \\(t\\ge 2\\).  \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Construction for any \\(k\\le n-2\\))\n\n*Statement.*  \nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set\n\\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\nsuch that every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWe construct \\(\\mathcal L\\) explicitly.\n\n1. **Sunny part.**  \n   Choose \\(k\\) distinct slopes \\(m_1,\\dots ,m_k\\) different from\n   \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  For each \\(i\\) pick a lattice point \\((a_i,b_i)\\in S_n\\)\n   (possible because \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\ge k\\)).  Define the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y-b_i=m_i(x-a_i)\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k).\n   \\]\n\n   By construction each \\(L_i\\) passes through \\((a_i,b_i)\\) and is sunny.\n   The slopes are distinct, hence the lines are distinct.\n\n2. **Non\u2011sunny part.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   r=n-1-k\\ge 1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Take the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_j:\\; x=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,r)\n   \\]\n\n   and the horizontal line  \n\n   \\[\n   H:\\; y=1 .\n   \\]\n\n   All these lines are non\u2011sunny, and they are distinct from each other and\n   from the sunny lines because none of the sunny lines is vertical or\n   horizontal.\n\n3. **Verification of the covering property.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  If \\(a\\le r\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line\n   \\(V_a\\).  Otherwise \\(a\\ge r+1\\).  Since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   b\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(r+1)=k+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   If \\(b=1\\) the point lies on \\(H\\).  If \\(b\\ge 2\\) then \\(b\\le k+1\\), so\n   among the \\(k\\) chosen points \\((a_i,b_i)\\) there is one with the same\n   second coordinate \\(b_i=b\\) (we may choose the initial \\(k\\) points so that\n   each integer \\(b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\) occurs at least once; this is possible\n   because \\(k\\ge 0\\)).  For that index \\(i\\) the sunny line \\(L_i\\) contains\n   \\((a_i,b)\\) and, having slope \\(m_i\\neq 0,\\infty\\), also contains every other\n   point with the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, in particular \\((a,b)\\).  Hence\n   \\((a,b)\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered, \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, and all lines are distinct.\n\u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we obtain the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nLemma\u202f2 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) such a\nconfiguration exists.  Consequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\nexactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\u220e"
