her point of \\(S_n\\) that was on \\(L\\).  Replacing \\(L\\) by\n\\(L'\\) yields a family with exactly one non\u2011sunny line, contradicting the\nminimality of \\(t=0\\).\n\n*Case 2 \u2013 exactly one line is non\u2011sunny (\\(t=1\\)).*  \nIf \\(L\\) is the unique non\u2011sunny line we are done.  Otherwise \\(L\\) is sunny.\nRotate \\(L\\) about \\((1,1)\\) until its slope becomes \\(-1\\); the resulting line\n\\(L'\\) is non\u2011sunny.  As in Case\u202f1 the rotation can be made so small that all\npoints of \\(S_n\\) formerly covered by \\(L\\) remain covered (now by \\(L'\\)).\nThus we obtain a family with **two** non\u2011sunny lines, contradicting the\nassumption that only one exists.\n\nBoth cases lead to a contradiction; therefore \\(t\\ge 2\\).  \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Construction for any \\(k\\le n-2\\))\n\n*Statement.*  \nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set\n\\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines such\nthat every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSet  \n\n\\[\nr=n-1-k\\qquad(\\text{so }r\\ge 1).\n\\]\n\nWe construct \\(\\mathcal L\\) as the union of a sunny part \\(\\mathcal S\\) and a\nnon\u2011sunny part \\(\\mathcal N\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Sunny part \\(\\mathcal S\\)\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) consider the lattice point  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;n+1-i)\\in S_n .\n\\]\n\nChoose a slope  \n\n\\[\nm_i\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the numbers \\(m_1,\\dots ,m_k\\) are pairwise distinct (for instance\n\\(m_i=i\\)).  Define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y-(n+1-i)=m_i\\bigl(x-i\\bigr) .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) passes through \\(P_i\\) and, because its slope is different from\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\), it is sunny.  Distinct slopes guarantee that the lines are\npairwise distinct, and none of them coincides with a vertical or horizontal\nline, so they are also distinct from the lines that will appear in \\(\\mathcal\nN\\).\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal S=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Non\u2011sunny part \\(\\mathcal N\\)\n\nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\; x=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,r)\n\\]\n\nand the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nH:\\; y=1 .\n\\]\n\nAll these lines are non\u2011sunny, and they are distinct from each other and from\nthe sunny lines because none of the sunny lines is vertical or horizontal.\nSet \\(\\mathcal N=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_r,H\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Verification of the covering property\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le r\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\in\\mathcal N\\).\n\n*If \\(a>r\\)*, then \\(a\\ge r+1=n-k\\).  Using \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(n-k)=k+1 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(b\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\).\n\n*If \\(b=1\\)*, the point lies on the horizontal line \\(H\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\ge 2\\)*, then \\(b\\le k+1\\) and therefore \\(b-1\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).\nConsider the sunny line \\(L_{b-1}\\in\\mathcal S\\).  By construction,\n\\(L_{b-1}\\) passes through the point  \n\n\\[\nP_{b-1}=(b-1,\\;n+2-b).\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(P_{b-1}\\) and \\((a,b)\\) have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate difference:\n\n\\[\nb-(n+2-b)=2b-n-2,\n\\qquad\nn+2-b-b = n+2-2b .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(L_{b-1}\\) has slope \\(m_{b-1}\\neq 0\\), the line contains **all**\npoints whose \\(y\\)-coordinate equals \\(b\\); in particular it contains\n\\((a,b)\\).  (Geometrically, a line with non\u2011zero slope that passes through one\npoint with ordinate \\(b\\) necessarily contains every point with the same\nordinate after a suitable horizontal translation; the chosen slopes are\nnon\u2011zero, so the line is not horizontal.)\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of\n\\(\\mathcal L=\\mathcal S\\cup\\mathcal N\\).\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(L_i\\)\u2019s)\nand \\(n-k\\) non\u2