r with sunny lines of distinct slopes.  \n   *When \\(k=n\\) we simply take \\(n\\) sunny lines of pairwise different slopes, each passing through a distinct point of the lattice; the covering condition is satisfied because the set of slopes can be chosen so that each line contains at least one lattice point.  \n   *When \\(k=n-1\\) we keep the same family and replace one sunny line by a vertical line \\(x=c\\) (or a horizontal line \\(y=c\\)) that passes through many lattice points, thereby reducing the number of sunny lines by one while still covering the whole set.  \n   *When \\(k=n-2\\) we replace two sunny lines by a vertical line and a horizontal line; the two non\u2011sunny lines together cover all points on the two sides of the triangle, and the remaining \\(n-2\\) sunny lines cover the interior.  \n\nBoth parts together give the exact description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to be covered.  \nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Necessity: at most two non\u2011sunny lines\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers every point of \\(T_{n}\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N:=\\{L\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L\\text{ is non\u2011sunny}\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal S:=\\mathcal L\\setminus\\mathcal N .\n\\]\n\nDenote \\(m:=|\\mathcal N|\\).  \nWe shall prove that \\(m\\le 2\\).\n\nSuppose, for a contradiction, that \\(m\\ge 3\\).  \nAmong all admissible families with at least three non\u2011sunny lines, choose one for which the number of points of \\(T_{n}\\) covered **exclusively** by non\u2011sunny lines is maximal.  \nCall such a family *extremal*.\n\nEvery non\u2011sunny line has one of the three slopes \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\).  \nConsequently \\(\\mathcal N\\) contains at most three lines, one of each slope.  \nSince \\(m\\ge 3\\), the extremal family must contain **exactly** one horizontal line, one vertical line and one line of slope \\(-1\\); denote them respectively by  \n\n\\[\nH:\\;y=c,\\qquad V:\\;x=d,\\qquad D:\\;x+y=e .\n\\]\n\nAll three constants \\(c,d,e\\) are integers because each of the three lines must pass through at least one lattice point of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Claim 1  \nThe three lines \\(H,V,D\\) together cover **all** points of the two sides of the triangle \\(T_{n}\\), i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\\cup\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\cup\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\n\\subseteq H\\cup V\\cup D .\n\\]\n\n*Proof of Claim.*  \nThe side \\(\\{(a,1)\\}\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\); therefore we must have \\(c=1\\).  \nSimilarly the side \\(\\{(1,b)\\}\\) forces \\(d=1\\).  \nFinally the hypotenuse \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\\) is precisely the line \\(x+y=n+1\\), so \\(e=n+1\\).  \u220e\n\nThus the three non\u2011sunny lines are forced to be  \n\n\\[\nH:\\;y=1,\\qquad V:\\;x=1,\\qquad D:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Claim 2  \nNo point of the interior of the triangle, i.e. any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) with \\(a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n\\), can lie on any of the three lines in (2.1).\n\n*Proof of Claim.*  \nFor such a point we have \\(b\\neq 1\\) and \\(a\\neq 1\\), so it is not on \\(H\\) nor on \\(V\\).  \nMoreover \\(a+b\\le n\\) whereas the line \\(D\\) satisfies \\(x+y=n+1\\); hence the point is not on \\(D\\). \u220e\n\nConsequently the interior points of \\(T_{n}\\) must be covered by the remaining \\(n-m\\) lines, all of which are sunny.  \nSince the interior contains  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n\\}\\,|\n= \\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2}\n\\]\n\npoints, at least one sunny line is needed for each of these points unless a sunny line passes through several of them.  \nHowever a sunny line can intersect the integer lattice in at most one point of the interior, because any two distinct interior points have different slopes \\(\\frac{b_{2}-b_{1}}{a_{2}-a_{1}}\\) which is never \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nHence we would need at least \\(\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2}\\) sunny lines, which is impossible when \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(m\\ge 3\\) (the total number of lines would then exceed \\(n\\)).  \n\nThis contradiction shows that the assumption \\(m\\ge 3\\) is false; therefore  \n\n\\[\nm\\le 2 .\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k=|\\mathcal S|=n-m\\), (2.2) yields the **necessary condition**\n\n\\[\nk\\ge n-2 .\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Sufficiency: constructions for \\(k=n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for each integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(n-2\\le k\\le n\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_{n}\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2.1  The case \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny)\n\nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_{i}:\\; y = \\alpha_{i}x + \\beta_{i},\n\\]\n\nwhere the slopes \\(\\alpha_{i}\\) are chosen to be distinct rational numbers different from \\(0,-1\\) and the intercepts \\(\\beta_{i}\\) are chosen so that each line passes through the lattice point \\((i,1)\\).  \nExplicitly, take \\(\\alpha_{i}= \\frac{i}{i+1}\\) and \\(\\beta_{i}=1-\\alpha_{i}i\\).  \nThen \\((i,1)\\in L_{i}\\) and the slope \\(\\alpha_{i}\\neq 0,-1\\).  \n\nBecause the points \\((i,1)\\) for \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) belong to \\(T_{n}\\), every point of the bottom side of the triangle is covered.  \nSimilarly, by symmetry we may replace the set \\(\\{(i,1)\\}\\) with the set \\(\\{(1,i)\\}\\) and obtain a family of \\(n\\) sunny lines covering the left side.  \nFinally, each interior point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) lies on the line that joins \\((a,1)\\) and \\((1,b)\\); this line has slope \\(\\frac{b-1}{1-a}\\neq 0,-1\\) and therefore is sunny.  \nThus the \\(n\\) sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\{\\,\\text{line through }(a,1)\\text{ and }(1,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\,\\bigr\\}\n\\]\n\ncover the whole set \\(T_{n}\\).  Hence \\(k=n\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2.2  The case \\(k=n-1\\) (exactly one non\u2011sunny line)\n\nKeep the construction for \\(k=n\\) and replace the sunny line that passes through the point \\((1,1)\\) by the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV:\\;x=1 .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(V\\) is non\u2011sunny (slope \\(\\infty\\)) and contains all points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\); consequently the left side of the triangle remains covered.  \nAll other points are still covered by the remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, because none of them relied on the former line through \\((1,1)\\).  \nThus we obtain a configuration with exactly one non\u2011sunny line, i.e. \\(k=n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2.3  The case \\(k=n-2\\) (two non\u2011sunny lines)\n\nStart from the configuration for \\(k=n\\) and replace two sunny lines as follows:\n\n* replace the line through \\((1,1)\\) by the vertical line \\(V:x=1\\);\n* replace the line through \\((1,2)\\) (or any other point on the left side) by the horizontal line \\(H:y=1\\).\n\nThe two non\u2011sunny lines \\(V\\) and \\(H\\) together cover the entire left side \\(\\{(1,b)\\}\\) and the bottom side \\(\\{(a,1)\\}\\) of the triangle.  \nAll interior points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) are still covered by the remaining \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, because each such point lies on the line joining \\((a,1)\\) and \\((1,b)\\), and those two points are still present in the construction (the lines through them are unchanged).  \n\nHence we have a family of \\(n\\) lines containing exactly two non\u2011sunny lines, i.e. \\(k=n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Conclusion\n\nFrom (2.3) we have the necessary condition \\(k\\ge n-2\\).  \nThe explicit constructions of \u00a72.2 show that each integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\nn-2\\le k\\le n\n\\]\n\nindeed occurs.  Therefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\,\\}} .\n\\]"
