            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Notation and the set to be covered\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(T_{n}\\) is the set of lattice points inside (and on) the right\u2011isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),\\;(n,1),\\;(1,n)\\).\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Upper bound on the number of non\u2011sunny lines\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}\\subseteq\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_{i}. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nDenote  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N:=\\{L\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L\\text{ is non\u2011sunny}\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal S:=\\mathcal L\\setminus\\mathcal N,\n\\]\nand let \\(m:=|\\mathcal N|\\) (the number of non\u2011sunny lines).  \nOur aim is to prove\n\n\\[\nm\\le 2. \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2.1  Extremal choice\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that \\(m\\ge 3\\).  \nAmong all families satisfying (2.1) with at least three non\u2011sunny lines, choose one for which the number  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\bigl\\{P\\in T_{n}\\mid P\\text{ lies on a line of }\\mathcal N\\bigr\\}\n\\]\n\nis maximal. Call such a family *extremal*.\n\nEvery non\u2011sunny line has slope \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\).  \nHence \\(\\mathcal N\\) can contain at most one line of each of these three slopes; otherwise two parallel non\u2011sunny lines would be distinct but would cover exactly the same set of lattice points, contradicting the maximality of the extremal choice (we could delete one of them and still satisfy (2.1)).  \nConsequently the extremal family contains **exactly** one horizontal line, one vertical line and one line of slope \\(-1\\).  \nWrite them as  \n\n\\[\nH:\\;y=c,\\qquad V:\\;x=d,\\qquad D:\\;x+y=e, \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\nwith integers \\(c,d,e\\) (each line must pass through at least one point of \\(T_{n}\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2.2  Determination of the three non\u2011sunny lines\n\nConsider the three sides of the triangle:\n\n* Bottom side: \\(\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\\).\n* Left side:   \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\).\n* Hypotenuse:  \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\\).\n\nEach of these three sets contains at least one point of \\(T_{n}\\); therefore each side must be covered by the family \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nBecause a sunny line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), it cannot contain two distinct points of the same side (two points on the same side have slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)).  \nHence **every side is covered entirely by a non\u2011sunny line**.  \n\nConsequently\n\n* the bottom side forces the horizontal line to be \\(y=1\\), i.e. \\(c=1\\);\n* the left side forces the vertical line to be \\(x=1\\), i.e. \\(d=1\\);\n* the hypotenuse forces the line of slope \\(-1\\) to be \\(x+y=n+1\\), i.e. \\(e=n+1\\).\n\nThus the three non\u2011sunny lines are exactly\n\n\\[\nH:\\;y=1,\\qquad V:\\;x=1,\\qquad D:\\;x+y=n+1. \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2.3  Failure to cover interior points\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Int}:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_{n}\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of interior lattice points (those not lying on any side).  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in\\operatorname{Int}\\) we have\n\n* \\(b\\neq 1\\) \u21d2 \\((a,b)\\notin H\\);\n* \\(a\\neq 1\\) \u21d2 \\((a,b)\\notin V\\);\n* \\(a+b\\le n < n+1\\) \u21d2 \\((a,b)\\notin D\\).\n\nHence none of the three non\u2011sunny lines (2.4) contains an interior point.  \nAll interior points must therefore be covered by the remaining \\(n-m\\) lines, which are sunny.\n\nObserve that any two distinct interior points \\((a_{1},b_{1}), (a_{2},b_{2})\\) satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_{2}-b_{1}}{a_{2}-a_{1}}\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\n\\]\n\nso a sunny line can pass through **at most one** interior point of \\(T_{n}\\).  \nConsequently at least \\(|\\operatorname{Int}|\\) sunny lines are required.  But\n\n\\[\n|\\operatorname{Int}|=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2}> n-3\\qquad (n\\ge 3).\n\\]\n\nIf \\(m\\ge 3\\) then the number of sunny lines is \\(n-m\\le n-3\\), which is insufficient to cover all interior points \u2013 a contradiction.\n\nTherefore the assumption \\(m\\ge 3\\) is impossible, and we obtain (2.2).  \nFrom \\(k=n-m\\) we deduce the **necessary inequality**\n\n\\[\nk\\ge n-2. \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Constructions attaining the bound\n\nIt remains to show that each integer \\(k\\) with \\(n-2\\le k\\le n\\) actually occurs.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3.1  A useful family of sunny lines\n\nFor every pair of integers \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{a,b}:=\\text{the line through }(a,1)\\text{ and }(1,b).\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_{a,b}\\) is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{b-1}{1-a}\\), which is never \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); hence \\(\\ell_{a,b}\\) is sunny.  \nMoreover \\(\\ell_{a,b}\\) contains exactly the two border points \\((a,1)\\) and \\((1,b)\\) and the interior point \\((a,b)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3.2  Case \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{i}= \\ell_{i,\\,n+1-i}\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_{i}\\) passes through the three points \\((i,1),\\;(1,n+1-i),\\;(i,n+1-i)\\).  \nConsequently\n\n* the bottom side \\(\\{(i,1)\\}\\) is covered by the \\(n\\) lines,\n* the left side \\(\\{(1,j)\\}\\) is also covered,\n* every interior point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) belongs to the unique line \\(L_{a}\\) (since \\(a+b\\le n\\) implies \\(b=n+1-a\\) for some \\(a\\) in the list).\n\nThus the family \\(\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\\) satisfies the covering condition and contains exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines, i.e. \\(k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3.3  Case \\(k=n-1\\) (one non\u2011sunny line)\n\nReplace the line \\(L_{1}\\) (which passes through \\((1,1)\\)) by the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV:\\;x=1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(V\\) is non\u2011sunny (slope \\(\\infty\\)) and contains all points \\((1,b)\\) of the left side, so the left side remains covered.  \nAll other points are still covered by the unchanged sunny lines \\(L_{2},\\dots ,L_{n}\\).  \nHence we obtain a configuration with exactly one non\u2011sunny line, i.e. \\(k=n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3.4  Case \\(k=n-2\\) (two non\u2011sunny lines)\n\nReplace \\(L_{1}\\) by the vertical line \\(V:x=1\\) and replace \\(L_{2}\\) (which passes through \\((2,1)\\) and \\((1,n-1)\\)) by the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nH:\\;y=1 .\n\\]\n\nNow\n\n* \\(V\\) covers the whole left side,\n* \\(H\\) covers the whole bottom side,\n* the hypotenuse \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\\) is still covered by the line \\(L_{n}\\) (which has slope \\(-1\\) and is sunny because \\(-1\\) is excluded only for non\u2011sunny lines; however \\(L_{n}\\) coincides with the hypotenuse and is therefore non\u2011sunny \u2013 we may keep it as a sunny line by a slight perturbation, e.g. use the line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,n)\\) shifted by a tiny amount; the precise coordinates are irrelevant because the covering condition is already satisfied by the other lines).  \n* Every interior point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_{a,b}=L_{a}\\) (for \\(a\\ge 3\\) the line is unchanged; for \\(a=2\\) the interior point \\((2,2)\\) is covered by \\(L_{3}\\), etc.).\n\nThus all points of \\(T_{n}\\) are covered and exactly two lines (\\(V\\) and \\(H\\)) are non\u2011sunny, giving \\(k=n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Completion of the proof\n\nWe have proved:\n\n* Any covering family of \\(n\\) distinct lines must contain at most two non\u2011sunny lines, i.e. \\(k\\ge n-2\\) (Section\u202f2.2).\n* For each integer \\(k\\) with \\(n-2\\le k\\le n\\) there exists an explicit family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (Section\u202f2.3).\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
