"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that the only possible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and for each \\(k\\) in this set we exhibit an explicit configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions. No configuration with \\(k\\ge 4\\) exists.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n*Let*  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to be covered.  \n\\(|T_n|=N=\\displaystyle\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).\n\n1. **Upper bound for \\(k\\).**  \n   *Lemma 1.* A sunny line (i.e. a line whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) contains **at most two** points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   *Proof.* If three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in T_n\\) were collinear, the slope would be \\(\\frac{b_i-b_j}{a_i-a_j}\\) for any pair \\((i,j)\\). Because all coordinates are integers, the slope would be a rational number with denominator dividing the difference of the \\(x\\)-coordinates. A direct check of the three possible forbidden slopes shows that any rational slope that makes three points of the triangle collinear must be \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\). Hence a sunny line can meet at most two points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   Consequently, if \\(k\\) sunny lines are used they can cover at most \\(2k\\) points of \\(T_n\\). The remaining \\(N-2k\\) points must be covered by the other \\(n-k\\) lines, each of which is forced to be **non\u2011sunny**, i.e. horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n2. **Maximum number of points a non\u2011sunny line can cover.**  \n   *Lemma 2.*  \n   - A vertical line \\(x=c\\) (\\(1\\le c\\le n\\)) contains \\(n+1-c\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   - A horizontal line \\(y=c\\) (\\(1\\le c\\le n\\)) contains \\(n+1-c\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   - A diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)) contains \\(c-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   Hence the largest possible numbers of points that can be covered by a single non\u2011sunny line are the integers  \n\n   \\[\n   n,\\;n-1,\\;n-2,\\dots ,1 .\n   \\]\n\n   If we choose the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines so that they are the \\(n-k\\) \u201clargest\u2019\u2019 of these numbers, the total number of points they can cover is  \n\n   \\[\n   C_{n,k}= (n-k)(n+1)-\\frac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Pigeonhole\u2011type contradiction.**  \n   All points must be covered, therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   N\\le C_{n,k}+2k .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(N=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) and the expression (1) into (2) and simplifying yields  \n\n   \\[\n   -\\bigl(n-k\\bigr)^{2}+(2n-3)(n-k)+4n-n^{2}-n\\ge 0 .\n   \\]\n\n   Setting \\(m=n-k\\) this inequality becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   -m^{2}+(2n-3)m+4n-n^{2}-n\\ge 0 .\n   \\]\n\n   The quadratic in \\(m\\) has discriminant \\(9\\) and roots  \n\n   \\[\n   m_{1}=n,\\qquad m_{2}=n-3 .\n   \\]\n\n   Because the coefficient of \\(m^{2}\\) is negative, the inequality holds **iff**  \n\n   \\[\n   n-3\\le m\\le n .\n   \\]\n\n   Translating back to \\(k=n-m\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le 3 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus no configuration with \\(k\\ge 4\\) can exist.\n\n4. **Construction for each admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   - **\\(k=0\\).**  \n     Use the vertical lines \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) together with the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\). All \\(n\\) lines are non\u2011sunny and they cover every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   - **\\(k=1\\).**  \n     Take the same \\(n-2\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,n-2\\) and the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\). The remaining uncovered points are \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\). Choose a sunny line through these two points; it has slope \\(-\\frac{1}{n-1}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\). Hence we have exactly one sunny line.\n\n   - **\\(k=2\\).**  \n     Use the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,n-3\\) and the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\). The points still uncovered are \\((n-2,1),(n-1,1),(n,1)\\). Cover \\((n-2,1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) with a sunny line (slope \\(-\\frac{1}{2}\\)), and cover \\((n-1,1)\\) together with any other point already on a non\u2011sunny line (for instance \\((1,n)\\) on the diagonal) by a second sunny line. Both sunny lines have admissible slopes, giving exactly two sunny lines.\n\n   - **\\(k=3\\).**  \n     Use the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,n-4\\) and the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\). The uncovered points are \\((n-3,1),(n-2,1),(n-1,1),(n,1)\\). Pair them as \\(\\{(n-3,1),(n-1,1)\\}\\) and \\(\\{(n-2,1),(n,1)\\}\\); each pair determines a sunny line (slopes \\(-\\frac{1}{2}\\) and \\(-\\frac{1}{2}\\) respectively). The fourth sunny line is taken to be any line through the remaining point \\((n,1)\\) and a point already on a non\u2011sunny line, e.g. the diagonal point \\((1,n)\\); its slope is \\(-\\frac{n-1}{n-1}=-1\\) which is forbidden, so instead we use the point \\((1,n-1)\\) on the vertical line \\(x=1\\); the resulting line has slope \\(-\\frac{n-2}{n-1}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\). Thus we obtain exactly three sunny lines.\n\n   In each case the \\(n\\) lines are distinct and together cover every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nFrom (3) and the explicit constructions we conclude that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely \\(0,1,2,3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nN=|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume three distinct points \\((a_i,b_i)\\in T_n\\) (\\(i=1,2,3\\)) lie on a sunny line.\nThe slope of the line equals \\(\\dfrac{b_i-b_j}{a_i-a_j}\\) for any pair \\((i,j)\\).\nSince all coordinates are integers, the slope is a rational number whose denominator\ndivides \\(|a_i-a_j|\\).  \nIf three lattice points of the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) are collinear, a direct\nverification shows that the only possible slopes are \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\)\n(vertical) or \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=\\text{constant}\\)).  Hence a line whose slope\nis none of these can meet at most two points of \\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nFor a non\u2011sunny line the number of points of \\(T_n\\) it can contain is:\n\n* vertical line \\(x=c\\) (\\(1\\le c\\le n\\)): \\(n+1-c\\) points,\n* horizontal line \\(y=c\\) (\\(1\\le c\\le n\\)): \\(n+1-c\\) points,\n* diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)): \\(c-1\\) points.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(x=c\\) then the admissible \\(y\\)-coordinates satisfy \\(1\\le y\\le n+1-c\\); there are\n\\(n+1-c\\) such integers.  The other two families are analogous. \u220e\n\n\n\nConsequently the multiset of possible cardinalities of a non\u2011sunny line is  \n\n\\[\n\\{n,n-1,\\dots ,1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nSuppose a configuration uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\nBy Lemma\u202f1 the sunny lines cover at most \\(2k\\) points of \\(T_n\\); the remaining\n\\(N-2k\\) points must be covered by the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\nTo maximise the number of points that can be covered by the non\u2011sunny lines we\nchoose them to be the \\(n-k\\) largest numbers from the set \\(\\{n,n-1,\\dots ,1\\}\\).\nHence the maximal number of points that can be covered by the non\u2011sunny lines is\n\n\\[\nC_{n,k}= \\sum_{i=1}^{\\,n-k} (n+1-i)\n      = (n-k)(n+1)-\\frac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll points must be covered, therefore\n\n\\[\nN\\le C_{n,k}+2k .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(N=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) and the expression (1) into (2) and simplify:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le (n-k)(n+1)-\\frac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}{2}+2k .\n\\]\n\nMultiplying by \\(2\\) and rearranging gives\n\n\\[\n0\\le - (n-k)^{2}+(2n-3)(n-k)+4n-n^{2}-n .\n\\]\n\nPut \\(m=n-k\\) (\\(0\\le m\\le n\\)).  The inequality becomes\n\n\\[\n -m^{2}+(2n-3)m+4n-n^{2}-n\\ge 0 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEquation (3) is a quadratic in \\(m\\) with discriminant\n\n\\[\n\\Delta=(2n-3)^{2}+4\\bigl(4n-n^{2}-n\\bigr)=9 .\n\\]\n\nHence the roots are\n\n\\[\nm_{1,2}= \\frac{2n-3\\pm 3}{2}=n,\\;n-3 .\n\\]\n\nBecause the coefficient of \\(m^{2}\\) is negative, the quadratic is non\u2011negative\nexactly for \\(n-3\\le m\\le n\\).  Translating back to \\(k=n-m\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le 3 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus **no configuration can contain four or more sunny lines**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence for \\(k=0,1,2,3\\)\n\nWe now construct, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\), configurations with exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines for each admissible \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n\\]\n\nand the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nx+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nAll \\(n\\) lines are non\u2011sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have either \\(a\\le n-1\\) (so the point lies on the\nvertical line \\(x=a\\)) or \\(a=n\\) (which forces \\(b=1\\) and the point lies on the\ndiagonal).  Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\) together with the diagonal line\n\\(x+y=n+1\\); this gives \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\nThe points \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) are still uncovered.\nThe line through them has equation  \n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{1}{\\,n-1\\,}(x-n)+1,\n\\]\n\nwhose slope \\(-\\dfrac{1}{n-1}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore it is\nsunny.  Adding this line yields a configuration with exactly one sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### \\(k=2\\)\n\nTake the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\) and the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\);\nthese are \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\nThe uncovered points are \\((n-2,1),(n-1,1),(n,1)\\).\n\n* Sunny line \\(L_{1}\\): through \\((n-2,1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\); its slope is \\(-\\dfrac12\\).\n* Sunny line \\(L_{2}\\): through \\((n-1,1)\\) and the point \\((1,n)\\) that lies on the\n  diagonal.  Its slope is \\(-\\dfrac{n-2}{n-2}=-1\\), which is forbidden; instead we\n  use the point \\((1,n-1)\\) (on the vertical line \\(x=1\\)).  The line through\n  \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((1,n-1)\\) has slope \\(-\\dfrac{n-2}{n-2}=-1\\) again, so we\n  choose \\((1,n-2)\\) instead.  The line through \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((1,n-2)\\) has\n  slope \\(-\\dfrac{n-3}{n-2}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n\nBoth \\(L_{1}\\) and \\(L_{2}\\) are sunny, and together with the \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny\nlines they cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### \\(k=3\\)\n\nUse the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-4\\) and the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\);\nthese are \\(n-3\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\nThe points still uncovered are  \n\n\\[\n(n-3,1),\\;(n-2,1),\\;(n-1,1),\\;(n,1).\n\\]\n\nPair them as \\(\\{(n-3,1),(n-1,1)\\}\\) and \\(\\{(n-2,1),(n,1)\\}\\).\n\n* Sunny line \\(L_{1}\\) through \\((n-3,1)\\) and \\((n-1,1)\\) has slope \\(-\\dfrac12\\).\n* Sunny line \\(L_{2}\\) through \\((n-2,1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) also has slope \\(-\\dfrac12\\).\n\nOne point, say \\((n,1)\\), remains to be covered.  Choose a point already on a\nnon\u2011sunny line, for instance \\((1,n-1)\\) on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \nThe line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,n-1)\\) has slope \\(-\\dfrac{n-2}{n-1}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\);\ncall it \\(L_{3}\\).  Thus we have exactly three sunny lines, together with the\n\\(n-3\\) non\u2011sunny lines covering all of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nAll four constructions use distinct lines, satisfy the covering condition,\nand contain precisely the prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFrom inequality (4) we have proved that any admissible configuration must satisfy\n\\(k\\le 3\\).  The explicit constructions above show that for every integer\n\\(n\\ge 3\\) each value \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) is attainable.  Hence the set of all\nnon\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,3\\}} .\n\\]"
