_n\\). Therefore the statement of Lemma\u202f1 is incorrect.\n\n**Classification:** Critical Error.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 Proof\n\n> \u201c*Proof.*  \n> Assume three distinct points \\((a_i,b_i)\\in T_n\\) (\\(i=1,2,3\\)) are collinear.\n> The slope of the line equals \\(\\dfrac{b_i-b_j}{a_i-a_j}\\) for any pair\n> \\((i,j)\\).  Because all coordinates are integers, the slope is a rational number.\n> If three lattice points inside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) are collinear, a\n> straight\u2011forward check of all possibilities shows that the only possible slopes\n> are \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).\n> Hence a line whose slope is none of these can meet at most two points of\n> \\(T_n\\). \u220e\u201d\n\n**Verification:** The argument is insufficient and, as shown above, outright false. The \u201cstraight\u2011forward check\u201d does not rule out slopes such as \\(2\\) or \\(\\frac12\\). A correct proof would need to consider the step size \\((q,p)\\) of a line with rational slope \\(p/q\\) and show that for certain values of \\(p,q\\) three points can still lie inside the triangle. The presented reasoning therefore fails.\n\n**Classification:** Critical Error (also a justification gap, but the falsity makes it a critical error).\n\n---\n\n### 4. Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 Statement and Proof\n\n> \u201c**Lemma\u202f2**  \n> For a non\u2011sunny line the number of points of \\(T_n\\) it can contain is  \n> * vertical line \\(x=c\\) (\\(1\\le c\\le n\\)): \\(n+1-c\\) points,  \n> * horizontal line \\(y=c\\) (\\(1\\le c\\le n\\)): \\(n+1-c\\) points,  \n> * diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)): \\(c-1\\) points.  \n> *Proof.* \u2026\u201d\n\n**Verification:** Correct. For each family the admissible integer coordinates are counted directly, and the formulas are accurate. No issue.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Multiset of possible cardinalities of a non\u2011sunny line\n\n> \u201cConsequently the