d. Hence the derived quadratic condition and the conclusion \\(k\\le3\\) are not justified.\n\n**Classification:** Critical Error (consequence of the earlier false bound).\n\n---\n\n### 8. Solving the quadratic and obtaining \\(k\\le3\\)\n\n> \u201c\u2026 the discriminant \\(\\Delta=9\\); roots \\(m_{1,2}=n,\\;n-3\\); \u2026 we obtain  \n> \\[\n> 0\\le k\\le 3 .\n> \\tag{4}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Verification:** The algebraic steps are correct given the preceding inequality. However, because the premise is false, the conclusion does not follow. Thus this part, while algebraically sound, does not rescue the proof.\n\n**Classification:** No new error, but the result is unreliable.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Existence constructions \u2013 \\(k=0\\)\n\n> \u201cLines: \\(x=1, x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) (vertical) and \\(x+y=n+1\\) (diagonal).  \n> \u2026 Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered.\u201d\n\n**Verification:** This construction is correct. The vertical lines cover all points with \\(a\\le n-1\\); the diagonal covers the remaining point \\((n,1)\\). Thus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists. No issue.\n\n---\n\n### 10. Existence constructions \u2013 \\(k=1\\)\n\n> \u201cLines: vertical \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\) and the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) (these are\n> \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines).  The points \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) are still\n> uncovered.  The line through them has equation  \n> \\[\n> y=-\\frac{1}{\\,n-1\\,}(x-n)+1,\n> \\]  \n> whose slope \\(-\\dfrac{1}{n-1}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); it is sunny.\n> Adding this line yields exactly one sunny line.\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \n- **Incorrect claim of uncovered points:** \\((n,1)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) and is already covered.  \n- **Incorrect line equation:** The line through \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) is horizontal, slope \\(0\\), which is *non\u2011sunny*. The displayed equation does **not** pass through \\((n-1,1)\\); substituting \\(x=n-1\\) gives \\(y=1+\\frac{1}{n-1}\\neq1\\).  \