\u202f2 \u2013 \u201cConstruction for any \\(k\\le n-2\\)\u201d\n\n**Statement:**  \n\u201cFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines such that every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\u201d\n\n**Construction Overview (no error):**  \n- Define \\(r=n-1-k\\) (so \\(r\\ge1\\)).  \n- Build \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_i\\) through points \\(P_i=(i,n+1-i)\\) with distinct non\u2011forbidden slopes \\(m_i\\).  \n- Build \\(r\\) vertical lines \\(V_j: x=j\\) for \\(j=1,\\dots,r\\) and one horizontal line \\(H: y=1\\).  \n- Total lines: \\(k+r+1=n\\).  \n- Distinctness of all lines is clear.\n\n**Verification of the covering property \u2013 Critical Error Identified**\n\nThe proof splits into cases for a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n1. **Case \\(a\\le r\\):**  \n   - The point lies on vertical line \\(V_a\\). This part is correct.\n\n2. **Case \\(a>r\\):**  \n   - Derives \\(b\\le k+1\\). This inequality is correct.\n\n3. **Subcase \\(b=1\\):**  \n   - The point lies on horizontal line \\(H\\). Correct.\n\n4. **Subcase \\(b\\ge2\\):**  \n   - Sets \\(i=b-1\\) and considers sunny line \\(L_i\\).  \n   - The proof claims: *\u201cSince \\(L_{b-1}\\) has slope \\(m_{b-1}\\neq0\\), the line contains **all** points whose \\(y\\)-coordinate equals \\(b\\); in particular it contains \\((a,b)\\).\u201d*  \n\n   **Why this is false:**  \n   - A line with slope \\(m\\neq0\\) is not horizontal. Its equation can be written as \\(y = m(x - x_i) + y_i\\) where \\((x_i,y_i)\\) is a point on the line (here \\((i, n+2-i)\\)). For a fixed \\(y=b\\), solving for \\(x\\) yields a **unique** value \\(x = (b - y_i)/m + x_i\\). Thus the line contains **exactly one** point with ordinate \\(b\\), not all points with that ordinate.  \n   - Consequently, there is no guarantee that the specific point \\((a,b)\\) (with arbitrary \\(a\\)) lies on \\(L_{b-1}\\). The