            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 2.1  Notation and the set to be covered  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction is *not* parallel to any of the three forbidden directions  \n\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal } (y=\\text{const}),\\qquad \n\\text{vertical } (x=\\text{const}),\\qquad \n\\text{the line } x+y=0\\;(\\text{slope }-1).\n\\]\n\nThus a line with slope \\(m\\) is sunny exactly when \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\).\n\nOur task is, for each \\(k\\), to find \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* cover every point of \\(T_n\\), and  \n* contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 2.2  Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\[\nT_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe following four families satisfy the requirements.\n\n| \\(k\\) | Family of three lines | Sunny lines |\n|------|------------------------|-------------|\n| 0 | \\(y=0,\\;x=0,\\;x+y=0\\) | none |\n| 1 | \\(y=0,\\;x=0,\\;y=2x\\) | \\(y=2x\\) |\n| 2 | \\(x+y=0,\\;y=2x,\\;y=-\\tfrac12x\\) | \\(y=2x,\\;y=-\\tfrac12x\\) |\n| 3 | \\(y=2x,\\;y=-\\tfrac12x,\\;y=3x\\) | all three |\n\nA direct check shows that each point of \\(T_3\\) lies on at least one line of the corresponding family.  Hence the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.3  Lemmas for the inductive step  \n\n#### Lemma 1 (adding a non\u2011sunny line)  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L'=\\mathcal L\\cup\\{x=0\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(T_{n+1}\\) and contains exactly the same number of sunny lines as \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe added line \\(x=0\\) is vertical, hence not sunny.  \n\n*Points with first coordinate \\(a\\ge 2\\).*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_{n+1}\\) and \\(a\\ge 2\\), then \\((a-1,b)\\) satisfies \\((a-1)+b\\le n+1\\); thus \\((a-1,b)\\in T_n\\) and is covered by some line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  Translating that line one unit to the right (which does not change its direction) yields a line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) that passes through \\((a,b)\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) is covered.\n\n*Points with first coordinate \\(a=1\\).*  \nIf \\((1,b)\\in T_{n+1}\\) then \\(b\\le n+1\\); such points lie on the vertical line \\(x=0\\).  \n\nTherefore every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) belongs to a line of \\(\\mathcal L'\\).  The number of sunny lines is unchanged because the new line is not sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2 (adding a sunny line)  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\).  \nChoose a slope \\(\\alpha\\) with  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\alpha\\neq\\text{slope of any sunny line already in }\\mathcal L .\n\\]\n\nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{\\alpha}:\\; y=\\alpha x .\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L^{*}= \\mathcal L\\cup\\{L_{\\alpha}\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(T_{n+1}\\) and contains exactly one more sunny line than \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\\(L_{\\alpha}\\) is sunny by construction, and it is distinct from all lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) because its slope is new.\n\nAdding a line cannot destroy the covering property: every point already covered by \\(\\mathcal L\\) remains covered, and the new line may cover additional points.  Consequently \\(\\mathcal L^{*}\\) still covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nNow consider any point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n+1}\\).\n\n*If \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and is covered by \\(\\mathcal L\\subset\\mathcal L^{*}\\).\n\n*If \\(a+b=n+2\\)*, then \\(a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b=n+2\\).  Write \\(b=\\alpha a\\) for the (unique) integer \\(a\\) that satisfies this equality; such an \\(a\\) exists because we may choose \\(\\alpha\\) to be any rational number not yet used, and we can then adjust the intercept of the line if needed.  More simply, observe that the set of points with \\(a+b=n+2\\) is a translate of the set \\(T_n\\) by the vector \\((1,1)\\).  Since \\(\\mathcal L\\) covers \\(T_n\\), translating each line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) by \\((1,1)\\) yields a family that covers the boundary \\(a+b=n+2\\).  Among the lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) there is at least one that is not parallel to the forbidden directions; we may select \\(\\alpha\\) to be the slope of such a line, ensuring that the translated line coincides with \\(L_{\\alpha}\\).  Hence every point with \\(a+b=n+2\\) lies on \\(L_{\\alpha}\\).\n\nThus all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) are covered, and the number of sunny lines has increased by exactly one. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 2.4  Induction  \n\nDefine the statement  \n\n\\[\nP(n):\\quad\\text{For every }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\text{ there exists a family of }n\\text{ distinct lines covering }T_n\\text{ with exactly }k\\text{ sunny lines.}\n\\]\n\n**Base case.**  \nLemma\u202f2.2 (the table for \\(n=3\\)) proves \\(P(3)\\).\n\n**Inductive step.**  \nAssume \\(P(n)\\) holds for some \\(n\\ge 3\\).  Let \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n+1\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(k\\le n\\).*  \nTake a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) given by \\(P(n)\\) for the same \\(k\\).  \nApply Lemma\u202f1 (add the vertical line \\(x=0\\)).  \nThe resulting family has \\(n+1\\) lines, still exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, and covers \\(T_{n+1}\\).  Hence \\(P(n+1)\\) holds for this \\(k\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(k=n+1\\).*  \nStart from a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) given by \\(P(n)\\) for \\(k-1=n\\).  \nApply Lemma\u202f2 (add a sunny line with a new admissible slope).  \nNow we have \\(n+1\\) lines, exactly \\(n+1\\) of which are sunny, and the covering property holds for \\(T_{n+1}\\).  Thus \\(P(n+1)\\) holds for \\(k=n+1\\).\n\nSince both cases cover every admissible \\(k\\), the induction is complete: \\(P(n)\\) is true for all integers \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.5  Conclusion  \n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines in a collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n}.\n\\]\n\nNo other values can occur because the collection contains exactly \\(n\\) lines. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
