            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1 Number of points to be covered\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the equation \\(a+b=s\\) has\n\\(s-1\\) positive integer solutions.  Hence  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=1+2+\\dots +n=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2 Non\u2011sunny lines\n\nA line is *non\u2011sunny* iff it is parallel to one of the three directions\n\\(x\\)-axis, \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n*Horizontal line* \\(y=c\\) (\\(c\\ge 1\\)).  \nPoints of \\(S_n\\) on it are \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); there are\n\\(n+1-c\\le n\\) of them.\n\n*Vertical line* \\(x=c\\) (\\(c\\ge 1\\)).  \nSymmetrically it contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Diagonal line* \\(x+y=c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)).  \nIts points in \\(S_n\\) are the solutions of \\(a+b=c\\); there are \\(c-1\\le n\\).\n\nThus **any** non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\). \\tag{2}\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3 Sunny lines\n\nA *sunny* line is not parallel to any of the three prohibited directions.\nConsequently it meets each horizontal line, each vertical line and each line\n\\(x+y=c\\) in at most one point.  Therefore a sunny line can introduce at most\nthree points of \\(S_n\\) that are not already covered by the collection of\nnon\u2011sunny lines. \\tag{3}\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4 Necessary lower bound for \\(k\\)\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\), exactly\n\\(k\\) of them being sunny.  The remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non\u2011sunny.\n\nFrom (2) the non\u2011sunny lines together cover at most \\((n-k)n\\) points.\nFrom (3) the sunny lines can add at most \\(3k\\) further points.  Hence\n\n\\[\n|S_n|\\le (n-k)n+3k .\n\\]\n\nInsert (1) and rearrange:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le n^{2}-nk+3k\n\\Longrightarrow\nk\\ge\\frac{n-2}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is integral,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must contain at least\n\\(\\lceil (n-2)/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5 Construction attaining the bound\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nm=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\\qquad\nk_0=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\n#### 2.5.1 The non\u2011sunny part\n\nTake the \\(m\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=m\n\\]\n\nand the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m .\n\\]\n\nThese \\(2m\\) lines are non\u2011sunny.  Any point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with\n\\(\\min\\{a,b\\}\\le m\\) lies on at least one of them.  Hence the only points of\n\\(S_n\\) possibly uncovered are those with \\(a,b>m\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n#### 2.5.2 The uncovered points\n\n*If \\(n\\) is even*, write \\(n=2m\\).  Then the condition \\(a,b>m\\) forces\n\\(a+b>2m=n\\), contradicting \\(a+b\\le n+1=2m+1\\).  Hence **no** point of\n\\(S_n\\) remains uncovered; the \\(2m=n\\) non\u2011sunny lines already cover all of\n\\(S_n\\).\n\n*If \\(n\\) is odd*, write \\(n=2m+1\\).  The uncovered points are exactly  \n\n\\[\n(m+1,m+1),\\qquad (m+1,m+2),\\qquad (m+2,m+1).\n\\]\n\nThey are three distinct points, not collinear.\n\n#### 2.5.3 Adding sunny lines\n\n- **Even \\(n\\).**  \n  Here \\(k_0=m-1\\).  Replace any one of the \\(2m\\) non\u2011sunny lines by a\n  sunny line (for instance the line through \\((1,1)\\) of slope \\(2\\)).  The\n  resulting family contains exactly \\(k_0\\) sunny lines and still covers all\n  points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n- **Odd \\(n\\).**  \n  The three uncovered points are covered by three distinct sunny lines,\n  e.g.\n\n  \\[\n  \\ell_1:\\; y=m+1,\\qquad\n  \\ell_2:\\; y=m+2,\\qquad\n  \\ell_3:\\; x=m+2,\n  \\]\n\n  each of which is not parallel to the prohibited directions.  Together with\n  the \\(2m=n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines we obtain a family of \\(n\\) lines containing\n  exactly  \n\n  \\[\n  k_0=m+1=\\frac{n-1}{2}=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n  \\]\n\n  sunny lines.\n\nThus for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have exhibited a configuration with precisely\n\\(k_0=\\lceil (n-2)/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines that satisfies the covering\ncondition.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6 Completing the set of admissible \\(k\\)\n\nIf a configuration with \\(k_0\\) sunny lines exists, adding any further sunny\nlines (up to a total of \\(n\\) lines) does not destroy the covering property,\nbecause all points of \\(S_n\\) are already covered.  Hence for every integer\n\\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\nk_0\\le k\\le n\n\\]\n\nthere exists a suitable family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\)\nsunny lines.\n\nCombined with the necessary bound (4), this shows that the admissible values\nof \\(k\\) are precisely those described in the statement of the theorem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Theorem.**  \nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for\nwhich there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with\n\\(a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\Bigl\\{\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil,\\;\n               \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+1,\\dots ,n\\Bigr\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
