n   The remaining vertical lines \\(V_{k+1},\\dots ,V_n\\) cover all points whose \\(x\\)-coordinate is larger than \\(k\\).  \n\n   Hence **every** lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is covered:\n   * if \\(a\\le k\\) it lies on the sunny line \\(S_a\\);\n   * if \\(a>k\\) it lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\n   The family consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\) and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny vertical lines, all distinct.  \n\n3. **Conclusion** \u2013 the construction works for every \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\); together with the upper bound this yields the desired set of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Lower bound on the number of lines)\n\nThe points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,n+1-i)\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\nare pairwise non\u2011collinear. Consequently any family of lines that covers all points of \\(S_n\\) must contain at least \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf two distinct indices \\(i\\neq j\\) satisfied \\(P_i,P_j\\) on a common line, that line would have slope  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n+1-j)-(n+1-i)}{j-i}= -1,\n\\]\n\ni.e. it would be parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\). But the two points are not collinear with any third point of \\(S_n\\); in particular the line through \\(P_i\\) and \\(P_j\\) does not contain any other point of \\(S_n\\). Hence a single line can cover at most one of the \\(P_i\\)\u2019s, and at least \\(n\\) lines are necessary. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Construction for any \\(k\\le n\\))\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) covers all points of \\(S_n\\);  \n* exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) are sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nDefine two families of lines.\n\n1. **Vertical lines** (non\u2011sunny)  \n\n   \\[\n   V_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=k+1,\\dots ,n).\n   \\]\n\n2. **Sunny lines** for the first \\(k\\) columns.  \n   For each \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   S_j:\\; y = -\\frac{n}{j}\\,x + (n+1).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(-\\dfrac{n}{j}\\) is finite, non\u2011zero and different from \\(-1\\) because \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(1\\le j\\le k\\le n\\); therefore each \\(S_j\\) is sunny.  \n   Substituting \\(x=j\\) into (1) gives \\(y=n+1-j\\); thus \\(S_j\\) passes through the point \\(P_j=(j,n+1-j)\\).  \n   Substituting \\(x=j\\) and \\(y=1\\) also satisfies (1), so \\(S_j\\) contains the point \\((j,1)\\).  \n   Because the equation of \\(S_j\\) is linear, it contains **every** point \\((j,b)\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\le b\\le n+1-j .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\nNow set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\cup\\{V_{k+1},\\dots ,V_n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe lines are distinct: the vertical lines have infinite slope, whereas each \\(S_j\\) has a finite slope; among the \\(S_j\\) the slopes \\(-\\dfrac{n}{j}\\) are different for different \\(j\\).\n\nWe verify that every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n*Case 1.* \\(a\\le k\\).  \nThen \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) implies \\(b\\le n+1-a\\). By (2) the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the sunny line \\(S_a\\).\n\n*Case 2.* \\(a>k\\).  \nThe vertical line \\(V_a\\) is defined for all such \\(a\\); clearly \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(x=a\\) and therefore lies on \\(V_a\\).\n\nThus all points of \\(S_n\\) are covered.  \nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are sunny, while the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are vertical and hence non\u2011sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Impossibility of \\(k>n\\))\n\nIf a configuration satisfies the covering condition, then the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 at least \\(n\\) distinct lines are needed. Since the total number of lines in the configuration is exactly \\(n\\) (the problem asks for \u201c\\(n\\) distinct lines\u201d), the number of sunny lines is at most \\(n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two requirements of the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLemma\u202f3 shows that no \\(k>n\\) is possible.  \nLemma\u202f2 provides, for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), an explicit configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(S_n\\).  \nHence the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely the integers from \\(0\\) to \\(n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus the answer is \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).**"
