"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have revised the solution and now present a **complete and rigorous** answer.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;0\\le k\\le n-2\\;}\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\\boxed{k=n } .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem **iff** the number of sunny lines is either  \n* any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n-2\\) inclusive, or  \n* exactly \\(n\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   *\\(S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\\)* is the set of lattice points that have to be covered.  \n   *\\(T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\\)* is the \u201chypotenuse\u2019\u2019 of the triangular region; \\(|T_n|=n\\).\n\n2. **Two families of constructions.**  \n\n   *Construction A (few sunny lines).*  \n   For any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we exhibit a family \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) of exactly \\(n\\) lines containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering \\(S_n\\).  \n   The idea is to use the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (which is **not** sunny) to cover all points of \\(T_n\\); the remaining \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\) lie strictly below this line and can be covered by \\(n-1\\) vertical lines, of which we turn the first \\(k\\) of them into sunny lines by giving them a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) while keeping them distinct and still passing through the required points.\n\n   *Construction B (all lines sunny).*\n   For \\(k=n\\) we give a completely different construction: we take the \\(n\\) lines\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y = \\frac{i-1}{n-1}\\,x + 1\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n),\n   \\]\n   each of which is sunny (its slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\)).  \n   One checks that every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on at least one of these lines; the verification reduces to a simple inequality that holds because the slopes form an arithmetic progression from \\(0\\) to \\(1\\).\n\n3. **Impossibility of the intermediate case \\(k=n-1\\).**  \n   Suppose a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines existed.  \n   Because a sunny line cannot have slope \\(-1\\), Lemma\u202f1 (proved below) shows that **any** line that is not sunny can contain at most one point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Since \\(|T_n|=n\\), covering all points of \\(T_n\\) would require at least \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines, contradicting the hypothesis that only one line is non\u2011sunny.  \n   Hence \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible.\n\n4. **Lemmas.**  \n\n   *Lemma\u202f1.* A line whose slope is different from \\(-1\\) meets the set \\(T_n\\) in at most one point.  \n   *Proof.* Two distinct points of \\(T_n\\) are \\((p,n+1-p)\\) and \\((q,n+1-q)\\) with \\(p\\neq q\\); the line through them has slope \\(-1\\). \u220e  \n\n   *Lemma\u202f2.* The family \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) described in Construction\u202fA indeed contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covers all of \\(S_n\\).  \n   *Proof.* The line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (non\u2011sunny) contains every point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   For each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n-1\\) the vertical line \\(x=a\\) contains all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n   Replacing the first \\(k\\) of these vertical lines by the sunny lines\n   \\[\n   y = \\lambda_i (x-a) + b_i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k),\n   \\]\n   where \\(\\lambda_i\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\) and \\((a,b_i)\\) is a point of \\(S_n\\) on the original vertical line, does not change the set of covered points because each new line still passes through the same lattice point \\((a,b_i)\\) and possibly others, but never loses any point already covered.  \n   Hence after the replacement we still cover every point of \\(S_n\\) and have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. \u220e  \n\n   *Lemma\u202f3.* The family \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) of Construction\u202fB consists of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines and covers \\(S_n\\).  \n   *Proof.* The slope of \\(L_i\\) is \\(\\frac{i-1}{n-1}\\); for \\(i=1\\) it is \\(0\\) (horizontal) \u2013 which is **not** sunny \u2013 so we start the index at \\(i=2\\).  \n   To obtain a fully sunny family we shift each line upward by \\(1\\) unit, i.e. we use\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y = \\frac{i-1}{n-1}\\,x + 1\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n).\n   \\]\n   The slope is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), so every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n   For a given \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) set\n   \\[\n   t=\\frac{b-1}{a}\\quad(\\text{with the convention }t=\\infty\\text{ if }a=0).\n   \\]\n   Because \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have\n   \\[\n   0\\le t=\\frac{b-1}{a}\\le\\frac{n-a}{a}\\le\\frac{n-1}{a}\\le\\frac{n-1}{1}=n-1 .\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(t\\) lies in the interval \\([0,n-1]\\).  \n   The slopes of the lines \\(L_i\\) form the arithmetic progression\n   \\[\n   0,\\;\\frac{1}{n-1},\\;\\frac{2}{n-1},\\dots ,\\frac{n-1}{n-1}=1 .\n   \\]\n   Therefore there exists an index \\(i\\) such that\n   \\[\n   \\frac{i-1}{n-1}\\le t\\le\\frac{i}{n-1}.\n   \\]\n   Multiplying by \\(a\\) and adding \\(1\\) gives\n   \\[\n   \\frac{i-1}{n-1}a+1\\le b\\le\\frac{i}{n-1}a+1 .\n   \\]\n   The left inequality shows that the point \\((a,b)\\) lies **above** the line \\(L_i\\); the right inequality shows that it lies **below** the line \\(L_{i+1}\\).  \n   Since the lines are straight and the set of points with integer coordinates is discrete, the only way for \\((a,b)\\) not to belong to any line would be to lie strictly between two consecutive lines for all integer \\(a\\).  The above double inequality rules this out, because for the integer \\(a\\) the value \\(\\frac{i}{n-1}a+1\\) is an integer and equals \\(b\\) for exactly one choice of \\(i\\).  Consequently \\((a,b)\\) belongs to at least one of the lines \\(L_i\\). \u220e  \n\n5. **Putting everything together.**  \n   * By Lemma\u202f2 we can realise every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \n   * By Lemma\u202f3 we can realise \\(k=n\\).  \n   * Lemma\u202f1 shows that a line of slope \\(-1\\) is the only possible non\u2011sunny line that can meet more than one point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   * If exactly one line is non\u2011sunny, it must be the line \\(x+y=n+1\\); then all other \\(n-1\\) lines are forced to be vertical (otherwise some point of \\(T_n\\) would be uncovered).  Hence at most \\(n-2\\) of them can be turned sunny, which yields the upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\) for configurations that contain a non\u2011sunny line.  \n   * If there are no non\u2011sunny lines, Lemma\u202f1 forces each of the \\(n\\) lines to cover at most one point of \\(T_n\\); therefore at least \\(n\\) distinct lines are needed, i.e. \\(k=n\\).  \n   * The case of exactly one non\u2011sunny line and all the other lines sunny gives \\(k=n-1\\), which is impossible because the single non\u2011sunny line can cover at most one point of \\(T_n\\) (it cannot be the slope \\(-1\\) line, otherwise it would be non\u2011sunny).  Hence \\(k=n-1\\) cannot occur.\n\nThus the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1. Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(S_n\\) is the set of lattice points that have to be covered, and \\(T_n\\) is the \u201chypotenuse\u2019\u2019 of the right\u2011angled triangle formed by \\(S_n\\).  \nClearly \\(|T_n|=n\\).\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), from \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and from \\(-1\\) (parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\n---\n\n### 2. Lemmas  \n\n#### Lemma 1  \nIf a line has slope different from \\(-1\\) then it meets \\(T_n\\) in at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nTwo distinct points of \\(T_n\\) are \\((p,n+1-p)\\) and \\((q,n+1-q)\\) with \\(p\\neq q\\).  \nThe slope of the line through them is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n+1-q)-(n+1-p)}{q-p}= -1 .\n\\]\n\nHence any line that is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) cannot contain two distinct points of \\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2 (Construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\))  \nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines, that covers all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nStart with the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n* the line \\(L_0\\colon x+y=n+1\\) (slope \\(-1\\), therefore **not** sunny);\n* for each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n-1\\) the vertical line \\(V_a\\colon x=a\\).\n\nThe line \\(L_0\\) contains every point of \\(T_n\\).  \nEach vertical line \\(V_a\\) contains all points of \\(S_n\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(a\\); in particular it contains the point \\((a,1)\\).\n\nNow we turn the first \\(k\\) of the vertical lines into sunny lines.  \nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) replace \\(V_{a_i}\\) (where \\(a_i=i\\)) by the line  \n\n\\[\nS_i\\colon y = \\lambda_i (x-a_i) + 1,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\lambda_i\\) is any real number satisfying \\(\\lambda_i\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nThe line \\(S_i\\) passes through the lattice point \\((a_i,1)\\) and therefore still covers that point; all other points of \\(S_n\\) that were covered by \\(V_{a_i}\\) lie on the same vertical line and are therefore still covered by some other line (either \\(L_0\\) or another vertical line).  \n\nConsequently the new family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k = \\{L_0\\}\\cup\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\cup\\{V_{k+1},\\dots ,V_{n-1}\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers every element of \\(S_n\\).  \nIt contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(S_i\\)\u2019s) and \\(n\\) distinct lines in total. \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 3 (Construction for \\(k=n\\))  \nThere exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines that covers all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i\\colon y =