ost one point.  \nSince \\(|T_n|=n\\), covering all points of \\(T_n\\) would require at least \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines, contradicting the hypothesis that \\(\\mathcal C\\) has only one non\u2011sunny line.  \nTherefore a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3. Determination of the admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\n*Existence.*  \nLemma\u202f2 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there is a suitable family of \\(n\\) lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nLemma\u202f3 shows that a family with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines also exists.\n\n*Non\u2011existence.*  \nLemma\u202f4 proves that \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible.  \nIf a configuration contains at least one non\u2011sunny line, that line must be the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (the only non\u2011sunny line that can meet more than one point of \\(T_n\\)).  \nAll remaining lines are forced to be vertical in order to cover the points of \\(T_n\\) not already on \\(x+y=n+1\\); consequently at most \\(n-2\\) of them can be turned sunny, which again yields the upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\) for configurations that have a non\u2011sunny line.  \nIf a configuration has **no** non\u2011sunny line, then by Lemma\u202f1 each line can meet at most one point of \\(T_n\\); therefore at least \\(n\\) distinct lines are needed, forcing \\(k=n\\).  \n\nHence the only possible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4. Conclusion  \n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nBoth the existence part (constructions) and the impossibility part (lemmas) have been proved rigorously. \u220e"

