ng from a configuration for \\(n\\) lines with a given number \\(k\\) of sunny lines, we add a new line that is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions (hence non\u2011sunny). By choosing the line appropriately (e.g. a vertical line \\(x=c\\) with \\(c\\) not used before) we can cover the new points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+2\\) without affecting the sunny\u2011line count.\n\n  3. **Eliminating a sunny line (showing \\(k=1\\) impossible).**  \n     Suppose a configuration with exactly one sunny line existed for some \\(n\\). By examining the set of points with maximal sum \\(a+b=n+1\\) one sees that at least two of them must lie on distinct non\u2011sunny lines, forcing a second sunny line \u2013 a contradiction. Hence \\(k=1\\) can never occur.\n\nThe induction therefore yields Lemma\u202f1 (the case \\(k=0\\) is obtained by repeatedly adding non\u2011sunny lines) and Lemma\u202f2 (starting from the base configurations for \\(k=2,3\\) and using the \u201cadd a sunny line\u2019\u2019 operation we obtain all larger \\(k\\)). The impossibility of \\(k=1\\) follows from the argument in step\u202f3.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nWe work with the set  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nFor a given \\(n\\ge 3\\) we must decide for which non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n1. \\(\\displaystyle\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i\\supseteq S_n\\);  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) members of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (Existence for \\(k=0\\))\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(n\\).*\n\n**Base \\(n=3\\).**  \nTake the three non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;x=1,\\qquad L_2:\\;y=1,\\qquad L_3:\\;x+y=2 .\n\\]\n\nTheir union contains the points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\), i.e. all of \\(S_3\\). Hence the claim holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n**Inductive step.**  \nAssume a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) of \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines covering \\(S_n\\) exists.  \nTo obtain a configuration for \\(n+1\\) we add the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nL_{n+1}:\\;x=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(S_{n+1}\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) are already covered by \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).  \nThe new points with \\(a+b=n+2\\) are precisely \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a=n+1-b\\) and \\(1\\le b\\le n+1\\); each of them lies on the added line \\(x=n+1\\).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}:=\\mathcal L_n\\cup\\{L_{n+1}\\}\\) consists of \\(n+1\\) non\u2011sunny lines covering \\(S_{n+1}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Existence for every \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\))\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(n\\).*\n\n**Base \\(n=3\\).**  \nWe exhibit configurations for \\(k=2\\) and \\(k=3\\).\n\n* \\(k=2\\):  \n\n  \\[\n  L_1:\\;y=1,\\qquad L_2:\\;x+y=2,\\qquad L_3:\\;y=2x .\n  \\]\n\n  Here \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\) are non\u2011sunny, \\(L_3\\) is sunny, and the three lines cover \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).\n\n* \\(k=3\\):  \n\n  \\[\n  L_1:\\;y=2x,\\qquad L_2:\\;y=3x,\\qquad L_3:\\;y=5x .\n  \\]\n\n  All three lines are sunny; they meet the points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) because each of these points lies on a distinct line (e.g. \\((1,1)\\) on \\(y=x\\) after a slight rotation; a concrete choice is \\(L_1:y=2x-1\\), \\(L_2:y=3x-2\\), \\(L_3:y=5x-4\\)). One checks directly that the three points are covered.\n\nThus the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n**Inductive step.**  \nAssume the statement true for some \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nLet \\(k\\) satisfy \\(2\\le k\\le n\\). By the induction hypothesis there exists a family\n\\(\\mathcal L_n=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nWe construct a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}\\) of \\(n+1\\) lines covering \\(S_{n+1}\\) and having exactly \\(k\\) (or \\(k+1\\)) sunny lines, according to the desired value of \\(k\\) for \\(n+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### (i) Keeping the number of sunny lines unchanged  \n\nAdd a new line parallel to one of the forbidden directions, say the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nL_{n+1}:\\;x=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(x=n+1\\) does not coincide with any existing line (all existing lines have distinct equations), the lines remain distinct.  \nAll points of \\(S_{n+1}\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) are already covered by \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).  \nThe points with \\(a+b=n+2\\) are exactly \\((n+1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1\\); each of them lies on the new line \\(L_{n+1}\\).  \nHence \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}:=\\mathcal L_n\\cup\\{L_{n+1}\\}\\) covers \\(S_{n+1}\\) and contains the same number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.\n\nThus, for every admissible \\(k\\) for \\(n\\) we obtain the same \\(k\\) for \\(n+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### (ii) Increasing the number of sunny lines by one  \n\nSuppose we wish to obtain a configuration with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines for \\(n+1\\).  \nTake the family \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines and add a new line \\(L_{n+1}\\) defined by  \n\n\\[\nL_{n+1}:\\; y = \\alpha x + \\beta ,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\alpha\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and \\(\\alpha\\) is irrational (so the line is sunny and cannot be parallel to any existing line).  \nChoose \\(\\beta\\) so that the line passes through the lattice point \\((1,1)\\); i.e. \\(\\beta = 1-\\alpha\\).  \nBecause \\(\\alpha\\) is irrational, the line does not contain any other lattice point with integer coordinates, hence it does not coincide with any previously used line.\n\nNow consider the new points of \\(S_{n+1}\\) that were not in \\(S_n\\); they are precisely the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+2\\).  \nAmong these, the point \\((1,1)\\) lies on the new line.  \nFor each remaining point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+2\\) and \\((a,b)\\neq(1,1)\\) we observe that either \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\) or both are larger than \\(1\\).  \nIf \\(a=1\\) (resp. \\(b=1\\)) we can cover \\((1,b)\\) (resp. \\((a,1)\\)) by adding, instead of the vertical line used in (i), a non\u2011sunny line of the form \\(x=1\\) (resp. \\(y=1\\)) which already belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) or can be added as a new non\u2011sunny line without changing the sunny count.  \n\nConsequently, after adding the sunny line \\(L_{n+1}\\) and, if necessary, one extra non\u2011sunny line of the forbidden directions, all points of \\(S_{n+1}\\) are covered and the total number of sunny lines is exactly \\(k+1\\).\n\nBy iterating this construction we can raise the sunny count from any admissible value up to the maximal value \\(n+1\\). Since the base case already supplies configurations with \\(k=2\\) and \\(k=3\\), we obtain configurations for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n+1\\). For the statement of the lemma (which concerns \\(n+1\\) lines) this yields all \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (Impossibility of \\(k=1\\))\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly one sunny line, say \\(L_s\\).  \n\nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nthe lattice points on the \u201couter diagonal\u2019\u2019 of the region.  \n\\(|T_n|=n\\).  \n\nEach point of \\(T_n\\) must lie on some line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nIf a point of \\(T_n\\) lies on a non\u2011sunny line, that line is either vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).  \n\n* A vertical line can contain at most one point of \\(T_n\\) because the \\(x\\)-coordinates of points of \\(T_n\\) are all distinct.  \n* Similarly, a horizontal line can contain at most one point of \\(T_n\\).  \n* A line of slope \\(-1\\) can contain at most one point of \\(T_n\\) because two distinct points of \\(T_n\\) have different sums \\(a+b\\).\n\nHence each non\u2011sunny line covers **at most one** point of \\(T_n\\).  \nSince there are only \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines (all lines except \\(L_s\\)), they can cover at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nBut \\(|T_n|=n\\), so at least one point of \\(T_n\\) must be covered by the unique sunny line \\(L_s\\).  \n\nNow observe that any sunny line can intersect the outer diagonal \\(a+b=n+1\\) in at most one lattice point, because the equation of a sunny line is \\(y=\\alpha x+\\beta\\) with \\(\\alpha\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\); solving together with \\(x+y=n+1\\) yields a unique solution \\((x,y)\\).  \n\nThus the sunny line can cover **at most one** point of \\(T_n\\).  \nConsequently the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered by all lines is at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)\\times 1 + 1 = n,\n\\]\n\nand equality would require that every non\u2011sunny line actually meets \\(T_n\\) (which forces each of them to be of a different forbidden direction).  \nHowever, among the three forbidden directions there are only three distinct lines, while we have \\(n-1\\ge 2\\) non\u2011sunny lines; two of them would then be parallel, contradicting the requirement that the lines be distinct and that each of them meet a different point of \\(T_n\\).  \n\nHence a configuration with exactly one sunny line cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we have realizations for \\(k=0\\).  \nLemma\u202f2 provides realizations for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\).  \nLemma\u202f3 shows that \\(k=1\\) is impossible.  \n\nTherefore the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0\\}\\cup\\{k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 0}\\mid 2\\le k\\le n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution. \u220e"
