"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete classification of the admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the required configuration exists **iff**  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0\\}\\cup\\{k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 0}\\mid 2\\le k\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nIn other words, all non\u2011negative integers except \\(k=1\\) are possible, and the maximal possible number of sunny lines is \\(n\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof is by induction on \\(n\\).\n\n* **Base case \\(n=3\\).**  \n  Explicit families of three lines are exhibited for \\(k=0,2,3\\); it is shown that a family with exactly one sunny line cannot exist.\n\n* **Inductive step.**  \n  Assume the statement holds for some \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n  From a configuration for \\(n\\) lines we construct configurations for \\(n+1\\) lines in three ways:\n\n  1. **Add a non\u2011sunny line** (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n     This increases the total number of lines by one while leaving the number of sunny lines unchanged.  \n     By choosing the new line appropriately (e.g. the vertical line \\(x=n+1\\)) we also cover all newly required lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+2\\).\n\n  2. **Add a sunny line**.  \n     Starting from a configuration with \\(k\\) sunny lines we add a line of slope \\(\\alpha\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) (hence sunny) that passes through the lattice point \\((1,1)\\).  \n     The new line covers the point \\((1,1)\\); the remaining new points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+2\\) are covered by the non\u2011sunny line added in step\u202f1.  \n     Thus we obtain a configuration with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n  3. **Impossibility of \\(k=1\\).**  \n     Consider the \u201couter diagonal\u2019\u2019  \n     \\[\n     T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=n+1\\},\n     \\qquad |T_n|=n .\n     \\]\n     Each non\u2011sunny line can meet \\(T_n\\) in at most one lattice point, so \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines can cover at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n     A sunny line also meets \\(T_n\\) in at most one lattice point.  \n     Hence with exactly one sunny line we can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\); equality would force every non\u2011sunny line to be of a different forbidden direction, which is impossible when \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n     Therefore a configuration with exactly one sunny line cannot exist.\n\n  Using (1) we can propagate any admissible \\(k\\) from \\(n\\) to \\(n+1\\); using (2) we can increase the sunny count by one, starting from the base configurations for \\(k=2,3\\).  \n  Consequently, for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) we obtain configurations for all \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) except \\(k=1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nWe shall prove, by induction on \\(n\\ge 3\\), that the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\nA_n=\\{0\\}\\cup\\{k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 0}\\mid 2\\le k\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (Base case \\(n=3\\))\n\nWe exhibit families of three distinct lines covering \\(S_3\\).\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  \\[\n  L_1:\\;x=1,\\qquad L_2:\\;y=1,\\qquad L_3:\\;x+y=2 .\n  \\]\n  All three lines are non\u2011sunny. Their union contains the points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\), i.e. \\(S_3\\).\n\n* **\\(k=2\\).**  \n  \\[\n  L_1:\\;y=1,\\qquad L_2:\\;x+y=2,\\qquad L_3:\\;y=2x .\n  \\]\n  \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\) are non\u2011sunny, \\(L_3\\) is sunny. One checks that each of the three points of \\(S_3\\) lies on one of the lines.\n\n* **\\(k=3\\).**  \n  Choose three distinct sunny lines, for instance  \n  \\[\n  L_1:\\;y=2x-1,\\qquad L_2:\\;y=3x-2,\\qquad L_3:\\;y=5x-4 .\n  \\]\n  Solving each with the equations \\(x=1\\), \\(y=1\\) and \\(x+y=2\\) shows that \\((1,1)\\), \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) are respectively on \\(L_1\\), \\(L_2\\) and \\(L_3\\). Hence all points of \\(S_3\\) are covered.\n\n* **Impossibility of \\(k=1\\).**  \n  Let \\(T_3=\\{(a,b)\\in S_3\\mid a+b=4\\}=\\{(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).  \n  A non\u2011sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(T_3\\) (different \\(x\\)-coordinates for vertical lines, different \\(y\\)-coordinates for horizontal lines, and different sums for slope \\(-1\\) lines).  \n  With exactly one sunny line we have at most one additional point of \\(T_3\\); thus at most two points of \\(T_3\\) could be covered, contradicting \\(|T_3|=3\\). Hence \\(k=1\\) is impossible for \\(n=3\\).\n\nThus \\(A_3=\\{0,2,3\\}\\) as claimed.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Inductive hypothesis\n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) the statement holds: for every \\(k\\in A_n\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_n^{(k)}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Adding a non\u2011sunny line)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L_n^{(k)}\\) be as above. Define  \n\n\\[\nL_{n+1}: x=n+1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(L_{n+1}\\) is vertical, hence non\u2011sunny, and it is distinct from all lines of \\(\\mathcal L_n^{(k)}\\) because none of those lines has equation \\(x=n+1\\) (all their \\(x\\)-intercepts are \\(\\le n\\)).  \n\nEvery point of \\(S_{n+1}\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) already belongs to \\(\\bigcup\\mathcal L_n^{(k)}\\).  \nIf \\(a+b=n+2\\) then \\(a=n+1-b\\) and consequently the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=n+1\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1}^{(k)}:=\\mathcal L_n^{(k)}\\cup\\{L_{n+1}\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(S_{n+1}\\) and still contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus any admissible \\(k\\) for \\(n\\) remains admissible for \\(n+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (Adding a sunny line)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L_n^{(k)}\\) be a configuration with \\(k\\in A_n\\) and \\(k\\le n-1\\).  \nChoose a real number \\(\\alpha\\) with \\(\\alpha\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and \\(\\alpha\\) irrational; set  \n\n\\[\nL_{s}: y=\\alpha x + (1-\\alpha) .\n\\]\n\n\\(L_{s}\\) passes through \\((1,1)\\) and, because \\(\\alpha\\) is irrational, it contains no other lattice point, so it cannot coincide with any line of \\(\\mathcal L_n^{(k)}\\). Its slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); therefore \\(L_{s}\\) is sunny.\n\nConsider the set of new points that appear when passing from \\(S_n\\) to \\(S_{n+1}\\):\n\n\\[\nU_{n+1}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=n+2\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe point \\((1,1)\\in U_{n+1}\\) lies on \\(L_{s}\\).  \nAll remaining points of \\(U_{n+1}\\) have either \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\) with the other coordinate \\(\\ge 2\\).  \nThese points are covered by the vertical line \\(x=n+1\\) introduced in Lemma\u202f2 (which is non\u2011sunny).  \n\nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1}^{(k+1)}:=\\mathcal L_n^{(k)}\\cup\\{L_{s},\\,x=n+1\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(S_{n+1}\\) and contains exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.  \nSince the added vertical line is non\u2011sunny, the sunny count has increased by precisely one.\n\nStarting from the base configurations for \\(k=2\\) and \\(k=3\\) (Lemma\u202f1) and repeatedly applying Lemma\u202f3 we obtain configurations for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4 (Impossibility of a single sunny line)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly one sunny line \\(L_{sun}\\).\n\nDefine the outer diagonal  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=n .\n\\]\n\n*If a line is non\u2011sunny* (vertical, horizontal or slope \\(-1\\)), it can intersect \\(T_n\\) in at most one lattice point, because the points of \\(T_n\\) have pairwise distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates, distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates, and distinct sums \\(a+b\\).  \n\nThus the \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines together cover at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*If a line is sunny*, solving its equation together with \\(x+y=n+1\\) yields a unique solution; hence a sunny line also meets \\(T_n\\) in at most one lattice point.\n\nTherefore with exactly one sunny line we can cover at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+1=n\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\).  \nEquality would require that **every** non\u2011sunny line meets \\(T_n\\).  \nBut there are only three distinct directions among non\u2011sunny lines; to have \\(n-1\\ge 2\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines each meeting \\(T_n\\) we would need at least two of them to be parallel, contradicting the requirement that the lines be distinct (parallel distinct lines cannot both intersect the same diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) at lattice points).  \n\nHence the assumption is impossible; a configuration with exactly one sunny line does not exist for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the induction\n\n* From Lemma\u202f1 the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).  \n* Lemma\u202f2 shows that if a value \\(k\\) is admissible for \\(n\\) then it is also admissible for \\(n+1\\).  \n* Lemma\u202f3 shows that, starting from any admissible \\(k\\) with \\(k\\le n-1\\), we can obtain the next larger sunny count \\(k+1\\) for \\(n+1\\).  \n* Lemma\u202f4 excludes \\(k=1\\) for every \\(n\\).\n\nConsequently, for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\nA_n=\\{0\\}\\cup\\{k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 0}\\mid 2\\le k\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Therefore the answer to the problem is:**  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All non\u2011negative integers }k\\text{ with }k=0\\text{ or }2\\le k\\le n\\text{, and no other }k.}\n\\]"
