02f3 (Adding a sunny line)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L_n^{(k)}\\) be a configuration with \\(k\\in A_n\\) and \\(k\\le n-1\\).  \nChoose a real number \\(\\alpha\\) with \\(\\alpha\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and \\(\\alpha\\) irrational; set  \n\n\\[\nL_{s}: y=\\alpha x + (1-\\alpha) .\n\\]\n\n\\(L_{s}\\) passes through \\((1,1)\\) and, because \\(\\alpha\\) is irrational, it contains no other lattice point, so it cannot coincide with any line of \\(\\mathcal L_n^{(k)}\\). Its slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); therefore \\(L_{s}\\) is sunny.\n\nConsider the set of new points that appear when passing from \\(S_n\\) to \\(S_{n+1}\\):\n\n\\[\nU_{n+1}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=n+2\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe point \\((1,1)\\in U_{n+1}\\) lies on \\(L_{s}\\).  \nAll remaining points of \\(U_{n+1}\\) have either \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\) with the other coordinate \\(\\ge 2\\).  \nThese points are covered by the vertical line \\(x=n+1\\) introduced in Lemma\u202f2 (which is non\u2011sunny).  \n\nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1}^{(k+1)}:=\\mathcal L_n^{(k)}\\cup\\{L_{s},\\,x=n+1\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(S_{n+1}\\) and contains exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.  \nSince the added vertical line is non\u2011sunny, the sunny count has increased by precisely one.\n\nStarting from the base configurations for \\(k=2\\) and \\(k=3\\) (Lemma\u202f1) and repeatedly applying Lemma\u202f3 we obtain configurations for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4 (Impossibility of a single sunny line)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly one sunny line \\(L_{sun}\\).\n\nDefine the outer diagonal  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=n .\n\\]\n\n*If a line is non\u2011sunny* (vertical, horizontal or slope \\(-1\\)), it can intersect \\(T_n\\) in at most one lattice point, because the points of \\(T_n\\) have pairwise distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates, distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates, and distinct sums \\(a+b\\).  \n\nThus the \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines together cover at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*If a line is sunny*, solving its equation together with \\(x+y=n+1\\) yields a unique solution; hence a sunny line also meets \\(T_n\\) in at most one lattice point.\n\nTherefore with exactly one sunny line we can cover at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+1=n\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\).  \nEquality would require that **every** non\u2011sunny line meets \\(T_n\\).  \nBut there are only three distinct directions among non\u2011sunny lines; to have \\(n-1\\ge 2\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines each meeting \\(T_n\\) we would need at least two of them to be parallel, contradicting the requirement that the lines be distinct (parallel distinct lines cannot both intersect the same diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) at lattice points).  \n\nHence the assumption is impossible; a configuration with exactly one sunny line does not exist for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the induction\n\n* From Lemma\u202f1 the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).  \n* Lemma\u202f2 shows that if a value \\(k\\) is admissible for \\(n\\) then it is also admissible for \\(n+1\\).  \n* Lemma\u202f3 shows that, starting from any admissible \\(k\\) with \\(k\\le n-1\\), we can obtain the next larger sunny count \\(k+1\\) for \\(n+1\\).  \n* Lemma\u202f4 excludes \\(k=1\\) for every \\(n\\).\n\nConsequently, for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\nA_n=\\{0\\}\\cup\\{k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 0}\\mid 2\\le k\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Therefore the answer to the problem is:**  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All non\u2011negative integers }k\\text{ with }k=0\\