=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n   \\]\n   Their slopes are all distinct and none equals \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\);\n   therefore every line is sunny. A direct substitution shows that each point\n   \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on the unique line \\(L_{a}\\) (because \\(b = \\frac{a}{n+1-a}a + a\\)\n   simplifies to \\(a+b=n+1\\), and for points with smaller sum one of the earlier\n   lines works). Hence the covering condition holds.\n\n   *If \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\):* start from the construction for \\(k=n\\) and replace\n   exactly \\(n-k\\) of the sunny lines by three special non\u2011sunny lines:\n   \\[\n   X:\\; y=0,\\qquad Y:\\; x=0,\\qquad D:\\; x+y=0 .\n   \\]\n   These three lines together contain the points \\((1,1), (1,2), (2,1)\\) of\n   \\(S_n\\). The remaining \\(k\\) sunny lines are taken from the family\n   \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) after deleting the indices that correspond to the three\n   points already covered. Because the three non\u2011sunny lines are parallel to the\n   forbidden directions, they are not sunny; the other \\(k\\) lines remain sunny.\n   One checks that every point of \\(S_n\\) is still covered: the points on the\n   three non\u2011sunny lines are covered by them, and any other point \\((a,b)\\) has\n   \\(a+b\\ge 4\\) and therefore lies on one of the retained sunny lines.\n\n   *If \\(k=n-1\\):* suppose a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines existed.\n   Then exactly one line would be non\u2011sunny. As observed in the counting step,\n   a non\u2011sunny line can cover at most one point of \\(S_n\\). The remaining\n   \\(\\frac{(n+1)n}{2}-1\\) points would have to be covered by the \\(n-1\\) sunny\n   lines, each of which can contain at most two points of \\(S_n\\) (because two\n   points with the same sum \\(a+b\\) would force the line to have slope \\(-1\\)).\n   This would require at most \\(2(n-1)\\) points, but\n   \\(\\frac{(n+1)n}{2}-1 > 2(n-1)\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\), a contradiction.\n   Hence \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible.\n\n3. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound forces \\(k\\ge n-2\\); the explicit constructions show that every\n   integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and \\(k\\neq n-1\\) can be realised; the\n   impossibility argument rules out \\(k=n-1\\). Combining these facts yields the\n   exact set of admissible values stated above.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{(n+1)n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Coverage by a non\u2011sunny line)\n\nIf a line \\(L\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line\n\\(x+y=0\\), then \\(L\\) contains at most one point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(L\\) is horizontal, all its points have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate; in\n\\(S_n\\) distinct points have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates, so at most one lies on\n\\(L\\). The same argument works for a vertical line.  \nIf \\(L\\) has slope \\(-1\\), then all its points satisfy \\(x+y=c\\) for a constant\n\\(c\\). In \\(S_n\\) distinct points have distinct values of \\(a+b\\); hence at most\none point of \\(S_n\\) can lie on such a line. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Lower bound on the number of sunny lines)\n\nIn any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\), at least\n\\(n-2\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each non\u2011sunny line can cover at most one point of \\(S_n\\). Since\n\\(|S_n|=\\frac{(n+1)n}{2}\\) and we have only \\(n\\) lines, at most three of the\npoints of \\(S_n\\) can be covered by non\u2011sunny lines (there are only three\npossible non\u2011sunny directions). Consequently at least  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|-3=\\frac{(n+1)n}{2}-3\\ge