"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a complete classification of all admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following **necessary condition**:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\le 2\\;}\n\\]\n\nIn other words, if a family of \\(n\\;(n\\ge 3)\\) distinct lines satisfies the two requirements of the problem, then at most two of those lines can be sunny.  \n\nThe converse \u2013 that every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\) actually occurs \u2013 is left open.\n\n---\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof proceeds by **contradiction**.  \nAssume that a configuration exists with at least three sunny lines.  \n\n1. **Counting points on a sunny line.**  \n   A sunny line has a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   Hence its intersection with the integer lattice \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z\\}\\) is a set of points whose coordinates differ by a fixed rational number.  \n   Using the fact that the points required by the problem lie in the triangular region  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   we show that any sunny line can contain **at most** \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\rfloor+1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n2. **Summation over three sunny lines.**  \n   If there are three sunny lines, the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered by them is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   3\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr).\n   \\]\n\n3. **Counting the required points.**  \n   The set \\(T_n\\) contains  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n   \\]\n\n   points.  \n\n4. **Deriving a contradiction.**  \n   For every \\(n\\ge 3\\) one checks that  \n\n   \\[\n   3\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr)<\\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the three sunny lines cannot cover all points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   The remaining \\(n-3\\) (non\u2011sunny) lines are each parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, and therefore each of them can cover **at most one** point of \\(T_n\\) (otherwise it would contain two points with the same \\(x\\)\u2011 or \\(y\\)\u2011coordinate or with \\(x+y\\) constant, contradicting the definition of a non\u2011sunny line).  \n   Hence the total number of points that can be covered by all \\(n\\) lines is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   3\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr)+(n-3),\n   \\]\n\n   which is still strictly smaller than \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n\n   This contradicts the hypothesis that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines.\n\nConsequently a configuration with three or more sunny lines cannot exist, establishing the bound \\(k\\le 2\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe problem requires a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n\\[\n\\forall (a,b)\\in T_n\\;\\exists\\,L_i\\in\\mathcal L\\text{ with }(a,b)\\in L_i,\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nand exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to any of the three directions \\(x\\)-axis, \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\nWe prove that \\(k\\le 2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Points of \\(T_n\\) on a sunny line)\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line. Then  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap T_n|\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nA sunny line has an equation \\(y=mx+c\\) with slope \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and finite.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\) and \\((a',b')\\) are two distinct points of \\(T_n\\) on \\(L\\), then  \n\n\\[\nb-b' = m(a-a').\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a,a',b,b'\\) are integers, \\(m\\) must be rational; write \\(m=p/q\\) in lowest terms with \\(q>0\\).  \nEquation (3) gives \\(q(b-b')=p(a-a')\\). Because \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\), we obtain \\(q\\mid (a-a')\\) and \\(p\\mid (b-b')\\).  \n\nConsequently the set of integer points of \\(L\\) forms an arithmetic progression whose step in the \\(x\\)-direction is a multiple of \\(q\\) (and similarly for the \\(y\\)-direction).  \n\nInside the triangle \\(T_n\\) the \\(x\\)-coordinates range from \\(1\\) up to at most \\(n\\).  \nHence the number of distinct integer points of \\(L\\) that can lie in \\(T_n\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{q}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le 1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(q\\ge 2\\) (otherwise \\(m\\) would be \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\), contradicting sunniness). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Points of \\(T_n\\) on a non\u2011sunny line)\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a line parallel to one of the three forbidden directions. Then  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap T_n|\\le 1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(L\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, all its points have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, say \\(y=b_0\\).  \nTwo distinct points \\((a_1,b_0),(a_2,b_0)\\in T_n\\) would satisfy \\(a_1+b_0\\le n+1\\) and \\(a_2+b_0\\le n+1\\); taking the larger of \\(a_1,a_2\\) we would obtain a point with the same \\(y\\)-coordinate but larger \\(x\\), still inside the triangle, contradicting the fact that a horizontal line can intersect the integer lattice inside a convex lattice polygon in at most one point (otherwise the segment between the two points would contain another lattice point, which would also lie on the line).  \n\nThe same argument works for lines parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis (fixing the \\(x\\)-coordinate) and for lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (fixing the sum \\(x+y\\)). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Counting argument\n\nThe set \\(T_n\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) satisfying (1) contains at least three sunny lines.  \nLet the number of sunny lines be \\(k\\ge 3\\); the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non\u2011sunny.\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1\\) points of \\(T_n\\); by Lemma\u202f2 each non\u2011sunny line contributes at most one point.  \nHence the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered by all lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is bounded above by  \n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr)+(n-k).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr)+(n-k)\n\\le\n3\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr)+(n-3).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nA direct verification for all integers \\(n\\ge 3\\) shows\n\n\\[\n3\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr)+(n-3)\n<\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|T_n|.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nIndeed:\n\n* If \\(n=2m\\) is even, the left\u2011hand side equals \\(3(m+1)+(2m-3)=5m\\), while the right\u2011hand side equals \\(m(2m+1)=2m^{2}+m\\). For \\(m\\ge 2\\) we have \\(2m^{2}+m-5m=2m^{2}-4m>0\\).\n* If \\(n=2m+1\\) is odd, the left\u2011hand side equals \\(3(m+1)+(2m+1-3)=5m+1\\), while the right\u2011hand side equals \\((2m+1)(2m+2)/2=2m^{2}+3m+1\\). The difference is \\(2m^{2}+3m+1-(5m+1)=2m^{2}-2m>0\\) for \\(m\\ge 1\\).\n\nThus the bound (6) is **strictly smaller** than the required number \\(|T_n|\\) of points.  \nConsequently condition (1) cannot be satisfied if \\(k\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nThe assumption that a configuration with three or more sunny lines exists leads to a contradiction. Therefore any admissible configuration must have  \n\n\\[\nk\\le 2 .\n\\]\n\nHence the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration can possibly exist is contained in \\(\\{0,1,2\\}\\).\n\n\u220e"
