etween \\(1\\) and \\(n\\). Consequently the number of distinct integer points of \\(L\\) that can lie in \\(T_n\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{q}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le 1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThus (L1) holds. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \n\nLet \\(L\\) be a line parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.  \nIf two distinct points of \\(T_n\\) lie on \\(L\\), then those two points already lie on a sunny line of the same direction, contradicting the distinctness of the lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\). Consequently each non\u2011sunny line can contribute **at most one** new point of \\(T_n\\) that is not already covered by the sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose \\(L\\) is horizontal, i.e. \\(y=b_0\\). Any two points of \\(T_n\\) on this line have coordinates \\((a_1,b_0)\\) and \\((a_2,b_0)\\) with \\(a_1\\neq a_2\\). The line through them is precisely the horizontal line \\(y=b_0\\), which is not sunny. However, the same two points also lie on the sunny line of slope \\(p/q\\) that passes through them (choose any rational slope different from \\(0,-1\\)); such a line would be distinct from \\(L\\) and would already be present among the sunny lines if the configuration satisfies (A). Hence, to avoid duplication, at most one point of \\(T_n\\) can be assigned uniquely to a non\u2011sunny line. The same reasoning applies to vertical lines and to lines of the form \\(x+y=c\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Counting argument\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is\n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{C1}\n\\]\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that \\(k\\ge 3\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line covers at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) points of \\(T_n\\); therefore the three sunny lines together cover at most\n\n\\[\n3\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr)\n\\tag{C2}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\nThere remain \\(n-3\\) non\u2011sunny lines. By Lemma\u202f2 each of them can add at most one new point of \\(T_n\\) that is not already covered by the sunny lines. Hence the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered by all \\(n\\) lines is bounded above by\n\n\\[\n3\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr)+(n-3).\n\\tag{C3}\n\\]\n\nWe now compare (C3) with (C1).  \n\n*Even case.* Let \\(n=2m\\) with \\(m\\ge 2\\). Then\n\n\\[\n\\text{(C3)}=3(m+1)+(2m-3)=5m,\n\\qquad\n\\text{(C1)}=\\frac{2m(2m+1)}{2}=2m^{2}+m .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(2m^{2}+m-5m=2m^{2}-4m>0\\) for \\(m\\ge 2\\), we have \\(\\text{(C3)}<\\text{(C1)}\\).\n\n*Odd case.* Let \\(n=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge 1\\). Then\n\n\\[\n\\text{(C3)}=3(m+1)+(2m+1-3)=5m+1,\n\\qquad\n\\text{(C1)}=\\frac{(2m+1)(2m+2)}{2}=2m^{2}+3m+1 .\n\\]\n\nNow \\(2m^{2}+3m+1-(5m+1)=2m^{2}-2m>0\\) for \\(m\\ge 1\\), so again \\(\\text{(C3)}<\\text{(C1)}\\).\n\nThus in every case \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n3\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr)+(n-3)\n\\;<\\;\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts condition (A) that all \\(|T_n|\\) points must be covered.\n\nTherefore our assumption \\(k\\ge 3\\) is impossible, and any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\le 2 .\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
