ining points with non\u2011sunny lines.*  \n   Remove from \\(T_n\\) all points that already lie on one of the sunny lines \\(S_i\\); denote the remaining set by \\(T_n'\\).  \n   Observe that every point of \\(T_n'\\) has \\(b\\ge 2\\) (otherwise it would be one of the chosen \\(P_i\\)).  \n   Consequently the smallest possible sum of coordinates of a point in \\(T_n'\\) is \\(3\\), and the largest possible sum is still \\(n+1\\).  \n\n   For each integer \\(s\\) with \\(3\\le s\\le n+1\\) consider the line  \n\n   \\[\n   D_s:\\;x+y=s .\n   \\]\n\n   These lines are non\u2011sunny, pairwise distinct, and each of them contains **all** points of \\(T_n'\\) whose coordinates sum to \\(s\\).  \n   The number of such lines is \\((n+1)-3+1=n-1\\).  \n\n   *Step\u202f3 \u2013 count the lines.*  \n   We have used  \n\n   * \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\);  \n   * \\((n-1)\\) diagonal lines \\(D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\).\n\n   Since \\(k\\le n\\), the total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   k+(n-1)=n+(k-1)\\le 2n-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   To obtain exactly \\(n\\) lines we simply discard any \\(k-1\\) of the diagonal lines\n   \\(D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\) (they are redundant because the points they would cover are already\n   covered by the sunny lines).  \n   After discarding, we are left with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, all distinct, and together they cover every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n4. **Pigeonhole argument for the impossibility of \\(k>n\\).**  \n   Because we are required to use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of sunny lines can never exceed \\(n\\). This follows immediately from the pigeonhole principle: each line is either sunny or non\u2011sunny, so the total number of sunny lines is bounded above by the total number of lines, i.e. by \\(n\\).\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called 