\\setminus\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k} S_i .\n   \\]\n\n   Any point of \\(T_n'\\) has second coordinate \\(b\\ge 2\\); otherwise it would be\n   one of the points \\(P_i\\) and thus belong to some \\(S_i\\).\n\n3. **Covering the remaining points with diagonal lines.**  \n   For each integer \\(s\\) with \\(3\\le s\\le n+1\\) consider the line  \n\n   \\[\n   D_s:\\; x+y=s .\n   \\]\n\n   The lines \\(D_s\\) are non\u2011sunny (slope \\(-1\\)).  Every point \\((a,b)\\in\n   T_n'\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\ge 3\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); consequently it lies on the\n   unique diagonal line \\(D_{a+b}\\).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n' \\subseteq \\bigcup_{s=3}^{n+1} D_s .\n   \\]\n\n   The collection \\(\\{D_s\\mid 3\\le s\\le n+1\\}\\) consists of exactly \\(n-1\\)\n   distinct non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n4. **Adjusting the total number of lines to \\(n\\).**  \n   At this stage we have \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-1\\) diagonal lines, i.e.\n   \\(k+(n-1)=n+(k-1)\\) lines in total.  Because \\(k\\le n\\), we may delete any\n   \\(k-1\\) of the diagonal lines without losing coverage: each deleted diagonal\n   line \\(D_s\\) contains only points whose sum of coordinates is \\(s\\); those\n   points are already covered by the sunny lines, because for every \\(s\\) with\n   \\(3\\le s\\le n+1\\) there exists at least one \\(i\\) such that the sunny line\n   \\(S_i\\) meets the diagonal \\(D_s\\) (the two lines intersect at a unique\n   point, and that intersection belongs to \\(T_n\\)).  Hence after discarding\n   \\(k-1\\) diagonal lines we are left with exactly\n\n   \\[\n   k\\ \\text{sunny lines} \\quad\\text{and}\\quad (n-1)-(k-1)=n-k\\ \\text{non\u2011sunny lines},\n   \\]\n\n   altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines, and they still cover every point of\n   \\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Upper bound)\n\nIf a configuration uses exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, then the number of sunny\nlines cannot exceed \\(n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nEach line is either sunny or non\u2011sunny, and there are precisely \\(n\\) lines.\nTherefore the number of sunny lines is at most \\(n\\).  This is an immediate\napplication of the pigeonhole principle. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we obtain a configuration with \\(k=0\\).  \nLemma\u202f2 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) a configuration\nwith exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.  \nLemma\u202f3 proves that no configuration can have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines,\nwhich is already excluded by the requirement that the total number of lines\nequals \\(n\\).\n\nHence **every** integer \\(k\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n\n\\]\n\ncan be realised, and no other values are possible. \u220e"
