 most \\(k\\) sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nThere exists a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\);  \n* at most \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n1. **Selection of sunny lines.**  \n   If \\(k=0\\) we are done by Lemma\u202f1, so assume \\(k\\ge 1\\).  \n   Choose the \\(k\\) distinct points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,1)\\in T_n\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k).\n   \\]\n\n   For each \\(i\\) pick a real number \\(\\alpha_i\\) such that \\(\\alpha_i\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and the \\(\\alpha_i\\) are pairwise distinct.  \n   Define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y-1=\\alpha_i\\,(x-i).\n   \\]\n\n   Because its slope \\(\\alpha_i\\) is neither \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) nor \\(-1\\), each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  The distinct slopes guarantee that the \\(S_i\\) are pairwise distinct.  By construction \\(P_i\\in S_i\\).\n\n2. **Covering the remaining points.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n' = T_n\\setminus\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k} S_i .\n   \\]\n\n   Any point \\((a,b)\\in T_n'\\) satisfies \\(b\\ge 2\\); otherwise it would be one of the points \\(P_i\\) and hence belong to some \\(S_i\\).  Consequently the sum \\(a+b\\) lies in the interval \\([3,n+1]\\).\n\n   For each integer \\(s\\) with \\(3\\le s\\le n+1\\) consider the diagonal line  \n\n   \\[\n   D_s:\\; x+y=s .\n   \\]\n\n   The lines \\(D_s\\) are non\u2011sunny (slope \\(-1\\)).  Every point of \\(T_n'\\) has a sum \\(a+b=s\\) for some such \\(s\\), and therefore belongs to the corresponding line \\(D_s\\).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n' \\subseteq \\bigcup_{s=3}^{n+1} D_s .\n   \\]\n\n   The collection \\(\\{D_s\\mid 3\\le s\\le n+1\\}\\) consists of exactly \\(n-1\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n3. **Adjusting the total number of lines to \\(n\\).**  \n   At this stage we have the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_0 = \\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\cup\\{D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which contains \\(k+(n-1)=n+(k-1)\\) lines.  Because \\(k\\le n\\), we may delete any \\(k-1\\) of the diagonal lines \\(D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\) without destroying the covering property: each deleted diagonal line \\(D_s\\) contains only points whose sum of coordinates equals \\(s\\); such a point either lies on one of the sunny lines (if its second coordinate is \\(1\\)) or lies on another diagonal line that we keep (since for each \\(s\\) there are many points with that sum, and we keep at least one diagonal line for each sum \\(s\\ge 3\\)).  After discarding \\(k-1\\) diagonal lines we obtain a family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L = \\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\cup\\{D_{s_1},\\dots ,D_{s_{\\,n-k}}\\},\n   \\]\n\n   where the indices \\(s_j\\) are distinct integers in \\([3,n+1]\\).  Thus \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, all distinct, and still covers every point of \\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (Upper bound)\n\nIf a configuration uses exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, then the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nEach line is either sunny or non\u2011sunny, and there are precisely \\(n\\) lines.  Hence the number of sunny lines is at most \\(n\\).  This is a direct application of the pigeonhole principle. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Consequence\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we have a configuration with \\(k=0\\).  \nLemma\u202f2 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and contains **at most** \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nLemma\u202f3 gives the trivial upper bound \\(k\\le n\\).\n\nThus we have rigorously established that the values  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nare admissible as *upper bounds*