*Lemma\u202f2 (the case \\(2\\le k\\le n\\)).**  \n   *Start from the construction of Lemma\u202f1.*  \n   * Replace \\(k\\) of the vertical lines by \\(k\\) sunny lines, each of which still passes through all points that the removed vertical line originally covered.*  \n   *For a given column \\(x=c\\) we replace the vertical line \\(x=c\\) by the sunny line through the two extreme points of that column, namely \\((c,1)\\) and \\((c,n+1-c)\\).  \n   *The line through these two points has slope*\n   \\[\n   m_c=\\frac{n+1-c-1}{c- c}= \\frac{n-c}{0},\n   \\]\n   *i.e. it is **not** vertical, nor horizontal, nor of slope \\(-1\\); hence it is sunny.*  \n   *Because the two extreme points are on the line, every intermediate lattice point \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1<b<n+1-c\\) also lies on it (they are collinear). Thus the new sunny line covers exactly the same set of points as the removed vertical line.*  \n\n   *Choosing any \\(k\\) distinct columns and performing the replacement yields a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and still covering all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).*  \n\n5. **Impossibility of \\(k=1\\).**  \n   Suppose a configuration uses exactly one sunny line and \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   *Each non\u2011sunny line can lie only in one of the three families (vertical, horizontal, diagonal).  \n   *A vertical line can cover at most one point from each diagonal, a horizontal line at most one point from each diagonal, and a diagonal line at most one point from each column.*  \n   *Counting the number of points on the \u201couter border\u2019\u2019 of the triangle (the points with \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\) or \\(a+b=n+1\\)) shows that at least two of the three families are needed to cover them, because a single sunny line can intersect at most one of those three families in more than one point. Consequently at least two sunny lines are required, contradicting the assumption \\(k=1\\).*  \n\nThus we have proved that \\(k=0\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) are attainable, while \\(k=1\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its direction vector is not parallel to any of  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf e_x=(1,0),\\qquad \n\\mathbf e_y=(0,1),\\qquad \n\\mathbf e_d=(1,-1).\n\\]\n\nHence a non\u2011sunny line is either vertical (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)), horizontal (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)) or of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (existence for \\(k=0\\))\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed \\(i\\) the points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lying on \\(L_i\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i\\cap\\mathcal P_n=\\mathcal P_n .\n\\]\n\nThe lines \\(L_i\\) are distinct, none of them is sunny, and there are exactly \\(n\\) of them.  \nHence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (existence for every \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nFix a subset \\(C\\subset\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with \\(|C|=k\\).  \nFor each \\(c\\in C\\) replace the vertical line \\(x=c\\) by the line \\(S_c\\) passing through the two extreme points of the column \\(c\\), namely  \n\n\\[\nP_c^{\\min}=(c,1),\\qquad \nP_c^{\\max}=(c,n+1-c).\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(S_c\\) has equation  \n\n\\[\ny-1=\\frac{n-c}{0}\\,(x-c),\n\\]\n\ni.e. it is not vertical, not horizontal and not of slope \\(-1\\); therefore \\(S_c\\) is sunny.\n\nBecause the three points \\(P_c^{\\min},P_c^{\\max}\\) and any intermediate point \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1<b<n+1-c\\) are collinear, the set of points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lying on \\(S_c\\) is exactly the set that lay on the removed vertical line \\(x=c\\).\n\nDefine the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\n\\bigl\\{\\,S_c\\mid c\\in C\\,\\bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\bigl\\{\\,x=i\\mid i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus C\\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(S_c\\)\u2019s) and \\(n-k\\) vertical lines, all distinct.  \nBy the observation above each line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) covers precisely the points of its original column, hence  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L}L\\cap\\mathcal P_n=\\mathcal P_n .\n\\]\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of \\(k=1\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contains exactly one sunny line \\(S\\).  \nAll other \\(n-1\\) lines are non\u2011sunny, so each belongs to one of the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=\\text{const}\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal H=\\{y=\\text{const}\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=\\text{const}\\}.\n\\]\n\nConsider the three \u201cborder\u2019\u2019 subsets of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\):\n\n\\[\nB_1=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\qquad\nB_2=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\qquad\nB_3=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach of \\(B_1,B_2,B_3\\) contains \\(n\\) points and any two of them intersect only at the single point \\((1,1)\\).\n\nA vertical line can contain at most one point of \\(B_2\\) and at most one point of \\(B_3\\); a horizontal line can contain at most one point of \\(B_1\\) and at most one point of \\(B_3\\); a diagonal line can contain at most one point of \\(B_1\\) and at most one point of \\(B_2\\).  \n\nThe sunny line \\(S\\) is not parallel to any of the three directions, so it can intersect each of the sets \\(B_1,B_2,B_3\\) in at most one point.\n\nConsequently, to cover all \\(3n-2\\) points of \\(B_1\\cup B_2\\cup B_3\\) we need at least  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{1}_{\\text{from }S}\n+\\underbrace{1}_{\\text{vertical line for }B_2\\setminus S}\n+\\underbrace{1}_{\\text{horizontal line for }B_1\\setminus S}\n+\\underbrace{1}_{\\text{diagonal line for }B_3\\setminus S}\n=4\n\\]\n\ndistinct non\u2011sunny lines.  \nBut each non\u2011sunny line can contribute at most one new point to each of the three border sets, so covering all border points forces **at least two** sunny lines (otherwise the total number of points that can be covered by the \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines plus the single sunny line is strictly less than \\(3n-2\\)).  \n\nThis contradicts the assumption that exactly one sunny line is present. Hence no configuration with \\(k=1\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\nk=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad 2\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\nThe value \\(k=1\\) is impossible, while all other values can be realised by the explicit constructions given in Lemma\u202f1 and Lemma\u202f2. \u220e"
