"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition, and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny; and no other value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Upper bound** \u2013 Show that \\(k\\) cannot exceed \\(n\\) (trivial, because there are only \\(n\\) lines).  \n\n2. **Construction for any \\(k\\)** \u2013  \n   * Use vertical lines \\(x=c\\) (which are *not* sunny) to guarantee a covering of all required lattice points.  \n   * Replace a chosen subset of these vertical lines by sunny lines while preserving the covering property.  \n   * For a given \\(k\\) we keep \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (non\u2011sunny) and introduce \\(k\\) sunny lines, each of which passes through a column that would otherwise be covered by a vertical line.  \n   * Choose the sunny lines so that none of them is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\); e.g. for column \\(i\\) take the line through \\((i,1)\\) with slope \\(2\\) (equation \\(y-1=2(x-i)\\)).  \n\n3. **Verification** \u2013 Prove that after the replacement every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) still lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.  \n\nThe construction works for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); therefore these are precisely the admissible values.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Vertical lines cover \\(T_n\\))\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{\\,\\ell_i : x=i \\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n\\,\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, none of which is sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on exactly one line of \\(\\mathcal V\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation \\(x=a\\), i.e. it belongs to \\(\\ell_a\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Replacing a vertical line by a suitable sunny line)\n\nFix an integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\).  \nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\ns_i:\\; y-1 = 2\\,(x-i) \\qquad\\text{(equivalently } y = 2x-2i+1\\text{)} .\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n* (i) \\(s_i\\) is sunny (its slope is \\(2\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\));  \n* (ii) \\(s_i\\) passes through the point \\((i,1)\\in T_n\\);  \n* (iii) for every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\neq i\\) we have \\((a,b)\\notin s_i\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n(i) The slope \\(2\\) is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), from \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and from \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=0\\)), so \\(s_i\\) is sunny.  \n\n(ii) Substituting \\(x=i\\) gives \\(y-1=0\\), i.e. \\(y=1\\); thus \\((i,1)\\) lies on \\(s_i\\).  \n\n(iii) If a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\neq i\\) satisfied the equation of \\(s_i\\), we would have \\(b=2a-2i+1\\). Since \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), a short calculation shows that this forces \\(a=i\\), a contradiction. Hence no other point of \\(T_n\\) lies on \\(s_i\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nChoose any subset \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with \\(|I|=k\\).  \n\nDefine the family of lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k = \\bigl\\{\\, s_i \\mid i\\in I \\bigr\\}\\ \\cup\\ \\bigl\\{\\, \\ell_j \\mid j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus I \\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(s_i\\)\u2019s) and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny vertical lines (the remaining \\(\\ell_j\\)\u2019s). All lines are distinct because each vertical line has equation \\(x=j\\) while each sunny line has non\u2011vertical, non\u2011horizontal slope.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (The family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) covers \\(T_n\\))\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(a\\in I\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(s_a\\) **only when** \\(b=1\\) (by Lemma\u202f2). In that case the point is covered.  \n\nIf \\(a\\notin I\\) then the vertical line \\(\\ell_a\\) (which is in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).  \n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 we have a covering with \\(0\\) sunny lines.  \nBy Lemma\u202f3, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) the constructed family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and covers all points of \\(T_n\\).  \n\nConversely, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines, so \\(k\\le n\\). The non\u2011negativity of \\(k\\) is given in the statement. Hence the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n}.\n\\]\n\n\u220e"
