"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a complete classification of the admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following partial result:\n\n> **Lemma (Upper bound on sunny lines).**  \n> For any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\), if \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfy the covering condition of the problem, then at most **one** of those lines can be sunny.\n\nConsequently, the only possible values of \\(k\\) are \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).  \nI have not been able to settle whether both values actually occur for every \\(n\\); the existence of a configuration with exactly one sunny line remains open.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Structure of the required point set.**  \n   The points that must be covered are  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]  \n   For each fixed \\(a\\) the column \\(\\{(a,b):1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}\\) contains at least two points whenever \\(a\\le n-1\\).\n\n2. **Observation on sunny lines.**  \n   A sunny line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore its slope \\(m\\) satisfies \\(m\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n3. **Key counting argument.**  \n   Suppose two distinct sunny lines \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\) are present.  \n   Because their slopes are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), each of them can intersect a given vertical column in **at most one** point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Since every column with index \\(a\\le n-1\\) contains at least two points of \\(T_n\\), covering all points of such a column would require at least two distinct lines that are *vertical* (slope \\(\\infty\\)).  \n   Hence any column that is not covered by a vertical line must be covered entirely by sunny lines, which is impossible because a non\u2011vertical line meets the column in at most one lattice point of \\(T_n\\).  \n\n   From this contradiction we deduce that **no column can be left without a vertical line**; therefore at most one of the \\(n\\) lines may be non\u2011vertical, i.e. sunny.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The argument shows that any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines can contain at most one sunny line, giving the upper bound \\(k\\le 1\\).  \n   The construction with \\(k=0\\) (all vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\)) clearly satisfies the covering condition, so \\(k=0\\) is attainable.  \n   Whether a configuration with exactly one sunny line exists for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) is left unresolved.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) define the *column*\n\n\\[\nC_a=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}=T_n\\cap\\{x=a\\}.\n\\]\n\nObserve that  \n\n\\[\n|C_a| = n+1-a\\ge 2 \\qquad\\text{for }1\\le a\\le n-1,\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nand \\(|C_n|=1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Vertical lines cover a column)\n\nIf a line has equation \\(x=a\\) (vertical), then it contains **all** points of the column \\(C_a\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Immediate from the definition of \\(C_a\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (A sunny line meets a column in at most one point of \\(T_n\\))\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line; its slope \\(m\\) satisfies \\(m\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nFor any fixed \\(a\\) the intersection \\(L\\cap C_a\\) contains at most one point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(L\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\neq\\infty\\).  \nIf \\((a,b_1)\\) and \\((a,b_2)\\) with \\(b_1\\neq b_2\\) were both on \\(L\\), then substituting \\(x=a\\) would give two distinct values for \\(y\\), contradicting the functional form of the line.  \nIf \\(L\\) were vertical, its slope would be \\(\\infty\\), which is excluded by the definition of sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (At most one sunny line can appear)\n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(T_n\\).  \nThen \\(\\mathcal F\\) contains at most one sunny line.\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose, for contradiction, that \\(\\mathcal F\\) contains two distinct sunny lines \\(L_1,L_2\\).  \nConsider a column \\(C_a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n-1\\). By (1) this column contains at least two points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nIf neither \\(L_1\\) nor \\(L_2\\) is vertical, then by Lemma\u202f2 each of them can contribute at most one point of \\(C_a\\).  \nConsequently, at most two points of \\(C_a\\) could be covered by \\(L_1\\cup L_2\\).  \nAll remaining points of \\(C_a\\) would have to be covered by the other \\(n-2\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\).  \nHowever, any line that is not vertical meets the column in at most one point (Lemma\u202f2), while a vertical line meets the column in *all* its points (Lemma\u202f1).  \nSince we have only \\(n-2\\) lines left, to cover the whole column we would need at least one vertical line among them.  \n\nNow, if a vertical line \\(x=a\\) is present in \\(\\mathcal F\\), it already covers the entire column \\(C_a\\).  \nIn that case the two sunny lines cannot be needed for this column, and we may delete one of them without losing the covering property. Repeating this argument for each column forces all but possibly one of the sunny lines to be removable, contradicting the assumption that both \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\) are necessary.\n\nHence the assumption that two sunny lines are present leads to a contradiction; therefore \\(\\mathcal F\\) can contain at most one sunny line. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Consequences\n\n* The family of vertical lines \\(\\{x=1, x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines and clearly covers every point of \\(T_n\\). Hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n* By Lemma\u202f3 any admissible configuration satisfies \\(k\\le 1\\). Thus the only possible values of \\(k\\) are \\(0\\) and \\(1\\).\n\nThe existence of a configuration with exactly one sunny line for each \\(n\\ge 3\\) has not been established; constructing such a configuration (or proving its impossibility) remains an open sub\u2011problem. \u220e"
