ll covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThese two explicit families establish the existence of configurations with \\(k=0\\) and with \\(k=1\\) for every admissible \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1  \nFor every \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing **no** sunny line; i.e. \\(0\\in\\mathcal K_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\,x=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) are vertical, hence parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis and therefore non\u2011sunny.  \nFor any point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(b\\ge 1\\).  \nConsequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal L_0\\).  \nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered by \\(\\mathcal L_0\\), which consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2  \nFor every \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing **exactly one** sunny line; i.e. \\(1\\in\\mathcal K_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nStart from the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V_{n-1}=\\{\\,x=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich are \\(n-1\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\le n-1\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal V_{n-1}\\).  \nThe only point of \\(T_n\\) whose abscissa equals \\(n\\) is \\((n,1)\\) (indeed, if \\(a=n\\) then the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b=1\\)).  