r each \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the line \\(x=a\\) contains exactly the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\).  \n   Consequently the union of these \\(n\\) lines is precisely \\(T_n\\).  \n   Hence a configuration with **no** sunny line exists for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n5. **Construction for \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).**  \n\n   *Sunny part.*  \n   Choose an irrational slope \\(\\alpha\\) (e.g. \\(\\alpha=\\sqrt2\\)).  \n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y-(n+2-i)=\\alpha\\bigl(x-i\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the distinct lattice point \\((i,n+2-i)\\in T_n\\) and, by Lemma\u202f3, through no other point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Because the slopes are all equal to the same irrational number while the intercepts differ, the \\(\\ell_i\\) are distinct and sunny.\n\n   *Non\u2011sunny part.*  \n   After removing the \\(k\\) points covered by the sunny lines, the set that still has to be covered contains  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_n|-k=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-k\n   \\]\n\n   points.  \n   Use the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines taken from the three forbidden families as follows:\n\n   * first \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n-k}{3}\\rfloor\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,2,\\dots\\);\n   * next \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n-k}{3}\\rfloor\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,2,\\dots\\);\n   * the rest diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,3,\\dots\\).\n\n   The constants are chosen consecutively so that each line lies as far \u201cinside\u2019\u2019 the triangle \\(T_n\\) as possible.  \n   By Lemma\u202f2 each such line contains exactly \\(\\big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rceil\\) points of the still\u2011uncovered set until the points are exhausted.  \n   Because the total number of points that must be covered after the sunny lines have been placed is \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-k\\) and each non\u2011sunny line contributes at least one new point, a simple greedy placement (always pick the next line that covers the largest number of uncovered points) succeeds in covering all remaining points with the available \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n   Thus we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for every \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Steps\u202f4 and\u202f5 give explicit constructions for all integers \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n]\\).  \n   The extremal inequality (\u2605) shows that no other value of \\(k\\) can occur.  \n   Hence the admissible set of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### Notation  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 1 (Cardinality of \\(T_n\\))\n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the equation \\(a+b=s\\) has exactly \\(s-1\\) solutions in positive integers. Summing over \\(s\\),\n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 2 (Maximum points on a non\u2011sunny line)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a line parallel to one of the three forbidden directions\n\\(y=\\text{const}\\), \\(x=\\text{const}\\) or \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rceil .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n*Horizontal* lines \\(y=c\\) intersect \\(T_n\\) in the points \\((a,c)\\) with\n\\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); the number of such points equals \\(n+1-c\\), whose\nmaximum over admissible \\(c\\) is \\(\\big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rceil\\).\n\n*Vertical* lines are symmetric, giving the same bound.\n\n*Diagonal* lines \\(x+y=c\\) intersect \\(T_n\\) in the points \\((a,c-a)\\) with\n\\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) and \\(c-a\\ge 1\\). The admissible values of \\(a\\) form an\ninterval of length at most \\(\\big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rceil\\). \u220e\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 3 (Sunny lines contain at most one point of \\(T_n\\))\n\nIf \\(\\ell\\) is sunny then \\(|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le 1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTwo distinct points of \\(T_n\\) have integer coordinates, so the slope of the\nline through them is a rational number. All three forbidden slopes\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\) are rational; a sunny line has a slope different from each of\nthem, hence cannot pass through two distinct lattice points of \\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 4 (Necessary condition on the number of sunny lines)\n\nIf a configuration of \\(n\\) lines satisfies the covering requirement and\ncontains \\(s\\) sunny lines, then  \n\n\\[\n0\\le s\\le n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThere are exactly \\(n\\) lines, so the number of sunny ones cannot exceed \\(n\\)\nand cannot be negative. \u220e\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 5 (Extremal inequality)\n\nLet a feasible configuration contain \\(s\\) sunny lines. Then  \n\n\\[\ns+(n-s)\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rceil\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma\u202f3 the sunny lines together cover at most \\(s\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nEach of the remaining \\(n-s\\) non\u2011sunny lines covers at most\n\\(\\big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rceil\\) points (Lemma\u202f2).  \nSince all points of \\(T_n\\) must be covered, the total number of points that can\nbe covered is at least \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\), which yields (5). \u220e\n\nFor every integer \\(s\\) with \\(0\\le s\\le n\\) the left\u2011hand side of (5) equals  \n\n\\[\ns+(n-s)\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rceil\n= n\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rceil - (n-s)\\Big(\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rceil-1\\Big)\n\\ge n\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rceil - (n-0)\\Big(\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rceil-1\\Big)\n= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n\\]\n\nso inequality (5) imposes **no restriction** beyond \\(0\\le s\\le n\\).\n\nThus the only possible values of \\(k\\) are those in \\([0,n]\\); it remains to\nshow that each