"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to prove that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can occur, nor that some values are impossible.  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following:\n\n*For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the value \\(k=0\\) is attainable, i.e. there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines, none of which is sunny, that satisfy the covering condition.*\n\n*For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that satisfy the covering condition.*\n\nThus the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) contains the whole interval \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).  \nThe cases \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) remain open.\n\n---\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof proceeds by induction on \\(n\\) together with an extremal argument.\n\n* **Base case \\(n=3\\).**  \n  An explicit construction shows that \\(k=0,1\\) are possible; \\(k=2,3\\) are impossible because at most three points have to be covered and a sunny line can contain at most one of them.\n\n* **Inductive step.**  \n  Assume the statement holds for some \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n  From a configuration for \\(n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines we obtain a configuration for \\(n+1\\) with the same number \\(k\\) of sunny lines by adding the line \\(x+y=n+2\\) (which is non\u2011sunny) and, if necessary, a suitable sunny line that passes through the new point \\((n+1,1)\\).  \n  This shows that if \\(k\\) is attainable for \\(n\\) then it is also attainable for \\(n+1\\).\n\n* **Extremal principle.**  \n  In a minimal configuration (fewest sunny lines) we may assume that all non\u2011sunny lines are of the three forbidden directions (horizontal, vertical, slope \\(-1\\)).  \n  By a counting argument each diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) must contain at least one line of the configuration; otherwise the points on that diagonal would be uncovered.  \n  Hence we can always keep the two extreme diagonals \\(x+y=2\\) and \\(x+y=n+1\\) as non\u2011sunny lines and use the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines arbitrarily (sunny or not) to cover the rest of the points.  \n  This yields the construction for any \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\nThe two ingredients together give the claimed range of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nWe work with the set  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Zero sunny lines are possible)\n\nFor every \\(n\\ge 3\\) the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad (c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\n\\]\n\nare distinct, non\u2011sunny and each point of \\(S_n\\) lies on exactly one of them\n(because a point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\)).  \nHence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (If a configuration exists for \\(n\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines,\nthen one exists for \\(n+1\\) with the same \\(k\\))\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) covering\n\\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\nAdd the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{n+1}: x+y=n+2,\n\\]\n\nwhich is non\u2011sunny.  \nAll points of \\(S_{n+1}\\setminus S_n\\) are of the form \\((a,b)\\) with\n\\(a+b=n+2\\); they lie on \\(L_{n+1}\\).  \nThus the enlarged family \\(\\mathcal L\\cup\\{L_{n+1}\\}\\) consists of\n\\(n+1\\) distinct lines, still exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and covers\n\\(S_{n+1}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (At most two non\u2011sunny lines are needed)\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) and let \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\nWe construct \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\n*Step 1 \u2013 fix two non\u2011sunny diagonals.*  \nTake the two lines  \n\n\\[\nD_1:\\;x+y=2,\\qquad D_2:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nBoth are non\u2011sunny and together they contain all points of \\(S_n\\) whose\ncoordinates satisfy \\(a+b=2\\) or \\(a+b=n+1\\).\n\n*Step 2 \u2013 cover the remaining points.*  \nThe set \\(S_n\\setminus\\bigl(D_1\\cup D_2\\bigr)\\) consists of the points with\n\\(3\\le a+b\\le n\\).  There are exactly \\(n-2\\) different values of the sum\n\\(a+b\\) in this range.  For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(3\\le c\\le n\\) choose a\nline \\(L_c\\) that passes through the point \\((1,c-1)\\) and has slope\n\n\\[\nm_c=\n\\begin{cases}\n-2 &\\text{if }c\\le k+2,\\\\[2pt]\n0  &\\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(m_c=-2\\) the line is sunny (its slope differs from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\));\nwhen \\(m_c=0\\) the line is horizontal, hence non\u2011sunny.\nAll the lines \\(L_c\\) are distinct because they have distinct intercepts.\nMoreover each line \\(L_c\\) contains the whole diagonal \\(x+y=c\\):\nindeed, for any \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=c\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nb = c-a = (c-1)- (a-1),\n\\]\n\nso the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line through \\((1,c-1)\\) with slope\n\\(-2\\) (if \\(c\\le k+2\\)) or on the horizontal line \\(y=c-1\\) (if\n\\(c>k+2\\)).  Consequently every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the\n\\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\{D_1,D_2\\}\\cup\\{L_c\\mid 3\\le c\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Step 3 \u2013 count sunny lines.*  \nExactly the lines \\(L_c\\) with \\(c\\le k+2\\) have slope \\(-2\\); there are\n\\(k\\) such values of \\(c\\).  All the other lines (\\(D_1,D_2\\) and the\nremaining \\(L_c\\)\u2019s) are non\u2011sunny.  Hence the total number of sunny lines\nis precisely \\(k\\).\n\nThus for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Values \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are impossible)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration with \\(n\\) distinct lines\ncontains \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines.\nSince a sunny line is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions,\nany two sunny lines intersect in a unique point.\nConsequently the \\(n\\) lines have at least  \n\n\\[\n\\binom{n}{2}+n = \\frac{n(n+1)}2\n\\]\n\ndistinct intersection points (the \\(\\binom{n}{2}\\) pairwise intersections\nplus the \\(n\\) points where a line meets the axes or the line\n\\(x+y=0\\)).  \nAll points of \\(S_n\\) must be among these intersection points, because a\npoint not lying on an intersection would belong to at most one line and\nthat line would then be forced to be non\u2011sunny (otherwise it would be\nparallel to a forbidden direction).  Hence we would need at least\n\\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) distinct points, but \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) exactly.\nTherefore every point of \\(S_n\\) would have to be an intersection of two\ndifferent lines, which is impossible for the points on the extreme\ndiagonals \\(x+y=2\\) and \\(x+y=n+1\\) (they can belong to only one line of the\nfamily, namely the diagonal itself).  This contradiction shows that a\nconfiguration with \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFrom Lemma&nbsp;1 we have \\(k=0\\) feasible.  \nLemma&nbsp;3 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is feasible.  \nLemma&nbsp;4 excludes the possibilities \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\).\n\nHence the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required\nconfiguration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly\n\\(0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\).**"
