"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\; } .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct\nlines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny\nlines, while no configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) exists.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof is by induction on \\(n\\) together with an extremal (minimal\u2011\\(k\\))\nargument.\n\n* **Base case \\(n=3\\).**  \n  An explicit list of three lines shows that \\(k=0,1\\) are attainable and that\n  \\(k=2,3\\) are impossible.\n\n* **Inductive step.**  \n  Assume the statement holds for some \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n  From a configuration for \\(n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines we obtain a\n  configuration for \\(n+1\\) with the same number \\(k\\) of sunny lines by\n  adding the non\u2011sunny diagonal line \\(x+y=n+2\\).  This shows that if a\n  particular \\(k\\) is attainable for \\(n\\) then it is also attainable for\n  \\(n+1\\).\n\n* **Extremal construction (showing that every \\(k\\le n-2\\) is attainable).**  \n  Fix the two non\u2011sunny diagonal lines  \n\n  \\[\n  D_{1}:x+y=2,\\qquad D_{2}:x+y=n+1 .\n  \\]\n\n  They already cover all points of the set  \n\n  \\[\n  S_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n  \\]\n\n  whose sum equals \\(2\\) or \\(n+1\\).  \n  For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(3\\le c\\le n\\) we introduce a line  \n\n  \\[\n  L_{c}:\\;y = (c-2)-x .\n  \\]\n\n  The slope of \\(L_{c}\\) is \\(-1\\) when \\(c\\) is even and \\(+1\\) when \\(c\\) is\n  odd; in either case the line is **sunny** (its slope is different from\n  \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  Moreover \\(L_{c}\\) passes through the two points\n  \\((1,c-1)\\) and \\((c-1,1)\\), both of which belong to the diagonal\n  \\(x+y=c\\).  Consequently every point of the diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) is covered\n  either by \\(D_{1},D_{2}\\) (if \\(c=2\\) or \\(c=n+1\\)) or by the corresponding\n  line \\(L_{c}\\) (if \\(3\\le c\\le n\\)).  The family  \n\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal F_{k}= \\{D_{1},D_{2}\\}\\cup\\{L_{c}\\mid 3\\le c\\le n\\}\n  \\]\n\n  consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n  To obtain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines we replace the first \\(k\\) of the\n  lines \\(L_{c}\\) (for the smallest values of \\(c\\)) by the **horizontal**\n  lines \\(y=c-1\\) (which are non\u2011sunny).  The remaining \\(n-2-k\\) lines\n  \\(L_{c}\\) are left unchanged and stay sunny.  Because each diagonal\n  \\(x+y=c\\) still contains at least one line of the family (either the\n  horizontal line \\(y=c-1\\) or the sunny line \\(L_{c}\\)), every point of\n  \\(S_{n}\\) remains covered.  Thus we have produced a configuration with\n  exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for any \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n* **Impossibility of \\(k=n-1,n\\).**  \n  Suppose a configuration with \\(n\\) distinct lines contains at least\n  \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  Any two sunny lines intersect in a unique point,\n  so the set of all intersection points of the \\(n\\) lines has size at least\n  \\(\\binom{n}{2}+n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).  All points of \\(S_{n}\\) must be among\n  these intersections, because a point that lies on only one line would\n  force that line to be non\u2011sunny (otherwise the line would be parallel to\n  one of the forbidden directions).  Hence every point of \\(S_{n}\\) would\n  have to be an intersection of two distinct lines.  This is impossible for\n  the points on the extreme diagonals \\(x+y=2\\) and \\(x+y=n+1\\), each of\n  which can belong to at most one line of the family (the diagonal itself).\n  Therefore a configuration with \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\nCombining the construction for \\(k\\le n-2\\) with the impossibility for\n\\(k=n-1,n\\) yields the claimed set of admissible values.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); otherwise\nit is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Base case)\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the following families work:\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the three diagonals \\(x+y=2,\\,x+y=3,\\,x+y=4\\) (all non\u2011sunny);\n* \\(k=1\\): the two diagonals \\(x+y=2,\\,x+y=4\\) together with the sunny line\n  \\(y=x+1\\).\n\nNo family of three lines can contain two or three sunny lines, because a\nsunny line can meet at most one point of \\(S_{3}\\) and we need to cover the\nfour points of \\(S_{3}\\).  Hence the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Inductive step)\n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) and some \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there\nexists a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_{n}\\) and\ncontaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\nAdd the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell : x+y=n+2 .\n\\]\n\n\\(\\ell\\) is non\u2011sunny.  Every point of \\(S_{n+1}\\setminus S_{n}\\) satisfies\n\\(a+b=n+2\\) and therefore lies on \\(\\ell\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F'=\\mathcal F\\cup\\{\\ell\\}\n\\]\n\nis a family of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_{n+1}\\) and still having\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Hence any admissible \\(k\\) for \\(n\\) is also\nadmissible for \\(n+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Construction for all \\(k\\le n-2\\))\n\nFix the two non\u2011sunny diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nD_{1}:x+y=2,\\qquad D_{2}:x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(3\\le c\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_{c}:\\; y = (c-2)-x .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_{c}\\) equals \\(-1\\) when \\(c\\) is even and \\(+1\\) when\n\\(c\\) is odd; in either case it is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so\n\\(L_{c}\\) is sunny.  Moreover \\(L_{c}\\) passes through the two points\n\\((1,c-1)\\) and \\((c-1,1)\\), both of which satisfy \\(x+y=c\\); consequently\n\\(L_{c}\\) meets the diagonal \\(x+y=c\\).\n\nConsider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal G=\\{D_{1},D_{2}\\}\\cup\\{L_{c}\\mid 3\\le c\\le n\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal G\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  Every point\n\\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) belongs to the diagonal \\(x+y=a+b\\).  If \\(a+b=2\\) or\n\\(a+b=n+1\\) the point lies on \\(D_{1}\\) or \\(D_{2}\\) respectively.\nIf \\(3\\le a+b\\le n\\) the point lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=a+b\\) and that\ndiagonal contains the line \\(L_{a+b}\\) from (2); hence the point is covered.\nThus \\(\\mathcal G\\) covers \\(S_{n}\\) and contains exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny\nlines (all the \\(L_{c}\\)\u2019s).\n\nTo obtain a configuration with **exactly** \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le k\\le\nn-2\\)) we modify \\(\\mathcal G\\) as follows.\nReplace the first \\(n-2-k\\) lines \\(L_{c}\\) (those with the smallest values\nof \\(c\\)) by the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_{c}: y = c-1\\qquad (3\\le c\\le n-2-k+2).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(H_{c}\\) is non\u2011sunny (slope \\(0\\)).  The remaining \\(k\\) lines\n\\(L_{c}\\) are left unchanged and stay sunny.  The resulting family\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{k}= \\{D_{1},D_{2}\\}\\cup\\{H_{c}\\mid 3\\le c\\le n-2-k+2\\}\n                 \\cup\\{L_{c}\\mid n-1-k\\le c\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nstill contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  For any diagonal\n\\(x+y=c\\) with \\(3\\le c\\le n\\) we have either the horizontal line\n\\(H_{c}\\) (if \\(c\\) was among the replaced indices) or the sunny line\n\\(L_{c}\\) (otherwise); therefore every point of \\(S_{n}\\) remains covered.\nThe number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\) is precisely the number of\nremaining \\(L_{c}\\)\u2019s, namely \\(k\\).  Hence for every \\(k\\le n-2\\) a\nrequired configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\))\n\nAssume, to the contrary, that a family \\(\\mathcal H\\) of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines covers \\(S_{n}\\) and contains at least \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\nAny two sunny lines intersect in a unique point, and a sunny line is not\nparallel to any of the three forbidden directions, so it cannot coincide\nwith a horizontal, vertical or \\(-1\\)\u2011slope line.  Hence the total number of\nintersection points among the \\(n\\) lines is at least  \n\n\\[\n\\binom{n}{2}+n = \\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(S_{n}\\) must be one of these intersection points:\nif a point of \\(S_{n}\\) lay on exactly one line of \\(\\mathcal H\\), that line\nwould have to be non\u2011sunny (otherwise the line would be parallel to a\nforbidden direction, contradicting the definition of sunny).  Since at\nleast \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny, at most one line can be non\u2011sunny, so at\nmost one point of \\(S_{n}\\) could be covered by a single line.  But the two\nextreme diagonals \\(x+y=2\\) and \\(x+y=n+1\\) each contain more than one point\nof \\(S_{n}\\); each of those points lies on the corresponding diagonal line,\nand no other line of \\(\\mathcal H\\) can pass through them without being\nparallel to that diagonal (which would make it non\u2011sunny).  Consequently\nthe points on the extreme diagonals cannot all be intersections of two\ndifferent lines, contradicting (3).  Therefore a configuration with\n\\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nLemmas\u202f1\u20133 show that for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer\n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines\ncovering all points of \\(S_{n}\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\nLemma\u202f4 proves that \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are impossible.\n\nHence the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\u220e"
