hcal F'=\\mathcal F\\cup\\{\\ell\\}\n\\]\n\nis a family of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_{n+1}\\) and still having\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Hence any admissible \\(k\\) for \\(n\\) is also\nadmissible for \\(n+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Construction for all \\(k\\le n-2\\))\n\nFix the two non\u2011sunny diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nD_{1}:x+y=2,\\qquad D_{2}:x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(3\\le c\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_{c}:\\; y = (c-2)-x .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_{c}\\) equals \\(-1\\) when \\(c\\) is even and \\(+1\\) when\n\\(c\\) is odd; in either case it is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so\n\\(L_{c}\\) is sunny.  Moreover \\(L_{c}\\) passes through the two points\n\\((1,c-1)\\) and \\((c-1,1)\\), both of which satisfy \\(x+y=c\\); consequently\n\\(L_{c}\\) meets the diagonal \\(x+y=c\\).\n\nConsider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal G=\\{D_{1},D_{2}\\}\\cup\\{L_{c}\\mid 3\\le c\\le n\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal G\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  Every point\n\\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) belongs to the diagonal \\(x+y=a+b\\).  If \\(a+b=2\\) or\n\\(a+b=n+1\\) the point lies on \\(D_{1}\\) or \\(D_{2}\\) respectively.\nIf \\(3\\le a+b\\le n\\) the point lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=a+b\\) and that\ndiagonal contains the line \\(L_{a+b}\\) from (2); hence the point is covered.\nThus \\(\\mathcal G\\) covers \\(S_{n}\\) and contains exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny\nlines (all the \\(L_{c}\\)\u2019s).\n\nTo obtain a configuration with **exactly** \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le k\\le\nn-2\\)) we modify \\(\\mathcal G\\) as follows.\nReplace the first \\(n-2-k\\) lines \\(L_{c}\\) (those with the smallest values\nof \\(c\\)) by the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_{c}: y = c-1\\qquad (3\\le c\\le n-2-k+2).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(H_{c}\\) is non\u2011sunny (slope \\(0\\)).  The remaining \\(k\\) lines\n\\(L_{c}\\) are left unchanged and stay sunny.  The resulting