he argument relies only on elementary counting and the pigeon\u2011hole principle; no hidden assumptions are made.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nFor a line we use the usual notation \\(L:\\; y=mx+q\\) (with \\(m\\in\\mathbb R\\cup\\{\\infty\\}\\)).  \nA line is **sunny** iff its slope \\(m\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nm\\neq 0,\\qquad m\\neq\\infty,\\qquad m\\neq -1 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Existence of a covering with any prescribed number of sunny lines)\n\n*Let \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nThen there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) such that*\n\n1. *every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and*  \n2. *exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.*\n\n*Proof.*  \nChoose \\(k\\) distinct real numbers  \n\n\\[\nm_1,m_2,\\dots ,m_k\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause the set \\(T_n\\) is finite, we can select \\(k\\) distinct points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(a_i,b_i)\\in T_n\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k)\n\\]\n\nsuch that no two of them share the same abscissa, the same ordinate, nor the same sum \\(a_i+b_i\\).  \n(This is possible: start with any point of \\(T_n\\); each time we pick a new point we avoid at most three previously used\nvalues of \\(a\\), of \\(b\\) and of \\(a+b\\); since each of the three quantities can take at most \\(n\\) different values, after\nchoosing at most \\(n\\) points we never run out of admissible points.)\n\nFor each \\(i\\) define the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=m_i x + (b_i-m_i a_i).\n\\]\n\nBy construction \\(L_i\\) passes through \\(P_i\\) and has slope \\(m_i\\); hence it is sunny.  \nThe \\(k\\) lines are distinct because they have distinct slopes.\n\nNow remove the \\(k\\) points \\(P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\) from \\(T_n\\); denote the