rom the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V_{n-1}=\\{\\,x=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich are \\(n-1\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\le n-1\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal V_{n-1}\\).  \nThe only point of \\(T_n\\) whose abscissa equals \\(n\\) is \\((n,1)\\) (indeed, if \\(a=n\\) then the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b=1\\)).  \nHence the set \\(\\mathcal V_{n-1}\\) covers all points of \\(T_n\\) except possibly \\((n,1)\\).\n\nChoose a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) that passes through \\((n,1)\\).  \nOne convenient choice is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y=\\frac12(x-n)+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell\\) is \\(1/2\\), which is neither \\(0\\) (horizontal), nor \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), nor \\(-1\\); therefore \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_1=\\mathcal V_{n-1}\\cup\\{\\ell\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_1\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which (\\(\\ell\\)) is sunny.  \nBy construction \\((n,1)\\in\\ell\\), and every other point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to a vertical line of \\(\\mathcal V_{n-1}\\).  \nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_1\\), and the required conditions are satisfied. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Consequence  \n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 and Lemma\u202f2 we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0,\\,1\\in\\mathcal K_n\\quad\\text{for all integers }n\\ge 3\\,}.\n\\]\n\nHence for every admissible \\(n\\) there exist configurations of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) that contain either no sunny line or exactly one sunny line.\n\nThe determination of whether larger values of \\(k\\) (i.e. \\(k\\ge 2\\)) can occur remains open. The lemmas above constitute a rigorous partial answer to the original problem. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, gene