er with \\(X,Y\\) cover all points of \\(S_n\\). Thus a configuration\nwith exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines exists.\n\nHence for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2,n\\}\\) a suitable family of lines can be\nconstructed. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (A counting obstruction for \\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\ge4\\))\n\nAssume \\(n\\ge4\\) and suppose there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering\n\\(S_n\\) with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines. Then a contradiction arises.\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet the unique non\u2011sunny line be \\(M\\). By Lemma\u202f2 a non\u2011sunny line can contain at\nmost one point of \\(S_n\\); denote that point (if any) by \\(P\\). All remaining\n\\(|S_n|-1\\) points must be covered by the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\nConsider any sunny line \\(L\\). If \\(L\\) contained three distinct points of\n\\(S_n\\), two of those points would have the same sum \\(a+b\\), forcing the slope\nof \\(L\\) to be \\(-1\\), contradicting sunniness. Hence each sunny line contains at\nmost two points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nTherefore the total number of points that can be covered by the sunny lines is at\nmost \\(2(n-1)\\). We must have  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|-1\\le 2(n-1).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBut for \\(n\\ge4\\),\n\n\\[\n|S_n|-1=\\frac{(n+1)n}{2}-1\n      =\\frac{n^2+n-2}{2}\n      > 2n-2 = 2(n-1),\n\\]\n\nso (2) is impossible. Hence no such configuration exists for \\(n\\ge4\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5 (The case \\(n=3\\))\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) there is no configuration of three distinct lines covering\n\\(S_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\) with exactly two sunny lines (i.e. \\(k=n-1\\)).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf exactly one line were non\u2011sunny, it could cover at most one of the three\npoints (Lemma\u202f2). The remaining two points would have to lie on the two sunny\nlines. Any sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(S_3\\), but the two\nremaining poin