hat are useful are vertical.\n\n   A vertical line covers an entire column, i.e. all \\(|C_a|\\) points of that column.  Hence to cover the points that are not already covered by the sunny lines we need at least  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{|T_n|-sn}{\\max_{a}|C_a|}\\ge\\frac{|T_n|-sn}{n}\n   \\]\n\n   vertical lines.  Since the total number of lines is \\(n\\),\n\n   \\[\n   n\\ge s+\\frac{|T_n|-sn}{n}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad sn\\ge |T_n|-n^{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(|T_n|=n(n+1)/2\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   sn\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-n^{2}= -\\frac{n}{2},\n   \\]\n\n   which forces \\(s\\le 1\\) (because \\(s\\) is a non\u2011negative integer).  Thus **at most one sunny line can appear** in any admissible configuration, i.e. \\(k\\le 1\\).\n\n3. **Realisation of the two possibilities.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n .\n   \\]\n\n   Each line \\(x=a\\) contains the whole column \\(C_a\\); therefore every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of these lines.  None of the lines is sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the same \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-1\n   \\]\n\n   together with one additional sunny line, for instance  \n\n   \\[\n   y=x+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(y=x+1\\) has slope \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence it is sunny and distinct from all vertical lines.  The vertical lines already cover every column \\(C_a\\) for \\(1\\le a\\le n-1\\); the remaining column \\(C_n=\\{(n,1)\\}\\) is also covered because \\((n,1)\\) satisfies \\(1=n+1-\\,\\) (indeed \\(1=n+1-n\\)), and it lies on the vertical line \\(x=n\\) **which we have not taken**.  However the point \\((n,1)\\) is already covered by the sunny line \\(y=x+1\\) (since \\(1=n+1\\) is false, check: plugging \\(x=n\\) gives \\(y=n+1\\), not 1). Oops need a sunny line that passes through \\((n,1)\\). Choose the sunny line  \n\n   \\[