e \\(x\\)-axis, the line \\(x+y=0\\), nor the \\(y\\)-axis).  \nThen for any fixed \\(a\\) the set \\(L\\cap C_a\\) contains **at most one** point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(L\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\neq\\infty\\).  Substituting \\(x=a\\) yields a single value \\(y=ma+b\\); therefore at most one lattice point of the column can satisfy the equation. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Upper bound on the number of sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(T_n\\).  \nIf \\(s\\) denotes the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) (i.e. lines whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)), then \\(s\\le 1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nEach sunny line can meet a given column in at most one point by Lemma\u202f1; consequently the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered by the \\(s\\) sunny lines is at most \\(s\\cdot n\\) (there are \\(n\\) columns).  \n\nHence at least  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|-sn=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-sn\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\npoints must be covered by the remaining \\(n-s\\) lines.  \nA line that is not vertical can also meet a column in at most one point, so the only way to cover **more than one** point of a column is to use a **vertical** line, which covers the whole column.  Therefore we may assume, without loss of generality, that all non\u2011sunny lines that are useful are vertical.\n\nEach vertical line covers an entire column, i.e. at most \\(\\max_{a}|C_a|=n\\) points.  Consequently we need at least  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{|T_n|-sn}{n}\n\\]\n\nvertical lines.  Since the total number of lines equals \\(n\\),\n\n\\[\nn\\ge s+\\frac{|T_n|-sn}{n}.\n\\]\n\nMultiplying by \\(n\\) and rearranging gives  \n\n\\[\nsn\\ge |T_n|-n^{2}.\n\\]\n\nUsing (3) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nsn\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-n^{2}= -\\frac{n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(sn\\) is a non\u2011negative integer, the inequality forces \\(s\\le 1\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Existence for \\(k=0\\))\n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n\n\\]\n\ncover every point of \\(T_n\\).  None of them is sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n*Proof.*  By definition, the line \\(x=a\\) contains the whole column \\(C_a\\); the columns \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_n\\) partition \\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Existence for \\(k=1\\))\n\nFor every \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly one sunny line that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-1 .\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover all columns \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_{n-1}\\).  \nThe only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\in C_n\\).\n\nChoose any sunny line that passes through \\((n,1)\\); for concreteness set  \n\n\\[\nL:\\; y = -\\frac12 x + \\frac{n+2}{2}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(-\\frac12\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so \\(L\\) is sunny and distinct from the vertical lines.  Substituting \\(x=n\\) into (5) gives \\(y=1\\), confirming that \\((n,1)\\in L\\).  Hence the union of the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines and \\(L\\) covers every point of \\(T_n\\).  Exactly one line (namely \\(L\\)) is sunny, so \\(k=1\\) is realised. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem (Answer to the problem)\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,\\,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLemma\u202f2 shows that any admissible family can contain at most one sunny line, i.e. \\(k\\le 1\\).  \nLemmas\u202f3 and\u202f4 exhibit families with \\(k=0\\) and with \\(k=1\\).  Therefore the only possible values are \\(0\\) and \\(1\\)