are accurate for all $n\\ge3$.\n\n### 3. Lemma\u202f1 (Intersection of a non\u2011vertical line with a column)\n> \u201cLet $L$ be a line whose slope is finite and different from $0$ and $-1$ \u2026 then $L\\cap C_a$ contains **at most one** point of $T_n$.\u201d\n\n*Correct.*  Writing $L$ as $y=mx+b$ gives a unique $y$ for $x=a$, so at most one lattice point of the column can lie on $L$.\n\n### 4. Lemma\u202f2 (Upper bound on the number of sunny lines)\n\n#### 4.1 Statement of the Lemma\n> \u201cIf $s$ denotes the number of sunny lines \u2026 then $s\\le 1$.\u201d\n\n*This is the claim that later turns out to be false.*\n\n#### 4.2 First part of the proof (counting points covered by sunny lines)\n> \u201cEach sunny line can meet a given column in at most one point \u2026 the total number of points of $T_n$ that can be covered by the $s$ sunny lines is at most $s\\cdot n$ \u2026 Hence at least $|T_n|-sn$ points must be covered by the remaining $n-s$ lines.\u201d\n\n*Correct reasoning.*  Since there are $n$ columns, a sunny line contributes at most one point per column, so the bound $s\\cdot n$ is valid.\n\n#### 4.3 Claim about non\u2011sunny lines\n> \u201cA line that is not vertical can also meet a column in at most one point, so the only way to cover **more than one** point of a column is to use a **vertical** line \u2026 we may assume, without loss of generality, that all non\u2011sunny lines that are useful are vertical.\u201d\n\n*Justification Gap.*  The argument correctly observes that a non\u2011vertical line cannot cover two points of the **same** column, but it does **not** justify that we may discard horizontal or $x+y=0$ lines altogether. Horizontal lines can cover many points **across different columns** (one per column), and diagonal lines ($x+y=0$) can also cover many points. Ignoring these possibilities may weaken the bound, and the \u201cwithout loss of generality\u2019\u2019 claim is not proved.\n\n#### 4.4 Derivation of the inequality\n> \u201cEach