tinct lines such that  \n\n* every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\);  \n* exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) are sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFix the point \\(P_0=(0,0)\\notin T_n\\).\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) let \\(L_i\\) be the line through \\(P_0\\) and the lattice point \\((i,\\,n+1-i)\\). Its equation is  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=\\frac{n+1-i}{i}\\,x .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(i\\ge1\\) and \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n* the slope \\(\\frac{n+1-i}{i}\\) is finite, so \\(L_i\\) is not vertical;\n* the slope is positive, hence different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\);\n* the slope is not \\(0\\) (the numerator \\(n+1-i\\ge2\\));\n* the slope is not \\(-1\\) (both numerator and denominator are positive).\n\nThus each \\(L_i\\) is sunny. The slopes are distinct for different \\(i\\), so the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are distinct. Moreover \\(L_i\\) contains the point \\((i,\\,n+1-i)\\), i.e. the top point of column \\(C_i\\).\n\nFor every column index \\(a\\) that is **not** among \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) (that is, for \\(a=k+1,\\dots ,n\\)) define the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_a:\\; x=a .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(V_a\\) contains the whole column \\(C_a\\).\n\nNow set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\cup\\{V_{k+1},\\dots ,V_n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines.  \nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to some column \\(C_a\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le k\\) then the point \\((a,\\,n+1-a)\\) (the highest point of \\(C_a\\)) lies on the sunny line \\(L_a\\); all other points of the same column are also covered because the vertical line \\(V_a\\) is **not** present, but they are already covered by the vertical lines \\(V_{k+1},\\dots ,V_n\\) for other columns? Wait: for \\(a\\le k\\) we have not taken \\(V_a\\). However each column \\(C_a\\) with \\(a\\le k\\) contains exactly the points \\((a,b)\\) where \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\). The line \\(L_a\\) passes through the point \\((a,n+1-a)\\) only; th