            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) define the column  \n\n\\[\nC_a=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}=T_n\\cap\\{x=a\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n|C_a|=n+1-a\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}|C_a|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Maximum number of points a line can cover)\n\nEvery line in the plane meets each column \\(C_a\\) in at most one point, except a vertical line \\(x=a\\) which meets the whole column. Consequently **any** line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is not vertical it has an equation \\(y=mx+b\\) with a finite slope \\(m\\). Substituting \\(x=a\\) yields a single value of \\(y\\); hence at most one point of \\(C_a\\) lies on the line. A vertical line \\(x=a\\) meets exactly the points of \\(C_a\\), whose cardinality is at most \\(n\\) (the largest column is \\(C_1\\) with \\(|C_1|=n\\)). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Existence for every \\(k\\), \\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family \\(\\mat