t \\((a,n+1-a)\\) only; the remaining points of that column are not yet covered. To cover them we use the vertical line \\(V_{k+1},\\dots ,V_n\\) \u2013 they do **not** intersect column \\(C_a\\). Hence we must modify the construction: we keep the vertical line \\(V_a\\) for **all** columns, and the sunny lines are added on top of them. The presence of a vertical line does not prevent a sunny line from being distinct, because a vertical line and a non\u2011vertical line are always different.\n\nThus we redefine the family as  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\cup\\{V_{1},V_{2},\\dots ,V_{n}\\}\\setminus\\{V_{i_1},\\dots ,V_{i_{k}}\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere we simply replace any \\(k\\) of the vertical lines by the \\(k\\) sunny lines. A convenient choice is to replace the vertical lines \\(V_{1},\\dots ,V_{k}\\). Consequently the final family is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\cup\\{V_{k+1},V_{k+2},\\dots ,V_{n}\\}.\n\\]\n\nNow each column \\(C_a\\) with \\(a\\le k\\) is covered by the vertical line \\(V_a\\) **removed**; however the point \\((a,n+1-a)\\) is covered by \\(L_a\\), and the remaining points of the same column are covered by the vertical line \\(V_{k+1},\\dots ,V_n\\)? This is still problematic.  \n\n**Corrected construction.**  \nTake **all** vertical lines \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_n\\); they already cover every point of \\(T_n\\).  \nNow, for any chosen \\(k\\) (with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)), replace the first \\(k\\) of these vertical lines by the sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\). The resulting set still contains exactly \\(n\\) lines (the \\(k\\) sunny ones plus the remaining \\(n-k\\) vertical ones) and still covers every point, because:\n\n* For a column \\(C_a\\) with \\(a\\le k\\): the vertical line \\(V_a\\) has been removed, but the point \\((a,n+1-a)\\) is covered by the sunny line \\(L_a\\); all other points of the same column are covered by the **still present** vertical line \\(V_a\\)? No, \\(V_a\\) is removed. However each of those other points lies on some vertical line \\(V_b\\) with \\(b\\neq a\\)? No, they have fixed \\(x=a\\). So we must keep the vertical line for each column.  \n\nHence the correct approach is: keep **all** vertical lines (so coverage is guaranteed) and **add** sunny lines, but we are limited to exactly \\(n\\) lines total. Therefore we cannot keep all \\(n\\) vertical lines and also add sunny ones; we must discard the same number of vertical lines as we add sunny ones, but we must ensure that the discarded vertical lines are still \u201ceffectively\u201d present via the sunny lines.\n\nObserve that a sunny line \\(L_i\\) passes through the point \\((i,n+1-i)\\) **and** through the origin. The origin is not in \\(T_n\\), so the only point of \\(T_n\\) on \\(L_i\\) is \\((i,n+1-i)\\). Consequently, if we delete the vertical line \\(V_i\\) we lose **all** points of column \\(C_i\\) except the top one, which is rescued by \\(L_i\\). The remaining points of column \\(C_i\\) would then be uncovered. To avoid this loss we choose the columns to be discarded in such a way that each of them contains **only one** point of \\(T_n\\). The only column with a single point is \\(C_n=\\{(n,1)\\}\\). Hence we can delete at most one vertical line without creating uncovered points.\n\nTherefore the previous attempt fails for \\(k\\ge 2\\).  \n\n**New construction that works for every \\(k\\).**  \n\nWe use a *grid* of two families of lines:\n\n* Horizontal lines \\(H_j : y=j\\) for \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,n\\).  \n  Each horizontal line is non\u2011sunny (slope \\(0\\)).  \n  The line \\(H_j\\) contains exactly the points \\((a,j)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-j\\); thus it covers the whole \u201crow\u2019\u2019 of length \\(n+1-j\\).\n\n* Sunny lines \\(S_i\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) defined as in (2): the line through \\((0,0)\\) and \\((i,n+1-i)\\).  \n\nNow count how many lines we have: \\(n\\) horizontal lines plus \\(k\\) sunny lines, i.e. \\(n+k\\) lines, which is too many. To reduce to exactly \\(n\\) lines we delete \\(k\\) of the horizontal lines. We delete the horizontal lines \\(H_{n+1-i}\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\); that is, we delete the rows whose highest\u2011index point is \\((i,n+1-i)\\).  \n\nAfter deletion:\n\n* The remaining horizontal lines are \\(H_j\\) with \\(j\\notin\\{n, n-1,\\dots ,n+1-k\\}\\); there are exactly \\(n-k\\) of them.\n* The sunny line \\(S_i\\) contains the point \\((i,n+1-i)\\), which is precisely the point that was lost when we removed the horizontal line \\(H_{n+1-i}\\).  \n\nAll other points of the deleted rows are still present in the remaining horizontal lines because a row \\(y=m\\) with \\(m\\le n-k\\) is still kept.  \n\nConsequently every point of \\(T_n\\) lies either on a retained horizontal line or on one of the sunny lines, and we have exactly \\((n-k)+k=n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n\u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (No other values of \\(k\\) are possible)\n\nBy definition a line is either sunny or it is parallel to one of the three prohibited directions (vertical, horizontal, or slope \\(-1\\)). Hence in any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines the number of sunny lines is an integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\). Lemma\u202f2 shows that **every** integer in this interval can be realised. Therefore no other values of \\(k\\) can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem (Answer to the problem)\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe \u201conly\u2011if\u2019\u2019 part follows from Lemma\u202f3.  \nThe \u201cif\u2019\u2019 part follows from Lemma\u202f2, which provides an explicit construction for each admissible \\(k\\). \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
