ma\u202f1 a sunny line can contribute at most one point of such a column, so at least one point of \\(C_a\\) would remain uncovered unless the line \\(x=a\\) (which contains the whole column) is present in \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nHence for each \\(a=2,3,\\dots ,n\\) the vertical line \\(x=a\\) must belong to \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at least \\(n-1\\) vertical lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Corollary 3  \nIn a covering family consisting of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of sunny lines is at most \\(0\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma\u202f2 at least \\(n-1\\) of the \\(n\\) lines must be vertical.  \nIf a sunny line were added, the remaining line count would be \\(n-(n-1)-1=0\\); i.e. the vertical line \\(x=1\\) would be missing.  \nColumn \\(C_1\\) contains \\(n\\) points (by (1) with \\(a=1\\)).  \nA sunny line can intersect \\(C_1\\) in at most one point (Lemma\u202f1), leaving at least \\(n-1\\) points of \\(C_1\\) uncovered\u2014a contradiction.  \nTherefore no sunny line can appear; the number of sunny lines must be \\(0\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Existence for \\(k=0\\))\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n\\,\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, none of which is sunny, and it covers every point of \\(T_n\\) because each point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(x=a\\) and hence lies on the line \\(x=a\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem  \n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0\\}} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nCorollary\u202f3 shows that any admissible configuration can contain no sunny lines, i.e. \\(k\\le 0\\).  \nLemma\u202f4 pr