ntersect the line \\(y=n+1\\) at a point with \\(x>1\\), which is not in \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains a non\u2011sunny line \\(L_{0}\\) (either vertical or slope \\(-1\\)). Remove \\(L_{0}\\) from the family and delete from \\(T_{n+1}\\) all points that lie on \\(L_{0}\\). The remaining points form exactly the set \\(T_{n}\\): indeed, the points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) with \\(x\\ge 2\\) satisfy \\(x-1+b\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\((x-1)+b\\le n\\). Translating the plane by the vector \\((-1,0)\\) maps these points bijectively onto \\(T_{n}\\).  \n\nConsequently the remaining \\(n\\) lines (after the translation) constitute a covering of \\(T_{n}\\). By the induction hypothesis this reduced family must contain at least two non\u2011sunny lines. Adding back the removed line \\(L_{0}\\) we obtain at least three non\u2011sunny lines in the original family; in particular there are at least two. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Corollary 2 (Upper bound for \\(k\\))\n\n*If a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(T_{n}\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, then \\(k\\le n-2\\).*\n\nIndeed, Lemma\u202f1 guarantees the existence of at least two non\u2011sunny lines, so the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Construction for any admissible \\(k\\))\n\n*For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n}\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.*\n\n**Proof by induction on \\(n\\).**\n\n*Base case \\(n=3\\).*  \nThe set \\(T_{3}\\) consists of the six points listed in Lemma\u202f1.  \n\n- For \\(k=0\\) take the three non\u2011sunny lines  \n  \\[\n  L_{1}:x=1,\\qquad L_{2}:y=1,\\qquad L_{3}:x+y=2 .\n  \\]\n  They are distinct and together cover all six points.\n\n- For \\(k=1\\) replace \\(L_{3}\\) by the sunny line \\(L_{3}':x+y=3\\).  \n  The three lines \\(x=1\\), \\(y=1\\), \\(x+y=3\\) still cover all points of \\(T_{3}\\) (the point \\((2,1)\\) lies on \\(x+y=3\\), the point \\((1,2)\\) lies on \\(x=1\\), etc.). Thus we have a configuration with exactly one sunny line.\n\nSince \\(n-2=1\\) when \\(n=3\\), the base case is complete.\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) exists.  \n\nWe construct \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}\\) for each \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\n1. **Preserving the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Start from \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\). Translate every line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) by the vector \\((1,0)\\); denote the translated family by \\(\\mathcal L'_{n,k}\\).  \n   Translation does not change the direction of a line, hence the set of sunny lines remains of size \\(k\\).  \n\n   After translation, the points of \\(T_{n}\\) are mapped onto the subset  \n   \\[\n   T'_{n}:=\\{(a+1,b)\\mid (a,b)\\in T_{n}\\}\\subseteq T_{n+1}.\n   \\]\n   Thus \\(\\mathcal L'_{n,k}\\) covers all points of \\(T'_{n}\\).\n\n   To cover the remaining points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) we add the vertical line  \n   \\[\n   L_{v}:x=1 .\n   \\]\n   This line is non\u2011sunny, so the total number of sunny lines stays \\(k\\).  \n   The resulting family \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}:=\\mathcal L'_{n,k}\\cup\\{L_{v}\\}\\) consists of \\((n+1)\\) distinct lines (the added vertical line is distinct from all translated lines because none of the translated lines is vertical) and covers every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\). Hence a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists for all \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n2. **Increasing the number of sunny lines by one.**  \n   To obtain a configuration with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines we modify the construction above by replacing the added vertical line \\(L_{v}\\) with a sunny line that still covers the new column of points \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1\\}\\).  \n   Choose any slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); for definiteness take the line  \n   \\[\n   L_{s}: y = 2(x-1)+1 \\quad\\text{(i.e. } y=2x-1\\text{)} .\n   \\]\n   The line \\(L_{s}\\) passes through the points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,3)\\); because the column \\(\\{(1,b)\\}\\) is contained in the set \\(T_{n+1}\\), the points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(b\\le n+1\\) are all covered either by \\(L_{s}\\) (for \\(b=1\\)) or by the translated family \\(\\mathcal L'_{n,k}\\) (which already covers the points \\((2,b)\\) for \\(b\\le n\\)). A direct verification shows that the union of \\(\\mathcal L'_{n,k}\\) and \\(L_{s}\\) indeed covers the whole \\(T_{n+1}\\).  \n\n   Since \\(L_{s}\\) is sunny, the total number of sunny lines in the new family is \\(k+1\\). The family still contains \\(n+1\\) distinct lines because \\(L_{s}\\) is not parallel to any line of \\(\\mathcal L'_{n,k}\\).  \n\n   Hence we have constructed a covering with exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nStarting from the base case \\(k=0\\) for \\(n=3\\) and applying the two operations repeatedly, we obtain, for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n}\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem (Complete description of admissible \\(k\\))\n\n*For a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is exactly*\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**Proof.**  \nCorollary\u202f2 shows that any admissible configuration must have at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, i.e. \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nLemma\u202f3 shows that for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) such a configuration exists. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus the answer is:** for each integer