            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1. Notation\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese are exactly the lattice points that must lie on at least one of the lines.  \nA line is called **sunny** if its direction is *not* parallel to any of the three forbidden directions  \n\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal }(y=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{vertical }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{slope }-1\\;(x+y=\\text{const}).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2. Upper bound\n\n#### Lemma 1  \n*For every \\(n\\ge 3\\), any family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_{n}\\) contains at least two non\u2011sunny lines.*\n\n**Proof.**  \nWe argue by induction on \\(n\\).\n\n*Base case \\(n=3\\).*  \n\\(T_{3}\\) consists of the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n\\]\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that at most one of the three covering lines is non\u2011sunny. Then at least two lines are sunny, i.e. have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\). Two distinct sunny lines intersect in a single point, so together they can contain at most three points of \\(T_{3}\\) (the intersection point and at most one further point on each line). The third line can contain at most three additional points, giving a total of at most six points. In order to cover all six points the third line would have to pass through the intersection of the first two, contradicting the hypothesis that the three lines are distinct. Hence at least two lines must be non\u2011sunny.\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the statement holds for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) and consider a covering \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(T_{n+1}\\) by \\(n+1\\) distinct lines.  \nThe point \\((1,n+1)\\in T_{n+1}\\) lies on the leftmost column of the triangle. Any line containing this point must be either\n\n* the vertical line \\(x=1\\), or\n* a line of slope \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)),\n\nbecause a line of any other slope would intersect the horizontal line \\(y=n+1\\) at a point with \\(x>1\\), which is not in \\(T_{n+1}\\). Consequently \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains a non\u2011sunny line \\(L_{0}\\).\n\nDelete \\(L_{0}\\) from \\(\\mathcal L\\) and delete from \\(T_{n+1}\\) all points that lie on \\(L_{0}\\). The remaining points are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\in T_{n+1}\\mid a\\ge 2\\}\n   =\\{(a-1,b)\\mid (a,b)\\in T_{n+1},\\,a\\ge 2\\}\n   =T_{n},\n\\]\n\nthe last equality following from the definition of \\(T_{n}\\). Translating the plane by the vector \\((-1,0)\\) carries the remaining \\(n\\) lines onto a family \\(\\mathcal L'\\) that covers \\(T_{n}\\). By the induction hypothesis \\(\\mathcal L'\\) contains at least two non\u2011sunny lines; translating back, \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at least those two lines together with \\(L_{0}\\). Hence \\(\\mathcal L\\) has at least two non\u2011sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Corollary 2 (Upper bound for the number of sunny lines)\n\nIf a covering family of \\(n\\) distinct lines contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, then by Lemma\u202f1 it must contain at least two non\u2011sunny lines, i.e.\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3. Existence of families with any admissible \\(k\\)\n\nWe now show that the bound of Corollary\u202f2 is sharp.\n\n#### Lemma 3  \n*For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_{n}\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.*\n\n**Proof.**  \nInduction on \\(n\\).\n\n*Base case \\(n=3\\).*  \n\n- \\(k=0\\): take the three non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  L_{1}:x=1,\\qquad L_{2}:y=1,\\qquad L_{3}:x+y=2 .\n  \\]\n\n  They are distinct and together contain all six points of \\(T_{3}\\).\n\n- \\(k=1\\): replace \\(L_{3}\\) by the sunny line \\(L_{3}':x+y=3\\).  \n  The three lines \\(x=1\\), \\(y=1\\), \\(x+y=3\\) still cover every point of \\(T_{3}\\) (the point \\((2,1)\\) lies on \\(x+y=3\\), the point \\((1,2)\\) lies on \\(x=1\\), etc.). Thus a configuration with exactly one sunny line exists.\n\nSince \\(n-2=1\\) when \\(n=3\\), the base case is complete.\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) exists.  \n\nWe construct families for \\(n+1\\).\n\n---\n\n**(i) Preserving the number of sunny lines.**  \n\nTake \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) and translate every line by the vector \\((1,0)\\); denote the translated family by \\(\\mathcal L'_{n,k}\\). Translation does not alter the direction of a line, hence \\(\\mathcal L'_{n,k}\\) still contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\nThe translation maps the set \\(T_{n}\\) onto  \n\n\\[\nT'_{n}:=\\{(a+1,b)\\mid (a,b)\\in T_{n}\\}\\subseteq T_{n+1},\n\\]\n\nso \\(\\mathcal L'_{n,k}\\) covers all points of \\(T'_{n}\\).\n\nTo cover the remaining column  \n\n\\[\nC:=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1\\}=T_{n+1}\\setminus T'_{n},\n\\]\n\nadd the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nL_{v}:x=1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(L_{v}\\) is non\u2011sunny, therefore the total number of sunny lines in  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}:=\\mathcal L'_{n,k}\\cup\\{L_{v}\\}\n\\]\n\nremains \\(k\\). The family consists of \\((n+1)\\) distinct lines (none of the translated lines is vertical), and it covers every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) because points of \\(C\\) lie on \\(L_{v}\\) and all other points lie in \\(T'_{n}\\). Thus a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists for every \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n---\n\n**(ii) Increasing the number of sunny lines by one.**  \n\nStarting from the family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) we again translate it to obtain \\(\\mathcal L'_{n,k}\\). Instead of the vertical line \\(L_{v}\\) we now add the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_{s}: y = x .\n\\]\n\n\\(L_{s}\\) has slope \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence it is sunny.  \n\n*Coverage of the new column.*  \nThe point \\((1,1)\\) lies on \\(L_{s}\\). For any \\(b\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n+1\\) the point \\((1,b)\\) belongs to the set \\(C\\) and can be written as \\((1,b)=(2,b-1)- (1,0)\\). The point \\((2,b-1)\\) lies in \\(T'_{n}\\) (because \\((2,b-1)=(1+(b-1),b-1)\\) satisfies \\((2)+(b-1)\\le n+2\\)), and therefore is covered by some line of \\(\\mathcal L'_{n,k}\\). Translating that covering line back by \\((-1,0)\\) shows that \\((1,b)\\) is also covered by the same line of \\(\\mathcal L'_{n,k}\\). Consequently every point of \\(C\\) is covered either by \\(L_{s}\\) (the point \\((1,1)\\)) or by a line of \\(\\mathcal L'_{n,k}\\).\n\nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,k+1}:=\\mathcal L'_{n,k}\\cup\\{L_{s}\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers all of \\(T_{n+1}\\), consists of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, and contains exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines (the \\(k\\) sunny lines of \\(\\mathcal L'_{n,k}\\) plus the newly added \\(L_{s}\\)).\n\n---\n\nStarting from the base families for \\(n=3\\) and applying repeatedly the constructions (i) and (ii), we obtain for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) satisfying the required conditions. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4. Conclusion\n\nFrom Corollary\u202f2 we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nLemma\u202f3 shows that this condition is also sufficient.  \n\nHence the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**The problem is solved.**\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
