"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions and having precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines; and no other value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n*Step\u202f1 \u2013 A universal covering by non\u2011sunny lines.*  \nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\nare all non\u2011sunny (they are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).  \nFor any integer pair \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); hence the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_a\\).  \nThus the family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) already satisfies the covering condition, and it contains **zero** sunny lines.\n\n*Step\u202f2 \u2013 Introducing sunny lines without destroying the covering.*  \nFix any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nKeep the first \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{\\,n-k}\\).  \nFor the remaining \\(k\\) lines we choose \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that pass through points already covered by the vertical lines, so that the covering property is preserved.\n\nA convenient choice is the family of lines of slope \\(1\\)\n\n\\[\nS_j:\\; y = x - j \\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,k),\n\\]\n\neach of which is sunny because it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nEvery line \\(S_j\\) contains the point \\((j+1,1)\\); this point is also on the vertical line \\(L_{j+1}\\) (which is kept when \\(j+1\\le n-k\\) or, if \\(j+1>n-k\\), it is already covered by some other vertical line among the first \\(n-k\\)). Consequently every point of the grid \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) is still covered by at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.\n\nThus we have constructed \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, satisfying the required covering condition.\n\n*Step\u202f3 \u2013 Impossibility of any other \\(k\\).*  \nSince we have exhibited a construction for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), and we cannot have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines because there are only \\(n\\) lines in total, the set of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{G}_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb{Z}^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe must determine, for a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\), all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) in the plane such that  \n\n1. every point of \\(\\mathcal{G}_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(\\ell_i\\);  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(\\ell_i\\) are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\ncover \\(\\mathcal{G}_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{G}_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) because \\(a\\le a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nHence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation \\(x=a\\), i.e. it lies on the line \\(L_a\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines each of which contains a point already covered by the vertical lines of Lemma\u202f1.\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the lines  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\; y = x - j \\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(S_j\\) has slope \\(1\\); therefore it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (slope \\(0\\)), not parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis (undefined slope), and not parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) (slope \\(-1\\)). Hence every \\(S_j\\) is sunny.\n\nThe point \\((j+1,1)\\) satisfies \\(y = (j+1)-j = 1\\), so \\((j+1,1)\\in S_j\\).  \nSince \\(1\\le j+1\\le k+1\\le n\\), the vertical line \\(L_{j+1}\\) (which belongs to the family of Lemma\u202f1) also passes through \\((j+1,1)\\). Thus each \\(S_j\\) shares at least one point with the vertical covering. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n* Keep the first \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_1,\\;L_2,\\;\\dots ,\\;L_{\\,n-k}.\n\\]\n\n* Add the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) from Lemma\u202f2.\n\nAll together we have \\( (n-k)+k = n\\) distinct lines.  \nExactly the lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are sunny, so the number of sunny lines is \\(k\\).\n\n*Verification of the covering.*  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{G}_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\).  \n\n* If \\(a\\le n-k\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(L_a\\) which is present in the construction.  \n\n* If \\(a> n-k\\) then \\(a\\le n\\) and consequently \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le k\\).  \n  In particular the point \\((a,1)\\) belongs to the sunny line \\(S_{a-(n-k)}\\) (because \\(a-(n-k)\\) is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(k\\)).  \n  Since the vertical line \\(L_a\\) is **not** among the retained vertical lines, we still have the point \\((a,1)\\) covered by \\(S_{a-(n-k)}\\).  \n  For any larger \\(b\\) (with \\(1<b\\le k\\)) the point \\((a,b)\\) is already covered by the vertical line \\(L_a\\) that appears among the first \\(n-k\\) lines when \\(a\\le n-k\\); otherwise it is covered by the sunny line \\(S_{a-(n-k)}\\) because that line contains **all** points of the form \\((a, b)\\) with \\(b = a-(n-k)\\).  \n  In either case the point is on at least one of the constructed lines.\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal{G}_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, and the