"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not obtained a complete classification of the admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nNevertheless I have proved the following decisive partial result:\n\n> **Lemma.**  \n> For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) it is impossible to cover all lattice points  \n> \\[\n>   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n> \\]\n> with exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines **none** of which is sunny (i.e. every line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n> Consequently any admissible configuration must contain at least one sunny line, i.e. \\(k\\ge 1\\).\n\nThe proof proceeds by contradiction and uses only elementary combinatorial geometry.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe argument is organized as follows.\n\n1. **Reduction to three families.**  \n   Because a non\u2011sunny line can be of only three possible directions, the set of \\(n\\) lines can be partitioned into three families:\n   \\[\n     \\mathcal H=\\{\\text{horizontal lines}\\},\\qquad\n     \\mathcal V=\\{\\text{vertical lines}\\},\\qquad\n     \\mathcal D=\\{\\text{lines parallel to }x+y=0\\}.\n   \\]\n   Let \\(|\\mathcal H|=h,\\;|\\mathcal V|=v,\\;|\\mathcal D|=d\\); thus \\(h+v+d=n\\).\n\n2. **Counting points on each family.**  \n   - A horizontal line \\(y=c\\) can contain at most one point of \\(S_n\\) for each admissible abscissa \\(a\\); hence it can cover at most \\(n+1-c\\) points of \\(S_n\\). Summing over all horizontal lines gives an upper bound \\(H\\le \\sum_{c\\in C_H}(n+1-c)\\), where \\(C_H\\) is the set of distinct ordinates of the horizontal lines.  \n   - An analogous bound holds for the vertical family.  \n   - A line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=t\\). Such a line meets \\(S_n\\) precisely in the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=t\\) and \\(1\\le a,b\\le t-1\\). Hence it can contain at most \\(t-1\\) points, and the total number of points covered by all diagonal lines is at most \\(\\sum_{t\\in T_D}(t-1)\\), where \\(T_D\\) is the set of distinct constants \\(t\\) occurring.\n\n3. **Summation of the three bounds.**  \n   Adding the three estimates yields an overall upper bound for the number of points of \\(S_n\\) that can be covered by the \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines:\n   \\[\n     |S_n|\\le\n       \\underbrace{\\sum_{c\\in C_H}(n+1-c)}_{\\text{horizontal}}\n     + \\underbrace{\\sum_{c\\in C_V}(n+1-c)}_{\\text{vertical}}\n     + \\underbrace{\\sum_{t\\in T_D}(t-1)}_{\\text{diagonal}} .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Optimization of the right\u2011hand side.**  \n   The right\u2011hand side of (1) is maximised when the sets \\(C_H, C_V, T_D\\) consist of the smallest possible distinct integers, because each summand is a decreasing function of its index.  \n   Consequently the maximal total is attained when the \\(h\\) horizontal lines use the ordinates \\(1,2,\\dots ,h\\), the \\(v\\) vertical lines use the abscissae \\(1,2,\\dots ,v\\), and the \\(d\\) diagonal lines use the constants \\(2,3,\\dots ,d+1\\) (the constant \\(1\\) would give the empty line \\(x+y=1\\)).  \n   Substituting these choices into (1) gives\n   \\[\n     |S_n|\\le\n       \\sum_{i=1}^{h}(n+1-i)\n     + \\sum_{i=1}^{v}(n+1-i)\n     + \\sum_{i=2}^{d+1}(i-1)\n     = h(n+1)-\\frac{h(h+1)}{2}\n       + v(n+1)-\\frac{v(v+1)}{2}\n       + \\frac{d(d+1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Contradiction.**  \n   The set \\(S_n\\) contains exactly\n   \\[\n     |S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n   \\]\n   points.  \n   Using \\(h+v+d=n\\) and elementary algebra, inequality (2) becomes\n   \\[\n     \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le\n       n(n+1)-\\frac{h(h+1)}{2}-\\frac{v(v+1)}{2}\n       +\\frac{d(d+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n   Rearranging,\n   \\[\n     \\frac{h(h+1)}{2}+\\frac{v(v+1)}{2}\n     \\le \\frac{d(d+1)}{2}+ \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(d=n-h-v\\), the right\u2011hand side of (3) expands to\n   \\[\n     \\frac{(n-h-v)(n-h-v+1)}{2}+ \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n   A straightforward computation shows that for any non\u2011negative integers \\(h,v\\) with \\(h+v\\le n\\) the inequality (3) is **strictly violated**; equivalently,\n   \\[\n     \\frac{h(h+1)}{2}+\\frac{v(v+1)}{2}\n     > \\frac{(n-h-v)(n-h-v+1)}{2}+ \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n   This contradiction shows that the assumption \u201call \\(n\\) lines are non\u2011sunny\u2019\u2019 cannot hold.\n\nThus any configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) must contain at least one sunny line, i.e. \\(k\\ge 1\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nAssume, for a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\), that there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) such that every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines.  \nSuppose, contrary to the claim, that **none** of the lines is sunny.  \nHence each line is parallel to one of the three directions:\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal }(y=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{vertical }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{diagonal }(x+y=\\text{const}).\n\\]\n\nLet\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{\\ell_i\\mid \\ell_i\\text{ horizontal}\\},\\;\n\\mathcal V=\\{\\ell_i\\mid \\ell_i\\text{ vertical}\\},\\;\n\\mathcal D=\\{\\ell_i\\mid \\ell_i\\text{ diagonal}\\},\n\\]\nand denote\n\\[\nh=|\\mathcal H|,\\qquad v=|\\mathcal V|,\\qquad d=|\\mathcal D|.\n\\]\nClearly \\(h+v+d=n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.1 Points covered by horizontal lines\n\nA horizontal line has equation \\(y=c\\) for some integer \\(c\\ge 1\\).  \nFor a fixed \\(c\\) the admissible abscissae \\(a\\) satisfy\n\\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\) (because \\(a+b=a+c\\le n+1\\)).  \nThus the line \\(y=c\\) can contain at most \\(n+1-c\\) points of the set\n\\[\nS_n:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\nIf the distinct horizontal lines have ordinates \\(c_1,\\dots ,c_h\\) (all different), the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) that lie on any horizontal line is at most\n\\[\nH\\le\\sum_{i=1}^{h}(n+1-c_i).\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n### 2.2 Points covered by vertical lines\n\nThe same reasoning applied to a vertical line \\(x=c\\) yields the bound\n\\[\nV\\le\\sum_{i=1}^{v}(n+1-c'_i),\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\nwhere \\(c'_1,\\dots ,c'_v\\) are the distinct abscissae of the vertical lines.\n\n### 2.3 Points covered by diagonal lines\n\nA line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=t\\) for some integer \\(t\\ge 2\\).  \nInside \\(S_n\\) the condition \\(x+y=t\\) forces \\(1\\le x\\le t-1\\) and \\(1\\le y\\le t-1\\); consequently the line contains exactly \\(t-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \nIf the diagonal lines have constants \\(t_1,\\dots ,t_d\\) (all distinct), the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) lying on any diagonal line is at most\n\\[\nD\\le\\sum_{i=1}^{d}(t_i-1).\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\n### 2.4 Global upper bound\n\nAdding (2.1)\u2013(2.3) we obtain\n\\[\n|S_n|\\le\n\\sum_{i=1}^{h}(n+1-c_i)\n+\\sum_{i=1}^{v}(n+1-c'_i)\n+\\sum_{i=1}^{d}(t_i-1).\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\n### 2.5 Maximising the right\u2011hand side\n\nEach summand in (2.4) is a decreasing function of its index.  \nHence the right\u2011hand side is maximised when the integers\n\\[\nc_1,\\dots ,c_h,\\;c'_1,\\dots ,c'_v,\\;t_1,\\dots ,t_d\n\\]\nare taken as small as possible, subject to being distinct and respecting the geometric constraints (for a diagonal line we need \\(t\\ge 2\\)).  \nThus we may assume\n\\[\nc_i=i\\;(1\\le i\\le h),\\qquad\nc'_i=i\\;(1\\le i\\le v),\\qquad\nt_i=i+1\\;(1\\le i\\le d).\n\\]\n\nSubstituting these choices into (2.4) gives\n\\[\n|S_n|\\le\n\\sum_{i=1}^{h}(n+1-i)\n+\\sum_{i=1}^{v}(n+1-i)\n+\\sum_{i=2}^{d+1}(i-1)\n= h(n+1)-\\frac{h(h+1)}{2}\n  + v(n+1)-\\frac{v(v+1)}{2}\n  + \\frac{d(d+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\n### 2.6 Exact size of \\(S_n\\)\n\nThe set \\(S_n\\) consists of all integer points with positive coordinates lying on or below the line \\(a+b=n+1\\).  \nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) there are exactly \\(s-1\\) points with \\(a+b=s\\).  \nHence\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\n### 2.7 Deriving a contradiction\n\nFrom (2.5) and (2.6) we obtain\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le\nh(n+1)-\\frac{h(h+1)}{2}\n+v(n+1)-\\frac{v(v+1)}{2}\n+\\frac{d(d+1)}{2},\n\\]\nwhere \\(d=n-h-v\\).  \nRearranging,\n\\[\n\\frac{h(h+1)}{2}+\\frac{v(v+1)}{2}\n\\le\n\\frac{(n-h-v)(n-h-v+1)}{2}\n+\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\nDefine \\(p=h\\) and \\(q=v\\).  Since \\(p,q\\ge 0\\) and \\(p+q\\le n\\), a direct expansion of the right\u2011hand side of (2.7) yields\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-p-q)(n-p-q+1)}{2}\n+\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n=\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n+\\frac{(n-p-q)(n-p-q+1)}{2}.\n\\]\nSubtracting the left\u2011hand side of (2.7) we obtain\n\\[\n\\Delta\n:=\\frac{(n-p-q)(n-p-q+1)}{2}\n+\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n-\\frac{p(p+1)}{2}\n-\\frac{q(q+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA short calculation shows\n\\[\n\\Delta\n= \\frac{1}{2}\\bigl[(n-p-q)^2+(n-p-q)+(n^2+n)-p^2-p-q^2-q\\bigr]\n= \\frac{1}{2}\\bigl[(n-p-q)^2-p^2-q^2 + n^2 + n - (p+q)\\bigr].\n\\]\n\nSince \\((n-p-q)^2-p^2-q^2 = n^2-2n(p+q)+(p+q)^2-p^2-q^2\n= n^2-2n(p+q)+2pq\\),\nwe have\n\\[\n\\Delta\n= \\frac{1}{2}\\bigl[n^2-2n(p+q)+2pq + n^2 + n - (p+q)\\bigr]\n= \\frac{1}{2}\\bigl[2n^2 -2n(p+q)+2pq + n - (p+q)\\bigr].\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(pq\\ge 0\\) and \\(p+q\\le n\\), the expression inside the brackets is strictly larger than \\(n^2\\); consequently \\(\\Delta>0\\).  \nThus inequality (2.7) cannot hold; we have reached a contradiction.\n\nThe contradiction arose from the assumption that all \\(n\\) lines are non\u2011sunny.  \nTherefore at least one of the \\(n\\) lines must be sunny.  Hence any admissible configuration satisfies \\(k\\ge 1\\).\n\n\u220e\n\n---\n\n*The above establishes a necessary condition on \\(k\\).  Determining whether every integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) can actually be realised, and whether further restrictions exist, remains open.*"
