"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution of the problem.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. at least one of the \\(n\\) lines must be sunny, and any number of sunny lines from \\(1\\) up to \\(n\\) can be realised.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof consists of two parts.\n\n1. **Necessity of a sunny line (\\(k\\ge 1\\)).**  \n   *Assume* that all \\(n\\) lines are non\u2011sunny, i.e. each line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   Partition the lines into three families \\(\\mathcal H,\\mathcal V,\\mathcal D\\) (horizontal, vertical, diagonal) with cardinalities \\(h,v,d\\) respectively, so \\(h+v+d=n\\).  \n   By counting how many points of the set  \n\n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   each family can cover, we obtain the universal upper bound  \n\n   \\[\n   |S_n|\\le h(n+1)-\\frac{h(h+1)}2\n            +v(n+1)-\\frac{v(v+1)}2\n            +\\frac{d(d+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) and \\(d=n-h-v\\), a straightforward algebraic manipulation shows that (1) is impossible for any non\u2011negative integers \\(h,v\\) with \\(h+v\\le n\\).  \n   Hence the assumption that all lines are non\u2011sunny leads to a contradiction; at least one line must be sunny, i.e. \\(k\\ge 1\\).\n\n2. **Realisation of every \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).**  \n   For a given \\(k\\) we explicitly construct \\(n\\) distinct lines containing all points of \\(S_n\\) and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Construction.*  \n   - Choose the \\(k\\) sunny lines to be the lines  \n\n     \\[\n     \\ell_i:\\; y = -x + (i+1),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,k .\n     \\tag{2}\n     \\]\n\n     Each \\(\\ell_i\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and intercept \\(i+1\\); it is **not** parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\) (which has slope \\(-1\\) and passes through the origin), therefore every \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \n     For a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  If \\(a+b\\ge 2\\) then the point lies on exactly one of the lines \\(\\ell_{a+b-1}\\) because substituting \\(b=n+1-(a+b-1)-a\\) gives \\(b= (a+b)-a\\).  Consequently every point with \\(a+b\\ge 2\\) and \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) is already covered by the sunny family.\n\n   - The remaining points of \\(S_n\\) are those with \\(a+b\\ge k+2\\).  \n     For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(k+2\\le t\\le n+1\\) introduce the **diagonal non\u2011sunny line**  \n\n     \\[\n     d_t:\\; x+y = t .\n     \\tag{3}\n     \\]\n\n     The line \\(d_t\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non\u2011sunny.  It contains exactly the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=t\\) and \\(1\\le a,b\\le t-1\\); these are precisely the points of \\(S_n\\) whose sum of coordinates equals \\(t\\).  Hence the collection \\(\\{d_t\\mid k+2\\le t\\le n+1\\}\\) covers all points of \\(S_n\\) whose coordinate sum is at least \\(k+2\\).\n\n   - Count the lines used.  \n     The sunny family contributes \\(k\\) lines.  \n     The diagonal family contributes \\((n+1)-(k+2)+1 = n-k\\) lines.  \n     Altogether we have \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n   This construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).  \n\nCombining the necessity (\\(k\\ge 1\\)) with the explicit realisation for all \\(k\\) in the stated range yields the complete answer.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(S_n\\) contains exactly  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\npoints (for each integer sum \\(s\\) there are \\(s-1\\) positive solutions of \\(a+b=s\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  At least one sunny line is necessary\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines\n\\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and that **none**\nof them is sunny.  Hence each line is parallel to one of the three\ndirections:\n\n* horizontal: \\(y=c\\);\n* vertical:   \\(x=c\\);\n* diagonal:   \\(x+y=t\\) (parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{\\ell_i\\mid \\ell_i\\text{ horizontal}\\},\\;\n\\mathcal V=\\{\\ell_i\\mid \\ell_i\\text{ vertical}\\},\\;\n\\mathcal D=\\{\\ell_i\\mid \\ell_i\\text{ diagonal}\\},\n\\]\n\nand denote  \n\n\\[\nh=|\\mathcal H|,\\qquad v=|\\mathcal V|,\\qquad d=|\\mathcal D|,\n\\qquad h+v+d=n .\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2.1.1  Points covered by horizontal lines  \n\nA horizontal line has equation \\(y=c\\) with an integer \\(c\\ge 1\\).  \nFor a fixed \\(c\\) the admissible abscissae satisfy \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\);\nhence the line can contain at most \\(n+1-c\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \nIf the distinct horizontal lines have ordinates \\(c_1,\\dots ,c_h\\) then\n\n\\[\n\\#\\bigl(S_n\\cap\\bigcup\\mathcal H\\bigr)\\le\n\\sum_{i=1}^{h}(n+1-c_i).\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2.1.2  Points covered by vertical lines  \n\nAnalogously, a vertical line \\(x=c\\) can contain at most \\(n+1-c\\) points\nof \\(S_n\\).  With distinct abscissae \\(c'_1,\\dots ,c'_v\\),\n\n\\[\n\\#\\bigl(S_n\\cap\\bigcup\\mathcal V\\bigr)\\le\n\\sum_{i=1}^{v}(n+1-c'_i).\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2.1.3  Points covered by diagonal lines  \n\nA line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=t\\) with integer\n\\(t\\ge 2\\).  Inside \\(S_n\\) it contains exactly the points with\n\\(a+b=t\\), i.e. \\(t-1\\) points.  For distinct constants\n\\(t_1,\\dots ,t_d\\),\n\n\\[\n\\#\\bigl(S_n\\cap\\bigcup\\mathcal D\\bigr)\\le\n\\sum_{i=1}^{d}(t_i-1).\n\\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2.1.4  Global upper bound  \n\nAdding (2.3)\u2013(2.5) we obtain\n\n\\[\n|S_n|\\le\n\\sum_{i=1}^{h}(n+1-c_i)\n+\\sum_{i=1}^{v}(n+1-c'_i)\n+\\sum_{i=1}^{d}(t_i-1).\n\\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\nEach summand is a decreasing function of its index; therefore the\nright\u2011hand side is maximised when the integers are taken as small as\npossible, subject to the distinctness constraints.  Consequently we may\nassume\n\n\\[\nc_i=i\\;(1\\le i\\le h),\\qquad\nc'_i=i\\;(1\\le i\\le v),\\qquad\nt_i=i+1\\;(1\\le i\\le d)\n\\]\n\n(the smallest admissible constant for a diagonal line is \\(2\\)).  Substituting\ninto (2.6) yields\n\n\\[\n|S_n|\\le\nh(n+1)-\\frac{h(h+1)}2\n+v(n+1)-\\frac{v(v+1)}2\n+\\frac{d(d+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\nUsing (2.1) and the relation \\(d=n-h-v\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le\nh(n+1)-\\frac{h(h+1)}2\n+v(n+1)-\\frac{v(v+1)}2\n+\\frac{(n-h-v)(n-h-v+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives\n\n\\[\n\\frac{h(h+1)}2+\\frac{v(v+1)}2\n\\le\n\\frac{(n-h-v)(n-h-v+1)}2+\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\nDefine \\(p=h\\) and \\(q=v\\) (\\(p,q\\ge0,\\;p+q\\le n\\)).  A direct expansion of\nthe right\u2011hand side of (2.9) shows that\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-p-q)(n-p-q+1)}2+\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n-\n\\Bigl(\\frac{p(p+1)}2+\\frac{q(q+1)}2\\Bigr)\n=\n\\frac12\\bigl[(n-p-q)^2-p^2-q^2+2n^2+n-(p+q)\\bigr] >0 .\n\\]\n\nThus the inequality (2.9) is **strictly violated** for every admissible\npair \\((p,q)\\).  This contradiction stems from the assumption that all\n\\(n\\) lines are non\u2011sunny.  Hence any covering of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct\nlines must contain at least one sunny line:\n\n\\[\nk\\ge 1 .\n\\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).  We exhibit \\(n\\) distinct\nlines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n1. **Sunny lines.**  \n   For \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y = -x + (i+1).\n   \\tag{2.11}\n   \\]\n\n   Eac