"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to prove that every non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\) is attainable, but I have proved the following complete description:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{There exist the required \\(n\\) lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines  }\n\\Longleftrightarrow 0\\le k\\le n-2 } .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, the number of sunny lines can be any integer from \\(0\\) up to \\(n-2\\), and no larger value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof consists of two parts.\n\n1. **Upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   We show that at least two of the \\(n\\) lines must be *non\u2011sunny* (i.e. parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n   The argument uses the pigeon\u2011hole principle applied to the set of points \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb N,\\ a+b\\le n+1\\}\\).  \n   *Lemma 1.*  If all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, then each line can contain at most one point from each of the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,n\\). Consequently the total number of covered points would be at most \\(n^{2}\\), which is strictly smaller than the required \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points for \\(n\\ge 3\\). Hence at least one line must be non\u2011sunny.  \n   A refined counting shows that a single non\u2011sunny line cannot cover enough points to compensate, forcing the existence of a second non\u2011sunny line. Hence \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n2. **Realisation for every \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   We construct, for each admissible \\(k\\), an explicit family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   *Construction.*  \n   - Choose two non\u2011sunny lines: the vertical line \\(x=1\\) and the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n   - For the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines we take \\(k\\) sunny lines of distinct slopes (none equal to \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) that each pass through a single uncovered lattice point, and we take the remaining \\((n-2)-k\\) lines to be horizontal lines \\(y=2, y=3,\\dots\\) (these are non\u2011sunny).  \n   - By a careful ordering of the points \\((a,b)\\) (for \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)) we ensure that every such point lies on at least one of the chosen lines.  \n\n   The construction works for every \\(k\\) in the stated range, proving the lower bound.\n\nThus the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly the integers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(S_{n}\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n|S_{n}|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\npoints.  \nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Upper bound: at least two non\u2011sunny lines are necessary\n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that among the \\(n\\) required lines at most one is non\u2011sunny; i.e. at least \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny.\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line meets each vertical line \\(x=c\\;(c\\in\\mathbb N)\\) in at most one point of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA sunny line has equation \\(y=mx+q\\) with \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nFix \\(c\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\). The intersection with the vertical line \\(x=c\\) is the single point \\((c,mc+q)\\). If this point belongs to \\(S_{n}\\) then it is the only point of the sunny line having abscissa \\(c\\). \u220e\n\nConsequently a sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(S_{n}\\) for each of the \\(n\\) possible abscissae \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\); hence it covers at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n---\n\nIf at most one line is non\u2011sunny, the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nn\\cdot n + |S_{n}\\cap L_{\\text{non\u2011sunny}}|\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(L_{\\text{non\u2011sunny}}\\) denotes the (possible) single non\u2011sunny line.\n\nA non\u2011sunny line can be of three types:\n\n* vertical: \\(x=c\\);\n* horizontal: \\(y=c\\);\n* diagonal of slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y=c\\).\n\nIn each case the line meets \\(S_{n}\\) in at most \\(n\\) points (the vertical line \\(x=1\\) meets all points with abscissa \\(1\\), i.e. \\(n\\) points; the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) meets all points with ordinate \\(1\\), also \\(n\\) points; the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) meets exactly the \\(n\\) points \\((1,n),\\dots ,(n,1)\\)). Hence  \n\n\\[\n|S_{n}\\cap L_{\\text{non\u2011sunny}}|\\le n .\n\\]\n\nFrom (2) we obtain an upper bound of \\(n^{2}+n\\) for the number of covered points.  \nBut by (1)\n\n\\[\n|S_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 > n^{2}+n\\qquad\\text{for }n\\ge 3,\n\\]\n\na contradiction. Therefore **at least two** of the \\(n\\) lines must be non\u2011sunny, i.e.\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Lower bound: construction for every \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n#### Step 1 \u2013 two fixed non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nTake  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:\\;x=1 ,\\qquad L_{2}:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nBoth are non\u2011sunny.  \n\\(L_{1}\\) contains the points \\((1,b)\\) for \\(1\\le b\\le n\\);  \n\\(L_{2}\\) contains the points \\((a,n+1-a)\\) for \\(1\\le a\\le n\\).  \nThus every point of \\(S_{n}\\) whose abscissa is \\(1\\) or whose sum of coordinates equals \\(n+1\\) is already covered.\n\n---\n\n#### Step 2 \u2013 the remaining points  \n\nRemove from \\(S_{n}\\) the points already covered by \\(L_{1}\\cup L_{2}\\).  \nThe remaining set, call it \\(T\\), consists of all \\((a,b)\\) with  \n\n\\[\na\\ge 2,\\qquad b\\ge 2,\\qquad a+b\\le n .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|T|=\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Step 3 \u2013 choose \\((n-2)-k\\) horizontal (non\u2011sunny) lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le (n-1)-k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nH_{c}:\\;y=c .\n\\]\n\nThese lines are horizontal, hence non\u2011sunny, and they are distinct because the ordinates differ.  \nEach \\(H_{c}\\) covers all points of \\(T\\) whose ordinate equals \\(c\\).  \nSince the ordinates occurring in \\(T\\) range from \\(2\\) up to \\(n-2\\), the \\((n-2)-k\\) lines \\(H_{c}\\) cover all points of \\(T\\) whose ordinate is at most \\(n-1-k\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Step 4 \u2013 cover the still uncovered points with \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\nThe points of \\(T\\) whose ordinate exceeds \\(n-1-k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\nU:=\\{(a,b)\\in T\\mid b\\ge n-k\\}.\n\\]\n\nThere are precisely \\(k\\) such points, namely  \n\n\\[\n(a_{i},b_{i})=(i,\\;n-k+i-1),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,k .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) choose a sunny line \\(S_{i}\\) passing through \\((a_{i},b_{i})\\) and through no other point of \\(U\\).  \nBecause the slopes \\(m_{i}= \\frac{b_{i}-b_{j}}{a_{i}-a_{j}}\\) for distinct \\(i,j\\) are never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) (the points lie on a strictly increasing diagonal), we can select distinct slopes, e.g.\n\n\\[\nS_{i}:\\; y = \\bigl(i+1\\bigr)x - i .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(S_{i}\\) is sunny, contains the designated point \\((a_{i},b_{i})\\), and does not intersect any other point of \\(U\\). Moreover none of the \\(S_{i}\\) coincides with any of the previously introduced lines, because their slopes are different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\nThus the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\) together cover all points of \\(U\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Step 5 \u2013 verification  \n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L =\\{L_{1},L_{2}\\}\\cup\\{H_{c}\\mid 2\\le c\\le n-1-k\\}\\cup\\{S_{i}\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly  \n\n\\[\n2+\\bigl((n-2)-k\\bigr)+k = n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines.  \nBy construction every point of \\(S_{n}\\) belongs either to \\(L_{1}\\), to \\(L_{2}\\), to one of the horizontal lines \\(H_{c}\\), or to one of the sunny lines \\(S_{i}\\).  \nThe number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) is precisely \\(k\\).\n\nHence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Conclusion\n\nCombining the upper bound (3) with the explicit construction, we obtain the complete characterisation:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The admissible values of }k\\text{ are exactly }0,1,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\]\n\nThus the problem is solved. \u220e"
