at can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{2n}_{\\text{points of }V\\cup H\\cup D}\n\\;+\\;(n-1)\\cdot 3\n=3n-3+2n=5n-3 .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n|S_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 >5n-3 .\n\\]\n\nThus the assumption is impossible; at least two of the \\(n\\) lines must be non\u2011sunny.  \nHence the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Construction for every \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n1. **Two fixed non\u2011sunny lines**  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{1}:x=1 ,\\qquad L_{2}:x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Both are non\u2011sunny.  \\(L_{1}\\) covers all points of \\(V\\); \\(L_{2}\\) covers all points of \\(D\\).\n\n2. **Points still uncovered**  \n\n   Remove from \\(S_{n}\\) the points on \\(L_{1}\\cup L_{2}\\).  \n   The remaining set is  \n\n   \\[\n   T:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Its cardinality is  \n\n   \\[\n   |T|=\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2 .\\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Horizontal non\u2011sunny lines**  \n\n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n-1-k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   H_{c}: y=c .\n   \\]\n\n   These \\((n-2)-k\\) lines are horizontal (non\u2011sunny) and together cover all points of \\(T\\) whose ordinate is at most \\(n-1-k\\).\n\n4. **The remaining \\(k\\) points**  \n\n   The points of \\(T\\) whose ordinate exceeds \\(n-1-k\\) are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   U:=\\{(i,\\,n-k+i-1)\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   There are precisely \\(k\\) such points.\n\n5. **Sunny lines for the points of \\(U\\)**  \n\n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) introduce the line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{i}:\\; y=(i+1)x-i .\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(S_{i}\\) is \\(i+1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(S_{i}\\) is sunny.  \n   Substituting \\(x=i\\) gives \\(y=(i+1)i-i=n-k+i-1\\), so \\((i,n-k+i-1)\\in S_{i}\\).  \n   Because the slopes are distinct, the lines \\(S_{i}\\) are pairwise different and each of them meets no other point of \\(U\\).\n\n6. **Verification**  \n\n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\n   \\{L_{1},L_{2}\\}\\cup\\{H_{c}\\mid 2\\le c\\le n-1-k\\}\\cup\\{S_{i}\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}\n   \\]\n\n   contains  \n\n   \\[\n   2+\\bigl((n-2)-k\\bigr)+k=n\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines.  \n   By construction every point of \\(S_{n}\\) lies either on \\(L_{1}\\), on \\(L_{2}\\), on one of the horizontal lines \\(H_{c}\\), or on one of the sunny lines \\(S_{i}\\).  \n   Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\) are sunny.\n\nThus for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Conclusion\n\nThe upper bound (2) shows that no configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, and the construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n-2\\) is attainable. Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The admissible values of }k\\text{ are precisely }0,1,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
