"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a complete classification of the admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following two statements  \n\n* **Lemma\u202f1.**  If a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition, then at least one of the lines is *non\u2011sunny* (i.e. it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n* **Lemma\u202f2.**  For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines that satisfies the covering condition and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nConsequently the only values of \\(k\\) that are *impossible* are \\(k=n\\); all other non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\le n\\) do occur.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof proceeds by a purely combinatorial counting argument together with an explicit construction.\n\n1. **Counting argument (Lemma\u202f1).**  \n   For each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) consider the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n   The points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\) all lie on this line; there are exactly \\(n+1-a\\) of them.  \n   If none of the \\(n\\) given lines were vertical, then each of those points would have to be covered by a line of a different direction.  \n   Because a non\u2011vertical line can intersect the vertical line \\(x=a\\) in at most one point, at least \\(n+1-a\\) distinct lines would be needed to cover the points on \\(x=a\\).  \n   Summing over all \\(a\\) gives a total of \\(\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) required incidences, which exceeds the available \\(n\\) lines unless at least one of the lines is vertical.  \n   The same argument works for horizontal lines and for lines of slope \\(-1\\). Hence at least one of the three families of parallel lines must appear, i.e. at least one line is non\u2011sunny.\n\n2. **Construction for arbitrary \\(k<n\\) (Lemma\u202f2).**  \n   *Step\u202f1 \u2013 non\u2011sunny lines.*  \n   Choose \\(n-1-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1-k\\) and \\(k\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=k\\).  \n   These \\(n-1\\) lines are all non\u2011sunny and together they already cover every point \\((a,b)\\) with either \\(a\\le n-1-k\\) or \\(b\\le k\\).  \n\n   *Step\u202f2 \u2013 the remaining points.*  \n   The points that are still uncovered are precisely those with  \n   \\[\n        n-k\\le a\\le n,\\qquad k+1\\le b\\le n+1-a .\n   \\]\n   Their total number is\n   \\[\n        \\sum_{a=n-k}^{n}\\bigl(n+1-a-(k+1)+1\\bigr)\n        =\\sum_{a=n-k}^{n}(n-a-k+1)\n        =\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n   *Step\u202f3 \u2013 a single sunny line.*  \n   All these remaining points lie on the line\n   \\[\n        L:\\; x+y = n+1 .\n   \\]\n   The line \\(L\\) has slope \\(-1\\), so it is *not* sunny.  \n   To obtain a sunny line we rotate \\(L\\) by a tiny angle \\(\\varepsilon>0\\) (choosing \\(\\varepsilon\\) so small that the rotated line still meets the triangle \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) in exactly the same set of lattice points).  \n   Denote the rotated line by \\(L_{\\varepsilon}\\).  Because the rotation changes the direction, \\(L_{\\varepsilon}\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor to the original line \\(x+y=0\\); hence \\(L_{\\varepsilon}\\) is sunny.  \n\n   *Step\u202f4 \u2013 completing the set of \\(n\\) lines.*  \n   Add the sunny line \\(L_{\\varepsilon}\\) to the \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines constructed in Step\u202f1.  \n   The resulting family consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the single line \\(L_{\\varepsilon}\\) together with the \\(k\\) horizontal lines from Step\u202f1) and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, and it covers every required lattice point.  \n\n   Because the construction works for any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\), Lemma\u202f2 follows.\n\nCombining Lemma\u202f1 (which rules out the case \\(k=n\\)) with Lemma\u202f2 (which shows that every other value of \\(k\\) is attainable) yields the final description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to be covered.  \nIts cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n   |T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its direction is different from the three directions\n\\[\n   d_1:\\text{horizontal},\\qquad d_2:\\text{vertical},\\qquad d_3:\\text{slope }-1 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1  \n*In any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\), at least one line is non\u2011sunny.*\n\n**Proof.**  \nAssume, for a contradiction, that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \nFix an integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\).  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) with first coordinate \\(a\\) are  \n\n\\[\n   P_a=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\},\n\\]\nand \\(|P_a|=n+1-a\\).\n\nBecause a sunny line is not vertical, it meets the vertical line \\(x=a\\) in at most one point.  \nConsequently, covering the set \\(P_a\\) requires at least \\(|P_a|=n+1-a\\) distinct lines.  \n\nSumming this lower bound over all \\(a\\) gives a required number of incidences\n\n\\[\n   \\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n\\qquad (n\\ge 3),\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts the hypothesis that only \\(n\\) lines are available.  \nHence at least one of the lines must be parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); i.e. at least one line is non\u2011sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2  \n*For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.*\n\n**Proof.**  \nLet \\(k\\) be fixed with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n1. **Non\u2011sunny part.**  \n   Choose the following \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n   * vertical lines \\(x=1,\\,x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1-k\\);  \n   * horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=k\\).\n\n   These lines are pairwise distinct and none of them is sunny.\n\n2. **Points already covered.**  \n   Any point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with either \\(a\\le n-1-k\\) (hence lying on the vertical line \\(x=a\\)) or \\(b\\le k\\) (hence lying on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\)) is already covered.\n\n3. **Remaining points.**  \n   The points that are still uncovered are precisely those with  \n\n   \\[\n        n-k\\le a\\le n,\\qquad k+1\\le b\\le n+1-a .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   For a fixed \\(a\\) in the range \\(n-k\\le a\\le n\\) the admissible values of \\(b\\) form the interval\n   \\([k+1,\\,n+1-a]\\), whose length is \\(n-a-k+1\\).  \n   Hence the number of uncovered points equals  \n\n   \\[\n        \\sum_{a=n-k}^{n}(n-a-k+1)\n        =\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   All these points satisfy the linear equation \\(x+y=n+1\\); they lie on the line  \n\n   \\[\n        L:\\;x+y=n+1,\n   \\]\n   which has slope \\(-1\\) and therefore is **not** sunny.\n\n4. **Turning \\(L\\) into a sunny line.**  \n   Choose a real number \\(\\varepsilon>0\\) so small that the line  \n\n   \\[\n        L_{\\varepsilon}:\\;x+y=n+1+\\varepsilon x\n   \\]\n   still passes through exactly the lattice points listed in (2).  \n   (Geometrically, \\(L_{\\varepsilon}\\) is obtained by rotating \\(L\\) around a point of \\(L\\) by an angle whose tangent is \\(\\varepsilon\\); for sufficiently small \\(\\varepsilon\\) the set of integer points of the triangle that lie on the line does not change.)  \n\n   The direction vector of \\(L_{\\varepsilon}\\) is \\((1,-1+\\varepsilon)\\); this vector is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, so \\(L_{\\varepsilon}\\) is sunny.\n\n5. **Final family of lines.**  \n   Take the \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines from step\u202f1 together with the sunny line \\(L_{\\varepsilon}\\).  \n   The total number of lines is \\(n\\).  \n   The family contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines: the line \\(L_{\\varepsilon}\\) plus the \\(k\\) horizontal lines (which are non\u2011sunny) give \\(k\\) sunny lines, while the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non\u2011sunny.  \n\n   By construction every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the chosen lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nLemma\u202f1 shows that a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines cannot exist.  \nLemma\u202f2 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) a configuration does exist.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer.**  \nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines are precisely the integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  No configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines exists, while for each \\(k\\) in the stated range a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines can be constructed."
