tailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its direction is different from the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal }(0),\\qquad \\text{vertical }(\\infty),\\qquad \\text{slope }-1 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1  \n*In any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\), at least one line is non\u2011sunny.*\n\n**Proof.**  \nAssume, to the contrary, that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \nFix an integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\).  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) having first coordinate \\(a\\) are  \n\n\\[\nP_a=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\},\n\\qquad |P_a|=n+1-a .\n\\]\n\nA sunny line is not vertical, therefore it meets the vertical line \\(x=a\\) in at most one point.  \nConsequently, covering the set \\(P_a\\) requires at least \\(|P_a|=n+1-a\\) distinct lines.  \n\nSumming over all possible \\(a\\) we obtain a lower bound on the total number of lines needed:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n\\qquad (n\\ge3).\n\\]\n\nThis contradicts the hypothesis that only \\(n\\) lines are available.  \nHence at least one of the lines must be parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); i.e. at least one line is non\u2011sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2  \n*For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.*\n\n**Proof.**  \nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \nWe construct the required family in several steps.\n\n#### Step\u202f1 \u2013 a non\u2011sunny backbone\nTake the following \\(n-1\\) lines, all of which are non\u2011sunny:\n\n* vertical lines \\(x=1,\\,x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1-k\\)   (there are \\(n-1-k\\) of them);\n* horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=k\\)      (there are \\(k\\) of them).\n\nThese lines are pairwise distinct.  \nAny point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with either \\(a\\le n-1-k\\) or \\(b\\le k\\) lies on one of the chosen lines, so such points are already covered.\n\n#### Step\u202f2 \u2013 the still uncovered points\nThe points that are not yet covered are precisely those satisfying  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\le a\\le n,\\qquad k+1\\le b\\le n+1-a . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, if \\(a\\ge n-k\\) then \\(a\\) is larger than every vertical line\u2019s abscissa, and if \\(b\\ge k+1\\) then \\(b\\) exceeds the ordinate of every horizontal line; the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is the defining condition of \\(T_n\\).  \n\nAll points described in (2) lie on the line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;x+y=n+1,\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nwhich has slope \\(-1\\) and therefore is non\u2011sunny.\n\nThe number of points in (2) is\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{a=n-k}^{n}\\bigl(n+1-a-(k+1)+1\\bigr)\n =\\sum_{a=n-k}^{n}(n-a-k+1)\n =\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n#### Step\u202f3 \u2013 a sunny line that covers exactly the points of (2)\nChoose a real number \\(\\varepsilon>0\\) so small that the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{\\varepsilon}:\\;x+y=n+1+\\varepsilon x\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\npasses through the same integer points of \\(T_n\\) as the line \\(L\\).  \nBecause the points of (2) are isolated in the integer lattice, a sufficiently tiny perturbation of the constant term does not create or delete any of them.  \n\nThe direction vector of \\(L_{\\varepsilon}\\) is \\((1,-1+\\varepsilon)\\).  \nSince \\(\\varepsilon\\neq0\\), this vector is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\); consequently \\(L_{\\varepsilon}\\) is a **sunny** line.\n\nThus \\(L_{\\varepsilon}\\) covers exactly the \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\\) points listed in (2).\n\n#### Step\u202f4 \u2013 producing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\nSo far we have \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines (the vertical and horizontal ones) and one sunny line \\(L_{\\varepsilon}\\); the total number of sunny lines is therefore **one**, not the required \\(k\\).\n\nTo increase the number of sunny lines we slightly rotate \\(k-1\\) of the horizontal lines.  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\nH_i:\\;y=i\n\\]\n\nbe a horizontal line.  \nChoose a distinct tiny angle \\(\\delta_i>0\\) (so small that the rotated line still contains the same integer points of \\(T_n\\) that \\(H_i\\) originally contained) and define  \n\n\\[\n\\widetilde H_i:\\; y=i+\\delta_i x .\n\\]\n\nThe direction vector of \\(\\widetilde H_i\\) is \\((1,\\delta_i)\\), which is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions; hence each \\(\\widetilde H_i\\) is sunny.  \nBecause the \\(\\delta_i\\) are distinct, the lines \\(\\widetilde H_i\\) are pairwise different and also different from all other lines already chosen.\n\nWe keep the horizontal line \\(y=k\\) unchanged (it remains non\u2011sunny) and keep all vertical lines unchanged.  \n\nNow the family consists of  \n\n* the \\(n-1-k\\) vertical lines (non\u2011sunny);\n* the single horizontal line \\(y=k\\) (non\u2011sunny);\n* the \\(k-1\\) rotated lines \\(\\widetilde H_1,\\dots ,\\widetilde H_{k-1}\\) (sunny);\n* the line \\(L_{\\varepsilon}\\) (sunny).\n\nCounting, we have  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1-k)+1}_{\\text{non\u2011sunny}}=n-k\\ \\text{non\u2011sunny lines},\n\\qquad\n\\underbrace{(k-1)+1}_{\\text{sunny}}=k\\ \\text{sunny lines},\n\\]\n\nand the total number of lines is \\(n\\).\n\nBecause each rotated horizontal line \\(\\widetilde H_i\\) still passes through exactly the same integer points of \\(T_n\\) as the original line \\(y=i\\), the covering property is preserved: every point of \\(T_n\\) lies either on a vertical line, on the unchanged horizontal line \\(y=k\\), on one of the \\(\\widetilde H_i\\), or on \\(L_{\\varepsilon}\\).  \n\nThus we have produced a configuration of \