ailable.  \nHence at least one of the lines must be parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); i.e. at least one line is non\u2011sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2  \n*For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.*\n\n**Proof.**  \nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \nWe construct the required family in several steps.\n\n#### Step\u202f1 \u2013 a non\u2011sunny backbone\nTake the following \\(n-1\\) lines, all of which are non\u2011sunny:\n\n* vertical lines \\(x=1,\\,x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1-k\\)   (there are \\(n-1-k\\) of them);\n* horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=k\\)      (there are \\(k\\) of them).\n\nThese lines are pairwise distinct.  \nAny point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with either \\(a\\le n-1-k\\) or \\(b\\le k\\) lies on one of the chosen lines, so such points are already covered.\n\n#### Step\u202f2 \u2013 the still uncovered points\nThe points that are not yet covered are precisely those satisfying  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\le a\\le n,\\qquad k+1\\le b\\le n+1-a . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, if \\(a\\ge n-k\\) then \\(a\\) is larger than every vertical line\u2019s abscissa, and if \\(b\\ge k+1\\) then \\(b\\) exceeds the ordinate of every horizontal line; the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is the defining condition of \\(T_n\\).  \n\nAll points described in (2) lie on the line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;x+y=n+1,\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nwhich has slope \\(-1\\) and therefore is non\u2011sunny.\n\nThe number of points in (2) is\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{a=n-k}^{n}\\bigl(n+1-a-(k+1)+1\\bigr)\n =\\sum_{a=n-k}^{n}(n-a-k+1)\n =\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n#### Step\u202f3 \u2013 a sunny line that covers exactly the points of (2)\nChoose a real number \\(\\varepsilon>0\\) so small that the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{\\varepsilon}:\\;x+y=n+1+\\varepsilon x\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\npasses through