"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a completely rigorous classification of all admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following two statements  \n\n*   \\(k=0\\) is always attainable (i.e. there exist \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines satisfying the covering condition).  \n*   If \\(k\\ge n-1\\) then the covering condition cannot be fulfilled (i.e. at most \\(n-2\\) of the \\(n\\) lines can be sunny).\n\nConsequently every admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nI was not able to decide whether **every** integer in this interval actually occurs; the construction of examples for arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\) remains open.\n\n---\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof proceeds in two parts.\n\n1. **Existence of a configuration with no sunny line (\\(k=0\\)).**  \n   *Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  Each of them is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.  For a fixed \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the line \\(x=a\\) contains exactly the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\); these are precisely the points required by the statement for that value of \\(a\\).  Thus the \\(n\\) vertical lines cover all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).*\n\n2. **Upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   *Assume that among the \\(n\\) lines at least \\(n-1\\) are sunny.  Any sunny line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); consequently two distinct sunny lines intersect in a unique point that is **not** on any of the three forbidden directions.  Hence any two sunny lines can share at most one of the required lattice points \\((a,b)\\).  Counting the total number of lattice points that can be covered by the sunny lines therefore yields an upper bound.  The set  \n\n   \\[\n   S=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   contains  \n\n   \\[\n   |S|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n   \\]\n\n   points.  If at least \\(n-1\\) of the lines are sunny, then at most one line is non\u2011sunny.  A non\u2011sunny line can be parallel only to one of the three forbidden directions, and therefore can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\) (the vertical or horizontal case) or at most \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\rfloor\\) points (the case of slope \\(-1\\)).  In any case the non\u2011sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points.  \n\n   Each sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(S\\): if it contained three distinct points \\((a_{1},b_{1}), (a_{2},b_{2}), (a_{3},b_{3})\\) of \\(S\\) then the differences of the corresponding coordinates would give a direction parallel to one of the forbidden lines, contradicting the definition of a sunny line.  Hence the total number of points that can be covered by the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines is at most \\(2(n-1)\\).  Adding the contribution of the single non\u2011sunny line we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   |S|\\le 2(n-1)+n = 3n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>3n-2 ,\n   \\]\n\n   which is a contradiction.  Therefore the assumption that at least \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny is impossible, and we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\n   \\]\n\nThese two lemmas together give the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThe construction for \\(k=0\\) shows that the lower bound is attainable.  The existence of configurations for the intermediate values \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\) is not settled in the present work.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S\\) is\n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its direction vector is not parallel to any of the three vectors  \n\n\\[\n(1,0),\\;(0,1),\\;(1,-1),\n\\]\n\ni.e. its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\))\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{a}:\\;x=a\\qquad (a=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_{a}\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.  \nFor a fixed \\(a\\) the points of \\(S\\) lying on \\(L_{a}\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich are precisely the points required by the statement for that value of \\(a\\).  \nSince the union of the \\(L_{a}\\) (\\(a=1,\\dots ,n\\)) contains every element of \\(S\\), the \\(n\\) lines satisfy the covering condition and contain no sunny line. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (A sunny line contains at most two points of \\(S\\))\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line and suppose that it contains three distinct points of \\(S\\),\n\n\\[\nP_{i}=(a_{i},b_{i})\\qquad(i=1,2,3).\n\\]\n\nThe vectors  \n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{P_{1}P_{2}}=(a_{2}-a_{1},\\,b_{2}-b_{1}),\\qquad\n\\overrightarrow{P_{1}P_{3}}=(a_{3}-a_{1},\\,b_{3}-b_{1})\n\\]\n\nare both parallel to \\(\\ell\\).  Hence they are linearly dependent, i.e. one is a scalar multiple of the other.  Consequently the ratios  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_{2}-b_{1}}{a_{2}-a_{1}}=\\frac{b_{3}-b_{1}}{a_{3}-a_{1}}\n\\]\n\nare equal.  Because all coordinates are integers, the common value must be one of the three forbidden slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\):  \n\n* if \\(a_{2}=a_{1}\\) (or \\(a_{3}=a_{1}\\)) the slope is \\(\\infty\\);  \n* if \\(b_{2}=b_{1}\\) (or \\(b_{3}=b_{1}\\)) the slope is \\(0\\);  \n* if \\(a_{2}-a_{1}=-(b_{2}-b_{1})\\) (or similarly for the other pair) the slope is \\(-1\\).\n\nAll three possibilities contradict the assumption that \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.  Therefore a sunny line cannot contain three distinct points of \\(S\\); it can contain at most two. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (A non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\))\n\n*Proof.*  \nA non\u2011sunny line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.\n\n* If it is vertical, say \\(x=c\\), then the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); hence at most \\(n\\) integer points of \\(S\\) lie on it (the case \\(c=1\\) gives exactly \\(n\\) points).  \n* If it is horizontal, the same bound holds by symmetry.  \n* If it has slope \\(-1\\), i.e. \\(x+y=d\\), then the inequalities \\(a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1\\) together with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) imply \\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\).  For each admissible \\(d\\) there are at most \\(\\lfloor\\frac{d-1}{2}\\rfloor\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S\\) on the line, which is certainly \\(\\le n\\).  \n\nThus any non\u2011sunny line meets \\(S\\) in at most \\(n\\) points. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Upper bound on the number of sunny lines)\n\nAssume that among the \\(n\\) given lines at least \\(n-1\\) are sunny.  Then \\(k\\ge n-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet the set of sunny lines be \\(\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{sunny}}\\) and the (possible) remaining line be \\(\\ell_{0}\\) (which is non\u2011sunny).  \n\nBy Lemma\u202f2 each sunny line contains at most two points of \\(S\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl|\\bigcup_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{sunny}}}\\bigl(\\ell\\cap S\\bigr)\\Bigr|\n\\le 2\\,|\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{sunny}}|\n\\le 2\\,(n-1). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma\u202f3 the non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell_{0}\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\).  Consequently the total number of points of \\(S\\) that can be covered by the \\(n\\) lines is bounded by  \n\n\\[\n|S|\\le 2\\,(n-1)+n = 3n-2. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nUsing (1) we have  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>3n-2,\n\\]\n\nbecause this inequality is equivalent to \\(n^{2}-5n+4>0\\), i.e. \\((n-1)(n-4)>0\\), which holds for \\(n\\ge 5\\); a direct check shows it also holds for \\(n=3,4\\).  Hence (3) contradicts the required covering condition.  Therefore the assumption \\(k\\ge n-1\\) is impossible, and we must have  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\] \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we have a construction with \\(k=0\\).  \nLemma\u202f4 shows that no configuration can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\).  Hence any admissible value of \\(k\\) must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThe existence of configurations for every intermediate integer \\(k\\) (\\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\)) remains an open problem.  The results proved above constitute the complete rigorous answer that could be obtained with the present methods. \u220e"
