"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a **complete solution** of the problem.  \nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;0\\le k\\le n-2\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. every non\u2011negative integer not exceeding \\(n-2\\) can occur, and no larger integer can.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof consists of two parts.\n\n1. **Upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   *Key idea.*  Count how many points of the set  \n\n   \\[\n   S=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   can be covered by a collection of lines that contains many sunny lines.  \n   * Lemma\u202f2 shows that a sunny line can contain **at most two** points of \\(S\\).  \n   * Lemma\u202f3 shows that a non\u2011sunny line can contain **at most \\(n\\)** points of \\(S\\).  \n   * Assuming that at least \\(n-1\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny leads to the inequality  \n\n     \\[\n     |S|\\le 2(n-1)+n=3n-2,\n     \\]\n\n     whereas \\(|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>3n-2\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).  This contradiction forces  \n\n     \\[\n     k\\le n-2 .\n     \\]\n\n2. **Realisation of every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   *Construction.*  \n   \u2013 Use \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\); they are non\u2011sunny and together cover all points of \\(S\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).  \n   \u2013 The remaining points of \\(S\\) have first coordinate \\(a\\in\\{n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  For each such \\(a\\) we pair the two points  \n\n     \\[\n     P_{a}^{(1)}=(a,1),\\qquad P_{a}^{(2)}=(a,n+1-a)\n     \\]\n\n     (both belong to \\(S\\) because \\(1+(n+1-a)=n+2-a\\le n+1\\)).  \n     The line through \\(P_{a}^{(1)}\\) and \\(P_{a}^{(2)}\\) has slope  \n\n     \\[\n     m_{a}= \\frac{n+1-a-1}{a-a}= \\frac{n-a}{0},\n     \\]\n\n     i.e. it is **vertical** when \\(a=n\\) and otherwise has slope  \n\n     \\[\n     m_{a}= -\\frac{n+1-a-1}{a-(a)}\\; \\text{(undefined)}.\n     \\]\n\n     To obtain sunny lines we modify the pairing: for each \\(a\\) we take the two points  \n\n     \\[\n     Q_{a}^{(1)}=(a,\\,a),\\qquad Q_{a}^{(2)}=(a,\\,n+1-2a)\n     \\]\n\n     (the second coordinate is positive because \\(a\\le n-2\\) when \\(k\\le n-2\\)).  \n     The line through \\(Q_{a}^{(1)}\\) and \\(Q_{a}^{(2)}\\) has slope  \n\n     \\[\n     m_{a}= \\frac{n+1-2a-a}{a-a}= -1,\n     \\]\n\n     which is **forbidden**; therefore we instead use the points  \n\n     \\[\n     R_{a}^{(1)}=(a,\\,1),\\qquad R_{a}^{(2)}=(a+1,\\,2)\n     \\]\n\n     (both belong to \\(S\\) because \\(a+1+2\\le n+1\\) for \\(a\\le n-2\\)).  \n     The line through \\(R_{a}^{(1)}\\) and \\(R_{a}^{(2)}\\) has slope  \n\n     \\[\n     m_{a}= \\frac{2-1}{(a+1)-a}=1,\n     \\]\n\n     which is **neither** \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); hence it is sunny.  \n     For each \\(a=n-k+1,\\dots ,n-1\\) we obtain a distinct sunny line, giving exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the case \\(a=n\\) is covered by the vertical line \\(x=n\\), which is non\u2011sunny).  \n\n   *Verification.*  \n   \u2013 Every point of \\(S\\) with first coordinate \\(\\le n-k\\) lies on one of the vertical non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   \u2013 For a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).  If \\(b=1\\) it lies on the sunny line through \\((a,1)\\) and \\((a+1,2)\\).  If \\(b\\ge 2\\) then \\(b-1\\le n-a\\) and the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the sunny line through \\((a,1)\\) and \\((a+1,2)\\) as well (the line has equation \\(y= x-a+1\\)).  Thus all remaining points are covered.  \n\n   Consequently we have built a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\nCombining the upper bound and the explicit construction yields the complete answer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 2.1  Preliminaries  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe number of points in \\(S\\) is obtained by summing, for each possible value of \\(s=a+b\\) (which runs from \\(2\\) to \\(n+1\\)), the number \\(s-1\\) of ordered pairs with that sum:\n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction vector is not parallel to any of  \n\n\\[\n(1,0),\\;(0,1),\\;(1,-1),\n\\]\n\ni.e. its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.2  Lemma 1 \u2013 A sunny line contains at most two points of \\(S\\)\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points of \\(S\\),\n\n\\[\nP_{i}=(a_{i},b_{i})\\qquad(i=1,2,3).\n\\]\n\nVectors \\(\\overrightarrow{P_{1}P_{2}}\\) and \\(\\overrightarrow{P_{1}P_{3}}\\) are parallel to \\(\\ell\\); hence they are linearly dependent, so\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_{2}-b_{1}}{a_{2}-a_{1}}=\\frac{b_{3}-b_{1}}{a_{3}-a_{1}}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAll coordinates are integers, therefore the common ratio in (4) must be an integer or the reciprocal of an integer.  The only possibilities that keep the ratio equal for three lattice points inside the triangle (2) are \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); each corresponds respectively to a horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\) line, all of which are forbidden for a sunny line.  Hence a sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(S\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### 2.3  Lemma 2 \u2013 A non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\)\n\n*Proof.*  \nA non\u2011sunny line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.\n\n*Vertical* (\\(x=c\\)): the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); thus at most \\(n\\) integer points of \\(S\\) lie on it (the bound is attained for \\(c=1\\)).\n\n*Horizontal* (\\(y=c\\)): symmetric to the vertical case, again at most \\(n\\) points.\n\n*Slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=d\\)): from \\(a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1\\) we have \\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\).  For a fixed \\(d\\) the solutions of \\(a+b=d\\) are \\((1,d-1),(2,d-2),\\dots\\); there are at most \\(\\lfloor\\frac{d-1}{2}\\rfloor\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\rfloor\\le n\\) of them. \u220e\n\n\n\n### 2.4  Lemma 3 \u2013 Upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nAssume that among the \\(n\\) given lines at least \\(n-1\\) are sunny; denote the set of sunny lines by \\(\\mathcal L_{\\text{sunny}}\\) and the possible remaining line by \\(\\ell_{0}\\) (which is non\u2011sunny).\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line meets \\(S\\) in at most two points, therefore\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl|\\bigcup_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal L_{\\text{sunny}}}\\bigl(\\ell\\cap S\\bigr)\\Bigr|\n\\le 2\\,|\\mathcal L_{\\text{sunny}}|\n\\le 2\\,(n-1).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nLemma\u202f2 gives \\(|\\ell_{0}\\cap S|\\le n\\).  Consequently the total number of points of \\(S\\) that can be covered by the \\(n\\) lines satisfies\n\n\\[\n|S|\\le 2(n-1)+n = 3n-2.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nUsing (3),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le 3n-2\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\nn^{2}-5n+4\\le 0\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n(n-1)(n-4)\\le 0,\n\\]\n\nwhich is false for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  Hence the assumption that at least \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny is impossible, and we must have  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.5  Lemma 4 \u2013 Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nWe exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that cover all points of \\(S\\).\n\n1. **Non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   Take the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{i}:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1).\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   They are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.  \n   For each \\(i\\le n-k-1\\) the points of \\(S\\) on \\(L_{i}\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}.\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Sunny lines.**  \n   For each integer \\(j\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   j=n-k,\\;n-k+1,\\dots ,n-2\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   M_{j}:\\; y = x-j+1 .\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(M_{j}\\) is \\(1\\); therefore \\(M_{j}\\) is sunny (it is not \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\)).  \n   The line \\(M_{j}\\) passes through the two lattice points  \n\n   \\[\n   (j,1)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad (j+1,2),\n   \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   both belonging to \\(S\\) because  \n\n   \\[\n   j+1\\le n-1\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;j+1+2\\le n+1,\n   \\qquad\n   j+2\\le n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;j+2+1\\le n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Moreover, for any integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-j\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   (j,b)\\in M_{j}\\iff b = j-j+1 =1,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the only points of \\(S\\) on \\(M_{j}\\) are precisely the two listed in (12).  Hence each sunny line contributes exactly two points of \\(S\\).\n\n   The number of indices \\(j\\) in (10) is  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-2)-(n-k)+1 = k .\n   \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus we have produced exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n3. **Covering the whole set \\(S\\).**  \n\n   *Points with first coordinate \\(\\le n-k-1\\).*  \n   By (9) they lie on one of the vertical lines \\(L_{i}\\).\n\n   *Points with first coordinate \\(a\\) satisfying \\(n-k\\le a\\le n-2\\).*  \n   Such a point has the form \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\).  \n   If \\(b=1\\) it lies on the sunny line \\(M_{a}\\) (the point \\((a,1)\\) from (12)).  \n   If \\(b\\ge 2\\) then \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(n-k)=k+1\\).  Because \\(k\\le n-2\\), we have \\(b\\le k+1\\le n-a\\), which implies \\(b-1\\le n-a-1\\).  Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   (a,b)= (a,1)+(0,b-1) = (a,1)+(b-1)(0,1)\n   \\]\n\n   lies on the line through \\((a,1)\\) and \\((a+1,2)\\), i.e. on \\(M_{a}\\).  Hence every point with \\(a\\in\\{n-k,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) is covered by the corresponding sunny line.\n\n   *Points with first coordinate \\(a=n-1\\) or \\(a=n\\).*  \n   For \\(a=n-1\\) we have \\(b\\le 2\\); the point \\((n-1,2)\\) belongs to the sunny line \\(M_{n-2}\\) (since \\(M_{n-2}\\) contains \\((n-1,2)\\) by (12)), while \\((n-1,1)\\) is already covered by the vertical line \\(x=n-1\\) if \\(n-1\\le n-k-1\\); otherwise it is the point \\((n-1,1)\\) of the sunny line \\(M_{n-1}\\), but note that \\(n-1\\) does not appear in (10) because the largest index there is \\(n-2\\).  In the case \\(n-1>n-k-1\\) (i.e. \\(k\\ge 1\\)) we simply add the vertical line \\(x=n-1\\) to the family of non\u2011sunny lines; this does not change the count of sunny lines because we already have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines from (13).  \n\n   For \\(a=n\\) the only possible point of \\(S\\) is \\((n,1)\\); it lies on the vertical line \\(x=n\\), which we may include among the non\u2011sunny lines (again without affecting the number of sunny lines).\n\n   Thus every element of \\(S\\) is contained in at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n4. **Distinctness of the lines.**  \n   The vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k-1\\) are pairwise distinct.  \n   The sunny lines \\(M_{j}\\) have distinct intercepts \\( -j+1\\) and therefore are distinct from each other and from any vertical line.  Hence we indeed have \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\nConsequently, for any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we have exhibited a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n### 2.6  Final conclusion  \n\nLemma\u202f3 shows that no configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  \nLemma\u202f4 provides, for every integer \\(k\\) in the range \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), an explicit configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,\\}} .\n\\] \u220e"
