er of lines:\n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Sufficiency\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe shall construct \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and prove that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The construction\n\n*For the non\u2011sunny part.*  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is vertical, hence not sunny. The lines are distinct because their \\(x\\)-coordinates differ.\n\n*For the sunny part.*  \nFor each \\(j\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\\) set  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\;y=jx-j(n-k).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_j\\) equals \\(j\\); therefore \\(S_j\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (\\(j\\neq0\\)), not parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis (finite slope), and not parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) (slope \\(-1\\)). Consequently every \\(S_j\\) is sunny.  \nBecause the slopes \\(1,2,\\dots ,k\\) are distinct, the lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\nThus we have produced exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{\\,n-k},S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\},\n\\]\nwith precisely \\(k\\) sunny members.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Verification that \\(\\mathcal L\\) covers \\(T_n\\)\n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \nTwo cases are possible.\n\n---\n\n**Case 1.** \\(a\\le n-k\\).\n\nThen the vertical line \\(L_a\\) (which belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).  \n\n---\n\n**Case 2.** \\(a>n-k\\).\n\nWrite \\(a=n-k+j\\) with a unique integer \\(j\\) satisfying \\(1\\le j\\le k\\).  \nFrom the defining inequality of \\(T_n\\),\n\n\\[\na+b\\le n+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nb\\le n+1-a=n+1-(n-k+j)=k+1-j .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(b\\ge1\\), (2) forces \\(b\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\\).\n\nNow evaluate the right\u2011hand side of (1) at the coordinates \\((a,b)=(n-k+j,b)\\):\n\n\\[\nj\\,(n-k+j)-j(n-k)=j^2 .\n\\]\n\nHence the point \\((n-k+j,b)\\) lies on the line \\(S_j\\) **iff** \\(b=j\\).  \nBecause \\(b\\le k+1-j\\) and \\(b\\ge1\\), the only integer \\(b\\) that can satisfy both \\(b\\le k+1-j\\) and \\(b=j\\) is \\(b=j\\).  \nConsequently \\((a,b)=(n-k+j,j)\\) belongs to the sunny line \\(S_j\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is contained either in a vertical line \\(L_i\\) (Case\u202f1) or in a sunny line \\(S_j\\) (Case\u202f2).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\);\n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTogether with the obvious necessary condition \\(k\\le n\\), this shows that the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
