\nto the line \\(x+y=0\\) (slope \\(-1\\)). Hence every \\(S_j\\) is sunny.\nBecause the intercepts \\(-(n-k+j-1)\\) are distinct, the lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{\\,n-k},S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  The covering property\n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n---\n\n**Case 1.** \\(a\\le n-k\\).\n\nThen the vertical line \\(L_a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n---\n\n**Case 2.** \\(a>n-k\\).\n\nWrite  \n\n\\[\na=n-k+j\\qquad\\text{with }j\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFrom the defining inequality of \\(T_n\\),\n\n\\[\na+b\\le n+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nb\\le n+1-a = k+1-j .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(b\\ge1\\), we have \\(1\\le b\\le k+1-j\\).\n\nEquation (6) for the line \\(S_j\\) can be rewritten as  \n\n\\[\nb = a-(n-k+j-1).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(a=n-k+j\\) from (7) into (9) gives \\(b=1\\).  \nHence the point \\((a,1)=(n-k+j,1)\\) lies on \\(S_j\\).\n\nMoreover, since the slope of \\(S_j\\) is \\(1\\), all integer points on \\(S_j\\) are obtained by adding the same integer \\(t\\ge0\\) to both coordinates:\n\n\\[\n(n-k+j+t,\\;1+t),\\qquad t=0,1,\\dots ,k-j .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nFor each such point we have  \n\n\\[\n(n-k+j+t)+(1+t)=n+1-t\\le n+1,\n\\]\n\nso every point of the form (10) belongs to \\(T_n\\).  \nConversely, any point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a>n-k\\) and satisfying \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) must satisfy (8); writing \\(a\\) as in (7) and using (9) we obtain \\(b=1+t\\) with \\(t=b-1\\), and then \\(a=n-k+j+t\\). Thus \\((a,b)\\) is exactly one of the points listed in (10) and therefore lies on the sunny line \\(S_j\\).\n\nConsequently every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs either to a vertical line \\(L_i\\) (Case\u202f1) or to a sunny line \\(S_j\\) (Case\u202f2).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* Necessarily \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) (inequality (5));\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (construction above).\n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
