 diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) (covers the points \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\); at most \\(n\\) points).\n\nConsequently each non\u2011sunny line covers **at most \\(n\\)** points of \\(S\\).\n\nThe set \\(S\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\npoints.  Using Lemma\u202f1 and Lemma\u202f2 we obtain the inequality\n\n\\[\n2k + t\\cdot n \\;\\ge\\; |S|\n      \\;=\\; \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(t=n-k\\), (2) becomes  \n\n\\[\n2k + (n-k)n \\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nk \\le n-2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus **any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\)**; in particular at least two lines are forced to be non\u2011sunny.\n\n*Construction for every \\(k\\le n-2\\).*  \nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nTake the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n1. **Sunny lines** \u2013 for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{k+1}\\,x + 1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(L_i\\) passes through the single point \\((1,1)\\) and through no other point of \\(S\\); therefore they are distinct sunny lines.\n\n2. **Two non\u2011sunny lines** \u2013 choose  \n\n   \\[\n   H:\\; y = 1 ,\\qquad V:\\; x = 1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The horizontal line \\(H\\) contains all points \\((a,1)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); the vertical line \\(V\\) contains all points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\).  Together they cover every point of \\(S\\) whose first or second coordinate equals \\(1\\).\n\n3. **Remaining \\(n-k-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines** \u2013 for each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n-k-1\\) take the diagonal line  \n\n   \\[\n   D_c:\\; x+y = c+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(D_c\\) contains exactly the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=c+1\\) and \\(a,b\\ge 1\\); there are precisely \\(c\\) such points, all belonging to \\(S\\).\n\nCounting the lines we have  \n\n\\[\nk\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;2\\;(\\text{horizontal, vertical})\\;+\\;(n-k-2)\\;(\\text{diagonals}) = n .\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nIf \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\) it lies on \\(V\\) or \\(H\\) respectively.  \nOtherwise \\(a+b\\ge 3\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); set \\(c=a+b-1\\) (so \\(2\\le c\\le n\\)).  \nIf \\(c\\le n-k-1\\) the point lies on the diagonal \\(D_c\\); if \\(c>n-k-1\\) then \\(c\\ge n-k\\) and consequently \\(a\\) or \\(b\\) equals \\(1\\), a case already covered.  \nThus **every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of the constructed lines**.\n\nAll lines are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and the total number of lines is \\(n\\).  Hence a configuration exists for every \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\nCombining the necessity (inequality (3)) and the explicit construction yields the complete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Necessity: a bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the two conditions of the problem.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nk:=|\\{L\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L\\text{ is sunny}\\}|,\\qquad\nt:=n-k\n\\]\n\nbe the numbers of sunny and non\u2011sunny lines, respectively.\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nA sunny line contains at most two points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose a sunny line \\(L\\) contains three distinct points \\((a_{1},b_{1}), (a_{2},b_{2}), (a_{3},b_{3})\\in S\\).  \nThe slope of \\(L\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_{2}-b_{1}}{a_{2}-a_{1}}\n   =\\frac{b_{3}-b_{1}}{a_{3}-a_{1}} .\n\\]\n\nSince all three points satisfy \\(a_{i}+b_{i}\\le n+1\\) and have integer coordinates, the only possible slopes are \\(0\\) (if the \\(b\\)\u2019s are equal), \\(\\infty\\) (if the \\(a\\)\u2019s are equal) or \\(-1\\) (if \\(a_{i}+b_{i}\\) is constant).  Each of these slopes is forbidden for a sunny line, a contradiction. \u220e\n\nConsequently the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 2.**  \nA non\u2011sunny line covers at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA non\u2011sunny line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.\n\n* Horizontal line \\(y=c\\) (\\(c\\ge 1\\)).  \nPoints of \\(S\\) on it have the form \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) such points.\n\n* Vertical line \\(x=c\\) (\\(c\\ge 1\\)).  \nPoints are \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); again at most \\(n\\).\n\n* Diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) (\\(c\\ge 2\\)).  \nPoints are \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\); the number of admissible \\(a\\) is at most \\(n\\) because \\(c\\le n+1\\). \u220e\n\nHence the \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines together cover at most \\(tn\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n---\n\nAll points of \\(S\\) must be covered, therefore\n\n\\[\n2k + tn \\;\\ge\\; |S| = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(t=n-k\\), (2) becomes\n\n\\[\n2k + (n-k)n \\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nk \\le n-2 . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus **any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\)**; equivalently, at least two of the \\(n\\) lines are forced to be non\u2011sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Sufficiency: construction for every \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nWe exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines that satisfy the covering condition and contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n1. **Sunny lines.**  \n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{k+1}\\,x + 1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(\\frac{i}{k+1}\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); hence each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n   All \\(L_i\\) pass through the point \\((1,1)\\) and through no other point of \\(S\\) (because any other point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) would give \\(b-1 = \\frac{i}{k+1}(a-1)\\), which cannot be an integer for the chosen rational slopes).  Thus the \\(L_i\\) are distinct.\n\n2. **Two non\u2011sunny lines covering the first row and column.**  \n\n   \\[\n   H:\\; y = 1 ,\\qquad V:\\; x = 1 .\n   \\]\n\n   \\(H\\) contains all points \\((a,1)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\);  \n   \\(V\\) contains all points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\).\n\n3. **Remaining non\u2011sunny diagonal lines.**  \n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n-k-1\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   D_c:\\; x+y = c+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(D_c\\) contains exactly the points \\((a,b)\\in S\\) with \\(a+b=c+1\\); there are \\(c\\) such points, all belonging to \\(S\\).\n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\nk\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;2\\;(\\text{horizontal, vertical})\\;+\\;(n-k-2)\\;(\\text{diagonals}) = n .\n\\]\n\nNow we verify that every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of these lines.\n\n* If a point \\((a,b)\\) has \\(a=1\\) it lies on \\(V\\); if \\(b=1\\) it lies on \\(H\\).\n* Otherwise \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\).  Put \\(c:=a+b-1\\).  \n  Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(2\\le c\\le n\\).  \n\n  - If \\(c\\le n-k-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a+b=c+1\\) and therefore belongs to the diagonal line \\(D_c\\).\n  - If \\(c\\ge n-k\\) then \\(a+b\\ge n-k+1\\).  Since \\(a,b\\ge 2\\), this forces either \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\), a contradiction.  Hence this case cannot occur.\n\nThus every point of \\(S\\) is covered.  The construction uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, all distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Conclusion\n\nFrom the extremal inequality (3) we have proved that any feasible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe explicit construction above shows that for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) such a configuration exists.\n\nTherefore the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required family of \\(n\\) lines exists is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
