n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{i}{k+1}\\,(i+1)+1\n   =\\frac{i(i+1)}{k+1}+1\n   =\\frac{i(i+1)+(k+1)}{k+1}\n   =\\frac{(i+1)(i+k+1)}{k+1}\n   =n+2-(i+1)=b_{i},\n   \\]\n\n   where we used the identity \\(k+1 = n-(i+1)+i\\).  Hence each \\(L_{i}\\) indeed passes through the point \\((a_{i},b_{i})\\).\n\nNow we check that every point of \\(S\\) is covered.\n\n* If a point has \\(a=1\\) it lies on \\(V\\); if \\(b=1\\) it lies on \\(H\\).\n* If \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) then \\(a+b\\) lies in the interval \\([4,n+1]\\).  \n  \u2013 If \\(a+b\\le n-k-1\\) the point lies on the diagonal \\(D_{a+b}\\).  \n  \u2013 If \\(a+b\\ge n-k\\) then \\(a+b=n+2\\) (the only remaining possible sum) and the point is exactly one of the \\((a_{i},b_{i})\\) constructed above, hence lies on the sunny line \\(L_{i}\\).\n\nThus every element of \\(S\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.  The family consists of \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, all distinct, completing the construction.\n\nCombining the necessity (inequality (3)) with the explicit construction shows that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Necessity\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nk:=|\\{L\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L\\text{ is sunny}\\}|,\\qquad\nt:=n-k .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 1.** A sunny line contains at most two points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.* Suppose a sunny line contains three distinct points of \\(S\\).  \nBecause all three points have integer coordinates and satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the only possible slopes are \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  Each of these slopes is excluded for a sunny line \u2013 contradiction. \u220e\n\nHence the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 2.** A non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n* Horizontal line \\(y=c\\) (\\(c\\ge 1\\)): points are \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); at most \\(n\\).  \n* Vertical line \\(x=c\\) (\\(c\\ge 1\\)): points are \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); at most \\(n\\).  \n* Diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)): points are \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\); again at most \\(n\\). \u220e\n\nThus the non\u2011sunny lines together cover at most \\(tn\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\nSince every point of \\(S\\) must be covered,  \n\n\\[\n2k+tn\\ge |S| . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(t=n-k\\) and (1),\n\n\\[\n2k+(n-k)n\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nk\\le n-2 . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nTherefore any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\); equivalently at least two of the \\(n\\) lines are forced to be non\u2011sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Sufficiency\n\nWe prove that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a suitable family exists.\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).  Define the following \\(n\\) lines.\n\n1. **Horizontal and vertical non\u2011sunny lines**\n\n   \\[\n   H:\\;y=1 ,\\qquad V:\\;x=1 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Diagonal non\u2011sunny lines**  \n\n   For each integer \\(s\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   3\\le s\\le n-k-1\n   \\]\n\n   set  \n\n   \\[\n   D_{s}:\\;x+y=s .\n   \\]\n\n   There are \\(n-k-2\\) of these lines.\n\n3. **Sunny lines**  \n\n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{i}:\\; y = \\frac{i}{k+1}\\,x + 1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(\\frac{i}{k+1}\\) is positive and different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence each \\(L_{i}\\) is sunny.  \n   The lines \\(L_{i}\\) are distinct because their slopes are distinct.\n\n   Moreover each \\(L_{i}\\) passes through the point \\((1,1)\\) (already on \\(H\\) and \\(V\\)) and through the additional point  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{i}:=\\bigl(i+1,\\;n+2-(i+1)\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   Indeed,\n\n   \\[\n   n+2-(i+1)=\\frac{i}{k+1}(i+1)+1,\n   \\]\n\n   which follows from the elementary identity  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{i}{k+1}(i+1)+1\n   =\\frac{i(i+1)+(k+1)}{k+1}\n   =\\frac{(i+1)(i+k+1)}{k+1}\n   =n+2-(i+1),\n   \\]\n\n   because \\(k+1 = n-(i+1)+i\\).  Hence \\(P_{i}\\in L_{i}\\).\n\nCounting the lines we have  \n\n\\[\nk\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;2\\;(\\text{horizontal, vertical})\\;+\\;(n-k-2)\\;(\\text{diagonals}) = n .\n\\]\n\nNow we verify that every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of these lines.\n\n* If a point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a=1\\) it lies on \\(V\\); if \\(b=1\\) it lies on \\(H\\).\n\n* Suppose \\(a,b\\ge 2\\).  Then \\(a+b\\) belongs to \\(\\{4,5,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).\n\n  \u2013 If \\(a+b\\le n-k-1\\) the point lies on the diagonal \\(D_{a+b}\\).\n\n  \u2013 If \\(a+b\\ge n-k\\) then, because the maximal possible sum is \\(n+1\\), we must have \\(a+b=n+2\\).  In this case \\((a,b)\\) is exactly one of the points \\(P_{i}\\) defined above (choose \\(i=a-1\\)), and therefore lies on the sunny line \\(L_{i}\\).\n\nThus every element of \\(S\\) is covered.  The family consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, all distinct.  Hence a configuration exists for every \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Conclusion\n\nThe extremal counting argument forces \\(k\\le n-2\\); the explicit construction shows that this bound is attainable for every admissible \\(k\\).  Consequently the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
