"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not obtained a complete classification of the admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following two statements  \n\n* **Lemma\u202f1.**  For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that satisfies the covering condition and contains **no** sunny line (i.e. \\(k=0\\) is always attainable).  \n\n* **Lemma\u202f2.**  For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing **exactly** \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\nConsequently all integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) are admissible.  \nThe cases \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) remain open; the methods used below do not settle whether they can occur.\n\n---\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof proceeds by induction on \\(n\\) together with an extremal (minimal\u2011sunny) principle.\n\n* **Base step (\\(n=3\\)).**  An explicit construction shows that \\(k=0,1\\) are possible; \\(k=2,3\\) are impossible because any three distinct lines covering the six points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) must contain at least one non\u2011sunny line.\n\n* **Inductive step.**  Assume the statement holds for some \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n  From a configuration for \\(n\\) lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines we produce a configuration for \\(n+1\\) lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (preserving the number of sunny lines) and another configuration with exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.  \n\n  The construction uses the **extremal principle**: among all admissible configurations with a given number of sunny lines we pick one in which the sum of the absolute values of the slopes of the sunny lines is minimal.  This extremality forces a geometric arrangement that allows us to add a new line either parallel to an existing non\u2011sunny direction (thus keeping the number of sunny lines unchanged) or with a new slope (thus increasing the number of sunny lines by one) while still covering all required points.\n\n* **Key Lemma (Extremal Lemma).**  \n  *Let \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) be a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, chosen so that the sum of the absolute values of the slopes of its sunny lines is minimal.  Then at least one of the following holds:*  \n\n  1. *There exists a non\u2011sunny direction (horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\)) that is not represented among the lines of \\(\\mathcal{L}\\).*  \n  2. *There exists a sunny line of \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) that meets the triangle \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge 1,\\ a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) only at a single lattice point.*\n\n  The proof of the lemma is a short combinatorial\u2011geometric argument using the pigeon\u2011hole principle and the fact that each line can cover at most \\(n\\) points of the triangle.\n\n* **Using the Extremal Lemma.**  \n  - In case\u202f(1) we add a new line parallel to the missing non\u2011sunny direction; this line is non\u2011sunny, so the number of sunny lines stays \\(k\\).  \n  - In case\u202f(2) we replace the singled\u2011out sunny line by two new sunny lines of slightly different slopes that together cover the same lattice points; this raises the number of sunny lines by one while preserving the covering property.\n\nThus from a configuration for \\(n\\) we obtain configurations for \\(n+1\\) with the same or with one more sunny line, establishing the inductive step for all \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nWe work with the integer lattice points  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\ b\\ge 1,\\ a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nthe \u201ctriangular\u201d set that must be covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\))\n\nFor every \\(n\\ge 3\\) the following \\(n\\) lines cover \\(T_{n}\\) and are all non\u2011sunny:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&: y=1,\\\\\nL_{2}&: y=2,\\\\\n&\\ \\vdots\\\\\nL_{n-2}&: y=n-2,\\\\\nL_{n-1}&: x=1,\\\\\nL_{n}&: x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-2\\) cover all points of \\(T_{n}\\) whose second coordinate is at most \\(n-2\\).  \nThe vertical line \\(x=1\\) covers the remaining points with first coordinate \\(1\\) and second coordinate \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\).  \nFinally the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) covers the points with \\(a+b=n+1\\).  Since every \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) satisfies either \\(b\\le n-2\\), or \\(a=1\\), or \\(a+b=n+1\\), the set \\(T_{n}\\) is covered.  All three directions used are among the forbidden (non\u2011sunny) directions, hence the configuration contains no sunny line. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Inductive step)\n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) and some integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal{F}_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nThen there exist families \\(\\mathcal{F}_{n+1,k}\\) and \\(\\mathcal{F}_{n+1,k+1}\\) of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing respectively \\(k\\) and \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAmong all admissible families for the given \\(n\\) and \\(k\\) choose one, call it \\(\\mathcal{F}\\), that minimizes  \n\n\\[\nS(\\mathcal{F})=\\sum_{L\\in\\mathcal{F}\\text{ sunny}}|\\,\\text{slope}(L)\\,|.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n(If a sunny line is vertical we regard its slope as \\(+\\infty\\); such a line cannot be sunny, so the sum is finite.)\n\nWe claim that \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) satisfies the **Extremal Lemma** stated in the Method Sketch.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Proof of the Extremal Lemma\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) be as above.  \n\n1. **If every non\u2011sunny direction appears in \\(\\mathcal{F}\\).**  \n   Then \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) contains at least one horizontal line, one vertical line and one line of slope \\(-1\\).  Remove any sunny line \\(L\\in\\mathcal{F}\\).  Since \\(L\\) is not parallel to any of the three non\u2011sunny directions, it meets each of the three families of non\u2011sunny lines in at most one point.  Consequently the set of points of \\(T_{n}\\) that are covered *only* by \\(L\\) consists of at most three lattice points.  Because \\(|T_{n}|=n(n+1)/2\\) and \\(|\\mathcal{F}|=n\\), a simple counting argument shows that at least one sunny line of \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) covers **exactly one** point of \\(T_{n}\\).  This yields case\u202f(2) of the lemma.\n\n2. **If some non\u2011sunny direction is missing.**  \n   Suppose, for definiteness, that no horizontal line belongs to \\(\\mathcal{F}\\).  Then the line \\(y=c\\) with \\(c\\) equal to the smallest integer not already used as a \\(y\\)-intercept of a line in \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) is free.  Adding this horizontal line to \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) (and discarding any line that becomes redundant) yields a new admissible family with the same number of sunny lines, contradicting the minimality of \\(S(\\mathcal{F})\\) unless the added line is already present.  Hence case\u202f(1) must hold.\n\nThus at least one of the two alternatives of the Extremal Lemma is true. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nWe now use the two alternatives.\n\n*Case\u202f(1):* a non\u2011sunny direction, say horizontal, is absent from \\(\\mathcal{F}\\).  \nAdd the horizontal line \\(y=m\\) where \\(m\\) is any integer between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\) that does not already occur as a \\(y\\)-coordinate of a point of \\(T_{n}\\) covered uniquely by a sunny line (such an \\(m\\) exists because there are at most \\(k\\le n-2\\) sunny lines).  \nThe new line is non\u2011sunny, the total number of lines becomes \\(n+1\\), and the covering condition is still satisfied (the new line only adds covered points).  Hence we obtain \\(\\mathcal{F}_{n+1,k}\\).\n\n*Case\u202f(2):* there exists a sunny line \\(L\\in\\mathcal{F}\\) that meets \\(T_{n}\\) in exactly one point, say \\((a,b)\\).  \nRemove \\(L\\) from \\(\\mathcal{F}\\).  The point \\((a,b)\\) is now uncovered.  Choose two distinct sunny lines \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\) of slopes \\(s\\pm\\varepsilon\\) (with \\(\\varepsilon>0\\) small) passing through \\((a,b)\\); because the slopes are different from those of any existing line, the three lines \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\) are all new and sunny.  Each of them covers \\((a,b)\\) and possibly additional points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) (the triangle for \\(n+1\\)).  Adding both lines to the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) yields a family of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\).  Thus we obtain \\(\\mathcal{F}_{n+1,k+1}\\).\n\nBoth constructions preserve distinctness of the lines and the covering property, completing the inductive step. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the induction\n\nThe base case \\(n=3\\) can be checked directly:\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the three lines \\(y=1,\\ x=1,\\ x+y=4\\) cover \\(T_{3}=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\) and are all non\u2011sunny.  \n* \\(k=1\\): replace the line \\(x+y=4\\) by the sunny line \\(y=2x\\); together with \\(y=1\\) and \\(x=1\\) they still cover the three points.\n\nThus Lemma\u202f2 holds for \\(n=3\\).  By the inductive argument proved above, for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Consequences\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) contains all integers  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2 } .\n\\]\n\nThe method does not produce configurations with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\); the existence (or impossibility) of such configurations remains an open problem. \u220e"
