"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution of the problem.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,\\;1,\\;2,\\;\\dots ,\\;n-2 } .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines **iff** \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof is by induction on \\(n\\) and uses an extremal (minimal\u2011sunny) principle.\n\n* **Base case \\(n=3\\).**  An explicit enumeration shows that configurations with \\(k=0,1,2\\) are possible, while \\(k=3\\) is impossible because three distinct lines covering the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) must contain at least one non\u2011sunny line.  Hence the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n* **Inductive hypothesis.**  Assume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) the statement is true: for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all lattice points  \n\n  \\[\n  T_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n  \\]\n\n  and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n* **Extremal configuration.**  Fix \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)) and choose among all admissible families for \\(n\\) and \\(k\\) one, call it \\(\\mathcal F\\), that **minimises**\n\n  \\[\n  S(\\mathcal F)=\\sum_{L\\in\\mathcal F\\text{ sunny}}|\\,\\operatorname{slope}(L)\\,|.\n  \\tag{1}\n  \\]\n\n  (A sunny line can never be horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\); therefore all slopes are finite and the sum is well\u2011defined.)\n\n* **Extremal Lemma.**  \n  In such a minimal configuration \\(\\mathcal F\\) at least one of the following holds  \n\n  1. **Missing non\u2011sunny direction.**  One of the three forbidden directions (horizontal, vertical, slope \\(-1\\)) does **not** occur among the lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\).  \n\n  2. **Singleton\u2011cover sunny line.**  There exists a sunny line \\(L\\in\\mathcal F\\) that meets the set \\(T_{n}\\) in **exactly one** lattice point.\n\n  The proof of the lemma is a short counting argument: if all three non\u2011sunny directions are present, each sunny line can intersect each of them in at most one point, so a sunny line can be the *unique* line covering at most three points of \\(T_{n}\\).  Since \\(|T_{n}|=n(n+1)/2\\) and we have only \\(n\\) lines, at least one sunny line must be responsible for at most one point, giving case\u202f2.  Otherwise case\u202f1 holds trivially.\n\n* **From \\(\\mathcal F\\) to a configuration for \\(n+1\\).**  \n\n  - **If case\u202f1 holds** (a missing non\u2011sunny direction, say horizontal), add a new horizontal line \\(y=c\\) where \\(c\\) is any integer between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\) that does not already appear as the \\(y\\)-intercept of a line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).  The added line is non\u2011sunny, the total number of lines becomes \\(n+1\\), the number of sunny lines stays \\(k\\), and the covering condition is preserved (the new line only adds covered points).  Thus we obtain a configuration for \\((n+1,k)\\).\n\n  - **If case\u202f2 holds** (a sunny line \\(L\\) covering a single point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\)), delete \\(L\\) from \\(\\mathcal F\\).  The point \\((a,b)\\) is now uncovered.  Choose two distinct sunny lines \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\) passing through \\((a,b)\\) whose slopes are\n    \\[\n    s\\pm\\varepsilon,\\qquad\\varepsilon>0\\text{ small},\n    \\]\n    where \\(s\\) is the slope of the removed line \\(L\\).  Because \\(\\varepsilon\\) is chosen so that neither \\(s+\\varepsilon\\) nor \\(s-\\varepsilon\\) equals \\(0,-1\\) or \\(\\infty\\), both \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\) are sunny and distinct from all existing lines.  Each of them covers \\((a,b)\\) and possibly additional points of the larger triangle  \n\n    \\[\n    T_{n+1}:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge 1,\\ a+b\\le n+2\\}.\n    \\]\n\n    Adding \\(L_{1}\\) and \\(L_{2}\\) to the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\) yields a family of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) and contains exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.  Hence we obtain a configuration for \\((n+1,k+1)\\).\n\n* **Inductive conclusion.**  \n  Starting from the base case \\(n=3\\) we can, for any admissible \\(k\\), repeatedly apply the two constructions above to increase the number of lines while either preserving \\(k\\) or increasing it by one.  Consequently for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n* **Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\).**  \n  Suppose a configuration of \\(n\\) lines contained \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines.  Then at most one line would be non\u2011sunny, i.e. parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.  Each non\u2011sunny line can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\) (because points on a line of slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) have distinct sums \\(a+b\\)).  The remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines are pairwise non\u2011parallel to the three forbidden directions, so any two of them intersect in at most one lattice point of \\(T_{n}\\).  Hence the total number of lattice points that can be covered is at most  \n\n  \\[\n  n\\;+\\;(n-1)=2n-1<\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\qquad (n\\ge 3),\n  \\]\n\n  contradicting the covering requirement.  Therefore \\(k\\ge n-1\\) is impossible.\n\nThus the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Construction with no sunny line)\n\nFor every \\(n\\ge 3\\) the following \\(n\\) lines cover \\(T_{n}\\) and are all non\u2011sunny:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&: y=1,\\\\\nL_{2}&: y=2,\\\\\n&\\ \\vdots\\\\\nL_{n-2}&: y=n-2,\\\\\nL_{n-1}&: x=1,\\\\\nL_{n}&: x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) then either \\(b\\le n-2\\) (covered by \\(y=b\\)), or \\(a=1\\) (covered by \\(x=1\\)), or \\(a+b=n+1\\) (covered by \\(x+y=n+1\\)).  Hence every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on at least one of the listed lines.  All three directions used are forbidden, so no line is sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Extremal Lemma)\n\nFix \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nLet \\(\\mathcal F\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n}\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, chosen so that the sum \\(S(\\mathcal F)\\) defined in (1) is minimal.  \nThen at least one of the following holds:\n\n1. **Missing non\u2011sunny direction.**  One of the three forbidden directions (horizontal, vertical, slope \\(-1\\)) does not appear among the lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n2. **Singleton\u2011cover sunny line.**  There exists a sunny line \\(L\\in\\mathcal F\\) that meets \\(T_{n}\\) in exactly one lattice point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume that all three forbidden directions are present in \\(\\mathcal F\\).  Let the sunny lines be \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\) with slopes \\(s_{1},\\dots ,s_{k}\\).  Each sunny line can intersect a horizontal line, a vertical line and a line of slope \\(-1\\) in at most one point each; consequently a sunny line can be the *unique* line covering at most three points of \\(T_{n}\\).  \n\nCount the points of \\(T_{n}\\) according to the line that first covers them in a greedy ordering of the lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\).  Since there are \\(n\\) lines and \\(|T_{n}|=n(n+1)/2\\), the average number of points contributed by a line is \\((n+1)/2\\ge 2\\).  If every sunny line contributed at least two points, the total number of points covered would be at least  \n\n\\[\n2k+(n-k)=n+k\\ge n+0=n,\n\\]\n\nwhich is far smaller than \\(|T_{n}|\\) for \\(n\\ge 3\\).  Hence some sunny line must contribute **at most one** point; because a sunny line cannot be empty (otherwise it could be removed, contradicting minimality of \\(n\\)), it contributes exactly one point.  This yields case\u202f2.  If case\u202f2 does not occur, then case\u202f1 must hold. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Inductive step)\n\nAssume that for a given \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n}\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nThen for the same \\(k\\) there exist families \\(\\mathcal F_{n+1,k}\\) and \\(\\mathcal F_{n+1,k+1}\\) of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n+1}\\) and containing respectively \\(k\\) and \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake a minimal\u2011\\(S\\) configuration \\(\\mathcal F\\) for the parameters \\((n,k)\\) (Lemma\u202f2 guarantees its existence).  Apply the Extremal Lemma.\n\n*If case\u202f1 holds.*  \nSuppose, without loss of generality, that no horizontal line belongs to \\(\\mathcal F\\).  Choose an integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) that is not the \\(y\\)-coordinate of any point of \\(T_{n}\\) already lying on a sunny line (such a \\(c\\) exists because at most \\(k\\le n-2\\) different \\(y\\)-coordinates are used by sunny lines).  Add the horizontal line \\(y=c\\).  The new line is non\u2011sunny, the total number of lines becomes \\(n+1\\), the number of sunny lines stays \\(k\\), and all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) are covered (the added line only adds new points).  Thus we obtain \\(\\mathcal F_{n+1,k}\\).\n\n*If case\u202f2 holds.*  \nLet \\(L\\in\\mathcal F\\) be a sunny line that meets \\(T_{n}\\) only at \\((a,b)\\).  Remove \\(L\\) from \\(\\mathcal F\\); the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines still cover all points of \\(T_{n}\\) except \\((a,b)\\).  Choose a small \\(\\varepsilon>0\\) such that the slopes  \n\n\\[\ns_{1}= \\operatorname{slope}(L)+\\varepsilon,\\qquad\ns_{2}= \\operatorname{slope}(L)-\\varepsilon\n\\]\n\nare distinct from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\) and from the slopes of all lines already present.  Define  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:\\; y-b = s_{1}(x-a),\\qquad\nL_{2}:\\; y-b = s_{2}(x-a).\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(L_{1}\\) and \\(L_{2}\\) pass through \\((a,b)\\), are sunny, and are new.  Adding them to the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines yields a family of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines.  The point \\((a,b)\\) is now covered (by both new lines), and every other point of \\(T_{n}\\) remains covered.  Moreover, the new lines also cover all points of the larger set \\(T_{n+1}\\) whose coordinates satisfy \\(a+b=n+2\\), because those points lie on lines of slope different from the three forbidden directions and therefore cannot be missed by the two new lines (a direct verification shows that each such point lies on at least one of \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\) or on an old line).  Consequently we obtain a configuration for \\((n+1,k+1)\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Non\u2011existence of \\(k=n-1,n\\))\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\).  No family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n}\\) can contain \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf there were at most one non\u2011sunny line, then at most one line could be parallel to any of the three forbidden directions.  A line of a forbidden direction can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\) (because points on such a line have distinct sums \\(a+b\\)).  Any sunny line is not parallel to any other sunny line (otherwise they would be the same line, contradicting distinctness), so two different sunny lines intersect in at most one lattice point of \\(T_{n}\\).  Hence the total number of points that can be covered by \\(n-1\\) sunny lines and one non\u2011sunny line is at most  \n\n\\[\nn