; in other words a sunny line can cover **at most one** point of the set\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) and we have only \\(n\\) sunny lines, the pigeon\u2011hole principle forces the existence of a point of \\(S\\) that is not covered \u2013 a contradiction.  \nTherefore at least one of the \\(n\\) lines must be non\u2011sunny, i.e. \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\nCombining Steps\u202f1\u20133 we obtain the complete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|S|=1+2+\\dots +n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line in the plane is called **sunny** if its direction is different from the three forbidden directions:\nhorizontal (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)), vertical (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)) and diagonal of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).\nThus a non\u2011sunny line is parallel to one of those three families.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) satisfies \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); consequently it lies on the line \\(L_a\\).\nAll the \\(L_i\\) are vertical, hence none of them is sunny.\nThus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Existence for any \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\nChoose \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines \\(M_1,\\dots ,M_k\\) whose slopes are pairwise different and also different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\nFor each \\(j\\) pick the intercept of \\(M_j\\) so that the line does **not** pass through any point of \\(S\\); this is possible because each line of a forbidden direction contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\), while there are infinitely many possible intercepts.\n\nNow consider the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines\n\n\\[\nN_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\]\n\nBecause the \\(M_j\\) have been chosen to avoid all points of \\(S\\), every point of \\(S\\) must be covered by one of the vertical lines \\(N_i\\).  \nIndeed, for any \\((a,b)\\in S\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); if \\(a\\le n-k\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(N_a\\); if \\(a>n-k\\) then \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) and consequently \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le k\\). Since the sunny lines were chosen to avoid all points of \\(S\\), the case \\(a>n-k\\) cannot occur; hence every point of \\(S\\) is covered by some \\(N_i\\).\n\nThus we have constructed \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that satisfy the required covering property.  \nHence every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering all points of \\(S\\).\n\nDefine the three families of forbidden lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal H &=\\{\\,y=c\\mid 1\\le c\\le n\\,\\},\\\\\n\\mathcal V &=\\{\\,x=c\\mid 1\\le c\\le n\\,\\},\\\\\n\\mathcal D &=\\{\\,x+y=c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\,\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEach point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) belongs to exactly one line of each family:\n\\[\n(a,b)\\in H_a\\in\\mathcal H,\\qquad (a,b)\\in V_a\\in\\mathcal V,\\qquad (a,b)\\in D_{a+b}\\in\\mathcal D .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause a sunny line is **not** parallel to any line of \\(\\mathcal H,\\mathcal V,\\mathcal D\\), it can intersect each line of \\(\\mathcal H\\) (resp. \\(\\mathcal V,\\mathcal D\\)) in at most one point. Consequently a sunny line can contain at most one point of the set \\(S\\); otherwise two distinct points of \\(S\\) would lie on the same line of \\(\\mathcal H\\) (or \\(\\mathcal V\\) or \\(\\mathcal D\\)), contradicting the uniqueness in (2).\n\nHence every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) covers **at most one** element of \\(S\\).  \nWith only \\(n\\) lines we can therefore cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\nBut by (1) we have \\(|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>n\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nThus the assumption that \\(n\\) sunny lines suffice leads to a contradiction with the pigeon\u2011hole principle (the \\( \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points must be placed into only \\(n\\) \u201choles\u201d, each hole holding at most one point).\n\nTherefore at least one of the \\(n\\) lines must be non\u2011sunny, i.e. \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Conclusion\n\nWe have shown\n\n* for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines;\n* a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\nHence the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\}} .\n\\]"
