ne of slope \\(-1\\) that is **not**\nparallel to the forbidden family, e.g.  \n\n\\[\nL_n:\\;x+y=n+ \\tfrac12 .\n\\]\n\nThis line meets every point with \\(a+b=n\\) and, because its slope is \\(-1\\) but its intercept is not an integer,\nit is not parallel to any line of the forbidden diagonal family, therefore it is sunny.\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) satisfies either \\(a\\le n-1\\) (hence lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\)) or\n\\(a+b=n\\) (hence lies on \\(L_n\\)).  Thus all points are covered and exactly one line is sunny, i.e. \\(k=n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Construction for any \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe start from the configuration of \u00a72.3 (one sunny line, \\(n-1\\) vertical lines) and\nreplace successively vertical lines by sunny lines while preserving the covering property.\n\nSuppose we have already replaced \\(t\\) vertical lines (\\(0\\le t< n-1\\)) and obtained a family\n\\(\\mathcal F_t\\) consisting of  \n\n* the original sunny line \\(L_n\\);\n* \\(t\\) newly introduced sunny lines \\(M_1,\\dots ,M_t\\);\n* the remaining \\(n-1-t\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1-t\\).\n\nAll points of \\(S\\) are still covered: the points whose \\(x\\)-coordinate is at most \\(n-1-t\\) are covered by the\nremaining vertical lines, the points with larger \\(x\\)-coordinate are covered either by \\(L_n\\) or by one of the\n\\(M_i\\) (the induction hypothesis guarantees this).\n\nNow we replace the next vertical line, say \\(x=c\\) with \\(c=n-1-t\\), by a sunny line \\(M_{t+1}\\) that passes\nthrough **all** points of that column:\n\\[\n(c,1),\\;(c,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(c,\\,n+1-c\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nThese points are not collinear unless the line is vertical, because their \\(x\\)-coordinates are equal\nand their \\(y\\)-coordinates are distinct.  Hence there exists a unique line through any two of them,\nand that line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) (otherwise it would be parallel to