            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n*Definitions.*  \nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) let  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{G}_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its direction vector is not parallel to any of the three lines\n\\[\nx\\text{-axis }(y=0),\\qquad y\\text{-axis }(x=0),\\qquad x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nThus a sunny line is neither horizontal, nor vertical, nor of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Existence for \\(k=0\\))\n\nThe family of vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(\\mathcal{G}_n\\) and contains no sunny line.\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{G}_n\\). From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le n\\) and, of course, \\(a\\ge 1\\). Hence \\(a\\) is an integer with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), so \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation \\(x=a\\); i.e. it lies on the line \\(L_a\\).  \nEach \\(L_i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore it is not sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (A column needs a vertical line)\n\nFor any integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) define the column  \n\n\\[\nC_c=\\{(c,b)\\mid b\\ge 1,\\;c+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) is not vertical, then \\(\\ell\\cap C_c\\) contains at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nA non\u2011vertical line can be written uniquely as \\(y=mx+q\\) with a finite slope \\(m\\).  \nFixing \\(x=c\\) gives the single value \\(y=mc+q\\). Hence there is at most one point of the form \\((c,y)\\) on \\(\\ell\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (All lines must be vertical)\n\nLet \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(\\mathcal{G}_n\\). Then every \\(\\ell_i\\) is vertical; consequently the number of sunny lines in the set is \\(0\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, for contradiction, that some line \\(\\ell\\) among the \\(n\\) is not vertical.  \nConsider the column \\(C_c\\) where \\(c\\) is the integer \\(x\\)-coordinate of any point of \\(\\mathcal{G}_n\\) that is not already covered by a vertical line (such a column exists because \\(\\ell\\) is not vertical and therefore can cover at most one point of each column, by Lemma\u202f2).  \n\nSince \\(\\ell\\) can meet \\(C_c\\) in at most one point, the remaining points of \\(C_c\\) must be covered by other lines. The only way for a line to contain two distinct points with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate is to be vertical. Hence each column \\(C_c\\) requires a distinct vertical line in the family.  \n\nThere are exactly \\(n\\) columns \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_n\\). If any line is non\u2011vertical, we would need at least \\(n\\) vertical lines *in addition* to that line, which would exceed the allowed total of \\(n\\) lines. This contradiction shows that no non\u2011vertical line can be present; all \\(\\ell_i\\) are vertical. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Consequence\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f3 we obtain that any admissible configuration of \\(n\\) lines contains **no** sunny line. Therefore the only value of \\(k\\) for which a configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\nk=0 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Remarks and Open Part\n\nThe argument above establishes the existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\) (Lemma\u202f1) and shows that a configuration containing a sunny line would force the total number of lines to exceed \\(n\\). Hence, under the interpretation that each column \\(C_c\\) must be covered completely, the set of admissible \\(k\\) is \\(\\{0\\}\\).\n\nThe original problem asks for *all* non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which such a configuration exists. My current proof does not rule out more subtle arrangements in which a non\u2011vertical (sunny) line could, together with other non\u2011vertical lines, cover all points of a column without using a vertical line. Resolving whether such arrangements are possible for any \\(k>0\\) remains open. \n\nThus the present work provides a rigorous partial solution: it confirms that \\(k=0\\) is always possible and gives a strong necessary condition suggesting that larger \\(k\\) may be impossible, but a complete classification has not been achieved. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
