nce the line can contain at most \\(n+1-c\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \nIf the distinct horizontal lines have ordinates \\(c_1,\\dots ,c_h\\) then\n\n\\[\n\\#\\bigl(S_n\\cap\\bigcup\\mathcal H\\bigr)\\le\n\\sum_{i=1}^{h}(n+1-c_i).\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2.1.2  Points covered by vertical lines  \n\nAnalogously, a vertical line \\(x=c\\) can contain at most \\(n+1-c\\) points\nof \\(S_n\\).  With distinct abscissae \\(c'_1,\\dots ,c'_v\\),\n\n\\[\n\\#\\bigl(S_n\\cap\\bigcup\\mathcal V\\bigr)\\le\n\\sum_{i=1}^{v}(n+1-c'_i).\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2.1.3  Points covered by diagonal lines  \n\nA line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=t\\) with integer\n\\(t\\ge 2\\).  Inside \\(S_n\\) it contains exactly the points with\n\\(a+b=t\\), i.e. \\(t-1\\) points.  For distinct constants\n\\(t_1,\\dots ,t_d\\),\n\n\\[\n\\#\\bigl(S_n\\cap\\bigcup\\mathcal D\\bigr)\\le\n\\sum_{i=1}^{d}(t_i-1).\n\\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2.1.4  Global upper bound  \n\nAdding (2.3)\u2013(2.5) we obtain\n\n\\[\n|S_n|\\le\n\\sum_{i=1}^{h}(n+1-c_i)\n+\\sum_{i=1}^{v}(n+1-c'_i)\n+\\sum_{i=1}^{d}(t_i-1).\n\\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\nEach summand is a decreasing function of its index; therefore the\nright\u2011hand side is maximised when the integers are taken as small as\npossible, subject to the distinctness constraints.  Consequently we may\nassume\n\n\\[\nc_i=i\\;(1\\le i\\le h),\\qquad\nc'_i=i\\;(1\\le i\\le v),\\qquad\nt_i=i+1\\;(1\\le i\\le d)\n\\]\n\n(the smallest admissible constant for a diagonal line is \\(2\\)).  Substituting\ninto (2.6) yields\n\n\\[\n|S_n|\\le\nh(n+1)-\\frac{h(h+1)}2\n+v(n+1)-\\frac{v(v+1)}2\n+\\frac{d(d+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\nUsing (2.1) and the relation \\(d=n-h-v\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le\nh(n+1)-\\frac{h(h+1)}2\n+v(n+1)-\\frac{v(v+1)}2\n+\\frac{(n-h-v)(n-h-v+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives\n\n\\[\n\\frac{h(h+1)}2+\\frac{v(v+1)}2\n\\le\n\\frac{(n-h-v)(n-h-v+1)}2+\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\nDefine \\(p=h\\) and \