oint with the smallest ordinate in that column). Substituting \\(x=c\\) into the equation of \\(\\ell_c\\) gives \\(y=c-(c-1)=1\\); hence \\((c,1)\\) belongs to \\(\\ell_c\\). Every other point of column \\(c\\) has ordinate \\(b\\ge 2\\); such a point already belongs to some line of the original family (because the original family covered the whole set). Consequently after the replacement all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) remain covered. The line \\(\\ell_c\\) has slope \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); therefore it is sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Inductive construction)\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(k\\).*\n\n*Base \\(k=0\\).*  \n\\(\\mathcal L_0\\) is the family of Lemma\u202f1; it contains no sunny line.\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume \\(\\mathcal L_{k-1}\\) exists for some \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).  \nBecause \\(\\mathcal L_{k-1}\\) covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), at least one of its lines is vertical (otherwise the column containing the point \\((n,1)\\) could not be covered, as a non\u2011vertical line meets a fixed column in at most one point). Choose any vertical line \\(x=c\\) belonging to \\(\\mathcal L_{k-1}\\).  \n\nForm  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{k}= \\bigl(\\mathcal L_{k-1}\\setminus\\{x=c\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell_c\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\ell_c\\) is the sunny line defined in Lemma\u202f2.  \nBy Lemma\u202f2 the new family still covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nThe number of sunny lines has increased by exactly one, because \\(\\ell_c\\) is sunny and the removed line \\(x=c\\) was not.  \nAll lines remain distinct, because the slope of \\(\\ell_c\\) is \\(1\\) whereas every vertical line has undefined slope.  \n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) satisfies the required properties. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Upper bound)\n\nIn any admissible configuration the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe configuration consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines; each line is either sunny or not sunny. Hence the number of sunny lines is at most the total number of lines, i.e. at most \\(n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final conclusion\n\nLemma\u202f3 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nLemma\u202f4 shows that no configuration can contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines.  \n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThe problem is completely solved. \u220e"

