y \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and\n\\(|\\mathcal L_k|= (n-k)+k = n\\).\n\nWe now verify that \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Case 1.* \\(a\\le n-k\\).  \nThen the point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation \\(x=a\\) and therefore lies on the\nvertical line \\(x=a\\) belonging to \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n*Case 2.* \\(a> n-k\\).  \nWrite \\(a=n-k+j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\).  \nFor the line \\(\\ell_j\\) we have the equation  \n\n\\[\ny = x-\\bigl(n-k+j-1\\bigr).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x=a=n-k+j\\) into (3) yields \\(y=1\\).  \nThus the point \\((a,1)\\) belongs to \\(\\ell_j\\).\n\nBecause \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\), we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); hence  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a = n+1-(n-k+j)=k+1-j .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEquation (3) can be rewritten as  \n\n\\[\ny = 1 + (x-(n-k+j)) .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(x=a=n-k+j\\), the right\u2011hand side equals \\(1\\).  \nIncreasing the \\(x\\)-coordinate by one increases the right\u2011hand side by one,\nso for any integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le k-j\\) the point  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(a+t,\\;1+t\\bigr)\n\\]\n\nalso satisfies (3).  \nChoosing \\(t=b-1\\) (which is allowed by (4)) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(a+(b-1),\\;1+(b-1)\\bigr)= (a,b)\n\\]\n\nand therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_j\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is contained in at least one line of\n\\(\\mathcal L_k\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Upper bound)\n\nIn any admissible configuration the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe configuration is required to consist of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.\nEach line is either sunny or not sunny, so the number of sunny lines is at most\nthe total number of lines, i.e. at most \\(n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem (Answer to the problem)\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which\nthere exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and having\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLemma\u202f1 provides, for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), an explicit family of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\)\nsunny lines.  \nLemma\u202f2 shows that no configuration can contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines.\nHence the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely the integers from \\(0\\) to\n\\(n\\). \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
