\\[\n   (n-1)\\cdot n + n = n^2\n   \\]\n\n   points covered, whereas \\(|T_n|=\\frac{(n+1)n}{2}>n^2\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n   Hence at least two lines must be non\u2011sunny, giving the bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n3. **Inductive construction (Lemma\u202f2).**  \n   *Base case* \\(n=3\\).  \n   The triangle \\(T_3\\) consists of the points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n   The three lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_1:\\; y=1,\\qquad \\ell_2:\\; x=1,\\qquad \\ell_3:\\; x+y=2\n   \\]\n\n   cover all three points; each line is non\u2011sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is realizable.  \n   Replacing \\(\\ell_3\\) by the sunny line \\(y=2-x\\) gives a configuration with exactly one sunny line, and replacing \\(\\ell_2\\) by the sunny line \\(y=2x\\) gives a configuration with exactly two sunny lines. Thus the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume the statement true for some \\(n\\ge 3\\): for every \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F_n\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\n   From \\(\\mathcal F_n\\) we build a family \\(\\mathcal F_{n+1}\\) of \\(n+1\\) lines covering \\(T_{n+1}\\) as follows.  \n\n   - The set \\(T_{n+1}\\) consists of \\(T_n\\) together with the new \u201couter layer\u2019\u2019  \n\n     \\[\n     L_{n+1}= \\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+2,\\; a,b\\ge 1\\}.\n     \\]\n\n   - Choose any line \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal F_n\\) that is **not** parallel to the three forbidden directions (such a line exists unless \\(k=n-2\\); in that extreme case we take a non\u2011sunny line and later turn it sunny).  \n   - Replace \\(\\ell\\) by two distinct lines \\(\\ell',\\ell''\\) defined by  \n\n     \\[\n     \\ell' :\\; y = -x + (n+2),\\qquad \n     \\ell'' :\\; y = m x + c,\n     \\]\n\n     where the slope \\(m\\) is chosen so that \\(\\ell''\\) is sunny and passes through the point \\((1,n+1)\\).  \n     By construction \\(\\ell'\\) contains all points of \\(L_{n+1}\\) except \\((1,n+1)\\); the point \\((1,n+1)\\) lies on \\(\\ell''\\).  \n\n   - The remaining \\(n-1\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal F_n\\) are kept unchanged.  \n\n   The new family \\(\\mathcal F_{n+1}\\) consists of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, covers every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\), and the number of sunny lines is increased by exactly one (if \\(\\ell\\) was non\u2011sunny) or stays the same (if \\(\\ell\\) was already sunny).  \n\n   Consequently, starting from a configuration for \\(n\\) with any admissible \\(k\\), we obtain a configuration for \\(n+1\\) with any admissible \\(k\\) or \\(k+1\\). By iterating this \u201cadd\u2011a\u2011row\u2019\u2019 operation we can realise **every** integer \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n-1]\\) for the size \\(n+1\\).  \n\n   Since the inductive hypothesis guarantees the existence for all \\(k\\le n-2\\) at size \\(n\\), the construction yields the existence for all \\(k\\le (n+1)-2\\) at size \\(n+1\\). This completes the induction.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma\u202f1 gives the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \n   Lemma\u202f2 shows that this condition is also sufficient: for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a suitable family of \\(n\\) lines exists.  \n\nHence the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\frac{(n+1)n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Upper bound)\n\n*Statement.*  \nIn any collection \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\;(n\\ge 3)\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(T_n\\), at least two lines are non\u2011sunny. Consequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, to the contrary, that at most one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is non\u2011sunny; i.e. at least \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny.\n\nA sunny line has slope \\(\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\). Such a line can intersect the triangle bounded by the lines \\(x=0\\), \\(y=0\\) and \\(x+y=n+1\\) in at most \\(n\\) lattice points of \\(T_n\\): indeed the intersection of a line with the region is a line segment whose endpoints lie on the boundary; moving from one lattice point to the next along the segment changes either the \\(x\\)- or the \\(y\\)-coordinate by at least \\(1\\), so at most \\(n\\) steps are possible before leaving the triangle.\n\nThe same bound \\(n\\) also holds for a non\u2011sunny line, because a vertical, horizontal or slope \\(-1\\) line can meet the triangle in at most \\(n\\) lattice points as well.\n\nHence the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered by the \\(n\\) lines is at most  \n\n\\[\nn\\cdot n = n^{2}.\n\\]\n\nBut  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{(n+1)n}{2}>n^{2}\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge 3,\n\\]\n\na contradiction. Therefore at least two lines must be non\u2011sunny, and the claimed bound follows. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Existence for every admissible \\(k\\))\n\n*Statement.*  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n1. covers all points of \\(T_n\\);\n2. contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(n\\).*\n\n**Base \\(n=3\\).**  \n\\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \n\nThe three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\; y=1,\\qquad \n\\ell_2:\\; x=1,\\qquad \n\\ell_3:\\; x+y=2\n\\]\n\nare respectively horizontal, vertical and of slope \\(-1\\); all are non\u2011sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is realised.\n\nReplacing \\(\\ell_3\\) by the sunny line \\(y=2-x\\) yields a configuration with exactly one sunny line; replacing \\(\\ell_2\\) by the sunny line \\(y=2x\\) yields a configuration with exactly two sunny lines. Since \\(n-2=1\\), the admissible values are \\(k=0,1\\); both are obtained, establishing the base case.\n\n**Inductive step.**  \nAssume the statement true for some \\(n\\ge 3\\). Let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\). We shall construct a family for \\(n+1\\) lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTake a configuration \\(\\mathcal F_n\\) for size \\(n\\) that contains \\(k'\\) sunny lines, where  \n\n\\[\nk'=\n\\begin{cases}\nk, &\\text{if }k\\le n-2,\\\\[2pt]\nn-2, &\\text{if }k=n-1 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(k'\\le n-2\\) and the inductive hypothesis guarantees the existence of \\(\\mathcal F_n\\).\n\nThe set \\(T_{n+1}\\) consists of \\(T_n\\) together with the new outer layer  \n\n\\[\nL_{n+1}= \\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+2,\\; a,b\\ge 1\\}.\n\\]\n\nChoose a line \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal F_n\\) that is **not** parallel to the three forbidden directions. Such a line exists unless all \\(n\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal F_n\\) are non\u2011sunny; the latter can happen only when \\(k'=0\\). In the exceptional case we first replace one non\u2011sunny line by a sunny line (this is always possible because a sunny line can be drawn through any point of \\(T_n\\) that is not already covered by another line), thereby increasing the number of sunny lines by one; this adjustment does not affect the covering property.\n\nHaving a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) at hand, we delete \\(\\ell\\) and insert two new lines:\n\n* \\(\\ell' : y = -x + (n+2)\\).  \n  This line has slope \\(-1\\) (hence non\u2011sunny) and passes through every point of \\(L_{n+1}\\) **except** \\((1,n+1)\\).\n\n* \\(\\ell'' : y = m x + c\\) where the slope \\(m\\) is chosen different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) (so \\(\\ell''\\) is sunny) and the line is forced to pass through the remaining point \\((1,n+1)\\).  \n  For instance, take \\(m=2\\) and set \\(c=n-1\\); then \\(\\ell''\\) contains \\((1,n+1)\\) and is sunny.\n\nThe new family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{n+1}= \\bigl(\\mathcal F_n\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell',\\ell''\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n+1\\) distinct lines. All points of \\(T_n\\) are still covered because \\(\\ell\\) was covering a subset of them and the other \\(n-1\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal F_n\\) are unchanged; the points of the new layer \\(L_{n+1}\\) are covered by \\(\\ell'\\) and \\(\\ell''\\) as described.  \n\nRegarding sunny lines, we have:\n\n* If \\(\\ell\\) was sunny, we removed one sunny line and added one sunny line (\\(\\ell''\\)); the total number of sunny lines stays \\(k'\\).\n* If \\(\\ell\\) was non\u2011sunny, we removed a non\u2011sunny line and added one sunny line; the number of sunny lines becomes \\(k'+1\\).\n\nThus the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal F_{n+1}\\) is either \\(k'\\) or \\(k'+1\\). By the choice of \\(k'\\) we obtain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for the desired \\(k\\) (if \\(k\\le n-2\\) we keep the same count; if \\(k=n-1\\) we start from a configuration with \\(k'=n-2\\) and the above replacement raises the count to \\(n-1\\)).\n\nHence for every \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,(n+1)-2\\}\\) a suitable configuration of \\(n+1\\) lines exists. This completes the inductive step.\n\nBy induction the statement holds for all \\(n\\ge 3\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the solution\n\nLemma\u202f1 shows that any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nLemma\u202f2 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) such a configuration indeed exists.\n\nTherefore the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required family of \\(n\\) lines exists is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\] \u220e"
