replacing \\(\\ell_2\\) by the sunny line \\(y=2x\\) yields a configuration with \\(k=2\\). Since \\(n-2=1\\), the admissible values \\(k=0,1\\) are realised.\n\n  *Inductive step.*  \n  Assume the statement true for some \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n  Let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \n  Choose a configuration \\(\\mathcal F_n\\) for size \\(n\\) that contains  \n\n  \\[\n  k'=\n  \\begin{cases}\n  k, & k\\le n-2,\\\\[2pt]\n  n-2, & k=n-1 .\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\]\n\n  (the inductive hypothesis guarantees such a configuration).  \n\n  The set \\(T_{n+1}\\) consists of \\(T_n\\) together with the new outer layer  \n\n  \\[\n  L_{n+1}= \\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+2,\\; a,b\\ge 1\\}.\n  \\]\n\n  **Construction.**  \n  1. Pick a line \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal F_n\\) that is not parallel to the three forbidden directions.  \n     Such a line exists unless all lines of \\(\\mathcal F_n\\) are non\u2011sunny; in that extreme case we first replace any non\u2011sunny line by a sunny line through a point of \\(T_n\\) that is already covered, thereby increasing the sunny count by one (this does not affect the covering property).  \n\n  2. Delete \\(\\ell\\) and insert two new lines  \n\n     \\[\n     \\ell' :\\; y = -x + (n+2) \\qquad(\\text{slope }-1,\\text{ non\u2011sunny}),\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\ell'' :\\; y = m x + c \\qquad(\\text{choose }m\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\text{ so that }\\ell''\\text{ is sunny and passes through }(1,n+1)).\n     \\]\n\n     The line \\(\\ell'\\) contains every point of \\(L_{n+1}\\) except \\((1,n+1)\\); the point \\((1,n+1)\\) lies on \\(\\ell''\\).\n\n  3. Keep the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal F_n\\) unchanged.\n\n  The resulting family  \n\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal F_{n+1}= \\bigl(\\mathcal F_n\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell',\\ell''\\}\n  \\]\n\n  consists of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, covers all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\), and has\n\n  * the same number of sunny lines as \\(\\mathcal F_n\\) if \\(\\ell\\) was sunny (we removed