ement  \n\n> \u201cAll points of \\(T_n\\) remain covered because the only removed line \\(\\ell\\) is replaced by two lines that together cover the points of \\(L_{n+1}\\) and also cover any point of \\(T_n\\) that lay on \\(\\ell\\) (such points are either on \\(\\ell'\\) or on \\(\\ell''\\)).\u201d\n\n*Justification Gap.* No proof is supplied that every point of \\(\\ell\\cap T_n\\) lies on either \\(\\ell'\\) or \\(\\ell''\\). In general, a point of \\(\\ell\\) need not satisfy the equation of \\(\\ell'\\) (which is a fixed line \\(x+y=n+2\\)) nor lie on the specific sunny line \\(\\ell''\\) through \\((1,n+1)\\). Hence some points formerly covered by \\(\\ell\\) could become uncovered.\n\n#### 3.7 Distinctness of the new family  \n\n> \u201cThe new family \\(\\mathcal F_{n+1}= (\\mathcal F_n\\setminus\\{\\ell\\})\\cup\\{\\ell',\\ell''\\}\\) has \\(n+1\\) distinct lines.\u201d\n\n*Justification Gap.* It is not shown that \\(\\ell'\\) and \\(\\ell''\\) are distinct from each other (they are, because slopes differ) **and** that neither coincides with any of the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal F_n\\). Without this verification the claim of \u201c\\(n+1\\) distinct lines\u201d is unsupported.\n\n#### 3.8 Counting sunny lines after the construction  \n\n> \u201cIf the removed line \\(\\ell\\) was sunny, we removed one sunny line and added the sunny line \\(\\ell''\\); the total number of sunny lines stays \\(k'\\).  \n> If \\(\\ell\\) was non\u2011sunny, we removed a non\u2011sunny line and added the sunny line \\(\\ell''\\); the total number of sunny lines becomes \\(k'+1\\).\u201d\n\nThe arithmetic is correct **provided** the construction works and the lines are distinct. However, the argument does not address the case where we need to increase the sunny count by more than one (e.g., when \\(k=n-1\\) is targeted) nor does it guarantee that the resulting number matches the originally prescribed \\(k\\). This is a **Justification Gap**.\n\n#### 3.9 Completi