her they contain all points \\((1,b)\\) and \\((a,1)\\) with\n   \\(1\\le a,b\\le n\\). In particular they already cover all points of \\(S_n\\) whose\n   first or second coordinate equals \\(1\\).\n\n   *Step 2 \u2013 the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines.*  \n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_t:\\; y = -x + (t+1).\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(\\ell_t\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore cloudy.  \n   The point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(a+b=t+1\\) lies on \\(\\ell_t\\); conversely every point of\n   \\(S_n\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) belongs to exactly one of the lines \\(\\ell_t\\) (the one with\n   \\(t=a+b-1\\)).  \n   Hence the \\(n-2\\) cloudy lines \\(\\{y=1,\\;x=1,\\;\\ell_2,\\dots ,\\ell_{n-1}\\}\\) already cover\n   the whole set \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *Step 3 \u2013 turning some cloudy lines sunny.*  \n   To obtain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines we replace \\(n-2-k\\) of the cloudy lines\n   \\(\\ell_2,\\dots ,\\ell_{n-1}\\) by distinct sunny lines that still pass through the same\n   lattice points.  \n   For each \\(t\\) that we wish to \u201csunny\u2011ify\u201d, choose a rational slope \\(\\alpha_t\\) different\n   from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and let the new line be  \n\n   \\[\n   L_t:\\; y = \\alpha_t x + \\beta_t ,\n   \\qquad\\text{where }\\beta_t = (t+1)-\\alpha_t .\n   \\]\n\n   By construction the line \\(L_t\\) contains the lattice point \\((1,t)\\) (which is already\n   covered by the horizontal line \\(y=1\\)) and also the point \\((t,1)\\) (already covered by\n   the vertical line \\(x=1\\)).  Moreover, for any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(a+b=t+1\\) and\n   \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   b = (t+1)-a = -a + (t+1) ,\n   \\]\n\n   so the original cloudy line \\(\\ell_t\\) passes through \\((a,b)\\).  Because the new line\n   \\(L_t\\) shares the two extreme points \\((1,t)\\) and \\((t,1)\\) with \\(\\ell_t\\), the segment\n   of \\(\\ell_t\\) inside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is contained in the convex hull of those\n   two points; consequently every interior lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=t+1\\) also lies\n   on \\(L_t\\).  Hence replacing \\(\\ell_t\\) by \\(L_t\\) does **not** destroy the covering\n   property.\n\n   Because the slopes \\(\\alpha_t\\) can be chosen pairwise distinct and different from\n   \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), the resulting lines are all sunny and remain distinct from each other\n   and from the two fixed cloudy lines \\(y=1\\) and \\(x=1\\).\n\n   Performing this replacement for exactly \\(n-2-k\\) values of \\(t\\) yields a family of\n   \\(n\\) distinct lines containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of\n   \\(S_n\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The two arguments above show that\n\n   * any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\);\n   * for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration exists.\n\n   Therefore the set of all admissible \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** if its direction is different from the three forbidden directions\n\\((1,0),(0,1),(1,-1)\\); otherwise it is **cloudy**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Upper bound).\n\nIf \\(n\\ge 3\\) and a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of\n\\(S_n\\), then at least two of the lines are cloudy. Consequently the number \\(k\\) of\nsunny lines satisfies \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA sunny line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  Such a line can intersect the\nright\u2011angled triangle \\(\\{(x,y)\\mid x\\ge 0,\\;y\\ge 0,\\;x+y\\le n+1\\}\\) in at most one lattice\npoint, because any two distinct lattice points on the line would determine a rational\nslope equal to the difference of their coordinates, which would have to be one of the\nforbidden slopes.  Hence each sunny line contributes at most one point of \\(S_n\\).\n\nIf all but at most one of the \\(n\\) lines were sunny, the total number of covered points\nwould be at most  \n\n\\[\n1\\cdot (n-1)+|S_n|_{\\text{cloudy}}\\le (n-1)+n =2n-1,\n\\]\n\nwhere we used that a cloudy line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (the\nintersection of a line parallel to one of the three forbidden directions with the\ntriangle is a segment of integer length at most \\(n\\)).  For \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-1 < \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|S_n|,\n\\]\n\nso the covering would be impossible.  Therefore at least two lines must be cloudy,\nand \\(k=n-\\#\\text{cloudy}\\le n-2\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Construction for any admissible \\(k\\)).\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines,\nexactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that together contain every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBegin with the following \\(n\\) distinct **cloudy** lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y=1,\\\\\nL_2 &: x=1,\\\\\nL_t &: y=-x+(t+1)\\qquad (3\\le t\\le n).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe first two are horizontal and vertical, respectively; the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines\nare parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_2\\).  \n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_1\\).  \n* If \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) then set \\(t=a+b-1\\) (so \\(3\\le t\\le n\\)); the point satisfies\n  \\(b=-a+(t+1)\\) and therefore lies on \\(L_t\\).\n\nThus the family (1) covers all of \\(S_n\\).\n\nNow we turn exactly \\(n-2-k\\) of the lines \\(L_t\\) (\\(3\\le t\\le n\\)) into sunny lines.\nFix a set \\(T\\subset\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with \\(|T|=n-2-k\\).  For each \\(t\\in T\\) choose a\nrational number \\(\\alpha_t\\) such that \\(\\alpha_t\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) and the\n\\(\\alpha_t\\)\u2019s are pairwise distinct.  Define\n\n\\[\n\\widetilde L_t :\\; y=\\alpha_t x+\\beta_t,\\qquad\n\\beta_t=(t+1)-\\alpha_t .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEquation (2) guarantees that \\(\\widetilde L_t\\) passes through the two lattice points\n\\((1,t)\\) and \\((t,1)\\).  Both of these points already belong to the covering (the first\nto \\(L_1\\), the second to \\(L_2\\)).  Moreover, for any integer pair \\((a,b)\\) with\n\\(a+b=t+1\\) and \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) we have\n\n\\[\nb = (t+1)-a = -a+(t+1) .\n\\]\n\nHence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the original cloudy line \\(L_t\\).  Since \\(\\widetilde L_t\\) shares\nthe two extreme points \\((1,t)\\) and \\((t,1)\\) with \\(L_t\\), the whole segment of \\(L_t\\)\ninside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is contained in the line \\(\\widetilde L_t\\); consequently\nevery interior lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=t+1\\) also lies on \\(\\widetilde L_t\\).\nThus replacing \\(L_t\\) by \\(\\widetilde L_t\\) does not remove any covered point.\n\nThe lines \\(\\widetilde L_t\\) are sunny by construction, they are distinct because the\nslopes \\(\\alpha_t\\) are distinct, and they are distinct from \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\) because\ntheir slopes differ from \\(0\\) and \\(\\infty\\).  Consequently the final family consists of\n\n* the two fixed cloudy lines \\(L_1, L_2\\);\n* the \\(k\\) remaining cloudy lines \\(L_t\\) with \\(t\\notin T\\);\n* the \\(n-2-k\\) sunny lines \\(\\widetilde L_t\\) with \\(t\\in T\\).\n\nAltogether there are \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and they\ncover every point of \\(S_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem.\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there\nexist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.