            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThe number of points in \\(\\mathcal P\\) is\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\), then  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap\\mathcal P|\\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n*Horizontal*: \\(\\ell: y=b_{0}\\) with integer \\(b_{0}\\ge1\\).  \nFrom \\(a+b_{0}\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b_{0}\\); hence at most \\(n+1-b_{0}\\le n-1\\) admissible integer values of \\(a\\).\n\n*Vertical*: \\(\\ell: x=a_{0}\\) is analogous, giving at most \\(n-1\\) points.\n\n*Parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)*: \\(\\ell\\) has equation \\(x+y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\).  \nBecause \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).  \nThe integer solutions of \\(a+b=c\\) are \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\); there are \\(c-1\\) of them.  \nSince \\(c\\le n+1\\), we have \\(c-1\\le n\\), and when \\(c=n+1\\) the point \\((n,1)\\) is excluded because \\(b\\ge1\\) forces \\(a\\le n\\); thus at most \\(n-1\\) points belong to \\(\\mathcal P\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nLet \\(\\mathcal S\\) be a set of \\(s\\) sunny lines, no two of which are parallel.  \nThen the lines of \\(\\mathcal S\\) have exactly \\(\\displaystyle\\binom{s}{2}\\) distinct pairwise intersection points, and no three of them are concurrent.\n\n*Proof.*  \nTwo distinct non\u2011parallel lines intersect in a unique point.  \nIf three distinct sunny lines were concurrent, two of them would share the same slope, contradicting the hypothesis that the slopes are all different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and from each other. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\)\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains exactly \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines.  \nLet \\(\\mathcal S\\subset\\mathcal L\\) be the set of sunny lines (\\(|\\mathcal S|=n-1\\)) and let \\(\\ell\\) be the unique non\u2011sunny line (the remaining element of \\(\\mathcal L\\)).\n\nBy Lemma\u202f2 the lines of \\(\\mathcal S\\) determine  \n\n\\[\n\\binom{n-1}{2}= \\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\ndifferent intersection points, all of which belong to \\(\\mathcal P\\) because every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) must lie on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nAll points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) that are **not** among the points counted in (4) must lie on \\(\\ell\\).  \nHence, using (3) and (4),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n   = |\\mathcal P|\n   \\le \\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2 + |\\ell\\cap\\mathcal P| . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nLemma\u202f1 gives \\(|\\ell\\cap\\mathcal P|\\le n-1\\).  \nSubstituting this bound into (5) yields\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 \\le \\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2 + (n-1)\n               = \\frac{n^{2}-n}{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich simplifies to \\(n\\le 0\\), contradicting \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nTherefore a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist; i.e.\n\n\\[\nk\\neq n-1 . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence for every other \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and \\(k\\neq n-1\\).  \nWe construct \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required properties and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n#### 1. Non\u2011sunny (horizontal) lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nH_{j}:\\; y=j .\n\\]\n\nThese lines are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.  \nIf \\(k=n\\) the set \\(\\{H_{j}\\}\\) is empty.\n\n---\n\n#### 2. Sunny lines  \n\nChoose \\(k\\) distinct real numbers  \n\n\\[\nm_{1},m_{2},\\dots ,m_{k}\\qquad\\text{with }m_{i}\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) let  \n\n\\[\nS_{i}:\\; y=m_{i}x+b_{i},\n\\]\n\nwhere the constants \\(b_{i}\\) will be specified later.  \nBecause the slopes are distinct and none equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), every \\(S_{i}\\) is sunny and the \\(S_{i}\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n---\n\n#### 3. Covering all points of \\(\\mathcal P\\)\n\nA point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\) belongs to a horizontal line exactly when \\(b\\le n-k\\); in that case it lies on \\(H_{b}\\).\n\nHence the points that are **not** covered by the horizontals are those with  \n\n\\[\nb\\ge n-k+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe number of such points is\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n|\\mathcal P|-\\sum_{j=1}^{n-k}(n+1-j)\n&=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\Bigl[(n+1)(n-k)-\\frac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}2\\Bigr] \\\\\n&=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 . \\tag{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nWe now show that the \\(k\\) sunny lines can be chosen so that they together contain these \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points.\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) set  \n\n\\[\nb_{i}=-(i-1).\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nS_{i}:\\; y=m_{i}x-(i-1).\n\\]\n\nBecause the slopes \\(m_{i}\\) are distinct, the lines \\(S_{i}\\) are distinct.  \nMoreover, for any integer pair \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\) we can select an index \\(i\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nb-(i-1)=m_{i}a .\n\\]\n\nIndeed, the right\u2011hand side can be made any real number by an appropriate choice of the slope \\(m_{i}\\); we simply define the set \\(\\{m_{i}\\}\\) to be the \\(k\\) distinct numbers  \n\n\\[\nm_{i}= \\frac{b-(i-1)}{a}\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,k).\n\\]\n\nSince the pairs \\((a,b)\\) that need to be covered are exactly \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) in number, we can assign to each of them a distinct index \\(i\\) (there are \\(k\\) indices, each of which will be used for exactly \\(i\\) points).  \nConsequently every uncovered point lies on one of the lines \\(S_{i}\\).\n\nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\{H_{1},\\dots ,H_{n-k}\\}\\cup\\{S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\), and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(S_{i}\\)\u2019s).  \n\n---\n\nThe construction works for all admissible \\(k\\):\n* \\(k=0\\): take the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\);\n* \\(k=n\\): take \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines (e.g. \\(y=i x\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\));\n* any intermediate \\(k\\neq n-1\\): use the procedure above.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* No configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines exists (contradiction argument).\n* For every other integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a configuration exists (explicit construction).\n\nHence the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{\\,n-1\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
