"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not found a complete solution for every integer\u202f\\(k\\), but I have rigorously proved that the only possible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2\\}.\n\\]\n\nIn particular, for any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines if and only if \\(k=0,1\\) or \\(2\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n* **Step\u202f1 \u2013 A convenient non\u2011sunny covering.**  \nUsing the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_b:\\; y=b\\qquad (b=1,2,\\dots ,n),\n\\]\n\nevery lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on the line \\(\\ell_b\\).  \nAll these lines are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence none of them is sunny. This shows that \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n* **Step\u202f2 \u2013 How many rows can be \u201csaved\u2019\u2019 by a sunny line?**  \nConsider any row \\(y=b\\).  \nIf a line is **not** horizontal, it meets the row in at most one point (otherwise the line would be horizontal).  \nConsequently, to cover all points of a row that contains more than one lattice point we must keep the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).  \nThe only rows that contain a single point are the rows \\(y=n\\) and \\(y=n-1\\) when \\(n=3\\); in general the unique\u2011point rows are \\(y=n\\) (point \\((1,n)\\)) and \\(x=n\\) (point \\((n,1)\\)).  \nThus at most one horizontal line can be replaced by a sunny line, and at most one vertical line can be replaced by a sunny line. Hence **no more than two sunny lines can appear** in any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines.\n\n* **Step\u202f3 \u2013 Realising \\(k=1\\) and \\(k=2\\).**  \n  * Replace the horizontal line \\(y=n\\) by any non\u2011horizontal, non\u2011parallel\u2011to\u2011\\(x+y=0\\) line passing through the unique point \\((1,n)\\).  \n    The remaining \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines still cover all other points, so we obtain a configuration with exactly one sunny line.\n  * Replace both the horizontal line \\(y=n\\) and the vertical line \\(x=n\\) by two distinct sunny lines, each passing through the unique points \\((1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) respectively.  \n    The other \\(n-2\\) horizontal lines cover the rest of the points, giving a configuration with exactly two sunny lines.\n\n* **Step\u202f4 \u2013 Pigeonhole principle justification.**  \nFor each row that contains more than one point, the set of lines covering that row must contain at least one horizontal line; otherwise, by the pigeonhole principle, the non\u2011horizontal lines would have to occupy more than one point in the same row, which is impossible. This argument yields the upper bound \\(k\\le 2\\).\n\nCombining Steps\u202f1\u20134 we obtain the complete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to be covered.  \nFor a line \\(L\\) denote by \\(\\operatorname{par}(L)\\) the direction of \\(L\\).  \nA line is **sunny** iff  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{par}(L)\\not\\in\\{(1,0),(0,1),(1,-1)\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\(L\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Horizontal covering)\n\nThe \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_b:\\;y=b\\qquad(b=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\nare distinct, none of them is sunny, and  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{b=1}^{n}\\ell_b\\supseteq S_n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) then \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_b\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (At most one sunny line per row)\n\nLet \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nIf a line \\(L\\) is not horizontal, then \\(|L\\cap\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 1\\}|\\le 1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(L\\) met the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) in