most two lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) can be sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5 (Realisation of \\(k=0,1,2\\))\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist families of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the family \\(\\{\\ell_b\\mid b=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) from Lemma\u202f1. All lines are horizontal, hence non\u2011sunny.\n\n*Case \\(k=1\\).*  \nReplace the horizontal line \\(\\ell_n: y=n\\) by any line \\(L\\) that\n\n1. passes through the unique point \\((1,n)\\);\n2. is not horizontal, not vertical and not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nSuch a line exists (e.g. the line through \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,n-1)\\) has slope \\(-1\\), which is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); choose instead the line through \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,n-2)\\), slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)).  \nThe resulting family consists of \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines and the single sunny line \\(L\\); it still covers every point of \\(S_n\\) because the only point that could have been lost, \\((1,n)\\), lies on \\(L\\). Hence \\(k=1\\) is attainable.\n\n*Case \\(k=2\\).*  \nReplace both \\(\\ell_n: y=n\\) and the vertical line \\(x=n\\) (which we may add to the family of Lemma\u202f1) by two distinct sunny lines \\(L_1,L_2\\) defined as follows:\n\n* \\(L_1\\) passes through \\((1,n)\\) and is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions;\n* \\(L_2\\) passes through \\((n,1)\\) and is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, and \\(L_2\\neq L_1\\).\n\nThe remaining \\(n-2\\) horizontal lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{n-1}\\) cover all points of \\(S_n\\) except possibly \\((1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\), which are covered by \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\) respectively. Thus we obtain a covering with exactly two sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem (Answer to the problem)\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLemma\u202f4 shows that no admissible configuration can contain more than two sunny lines, i.e. \\(k\\le 2\\).  \nLemma\u202f5 exhibits explicit configurations attaining \\(k=0,1,2\\).  \nTherefore the only possible values of \\(k\\) are \\(0,1,2\\). \u220e"
