 \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must lie on at least one of the chosen lines.\nA line is called **sunny** iff it is not parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n(1,0)\\;(x\\text{-axis}),\\qquad (0,1)\\;(y\\text{-axis}),\\qquad (1,-1)\\;(x+y=0).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (A covering with no sunny lines)\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{\\ell_b : y=b\\mid b=1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct horizontal lines, none of which is sunny, and  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal H}L\\supseteq S_n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) then \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (A non\u2011horizontal line meets a row in at most one point)\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a line that is not horizontal and let \\(b\\) be an integer.\nThen  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\in\\mathbb Z\\}|\\le 1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(L\\) contained two distinct points with the same \\(y\\)-coordinate \\(b\\), the segment joining them would be horizontal, forcing \\(L\\) itself to be horizontal, a contradiction. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Rows containing more than one required point)\n\nFor each \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) the row  \n\n\\[\nR_b=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains at least two points of \\(S_n\\); the row \\(R_n\\) contains exactly one point, namely \\((1,n)\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor \\(b\\le n-1\\) we have \\(n+1-b\\ge 2\\); thus the admissible abscissae are\n\\(a=1,2,\\dots ,n+1-b\\), giving at least two points.  \nWhen \\(b=n\\) the inequality \\(a+n\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a=1\\), so only \\((1,n)\\) remains. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Upper bound for the number of sunny lines)\n\nIn any family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(S_n\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n|\\{L\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L\\text{ is sunny}\\}|\\le 1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the \\(n\\) rows \\(R_1,\\dots ,R_n\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f3, each of the rows \\(R_1,\\dots ,R_{n-1}\\) contains at least two points of \\(S_n\\).  \nSuppose a row \\(R_b\\) with \\(b\\le n-1\\) were covered without using a horizontal line.  \nThen every point of \\(R_b\\) would have to be supplied by a non\u2011horizontal line.  \nLemma\u202f2 tells us that each such line can contribute at most one point to \\(R_b\\); therefore we would need at least two distinct non\u2011horizontal lines for the same row, which is impossible because a line is either horizontal or not, and a non\u2011horizontal line cannot be parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis.  \nHence each of the rows \\(R_1,\\dots ,R_{n-1}\\) forces the presence of **at least one horizontal line** in \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L\\) must contain at least \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines.  \nSince \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\), at most one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) can be non\u2011horizontal.  \nAny non\u2011horizontal line is, by definition, sunny (it cannot be vertical or parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) because those directions are also excluded from the definition of \u201csunny\u2019\u2019). Consequently the number of sunny lines is at most one. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5 (Existence of a covering with exactly one sunny line)\n\nFor every \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) that contains exactly one sunny line.\n\n*Proof.*  \nStart from the covering \\(\\mathcal H\\) of Lemma\u202f1.  \nThe horizontal line \\(y=n\\) covers only the single point \\((1,n)\\).  \nChoose any line \\(L\\) that\n\n1. passes through \\((1,n)\\);\n2. is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, th