on**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nWrite the three forbidden directions as  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nd_1 &: \\text{horizontal lines } y=c,\\\\\nd_2 &: \\text{vertical lines } x=c,\\\\\nd_3 &: \\text{lines of slope }-1,\\; x+y=c .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (A zero\u2011sunny configuration)\n\nThere exists a family \\(\\mathcal L^{(0)}=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines, each parallel to one of \\(d_1,d_2,d_3\\), such that  \n\n\\[\nT_n\\subseteq\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\; y = t .\n\\]\n\nThese are \\(n\\) distinct horizontal lines (parallel to \\(d_1\\)).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n\\); hence \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and the point lies on the line \\(y=b\\).  \u220e\n\n\n\nThus the value \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Replacing a non\u2011sunny line by a sunny one)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition, and suppose that \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains a line \\(L\\) parallel to one of the forbidden directions.  \nThen there exists a sunny line \\(S\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(\\bigcup_{M\\in\\mathcal L}M\\bigr)\\setminus L\\;\\cup\\;S\n\\]\n\nstill covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume without loss of generality that \\(L\\) is horizontal, i.e. \\(L:\\;y=c\\) for some integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nP_c=\\{(a,c)\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;a+c\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on \\(L\\).  By definition  \n\n\\[\nP_c=\\{(1,c),(2,c),\\dots ,(n+1-c,c)\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nConsider the line  \n\n\\[\nS:\\; y = -\\frac{1}{2}x + \\Bigl(c+\\frac{1}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIts