induction on \\(k\\).\n\n*Base case \\(k=0\\).*  Lemma\u202f1 provides a suitable family.\n\n*Inductive step.*  Assume a family \\(\\mathcal L^{(k)}\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.  \nIf \\(k=n\\) we are done.  Otherwise \\(\\mathcal L^{(k)}\\) contains at least one non\u2011sunny line.  \nApply Lemma\u202f2 to that line; we obtain a new family \\(\\mathcal L^{(k+1)}\\) in which the number of sunny lines has increased by one while the covering property is preserved.  \nThus a family with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines exists.\n\nBy induction the claim holds for every \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nLemma\u202f3 shows that for any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there is a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required covering condition and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Consequently **every** non\u2011negative integer not exceeding \\(n\\) is admissible.\n\n\u220e"
