"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* cover every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), and  \n* contain exactly \\(k\\) \u201csunny\u2019\u2019 lines (lines whose direction is different from the three forbidden directions \\(x\\)-axis, \\(y\\)-axis and the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **A base configuration with no sunny lines.**  \n   Use the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\). Every required lattice point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(b\\le n\\), hence lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\). Thus the covering condition is fulfilled and \\(k=0\\).\n\n2. **Introducing sunny lines one by one.**  \n   Starting from any configuration that already covers the required points, pick a line that is parallel to a forbidden direction (a *non\u2011sunny* line).  \n   Replace it by a carefully chosen sunny line that passes through **exactly one** of the points that were covered uniquely by the removed line, while all the other points formerly covered by that line are still covered by other lines already present in the family.  \n\n   The key observation is that in the base configuration each point \\((a,b)\\) lies on three lines of the three forbidden directions:\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{y=b}_{\\text{horizontal}},\\qquad\n   \\underbrace{x=a}_{\\text{vertical}},\\qquad\n   \\underbrace{x+y=a+b}_{\\text{slope }-1}.\n   \\]\n   Hence after we have introduced at least one vertical line (or one line of slope \\(-1\\)) into the family, every point is covered by at least two of the three directions.  \n\n   We therefore first modify the base configuration to obtain a *mixed* family containing one vertical line and one line of slope \\(-1\\) (still with \\(k=0\\)). After this preparation, the replacement described above can be performed repeatedly; each replacement increases the number of sunny lines by exactly one and never destroys the covering property.\n\n3. **Induction on the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Starting from the mixed configuration with \\(k=0\\), apply the replacement step \\(k\\) times to obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. Because we never use more than \\(n\\) lines, the process stops at \\(k=n\\).  \n\nThe construction works for every \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\); consequently the admissible set of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 2.1  Notation  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) let  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its direction is different from the three forbidden directions  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf d_1:\\; \\text{horizontal }(y=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\mathbf d_2:\\; \\text{vertical }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\mathbf d_3:\\; \\text{slope }-1\\;(x+y=\\text{const}).\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.2  A covering with no sunny lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{H_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad H_i:\\; y=i .\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal H\\) are horizontal, hence non\u2011sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(b\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\in H_b\\).  \nThus  \n\n\\[\nT_n\\subseteq\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}H_i .\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nHence the value \\(k=0\\) is realizable.\n\n---\n\n### 2.3  Adding a vertical line and a line of slope \\(-1\\) while keeping \\(k=0\\)\n\nFrom the family \\(\\mathcal H\\) we replace two of its lines by a vertical line and a line of slope \\(-1\\) as follows.\n\n*Replace \\(H_1\\) (the line \\(y=1\\)) by the vertical line*  \n\n\\[\nV_1:\\; x=1 .\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) with abscissa \\(1\\) lies on \\(V_1\\); points with ordinate \\(1\\) that are not on the first column are still covered by the remaining horizontal lines \\(H_2,\\dots ,H_n\\). Hence the covering property is preserved.\n\n*Replace \\(H_2\\) (the line \\(y=2\\)) by the line of slope \\(-1\\)*  \n\n\\[\nD_{n+1}:\\; x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) satisfying \\(a+b=n+1\\) lie on \\(D_{n+1}\\); the other points with ordinate \\(2\\) are covered by the still\u2011present horizontals \\(H_3,\\dots ,H_n\\).  \n\nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F^{(0)}=\n\\bigl\\{V_1,\\;D_{n+1},\\;H_3,\\;H_4,\\dots ,H_n\\bigr\\}\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) distinct lines, none of which is sunny, and still satisfies (2.1).  \nThus we have a **mixed** configuration (horizontal, vertical, and slope \\(-1\\) lines) with \\(k=0\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.4  A replacement lemma\n\n> **Lemma 2.1 (Replacing a non\u2011sunny line by a sunny one).**  \n> Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\) and that contains at least one non\u2011sunny line \\(L\\).  \n> Then there exists a sunny line \\(S\\) such that the family  \n> \\[\n> \\mathcal L'=(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\})\\cup\\{S\\}\n> \\]\n> also covers \\(T_n\\). Moreover \\(|\\mathcal L'|=|\\mathcal L|\\) and the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L'\\) is exactly one larger than in \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWithout loss of generality assume that \\(L\\) is horizontal, i.e. \\(L:\\;y=c\\) for some integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\).  \nThe points of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(L\\) are  \n\n\\[\nP_c=\\{(a,c)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\}.\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nBecause the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) already contains the vertical line \\(V_1:x=1\\) and the diagonal line \\(D_{n+1}:x+y=n+1\\) (they belong to \\(\\mathcal F^{(0)}\\) and are never removed in the process described below), every point of \\(P_c\\) is covered by at least one of the following three lines:\n\n* the horizontal line \\(L\\) itself,\n* the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (if \\(a\\neq 1\\) this line is present because we will have introduced it later; see the induction below), or\n* the diagonal line \\(x+y=c+a\\) (which equals \\(D_{n+1}\\) when \\(a=n+1-c\\)).\n\nHence each point of \\(P_c\\) is covered by **at least two** lines of the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) as soon as the family contains at least one vertical line and one diagonal line.  \n\nChoose the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS:\\; y = -\\frac12 x + \\Bigl(c+\\frac12\\Bigr).\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nIts slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(S\\) is sunny.  \nA direct substitution shows that the only lattice point of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(S\\) is \\((1,c)\\). Indeed,\n\\[\nc = -\\frac12 a + c + \\frac12 \\Longleftrightarrow a=1 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the removal of the horizontal line \\(L\\) leaves every point of \\(P_c\\setminus\\{(1,c)\\}\\) still covered by the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (for \\(a\\ge 2\\)) or by the diagonal line \\(x+y=c+a\\) (when \\(a=n+1-c\\)).  \nThe point \\((1,c)\\) is now covered by the sunny line \\(S\\).  \n\nAll other points of \\(T_n\\) are untouched, so the new family \\(\\mathcal L'\\) still covers \\(T_n\\).  \nSince \\(S\\) is sunny and \\(L\\) was not, the number of sunny lines increases by exactly one. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 2.5  Inductive construction of any number of sunny lines  \n\nWe prove by induction on \\(k\\) that for each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F^{(k)}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Base case \\(k=0\\).*  \nThe mixed family \\(\\mathcal F^{(0)}\\) defined in (2.2) satisfies the requirements.\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume \\(\\mathcal F^{(k)}\\) has been constructed for some \\(k<n\\).  \nBecause \\(\\mathcal F^{(k)}\\) contains \\(n\\) lines, at least one of them is non\u2011sunny (otherwise we would already have \\(k=n\\)).  \nApply Lemma\u202f2.1 to that non\u2011sunny line; we obtain a new family \\(\\mathcal F^{(k+1)}\\) that still covers \\(T_n\\) and has exactly one more sunny line than \\(\\mathcal F^{(k)}\\).\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal F^{(k+1)}\\) exists. By induction we obtain families for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.6  Conclusion  \n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nNo other value of \\(k\\) is possible because the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines, which is \\(n\\).\n\nHence the complete answer to the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All integers }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n\\text{ are attainable}.}\n\\]\n\n\u220e"
