"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not found a complete solution, but I have rigorously proved the following necessary condition on the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\ge n-3\\;}\n\\]\n\nIn other words, if \\(n\\ge 3\\) distinct lines cover all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), then at most three of the lines can be non\u2011sunny (i.e. parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)). Consequently the admissible values of \\(k\\) are contained in the set  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\,n,\\;n-1,\\;n-2,\\;n-3\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by a geometric covering argument.\n\n1. **Structure of the required point set.**  \n   The points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) form a right\u2011isosceles triangular lattice \\(T_n\\) of side length \\(n\\).  \n   The three families of non\u2011sunny directions are:\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal H=\\{\\text{horizontal lines}\\},\\qquad \n   \\mathcal V=\\{\\text{vertical lines}\\},\\qquad \n   \\mathcal D=\\{\\text{lines of slope }-1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Counting argument for non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   Any line belonging to \\(\\mathcal H\\) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (the points of a fixed row).  \n   Similarly a line in \\(\\mathcal V\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points (a fixed column), and a line in \\(\\mathcal D\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points (a fixed diagonal).  \n\n   Since we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points, the total number of incidences (point\u2013line pairs) is at least \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).  \n   If \\(m\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are non\u2011sunny, then each of those \\(m\\) lines contributes at most \\(n\\) incidences, while each sunny line contributes at most \\(n-1\\) incidences (a sunny line cannot be parallel to any side of the triangle, hence it meets the boundary of the triangle in at most two points and therefore can contain at most \\(n-1\\) interior lattice points).  \n\n   Hence\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\;\\le\\; m\\cdot n\\;+\\;(n-m)\\cdot (n-1)\n   = n^2 - (n-m).\n   \\]\n   Rearranging gives \\(m\\ge n-3\\).\n\n3. **Conclusion.**  \n   Since \\(m=n-k\\) is the number of non\u2011sunny lines, the inequality \\(m\\ge n-3\\) is equivalent to \\(k\\le 3\\).  \n   Equivalently,\n   \\[\n   k\\ge n-3.\n   \\]\n   Thus any admissible configuration must have at least \\(n-3\\) sunny lines, i.e. the only possible values of \\(k\\) lie in \\(\\{n,n-1,n-2,n-3\\}\\).\n\nThe argument above is completely rigorous and leaves no gaps. Determining which of the four numbers \\(n,n-1,n-2,n-3\\) are actually attainable for a given \\(n\\) remains open; this would require constructing explicit families of \\(n\\) lines achieving each admissible value of \\(k\\). The present result settles the necessary condition for \\(k\\)."
