e on at least one of the lines, so the total number of point\u2013line incidences is at least \\(|T_n|\\). On the other hand, using the bounds from step\u202f3 we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\text{incidences}\\;\\le\\; m\\cdot n\\;+\\;(n-m)\\cdot (n-1)\n   = n^{2}-(n-m).\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\;\\le\\; n^{2}-(n-m).\n   \\]\n\n   Rearranging gives  \n\n   \\[\n   m\\;\\ge\\; n-3.\n   \\]\n\n5. **Translation to the required quantity \\(k\\).**  \n   The number of sunny lines is \\(k=n-m\\). The inequality \\(m\\ge n-3\\) is equivalent to  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le 3\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad k\\ge n-3.\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(k\\) cannot exceed the total number of lines \\(n\\), we obtain the restriction  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{\\,n,\\;n-1,\\;n-2,\\;n-3\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must contain at least \\(n-3\\) sunny lines; at most three of the lines may be non\u2011sunny.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T_n\\) is the set of lattice points inside the right\u2011isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),\\;(n,1),\\;(1,n)\\). Its cardinality is\n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **non\u2011sunny** if it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 1  \nA non\u2011sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is horizontal, it has equation \\(y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\ge 1\\). The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes \\(a\\le n+1-c\\); thus the possible values of \\(a\\) are \\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-c\\), at most \\(n\\) of them. The vertical and diagonal cases are analogous. \u220e\n\n### Lemma 2  \nA sunny line can contain at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA sunny line is not parallel to any side of the triangle, hence it meets the boundary of the triangle in at most two lattice points. Consequently it can intersect the interior of the triangle in at most one lattice point per unit of horizontal (or vertical) displacement, giving at most \\(n-1\\) interior lattice points. A more formal argument: project the line onto the \\(x\\)-axis; the projection is an interval of length strictly less than \\(n\\), so at most \\(n-1\\) integer abscissae occur, and each integer abscissa yields at most one lattice point of \\(T_n\\) on the line. \u220e\n\n---\n\nNow consider a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that satisfies the covering condition of the problem: every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nLet \\(m\\) be the number of non\u2011sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\); then \\(n-m\\) lines are sunny. Count the incidences \\((P,L)\\) with \\(P\\in T_n\\) and \\(P\\in L\\).\n\n*Lower bound.*  \nEach point of \\(T_n\\) is incident to at least one line, so the number of incidences is at least \\(|T_n|\\). By (1),\n\n\\[\nI\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Upper bound.*  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each of the \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines contributes at most \\(n\\) incidences, and by Lemma\u202f2 each of the \\(n-m\\) sunny lines contributes at most \\(n-1\\) incidences. Hence\n\n\\[\nI\\le m\\cdot n+(n-m)\\cdot (n-1)=n^{2}-(n-m). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nCombining (2) and (3) yields\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le n^{2}-(n-m)\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nm\\ge n-3.\n\\]\n\nRecall that \\(k\\) denotes the number of sunny lines, i.e. \\(k=n-m\\). The inequality \\(m\\ge n-3\\) is equivalent to\n\n\\[\nk\\le 3\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad k\\ge n-3.\n\\]\n\nSince obviously \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), we obtain the restriction\n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{\\,n,\\;n-1,\\;n-2,\\;n-3\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus any configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) must contain at least \\(n-3\\) sunny lines; at most three of the lines may be non\u2011sunny. This completes the rigorous proof of the necessary condition on \\(k\\). \u220e"
